  ($lp$PRIN-01-001$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 1, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 1, $lp$A licensed salesperson in Arcadia closes a sale and the grateful buyer offers to pay her a $500 bonus directly. The salesperson may lawfully accept compensation for this licensed activity from:$lp$, $lp$["The buyer, if disclosed to all parties in writing", "Her employing broker only", "Escrow, if both brokers consent", "Any party to the transaction after closing"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Disclosure does not cure the problem — the law bars a salesperson from taking compensation for licensed activity from anyone but her broker.", "Correct. A salesperson may receive compensation for licensed activity only from her employing broker; the buyer could pay the bonus through the broker.", "Escrow pays brokerages, not salespersons; broker consent does not authorize direct payment to a salesperson.", "Timing is irrelevant — after closing or before, the payment must flow through the employing broker."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["salesperson", "broker"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-01-002$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 1, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 1, $lp$The California Real Estate Commissioner's primary responsibility is to:$lp$, $lp$["Resolve deposit disputes between buyers and sellers", "Set maximum commission rates for residential sales", "Enforce the Real Estate Law for the protection of the public", "Represent licensees' interests before the Legislature"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["Private contract disputes belong in the courts; the Commissioner disciplines licensees but does not adjudicate deposits.", "Commissions are always negotiable; no government body sets rates, and price-fixing would violate antitrust law.", "Correct. The Commissioner heads the DRE and enforces the Real Estate Law and Regulations to protect consumers.", "The DRE exists to protect the public, not to advocate for licensees — that is the role of trade associations."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["Real Estate Commissioner", "DRE"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-01-003$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 1, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 1, $lp$Which statement about the term 'Realtor®' is TRUE?$lp$, $lp$["All California licensees are Realtors®", "It is a license classification issued by the DRE", "It designates a licensee who belongs to the National Association of Realtors", "Only brokers may use the designation"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["Membership is voluntary; many licensees never join NAR and may not use the mark.", "The DRE issues salesperson and broker licenses; 'Realtor®' is a private trade-association mark, not a license.", "Correct. Realtor® identifies a NAR member bound by its Code of Ethics — a designation layered on top of the state license.", "Both salespersons and brokers may join NAR and use the designation."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["Realtor®", "NAR"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-01-004$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 1, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$Which of the following persons needs a California real estate license for the described activity?$lp$, $lp$["An owner selling her own Glendora duplex", "An attorney reviewing a purchase contract for a client in his law practice", "A trustee conducting a trustee's sale under a deed of trust", "A neighbor who, for a fee, finds tenants for several nearby rental owners"]$lp$::jsonb, 3, $lp$["Owners dealing with their own property are exempt — the activity is not 'for another.'", "Attorneys performing legal services in the ordinary course of practice are exempt.", "Trustees selling under a power of sale act under the trust deed's authority and are exempt.", "Correct. Soliciting and placing tenants for others for compensation is licensed activity; doing it repeatedly for fees requires a license."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["licensed activity"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-01-005$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 1, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 1, $lp$The California salesperson exam consists of:$lp$, $lp$["100 questions, 3 hours, 75% to pass", "150 questions, 3 hours 15 minutes, 70% to pass", "200 questions, 4 hours, 70% to pass", "150 questions, 3 hours 15 minutes, 75% to pass"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Wrong count, time, and passing score — these figures describe no California exam.", "Correct. 150 multiple-choice questions, 3 hours 15 minutes, and 70% (105 correct) to pass.", "200 questions and 4 hours describe the broker exam format, not the salesperson exam.", "The question count and time are right, but the passing score is 70%, not 75%."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$[]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-01-006$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 1, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$A salesperson's license was issued on June 1. It will expire, unless renewed, after:$lp$, $lp$["One year", "Two years", "Four years", "It does not expire while the licensee remains with the same broker"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["There is no one-year license term in California.", "Two years is a common term in other states — a classic distractor — but not California's.", "Correct. California real estate licenses run four years and renew with 45 hours of approved continuing education.", "Every license expires on schedule regardless of employment; staying with a broker does not extend it."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$[]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-01-007$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 1, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$An advertisement for a La Verne listing shows only a phone number and the words 'Charming 3BR — priced to sell — call today!' The ad is placed by a salesperson. This advertisement is best described as:$lp$, $lp$["Lawful, because ads need not identify the property owner", "Lawful puffing", "A prohibited blind ad, because it conceals that the advertiser is a licensee", "Permissible if the broker approved the wording"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["The defect is not about the owner's identity — it is that the ad hides the licensee/agency status.", "Puffing concerns exaggerated opinion about the property; this issue is concealment of licensed status.", "Correct. An ad by a licensee that fails to reveal the licensee is acting as an agent is a blind ad and is prohibited.", "Broker approval cannot authorize an ad that violates the disclosure regulations."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["blind ad"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-01-008$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 1, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$A buyer wins a $90,000 fraud judgment against a now-bankrupt licensee and cannot collect. The buyer's best recourse through the DRE is:$lp$, $lp$["Petitioning the Commissioner to garnish the licensee's future commissions", "Filing an application against the Consumer Recovery Account", "Requesting the DRE reissue the judgment as an administrative fine", "Asking NAR to pay the judgment from its ethics fund"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["The Commissioner has no garnishment power over private judgments.", "Correct. The Consumer Recovery Account pays victims of licensee fraud who hold uncollectible judgments, up to statutory limits, and suspends the licensee's license until repayment.", "The DRE cannot convert a civil judgment into its own fine.", "NAR is a trade association with no fund for paying members' fraud judgments."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["Consumer Recovery Account"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-01-009$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 1, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 3, $lp$A Covina home sells for $940,000. Total commission is 5%, split evenly between listing and selling sides. The buyer's-side salesperson keeps 60% of his side under his broker agreement. The salesperson receives:$lp$, $lp$["$47,000", "$23,500", "$14,100", "$28,200"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["$47,000 is the total commission (940,000 × 5%) — the calculation stopped too early.", "$23,500 is one side's share (half the total) — the broker split has not been applied yet.", "Correct. 940,000 × 0.05 = 47,000; ÷ 2 = 23,500 per side; × 0.60 = $14,100 to the salesperson.", "$28,200 applies 60% to the full commission instead of to one side."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["commission"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-01-010$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 1, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$For supervision under the Real Estate Law, a salesperson working at a brokerage on a 1099 independent-contractor basis is treated as:$lp$, $lp$["An independent principal responsible only for her own conduct", "The employee of her responsible broker", "A subagent of every client of the brokerage", "A co-broker with limited authority"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Tax status does not remove the broker's supervision duty or responsibility to the public.", "Correct. Regardless of independent-contractor tax treatment, license law treats the salesperson as the broker's employee for supervision and accountability.", "Subagency describes agency relationships with clients, not the broker–salesperson employment relationship.", "'Co-broker' is not a license relationship; a salesperson always acts under one responsible broker."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["responsible broker"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-01-011$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 1, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$A licensee from Texas moves to San Dimas and wants to sell homes in California. She must:$lp$, $lp$["Convert her Texas license under the interstate reciprocity compact", "Qualify for and pass the California exam like any other applicant", "Practice under a California broker for one year, then exchange licenses", "Register her Texas license with the DRE and pay a transfer fee"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["California belongs to no reciprocity compact for real estate licenses.", "Correct. California has no license reciprocity with any state; every applicant must meet California's education, application, and exam requirements.", "There is no supervised-practice pathway to exchanging an out-of-state license.", "The DRE has no registration or transfer process for out-of-state licenses."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["reciprocity"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-01-012$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 1, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$Which task may an UNLICENSED assistant at a West Covina brokerage lawfully perform?$lp$, $lp$["Hosting an open house alone and quoting the seller's bottom-line price to visitors", "Preparing and mailing the broker's marketing flyers at the broker's direction", "Calling expired listings to solicit new business for the team", "Negotiating a repair credit with the buyer's agent"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Discussing price and terms with prospects is licensed activity; an unlicensed person cannot host and negotiate alone.", "Correct. Clerical and administrative work — preparing and mailing marketing at a licensee's direction — does not require a license.", "Soliciting listings is squarely licensed activity.", "Negotiating terms, including repair credits, requires a license."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["licensed activity"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-01-013$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 1, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 3, $lp$An unlicensed 'consultant' negotiated the sale of an Upland fourplex for an investor who promised him 2% of the price. The investor now refuses to pay. If the consultant sues for the fee, a court will most likely:$lp$, $lp$["Award the fee, because the services were actually performed", "Award a reduced fee in quantum meruit", "Deny recovery, because he cannot lawfully collect compensation for unlicensed activity", "Refer the dispute to the Real Estate Commissioner for arbitration"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["Performance does not matter; the law refuses to enforce fee claims for unlicensed licensed-activity.", "Courts do not grant fallback recovery for activity that required a license the plaintiff lacked.", "Correct. A person must be licensed at the time of the act to maintain an action for compensation for licensed activity; the claim fails, and the conduct may also be criminally punishable.", "The Commissioner disciplines licensees; he does not arbitrate private fee disputes."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["licensed activity"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-01-014$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 1, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 1, $lp$To sit for the California salesperson examination, an applicant must first complete:$lp$, $lp$["Real Estate Principles only", "Real Estate Principles, Real Estate Practice, and one approved elective", "Any five college-level real estate courses", "Real Estate Practice and two years of experience"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Principles alone was sufficient decades ago — today all three statutory courses are required before the exam.", "Correct. Three 45-hour courses: Principles, Practice, and one elective (such as Legal Aspects of Real Estate).", "Five courses overshoots the salesperson requirement (eight courses is the broker education standard).", "Experience is a broker requirement; salesperson applicants need education, not experience."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$[]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-01-015$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 1, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$Broker Lin wants to run her Ontario brokerage as 'Inland Empire Home Experts.' Before advertising under that name she must:$lp$, $lp$["Record the name with the county recorder only", "Obtain a license from the DRE issued under that fictitious business name", "Register the name with NAR", "Nothing — brokers may use any trade name at will"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["A county FBN filing may also be needed for business law purposes, but license law requires more.", "Correct. A broker may operate under a fictitious business name only if the DRE has licensed the broker under that name.", "NAR governs the Realtor® mark, not trade-name licensing.", "Operating under an unlicensed DBA violates the Real Estate Law."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["fictitious business name (DBA)"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-01-016$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 1, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$The formal pleading that begins a DRE disciplinary proceeding against a CURRENT licensee is called a(n):$lp$, $lp$["Statement of issues", "Accusation", "Desist and refrain order", "Citation of record"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["A statement of issues is filed against an applicant seeking a license, not an existing licensee.", "Correct. Discipline against an existing licensee begins with an accusation, heard under the Administrative Procedure Act.", "A desist and refrain order halts specific conduct; it is not the disciplinary pleading itself.", "'Citation of record' is not a DRE disciplinary instrument."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["accusation"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-01-017$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 1, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$Which statement about real estate commissions in California is TRUE?$lp$, $lp$["The DRE publishes a schedule of customary rates by county", "Commission rates are set by local Realtor® associations", "Rates are fully negotiable between the client and the broker", "Residential commissions are capped at 6% by statute"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["No government schedule of rates exists; publishing one would encourage illegal price-fixing.", "An association setting rates would be a textbook antitrust violation.", "Correct. Commissions are always negotiable; certain contracts must even carry a notice stating that rates are not fixed by law.", "No statute caps residential commission rates."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["commission"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-01-018$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 1, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 3, $lp$Salesperson Diego, supervised by Broker Ruiz, drafts a two-page custom addendum creating a seller carry-back with novel default remedies for a Pomona sale, explaining the legal effects to both parties. Diego has most likely:$lp$, $lp$["Acted properly, because licensees may complete transaction documents", "Engaged in the unauthorized practice of law", "Acted properly if Broker Ruiz reviews the addendum", "Violated the statute of frauds"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Licensees may fill in blanks on standard forms incident to a transaction — drafting novel legal provisions and advising on legal effect goes beyond that.", "Correct. Drafting custom legal language and advising parties on its legal consequences is practicing law, which a real estate license does not authorize.", "Broker review does not convert legal drafting into permitted licensed activity.", "The statute of frauds concerns which contracts must be written; it is not violated by drafting one."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$[]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-02-001$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 2, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 1, $lp$Which of the following is classified as REAL property?$lp$, $lp$["A harvested avocado crop stored in the barn", "A mobile home resting on rented land, registered with HCD", "An orange tree growing in the backyard", "Patio furniture arranged around a built-in fire pit"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["Once severed (harvested), crops are personal property.", "A mobile home not permanently affixed on a foundation remains personal property.", "Correct. Growing trees attached by roots are part of the land — real property until severed.", "Furniture rests by its own weight and is movable — personal property (the built-in fire pit, by contrast, is real)."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["real property", "severance"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-02-002$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 2, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 1, $lp$Real property is transferred by deed. Personal property is transferred by:$lp$, $lp$["Assignment", "Bill of sale", "Novation", "Quitclaim"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Assignment transfers contract rights, not title to chattels generally.", "Correct. A bill of sale is the instrument that conveys personal property.", "Novation substitutes a new contract or party; it conveys nothing.", "A quitclaim is a deed — it transfers interests in real property."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["personal property"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-02-003$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 2, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$In the MARIA fixture analysis, which factor, when present, overrides all the others?$lp$, $lp$["The method of attachment", "The intention of the person attaching the item", "A written agreement between the parties", "The item's adaptation to the property"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["Attachment is evidence, but it yields to the parties' contract.", "Intention is the most important test courts apply in the absence of an agreement — but an agreement still controls.", "Correct. The parties may agree to treat an item as real or personal, and that agreement governs between them regardless of the other tests.", "Adaptation is persuasive evidence only."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["MARIA", "fixture"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-02-004$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 2, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$A dry-cleaning tenant bolted pressing machines to the floor of a leased Duarte storefront. Two weeks before the lease expires, the tenant unbolts and removes them, patching the bolt holes. The machines are:$lp$, $lp$["Fixtures that unlawfully damaged the landlord's real property", "Trade fixtures the tenant properly removed", "The landlord's property by accession", "Emblements"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Attachment alone does not make a business tenant's equipment part of the realty.", "Correct. Equipment installed by a business tenant for its trade remains the tenant's personal property and may be removed before the lease ends, repairing removal damage.", "Accession would apply only if the tenant abandoned the machines after the tenancy ended.", "Emblements are cultivated annual crops, not equipment."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["trade fixture", "accession"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-02-005$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 2, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$A tenant farmer's lease on Chino farmland ends October 1, but his strawberry crop — planted and tended by him — ripens in November. The crop belongs to:$lp$, $lp$["The landlord, because the lease ended before harvest", "The tenant, because emblements are the cultivator's personal property", "The landlord and tenant equally", "Whoever records a claim first"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["The end of the tenancy does not forfeit crops the tenant's labor produced.", "Correct. Annual cultivated crops (fructus industriales) are emblements — the tenant's personal property, with a right to re-enter and harvest.", "No equal-split doctrine applies to emblements.", "Crop ownership does not depend on recording."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["emblements"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-02-006$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 2, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 1, $lp$An owner whose land borders the San Gabriel River holds what type of water rights?$lp$, $lp$["Littoral", "Appropriative", "Riparian", "Prescriptive"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["Littoral rights attach to land on a lake or the ocean — non-flowing water.", "Appropriative rights are permit-based diversions for non-riparian land.", "Correct. Land bordering a flowing watercourse carries riparian rights, subject to reasonable and beneficial use.", "Prescription is a way to acquire an easement, not a water-rights class."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["riparian rights", "littoral rights"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-02-007$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 2, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$A winter storm suddenly shifts a creek channel, tearing away a strip of a Glendora parcel. This event is called ______, and the boundary ______.$lp$, $lp$["Accretion; moves with the new channel", "Avulsion; does not immediately change", "Reliction; moves to the old high-water mark", "Erosion; is fixed by the county surveyor"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Accretion is gradual gain of soil, not sudden loss, and this event was sudden.", "Correct. A sudden tearing away of land by water is avulsion, and unlike gradual processes it does not immediately shift the legal boundary.", "Reliction is land exposed by receding water.", "Erosion is gradual; surveyors document boundaries but a sudden shift is avulsion."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["avulsion", "accretion"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-02-008$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 2, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 1, $lp$Soil gradually deposited on a riverside parcel by the current is called:$lp$, $lp$["Alluvium", "Avulsion", "Reliction", "Dedication"]$lp$::jsonb, 0, $lp$["Correct. Alluvium is the soil deposited through accretion; the owner gains it.", "Avulsion is a sudden change, not gradual deposit.", "Reliction is land uncovered by receding water.", "Dedication is a gift of private land for public use."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["accretion / alluvium"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-02-009$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 2, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$Which of the following is an appurtenance that passes automatically with the land even if the deed does not mention it?$lp$, $lp$["The seller's stock in a mutual water company serving the parcel", "The seller's shares in a publicly traded water utility", "The seller's tractor stored on the parcel", "A month-to-month rental agreement for the seller's other property"]$lp$::jsonb, 0, $lp$["Correct. Mutual water company stock is appurtenant to the land it serves and transfers with it, inseparable from the parcel.", "Ordinary corporate shares are personal property with no tie to the parcel.", "Equipment is personal property; it passes only by agreement.", "A contract about different land is not appurtenant to this parcel."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["appurtenance"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-02-010$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 2, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$A developer excavating at the lot line causes the neighbor's soil to slump into the pit. The developer has violated the neighbor's right of:$lp$, $lp$["Subjacent support", "Lateral support", "Riparian use", "Quiet enjoyment"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Subjacent support concerns support from beneath, as when mineral rights are severed.", "Correct. Adjoining owners owe each other lateral support; excavation that collapses neighboring land breaches that duty.", "No watercourse is involved.", "Quiet enjoyment is a tenant's right against the landlord, not a boundary-support doctrine."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["lateral / subjacent support"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-02-011$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 2, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 3, $lp$A Pasadena seller's contract is silent about the antique dining-room chandelier, which is hard-wired and mounted to a ceiling medallion installed for it. Before closing, the seller replaces it with a basic light and takes the antique. The BEST analysis is:$lp$, $lp$["Proper, because chandeliers are always personal property", "Proper, because the seller replaced it with a working fixture", "Improper, because the chandelier was a fixture and the contract did not exclude it", "Improper, because used fixtures may never be replaced before closing"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["Hard-wired, adapted light fixtures are classic fixtures, not automatically personal.", "Substituting different property does not cure removing a fixture the buyer contracted to receive.", "Correct. By method of attachment and adaptation the chandelier was real property; silent contract means it passes with the house, and removal breaches the agreement.", "Sellers may replace items when the contract excludes them — the flaw here is the missing exclusion, not replacement itself."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["fixture", "MARIA"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-02-012$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 2, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$After a 1950s deed severed and reserved the mineral estate under an Upland parcel, the mineral rights are:$lp$, $lp$["Personal property of the prior owner", "A separate real property interest owned apart from the surface", "Extinguished if unused for five years", "Automatically reunited with the surface upon resale"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Minerals in place are real property; they become personal only when extracted.", "Correct. A severed mineral estate is its own real property interest, which can be owned, sold, and inherited separately from the surface.", "Mere non-use does not extinguish a mineral estate (statutory dormant-mineral procedures require formal action).", "Resale of the surface conveys only what the seller owns — not the previously reserved minerals."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["severance", "real property"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-02-013$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 2, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$In a close dispute over whether an installed item passes with the property, courts tend to favor:$lp$, $lp$["Seller over buyer, and landlord over tenant", "Buyer over seller, and tenant over landlord", "Whichever party installed the item", "Neither party; the item is sold at auction"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["The presumptions run the other way.", "Correct. Under the relationship-of-the-parties factor, close calls favor buyer over seller and tenant over landlord.", "Installation matters to intention but does not create an automatic preference.", "There is no auction rule for fixture disputes."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["MARIA"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-02-014$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 2, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$A condominium owner's interest is best described as:$lp$, $lp$["A leasehold in airspace plus a license to use common areas", "Personal property, because the unit cannot be moved", "Fee ownership of a unit of airspace plus an undivided interest in common areas", "A share of stock entitling the holder to occupy a unit"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["Condominium ownership is a freehold, not a leasehold or license.", "Immovability is a hallmark of real, not personal, property.", "Correct. A condo is real property: fee title to defined airspace (the unit) together with an undivided interest in the common areas.", "Stock plus occupancy right describes a cooperative, not a condominium."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["real property"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-02-015$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 2, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 3, $lp$A café tenant vacates a Montclair storefront when its lease expires, leaving its plumbed-in espresso machine behind. Six weeks later it returns to collect the machine. Who owns it?$lp$, $lp$["The tenant, because trade fixtures are always personal property", "The landlord, because trade fixtures left after the tenancy generally pass to the landlord", "The tenant, if it pays reasonable storage fees", "The city, as abandoned property"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Trade fixtures are the tenant's personal property only while the removal right lasts — through the end of the tenancy.", "Correct. Trade fixtures not removed by the end of the tenancy are treated as abandoned to the landlord by accession.", "Paying storage does not revive an expired removal right.", "Municipal abandoned-property rules do not apply to fixtures left on private leased premises."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["trade fixture", "accession"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-02-016$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 2, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 3, $lp$A buyer's contract for a Whittier home includes 'all attached floor coverings and window treatments.' At walk-through, custom-cut wall-to-wall carpet remains but the seller took the custom plantation shutters, leaving mounting frames. Which item was the seller entitled to take?$lp$, $lp$["The shutters, because they detach from their frames", "The carpet, because it can be rolled up", "Neither — both were fixtures and both were included by the agreement", "Both, because custom items reflect the seller's personal taste"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["Detachability does not defeat classification: shutters custom-fitted and mounted to the windows are adapted fixtures — and 'window treatments' were expressly included.", "Custom-cut installed carpet is adapted to the property and was expressly included as an attached floor covering.", "Correct. Under MARIA both items lean real (adaptation, attachment), and the agreement — which controls — included both. The seller breached by removing the shutters.", "Personal taste is not a MARIA factor; intention is judged objectively."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["MARIA", "fixture"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-03-001$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 3, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 1, $lp$Which of the following is a freehold estate?$lp$, $lp$["An estate for years", "A periodic tenancy", "A life estate", "An estate at sufferance"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["An estate for years is a leasehold — a less-than-freehold estate.", "A periodic tenancy is a leasehold that renews period to period.", "Correct. Freehold estates are ownership interests of indefinite duration: fee estates and life estates.", "An estate at sufferance is the weakest leasehold interest, not a freehold."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["freehold estate", "life estate"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-03-002$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 3, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 1, $lp$A deed conveying property with no qualifying or limiting language is presumed to transfer:$lp$, $lp$["A life estate", "Fee simple absolute", "A fee simple determinable", "A leasehold"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["A life estate must be expressly created; it is never presumed.", "Correct. California presumes a grantor conveys the largest estate owned — normally fee simple absolute — unless the deed says otherwise.", "A defeasible fee requires express conditional or durational language.", "A deed conveys title, not mere possession; leaseholds arise from leases."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["fee simple absolute"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-03-003$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 3, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$A grantor deeds land 'to the City, so long as the parcel is used as a public park.' The grantor has created a fee simple determinable and retained a:$lp$, $lp$["Right of re-entry", "Possibility of reverter", "Remainder", "Leased fee"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["A right of re-entry follows a fee simple subject to a condition subsequent, signaled by 'provided that' or 'but if.'", "Correct. Durational language ('so long as') creates a determinable fee that ends automatically, leaving the grantor a possibility of reverter.", "A remainder is held by a third party, not the grantor.", "A leased fee is a landlord's interest in leased property — no lease exists here."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["fee simple determinable", "possibility of reverter"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-03-004$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 3, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$A deed reads 'to Whittier College, provided that the property is used for classroom instruction; if not, the grantor may re-enter and terminate.' This estate is a:$lp$, $lp$["Fee simple determinable", "Fee simple subject to a condition subsequent", "Life estate pur autre vie", "Fee simple absolute"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["A determinable fee uses durational words and ends automatically; this deed requires the grantor to act.", "Correct. 'Provided that' plus an express re-entry power creates a fee simple subject to a condition subsequent, which the grantor must enforce to reclaim title.", "No measuring life is involved.", "The conditional language defeats absolute ownership."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["fee simple subject to condition subsequent", "right of re-entry (power of termination)"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-03-005$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 3, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$A deed grants property 'to Rosa for life, then to David.' David's interest is called a:$lp$, $lp$["Reversion", "Remainder", "Possibility of reverter", "Right of re-entry"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["A reversion returns to the grantor; David is a third party.", "Correct. A future interest given to a third party to take possession when a life estate ends is a remainder, and David is the remainderman.", "A possibility of reverter follows a determinable fee, not a life estate.", "A right of re-entry follows a condition subsequent."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["remainder / remainderman"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-03-006$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 3, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$A deed grants property 'to Rosa for life.' Nothing more is said. When Rosa dies, the property goes to:$lp$, $lp$["Rosa's heirs", "The grantor or the grantor's successors, by reversion", "The state, by escheat", "Whoever is in possession at Rosa's death"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["A life estate ends at death; it does not pass to the life tenant's heirs.", "Correct. With no third party named, the grantor retained a reversion and the property returns to the grantor or the grantor's successors.", "Escheat applies only when an owner dies intestate with no heirs — not here.", "Possession does not determine title."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["reversion", "life estate"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-03-007$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 3, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 3, $lp$Elena holds a life estate measured by her mother's life ('to Elena for the life of her mother'). Elena dies before her mother. Elena's interest:$lp$, $lp$["Terminates immediately at Elena's death", "Passes to Elena's heirs until her mother dies", "Converts to a fee simple absolute in Elena's estate", "Reverts to the grantor immediately"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Termination happens at the death of the *measuring* life, which is the mother, not Elena.", "Correct. In a life estate pur autre vie, the estate lasts until the measuring life ends; if the holder dies first, the remaining interest passes to the holder's heirs.", "No mechanism converts a life estate into a fee on the holder's death.", "The grantor's reversion (if any) takes effect only when the measuring life ends."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["pur autre vie", "life estate"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-03-008$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 3, $lp$transfer$lp$, 3, $lp$A life tenant sells 'the property' to a buyer by grant deed, without the remainderman joining. The buyer receives:$lp$, $lp$["Fee simple absolute, because a grant deed conveys full title", "An estate that ends when the measuring life ends", "Nothing; the deed is void", "A tenancy in common with the remainderman"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["The form of deed cannot enlarge what the grantor owns.", "Correct. No one conveys more than they hold: the buyer takes the life estate, which terminates when the measuring life ends, and the remainderman then takes possession.", "The deed is valid — it simply conveys only the life estate.", "No co-ownership arises; the interests are successive, not concurrent."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["life estate", "remainder / remainderman"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-03-009$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 3, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$A life tenant stops paying property taxes and lets the roof fail, causing structural damage. The remainderman's claim against the life tenant is for:$lp$, $lp$["Waste", "Constructive eviction", "Adverse possession", "Encroachment"]$lp$::jsonb, 0, $lp$["Correct. A life tenant owes a duty not to commit waste — permanent injury harming the future interest holder — and is generally responsible for taxes and ordinary repairs.", "Constructive eviction is a tenant's remedy against a landlord.", "Adverse possession is a way to acquire title by hostile possession, not a claim about neglect.", "Encroachment is a physical intrusion across a boundary."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["waste", "life tenant"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-03-010$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 3, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$A commercial lease runs from July 1, 2026 to June 30, 2031. To end the tenancy on the final day, the landlord must:$lp$, $lp$["Give 30 days' written notice", "Give 60 days' written notice", "Do nothing; the estate for years terminates automatically", "File an unlawful detainer action before expiration"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["Notice requirements apply to periodic tenancies, not fixed-term leases.", "60-day notice is a residential periodic-tenancy concept.", "Correct. An estate for years has definite start and end dates and terminates automatically on the end date without notice from either party.", "Unlawful detainer is the remedy for a tenant who refuses to leave, not a prerequisite to expiration."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["estate for years"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-03-011$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 3, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$A tenant whose one-year lease expired refuses to move out and keeps mailing rent checks the landlord has not cashed. The tenant now holds:$lp$, $lp$["A periodic tenancy", "An estate at sufferance", "An estate at will", "No interest; the tenant is a trespasser"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["A periodic tenancy would require the landlord's acceptance of rent creating a new arrangement; unaccepted checks do not establish it.", "Correct. A tenant who entered lawfully but remains after the right to possession ended holds an estate at sufferance — a holdover, removable by unlawful detainer.", "An estate at will requires the landlord's permission, which is absent here.", "The tenant is not a trespasser because the original entry was lawful."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["estate at sufferance", "unlawful detainer"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-03-012$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 3, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 1, $lp$A landlord facing a holdover tenant in California may lawfully:$lp$, $lp$["Change the locks after giving 24 hours' notice", "Remove the unit's front door to encourage departure", "Shut off water and electricity to the unit", "File an unlawful detainer action"]$lp$::jsonb, 3, $lp$["Lockouts are prohibited self-help regardless of notice.", "Removing doors is a classic illegal self-help eviction exposing the landlord to damages.", "Utility shutoffs to force a tenant out are unlawful.", "Correct. Unlawful detainer is California's summary court action and the only lawful route to recover possession."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["unlawful detainer", "estate at sufferance"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-03-013$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 3, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 1, $lp$In the memory aid PETE, which government power allows the state to take title when an owner dies without a will and without heirs?$lp$, $lp$["Police power", "Eminent domain", "Taxation", "Escheat"]$lp$::jsonb, 3, $lp$["Police power regulates use for public health, safety, and welfare.", "Eminent domain is a taking for public use with just compensation.", "Taxation supports the property tax lien.", "Correct. Escheat transfers property to the state when an owner dies intestate with no heirs."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["PETE", "escheat"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-03-014$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 3, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$A landlord's interest in property that is currently subject to a ten-year lease is called the:$lp$, $lp$["Leasehold", "Leased fee", "Remainder", "Estate at will"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["The leasehold is the tenant's possessory interest.", "Correct. The landlord retains the leased fee — ownership burdened by the tenant's right of possession.", "A remainder follows a life estate, not a lease.", "An estate at will is a type of tenancy, not the landlord's ownership interest."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["leased fee", "less-than-freehold (leasehold) estate"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-03-015$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 3, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 3, $lp$A five-year lease starts at $20,000/month with 4% increases on each anniversary. The monthly rent during the THIRD year is:$lp$, $lp$["$21,600.00", "$22,400.00", "$21,632.00", "$20,800.00"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["$21,600 applies two simple 4% increases to the original rent (20,000 × 1.08) rather than compounding.", "$22,400 applies three increases (20,000 × 1.12) — one too many for year three.", "Correct. Year 2 = 20,000 × 1.04 = 20,800; Year 3 = 20,800 × 1.04 = $21,632.00. Percentage escalations compound on the prior year's rent.", "$20,800 is the year-two rent, one increase short."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$[]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-03-016$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 3, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 3, $lp$A tenant takes possession of a $3,000/month apartment on the 21st of a 30-day month, owing rent only for days occupied including the day of possession. The prorated rent due is:$lp$, $lp$["$900", "$1,000", "$1,100", "$800"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["$900 counts nine days, excluding the day of possession the facts include.", "Correct. Daily rent = $3,000 ÷ 30 = $100. Days 21 through 30 inclusive = 10 days. 10 × $100 = $1,000.", "$1,100 counts eleven days, one more than the month contains from the 21st.", "$800 counts only eight days."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["proration"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-03-017$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 3, $lp$transfer$lp$, 3, $lp$Title to a home is vested 'Rosa, a life estate; remainder to David.' A buyer wants fee simple absolute. To deliver insurable fee title, the deed must be signed by:$lp$, $lp$["Rosa alone, as the party in possession", "David alone, as the ultimate owner", "Both Rosa and David", "Rosa, with David's interest extinguished automatically at closing"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["Rosa can convey only her life estate, which ends at her death.", "David can convey only his remainder, which carries no present possession.", "Correct. Combining the life estate and the remainder in one conveyance produces fee simple absolute, so both must sign.", "Closing does not extinguish a remainder; only a conveyance or the running of the measuring life affects it."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["life estate", "remainder / remainderman", "fee simple absolute"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-03-018$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 3, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$Which statement about a leasehold estate is TRUE?$lp$, $lp$["It is classified as real property because it concerns land", "It is classified as personal property (chattel real)", "It always terminates on the death of the tenant", "It gives the tenant legal title to the land"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Despite concerning land, a leasehold is not classified as real property.", "Correct. A less-than-freehold estate is personal property — chattel real — a favorite exam distinction.", "An estate for years survives the death of either party and binds heirs.", "A tenant holds possession; the landlord retains title."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["less-than-freehold (leasehold) estate", "chattel"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-04-001$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 4, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 1, $lp$Ownership in severalty means the property is owned by:$lp$, $lp$["Several persons in equal shares", "One person or entity alone", "Two or more persons with right of survivorship", "A married couple as community property"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["The word suggests multiple owners, which is precisely the trap; severalty is sole ownership.", "Correct. Severalty is ownership by one person or one entity, with the interest severed from all others.", "That describes joint tenancy.", "Community property is a form of concurrent ownership between spouses."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["severalty"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-04-002$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 4, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 1, $lp$The four unities required for a valid joint tenancy are:$lp$, $lp$["Time, title, interest, and possession", "Time, transfer, intent, and payment", "Title, taxes, interest, and possession", "Time, title, inheritance, and partition"]$lp$::jsonb, 0, $lp$["Correct. T-TIP: all joint tenants take at the same time, by the same title, in equal interests, with equal right of possession.", "Transfer, intent, and payment are not unities.", "Taxes is not a unity.", "Inheritance and partition are consequences of ownership form, not unities."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["T-TIP (four unities)", "joint tenancy"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-04-003$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 4, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$Two sisters own a home as joint tenants. One dies leaving a will devising 'all my real property' to her son. The son receives:$lp$, $lp$["A one-half interest as a tenant in common", "A one-half interest as a joint tenant", "Nothing from this property", "The entire property, subject to the sister's life estate"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["The decedent's interest never entered her estate, so nothing passed to be held in common.", "Survivorship does not admit new joint tenants by will.", "Correct. In joint tenancy the interest passes automatically to the surviving joint tenant at death; a will cannot defeat the right of survivorship.", "No life estate exists; the surviving sister owns the whole in severalty."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["right of survivorship", "joint tenancy"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-04-004$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 4, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$Three friends hold title as joint tenants, each owning one-third. One sells her interest to an outsider. Immediately after the sale:$lp$, $lp$["All three owners are tenants in common", "The outsider is a joint tenant with the other two", "The two remaining friends are joint tenants as to two-thirds; the outsider holds one-third as a tenant in common", "The sale is void without the other joint tenants' consent"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["The severance affects only the transferred interest; the remaining joint tenancy survives.", "The outsider took at a different time by a different deed, breaking unities of time and title.", "Correct. Severance converts only the conveyed share to a tenancy in common, while the untouched interests remain in joint tenancy with survivorship.", "A joint tenant may convey her interest without the others' consent."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["severance", "tenancy in common"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-04-005$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 4, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 1, $lp$When two unmarried people take title and the deed does not specify how, California presumes they hold as:$lp$, $lp$["Joint tenants", "Tenants in common", "Partners", "Owners in severalty"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Joint tenancy must be expressly stated in the deed; it is never presumed.", "Correct. Tenancy in common is California's default form of concurrent ownership when the deed is silent.", "A partnership requires a partnership relationship, not merely co-ownership.", "Severalty is sole ownership, impossible with two grantees."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["tenancy in common"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-04-006$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 4, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$Which characteristic belongs to tenancy in common but NOT to joint tenancy?$lp$, $lp$["Unity of possession", "The right to sell one's interest without the others' consent", "Ownership shares that may be unequal", "The right to seek partition"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["Both forms require unity of possession.", "Both joint tenants and tenants in common may convey their interests without consent.", "Correct. Joint tenancy requires equal interests (unity of interest); tenancy in common permits unequal shares such as 60/25/15.", "Co-owners under either form may bring a partition action."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["tenancy in common", "joint tenancy"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-04-007$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 4, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$Property acquired by one spouse during marriage by inheritance from a parent is presumed to be:$lp$, $lp$["Community property", "Separate property", "Community property with right of survivorship", "Joint tenancy property"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["The community presumption covers property acquired through the labor or earnings of either spouse, not gifts and inheritances.", "Correct. Property acquired during marriage by gift or inheritance is separate property, as is property owned before marriage or bought with separate funds.", "That is a vesting choice, not a presumption arising from inheritance.", "Joint tenancy requires an express deed statement."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["separate property", "community property"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-04-008$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 4, $lp$transfer$lp$, 2, $lp$A married couple holds their residence as community property. The husband alone signs a deed selling the home. The conveyance is:$lp$, $lp$["Valid, because each spouse owns an undivided one-half", "Defective, because both spouses must join in a conveyance of community real property", "Valid if the wife is notified within 30 days", "Valid as to the husband's half only, creating a tenancy in common with the buyer"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["The undivided one-half interest does not authorize a unilateral sale of the whole.", "Correct. Both spouses must join in a conveyance or encumbrance of community real property; a missing spousal signature will stop the closing.", "No notice provision cures the missing signature.", "California law does not treat the attempted conveyance as an effective half-transfer creating co-ownership with a stranger."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["community property"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-04-009$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 4, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 3, $lp$Four cousins own land as joint tenants, each one-quarter. Cousin A deeds her interest to a friend. Cousin B then dies with a will leaving everything to his son. Ownership is now:$lp$, $lp$["Friend 1/4; C 3/8; D 3/8", "Friend 1/4; son 1/4; C 1/4; D 1/4", "Friend 1/4; son 1/4; C 3/8; D 1/8", "C 1/2; D 1/2"]$lp$::jsonb, 0, $lp$["Correct. A's conveyance severs 1/4 to the friend as a tenant in common; B, C, and D hold 3/4 in joint tenancy, so B's death passes his share to C and D, giving each 3/8.", "The son takes nothing — survivorship within the surviving joint tenancy defeats the will.", "The son has no interest, and C and D share B's interest equally.", "The friend's severed one-quarter interest remains intact."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["severance", "right of survivorship"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-04-010$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 4, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 3, $lp$Three tenants in common — 50%, 30%, and 20% — sell for $690,000 with 7% total costs and a $310,000 loan payoff. The 30% owner nets:$lp$, $lp$["$207,000", "$114,210", "$99,510", "$165,850"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["$207,000 is 30% of the gross price, ignoring costs and the loan payoff.", "$114,210 applies the 30% share after costs but before the loan payoff.", "Correct. Net = 690,000 − 48,300 (7%) − 310,000 = 331,700; 331,700 × 0.30 = $99,510.", "$165,850 is the 50% owner's share."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["tenancy in common", "undivided interest"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-04-011$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 4, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$Co-owners deadlock: two want to sell, one refuses. The legal remedy available to any co-owner is:$lp$, $lp$["A quiet title action", "A partition action", "An unlawful detainer action", "A reformation action"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Quiet title resolves competing claims to title, which are not disputed here.", "Correct. A partition action asks the court to divide the property in kind or, more commonly for a single home, order it sold and divide the proceeds.", "Unlawful detainer recovers possession from a tenant.", "Reformation corrects a document that misstates the parties' agreement."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["partition action"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-04-012$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 4, $lp$transfer$lp$, 2, $lp$Title is vested in 'Maria Chen, Trustee of the Chen Family Trust dated March 3, 2015.' To sell, escrow will typically require:$lp$, $lp$["The complete trust instrument recorded in the county", "A certification of trust evidencing the trustee's authority", "Signatures from all trust beneficiaries", "A court order approving the sale"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Recording the entire trust would expose private terms unnecessarily and is not the standard practice.", "Correct. A certification of trust establishes the trustee's authority to act while keeping the trust's private provisions confidential.", "Beneficiaries do not sign; the trustee holds title and conveys.", "Court approval is required for some probate and conservatorship sales, not routine living-trust sales."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["certification of trust"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-04-013$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 4, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$A corporation with 500 shareholders owns an office building. The corporation holds title:$lp$, $lp$["In severalty", "As a tenant in common with its shareholders", "In joint tenancy with its officers", "As community property"]$lp$::jsonb, 0, $lp$["Correct. A corporation is a single legal person, so it owns in severalty no matter how many shareholders it has.", "Shareholders own stock, not an interest in the corporation's real property.", "Officers hold no title by virtue of office.", "Community property applies to spouses, not corporations."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["severalty"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-04-014$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 4, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 3, $lp$In California, a joint tenant obtains a loan secured by a deed of trust against the property. This act, by itself:$lp$, $lp$["Severs the joint tenancy immediately", "Does not sever the joint tenancy, because California follows lien theory", "Converts all owners to tenants in common", "Requires all joint tenants to sign or the loan is void"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Severance requires breaking a unity; a lien does not transfer title.", "Correct. In a lien-theory state like California the lender takes a lien rather than title, so recording a deed of trust does not by itself sever a joint tenancy.", "No conversion of the other owners' interests occurs.", "The lender will want all owners to sign for its own protection, but the encumbrance of one joint tenant's interest is not void without them."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["joint tenancy", "severance"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-04-015$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 4, $lp$transfer$lp$, 2, $lp$To clear a deceased joint tenant from title, the surviving joint tenant typically records:$lp$, $lp$["A quitclaim deed from the decedent's heirs", "An affidavit of death of joint tenant with a certified death certificate", "A grant deed from the executor", "A notice of default"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Heirs have no interest to quitclaim, since survivorship already operated.", "Correct. The affidavit of death of joint tenant, recorded with a certified death certificate, evidences that title vested in the survivor by operation of law.", "No executor's deed is needed because the interest never entered probate.", "A notice of default begins foreclosure and is unrelated."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["affidavit of death of joint tenant", "right of survivorship"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-04-016$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 4, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$A resident of a stock cooperative owns:$lp$, $lp$["Fee title to a unit of airspace plus an undivided interest in common areas", "Shares in the corporation plus a proprietary lease", "A life estate in the unit", "An undivided interest in the entire building as a tenant in common"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["That describes a condominium, which is real property.", "Correct. In a stock cooperative the corporation owns the building and residents own shares plus a proprietary lease — personal property, which is why co-op financing differs.", "No measuring life is involved.", "Co-op residents do not hold title as tenants in common."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["stock cooperative", "common interest development (CID)"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-04-017$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 4, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$Two co-owners of a Duarte duplex hold undivided one-half interests as tenants in common. This means each owner:$lp$, $lp$["Owns one specific unit outright", "Has the right to possess the entire property", "May exclude the other from half the building", "Must obtain the other's consent to sell his interest"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Co-ownership of an undivided interest does not assign specific physical portions.", "Correct. Unity of possession gives every co-owner the right to possess and use the whole property, not a divided piece.", "Neither co-owner may exclude the other from any part of the property.", "A tenant in common may convey his interest freely without consent."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["undivided interest", "concurrent ownership"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-04-018$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 4, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$Buyers ask their agent, 'Should we take title as joint tenants or as community property with right of survivorship?' The agent's BEST response is to:$lp$, $lp$["Recommend community property with right of survivorship for its tax advantages", "Recommend joint tenancy because it avoids probate", "Explain the general characteristics of each and refer them to their attorney or CPA", "Instruct escrow to select the vesting that is most common locally"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["Recommending a vesting for tax reasons is giving legal and tax advice beyond a licensee's authority.", "Both vestings can avoid probate; recommending one is still advising on a legal question.", "Correct. A licensee may describe the options generally but must refer the choice to an attorney or CPA, because vesting carries legal, estate, and tax consequences.", "Delegating a client's legal decision to escrow based on local habit serves no one and does not cure the advice problem."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["community property with right of survivorship", "joint tenancy"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-05-001$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 5, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 1, $lp$A judgment lien is BEST classified as:$lp$, $lp$["Voluntary and specific", "Involuntary and general", "Voluntary and general", "Involuntary and specific"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["The debtor did not agree to it, and it is not limited to one parcel.", "Correct. A judgment lien is imposed by law without the owner's consent (involuntary) and attaches to all of the debtor's real property in the county (general).", "Nothing about a judgment lien is voluntary.", "Judgment liens are general, not limited to a single identified parcel."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["involuntary lien", "general lien", "abstract of judgment"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-05-002$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 5, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 1, $lp$A deed of trust securing a purchase loan is:$lp$, $lp$["Voluntary and specific", "Involuntary and specific", "Voluntary and general", "A non-money encumbrance"]$lp$::jsonb, 0, $lp$["Correct. The borrower deliberately created it (voluntary) and it attaches to one identified parcel (specific).", "The owner signed it, so it is voluntary rather than imposed by law.", "It attaches to the secured parcel only, not all the borrower's property.", "It secures a debt, making it a money encumbrance."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["voluntary lien", "specific lien"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-05-003$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 5, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$Which encumbrance takes priority over all others regardless of when it arose?$lp$, $lp$["The first recorded deed of trust", "A perfected mechanic's lien", "Current property taxes and assessments", "A recorded lis pendens"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["A first trust deed outranks later liens but yields to property taxes.", "Mechanics' liens relate back to commencement of work, but taxes still come first.", "Correct. Property tax and assessment liens hold first priority regardless of recording date — the great exception to 'first in time, first in right.'", "A lis pendens clouds title but is not a lien with payment priority."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["priority", "special assessment"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-05-004$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 5, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 3, $lp$A foreclosure sale nets $540,000. Encumbrances: $9,000 property taxes; a 2019 first deed of trust of $395,000; a 2022 judgment lien of $60,000; a 2023 second deed of trust of $85,000. The 2023 second deed of trust receives:$lp$, $lp$["$85,000 in full", "$76,000", "$0", "$136,000"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["There is not enough money left to pay it in full after higher-priority claims.", "Correct. 540,000 − 9,000 (taxes) − 395,000 (2019 first) − 60,000 (2022 judgment) = $76,000 remaining, so the 2023 lien takes $76,000 and is short $9,000.", "Funds do remain after the higher-priority liens are satisfied.", "$136,000 is the balance before paying the 2022 judgment lien, which recorded earlier and outranks the 2023 trust deed."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["priority"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-05-005$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 5, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 3, $lp$Visible construction began August 3. A construction lender recorded its deed of trust September 12. An unpaid subcontractor recorded a mechanic's lien November 20. As to priority, the mechanic's lien generally:$lp$, $lp$["Ranks last, because it recorded last", "Relates back to August 3 and may outrank the September 12 deed of trust", "Ranks ahead of property taxes", "Is invalid because it recorded after the loan"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Mechanics' liens are the exception to ordinary recording-date priority.", "Correct. A perfected mechanic's lien relates back to the date work first commenced, which is why lenders inspect for commenced work before recording and funding.", "Nothing outranks property tax liens.", "Recording after the loan does not invalidate the lien; timing rules run from work and completion dates."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["mechanic's lien", "relation back"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-05-006$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 5, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$An owner records a document after construction finishes to shorten the time in which mechanics' liens may be filed. This document is a:$lp$, $lp$["Notice of default", "Notice of completion", "Preliminary notice", "Notice of nonresponsibility"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["A notice of default begins foreclosure proceedings.", "Correct. Recording a notice of completion shortens the statutory window for claimants to record mechanics' liens.", "A preliminary notice is served by claimants without a direct contract with the owner, to preserve their lien rights.", "A notice of nonresponsibility protects an owner from liens for work ordered by someone else, such as a tenant."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["notice of completion", "mechanic's lien"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-05-007$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 5, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$A driveway easement allows Lot 12 to cross Lot 11 for access. Lot 11 is the:$lp$, $lp$["Dominant tenement", "Servient tenement", "Holder of an easement in gross", "Licensee"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Lot 12 is dominant because it receives the benefit.", "Correct. The burdened parcel the easement crosses is the servient tenement.", "An easement in gross benefits a person or company, not a neighboring parcel.", "A licensee holds revocable permission, not an easement."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["servient tenement", "dominant tenement", "easement appurtenant"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-05-008$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 5, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$A utility company's recorded right to run power lines across hundreds of separate parcels is an example of an:$lp$, $lp$["Easement appurtenant", "Easement in gross", "Easement by necessity", "Encroachment"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["An appurtenant easement requires a benefited parcel; the utility has none.", "Correct. An easement in gross benefits a person or company rather than a dominant tenement — utility easements are the classic example.", "Necessity easements arise to provide access to landlocked parcels.", "An encroachment is an unauthorized physical intrusion, not a granted right."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["easement in gross"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-05-009$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 5, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$In California, a prescriptive easement requires open, notorious, hostile, and continuous use for:$lp$, $lp$["Three years", "Five years", "Seven years", "Ten years"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Three years is not the California prescriptive period.", "Correct. California's prescriptive period is five years of open, notorious, hostile, and continuous use.", "Seven years applies in some other states, not California.", "Ten years is a common distractor drawn from other jurisdictions."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["prescriptive easement"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-05-010$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 5, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 3, $lp$A neighbor has crossed an owner's land for eight years WITH the owner's written permission. The neighbor has acquired:$lp$, $lp$["A prescriptive easement, because eight years exceeds five", "An easement by implication", "Nothing beyond a revocable license", "Title by adverse possession"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["Permission defeats hostility, and hostility is required for prescription no matter how many years pass.", "Implication requires a prior apparent use existing when a parcel was divided, which is not present here.", "Correct. Use by permission is a license — personal, revocable, and creating no interest in land. This is why owners grant written permission to protect themselves.", "Adverse possession requires hostile possession and payment of taxes; permissive use fails at the first element."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["license", "prescriptive easement"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-05-011$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 5, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$An owner of a dominant tenement purchases the servient tenement. The easement is terminated by:$lp$, $lp$["Merger", "Abandonment", "Prescription", "Condemnation"]$lp$::jsonb, 0, $lp$["Correct. When one person owns both parcels, the easement is extinguished by merger — you cannot hold an easement over your own land.", "Abandonment requires non-use plus clear intent to abandon.", "Prescription in reverse requires the servient owner to block the use for the prescriptive period.", "Condemnation is a government taking."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["merger", "easement"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-05-012$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 5, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$A neighbor's fence sits three feet inside the property line. This is BEST described as a(n):$lp$, $lp$["Easement appurtenant", "Encroachment", "License", "Nonconforming use"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["No right of use was granted; this is an unauthorized physical intrusion.", "Correct. A physical intrusion of an improvement across a boundary is an encroachment, typically revealed by survey rather than title search.", "A license is permission to use, not a structure over the line.", "Nonconforming use concerns zoning, not boundaries."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["encroachment"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-05-013$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 5, $lp$transfer$lp$, 2, $lp$Zoning permits a 35-foot building height, but the subdivision's recorded CC&Rs limit height to 25 feet. An owner may build to:$lp$, $lp$["35 feet, because public law preempts private restrictions", "25 feet, because the stricter restriction governs", "30 feet, splitting the difference", "Any height, once the HOA is notified"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Private restrictions are not preempted; they bind those who took title subject to them.", "Correct. Where zoning and CC&Rs differ, the more restrictive controls — private restrictions may be stricter than public law but never looser.", "No averaging rule exists.", "Notice to the HOA does not waive a recorded restriction."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["CC&Rs"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-05-014$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 5, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$A 1953 CC&R document recorded against a listing contains a racially restrictive covenant. The licensee should understand that the provision is:$lp$, $lp$["Enforceable because it was lawful when recorded", "Void and unenforceable, with a statutory process available to strike the language", "Enforceable only against non-original owners", "Binding until the HOA votes to amend it"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Discriminatory covenants have long been unenforceable regardless of when recorded.", "Correct. Racially restrictive covenants are void and unenforceable, and California provides a statutory procedure to have the offending language removed from recorded documents.", "The provision binds no one; it is void as to all.", "No association vote is needed to nullify what is already void."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["CC&Rs", "protected class"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-05-015$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 5, $lp$transfer$lp$, 3, $lp$A preliminary report on a pending sale shows a lis pendens recorded four months ago. The listing agent should recognize that:$lp$, $lp$["It is a routine item that will clear at closing through proration", "Pending litigation affecting title will likely stop title insurance and lender funding until resolved or expunged", "It automatically expires after 90 days", "It can be removed by paying the amount claimed into escrow"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Prorations handle taxes and similar recurring charges, not litigation clouds.", "Correct. A lis pendens gives notice of litigation affecting title or possession; title companies will not insure and lenders will not fund over it until it is resolved or expunged by court order.", "There is no automatic 90-day expiration.", "A lis pendens is not a monetary lien with a payoff figure; the underlying lawsuit must be resolved or the notice expunged."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["lis pendens"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-05-016$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 5, $lp$financing$lp$, 3, $lp$An owner refinances the first loan. The existing second-position lender signs and records an agreement accepting a position behind the new first loan. That agreement is a:$lp$, $lp$["Novation", "Subordination agreement", "Assumption agreement", "Satisfaction of lien"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["A novation substitutes a new contract or party, extinguishing the original obligation.", "Correct. A subordination agreement voluntarily lowers a lienholder's priority — essential in refinances, because paying off the old first would otherwise promote the second automatically.", "An assumption transfers responsibility for an existing loan to a new borrower.", "A satisfaction evidences that a debt has been paid and the lien released."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["subordination agreement", "priority"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-05-017$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 5, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$A seller subdivides a parcel and conveys the rear portion, which now has no legal access to any public street. A court would most likely find an easement by:$lp$, $lp$["Prescription", "Necessity", "Express grant", "Condemnation"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Prescription requires five years of hostile use, and the parcel was just created.", "Correct. When a conveyance leaves a parcel landlocked, courts imply an easement by necessity so the land is not rendered useless.", "An express grant requires written easement language, which the facts do not provide.", "Condemnation is a government taking, not a private access remedy."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["easement by necessity"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-05-018$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 5, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$Which of the following is a NON-money encumbrance?$lp$, $lp$["A mechanic's lien", "A special assessment for a new sewer", "A recorded utility easement", "An attachment lien"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["A mechanic's lien secures payment for unpaid labor or materials.", "A special assessment is a lien securing the cost of a local improvement.", "Correct. An easement burdens the use of the land rather than securing a debt, making it a non-money encumbrance.", "An attachment lien secures a claim pending litigation."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["encumbrance", "easement"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-06-001$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 6, $lp$agency$lp$, 2, $lp$When a salesperson obtains a listing, the agency relationship legally exists between the seller and:$lp$, $lp$["The salesperson personally", "The salesperson's employing broker", "The multiple listing service", "Both the salesperson and the broker as co-agents"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["The salesperson acts for the broker; the listing does not belong to the salesperson.", "Correct. The broker is the agent of the client, and the salesperson is the broker's associate licensee — which is why dual agency arises at the brokerage level.", "The MLS is a private listing database, not a party to the agency.", "There is one agent — the broker — not two co-agents."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["associate licensee", "agency"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-06-002$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 6, $lp$agency$lp$, 1, $lp$In OLD CAR, which duty requires an agent to protect the principal's motivation and financial condition from the other side?$lp$, $lp$["Obedience", "Disclosure", "Confidentiality", "Accounting"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["Obedience concerns following lawful instructions.", "Disclosure requires telling the *principal* material information — the opposite direction.", "Correct. Confidentiality protects the principal's confidential information, including motivation and the lowest price they would accept, and it survives termination of the agency.", "Accounting concerns funds and property entrusted to the agent."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["OLD CAR", "fiduciary"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-06-003$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 6, $lp$agency$lp$, 2, $lp$A seller instructs the listing agent not to present offers from buyers with young children. The agent should:$lp$, $lp$["Comply, because obedience is a fiduciary duty", "Comply, but document the instruction in the file", "Refuse, because the instruction is unlawful discrimination", "Present the offers but recommend the seller reject them"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["Obedience extends only to lawful instructions.", "Documenting an unlawful instruction does not make following it lawful.", "Correct. Familial status is a protected class; the agent must refuse the instruction and should advise the seller of the law, withdrawing if the seller persists.", "Steering the seller toward rejecting protected-class buyers is the same violation in another form."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["OLD CAR", "protected class"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-06-004$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 6, $lp$agency$lp$, 2, $lp$A listing agent learns the buyer is willing to pay $30,000 more than the offer submitted. The agent must:$lp$, $lp$["Keep it confidential to protect the transaction", "Disclose it to the seller under the duty of disclosure", "Disclose it only if the seller specifically asks", "Disclose it to the buyer's agent first"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["The agent owes confidentiality to the *seller*, not the buyer; protecting the deal is not a recognized duty.", "Correct. The duty of disclosure requires telling the principal all material information affecting their interests, including a buyer's greater willingness to pay.", "The duty is affirmative and does not depend on the client asking.", "The information belongs to the seller's side of the analysis; disclosing it to the buyer's agent serves no duty."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["OLD CAR", "fiduciary"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-06-005$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 6, $lp$agency$lp$, 2, $lp$A brokerage lists a home; a different salesperson at the SAME brokerage represents the buyer. This situation is:$lp$, $lp$["Not dual agency, because different salespersons are involved", "Dual agency at the brokerage level, requiring informed written consent from both parties", "Subagency", "Prohibited in California under all circumstances"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Assigning different salespersons manages the conflict but does not eliminate brokerage-level dual agency.", "Correct. Because the broker is the agent of both clients, dual agency exists and requires informed written consent from buyer and seller.", "Subagency involves an agent of the agent, typically a cooperating broker acting for the seller.", "Dual agency is lawful in California with informed written consent."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["dual agency"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-06-006$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 6, $lp$agency$lp$, 3, $lp$A properly consented dual agent knows the sellers are financially distressed and that the roof leaks. The dual agent may disclose to the buyer:$lp$, $lp$["Both the distress and the leak", "The leak only", "The distress only", "Neither, without written permission"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Financial distress is confidential motivation that a dual agent may not reveal without permission.", "Correct. Property condition is never confidential — known material defects must be disclosed — while the sellers' financial motivation must be protected.", "This reverses the rule: motivation is protected and condition is disclosed.", "Material defects must be disclosed regardless of permission; silence would be concealment."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["dual agency", "material fact"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-06-007$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 6, $lp$agency$lp$, 2, $lp$An agent lists a property and, without disclosing his ownership interest, arranges for an LLC he partly owns to purchase it below market. The agent's gain on resale is recoverable by the seller as a:$lp$, $lp$["Referral fee", "Secret profit", "Liquidated damage", "Commission override"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["A referral fee is disclosed compensation for referring business.", "Correct. An undisclosed benefit derived from the agency is a secret profit, recoverable by the principal and grounds for license discipline.", "Liquidated damages are contractual pre-agreed damages for breach.", "A commission override is a share of commission paid within a brokerage structure."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["secret profit", "OLD CAR"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-06-008$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 6, $lp$agency$lp$, 2, $lp$Which statement is PUFFING rather than a representation of fact?$lp$, $lp$["'This home is zoned for commercial use.'", "'The roof was replaced in 2021.'", "'This is the most charming street in Claremont.'", "'The lot is 8,400 square feet.'"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["Zoning is verifiable fact; misstating it is misrepresentation.", "Roof age is a verifiable fact.", "Correct. An exaggerated subjective opinion no reasonable buyer would rely on as fact is puffing.", "Lot size is a verifiable fact and a frequent source of misrepresentation claims."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["puffing", "misrepresentation"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-06-009$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 6, $lp$agency$lp$, 3, $lp$An agent innocently tells a buyer the home has permitted square footage of 2,400 based on an outdated flyer. The permitted area is 1,950. The agent is:$lp$, $lp$["Not liable, because the statement was innocent", "Potentially liable, because even innocent misrepresentation of a material fact can support rescission or damages", "Liable only if the buyer can prove intent to deceive", "Protected because square footage is always considered puffing"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Lack of intent limits some remedies but does not eliminate liability.", "Correct. Innocent, negligent, and intentional misrepresentations can all create liability; a competent agent verifies square footage rather than repeating a flyer.", "Intent is required for fraud, not for all misrepresentation remedies.", "Square footage is a verifiable fact, never puffing."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["misrepresentation", "material fact"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-06-010$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 6, $lp$agency$lp$, 2, $lp$Agency created when a principal's own conduct leads a third party to reasonably believe someone represents them is called:$lp$, $lp$["Implied agency", "Ostensible agency", "Ratification", "Express agency"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Implied agency arises from the conduct of the parties to the relationship itself.", "Correct. Ostensible (apparent) agency arises from the principal's conduct causing a third party's reasonable belief.", "Ratification is after-the-fact approval of an unauthorized act.", "Express agency arises from a written or spoken agreement."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["ostensible (apparent) agency"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-06-011$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 6, $lp$agency$lp$, 2, $lp$An unauthorized person negotiates a lease for an owner. Learning all the facts, the owner accepts the rent and honors the lease. The agency was created by:$lp$, $lp$["Estoppel", "Ratification", "Necessity", "Subagency"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Estoppel concerns preventing a party from denying a state of affairs they created; the facts describe after-the-fact acceptance.", "Correct. Accepting the benefits of an unauthorized act with knowledge of the facts ratifies it, binding the principal.", "Necessity is a doctrine for easements, not agency creation here.", "Subagency describes an agent of an agent."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["ratification"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-06-012$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 6, $lp$agency$lp$, 2, $lp$A real estate broker hired to find a buyer for one specific property is acting as a:$lp$, $lp$["General agent", "Universal agent", "Special agent", "Subagent"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["A general agent has ongoing authority over a range of matters, like a property manager.", "A universal agent may act in all matters, typically under a broad power of attorney.", "Correct. A broker engaged for one transaction with limited authority is a special agent — the usual real estate role.", "A subagent is an agent of the agent."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["special agent"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-06-013$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 6, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 3, $lp$While showing a 1948 home, an agent sees a large brown ceiling stain under an upstairs bathroom, freshly painted over. The seller's TDS discloses no leaks. The agent must:$lp$, $lp$["Say nothing, since the TDS is the seller's disclosure", "Disclose the observation in writing to the buyer and recommend further inspection", "Diagnose the cause and estimate repair cost for the buyer", "Report the seller to the DRE before proceeding"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["The agent's own visual inspection duty is independent of the seller's TDS.", "Correct. The duty of reasonably competent and diligent visual inspection of accessible areas requires disclosing observed red flags and referring the buyer to a qualified inspector.", "Agents are not inspectors and must not diagnose causes or estimate repair costs.", "The immediate obligation is disclosure to the buyer, not a regulatory complaint."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["visual inspection duty", "material fact"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-06-014$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 6, $lp$agency$lp$, 2, $lp$A seller contributes to the compensation of the buyer's brokerage. This payment:$lp$, $lp$["Makes the buyer's broker an agent of the seller", "Creates dual agency automatically", "Does not change who the buyer's broker represents", "Requires the buyer's broker to disclose the buyer's maximum price"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["Compensation does not create agency; representation flows from the agency agreement.", "No dual agency arises merely because the seller contributed to compensation.", "Correct. Who pays and who is represented are separate questions — the buyer's broker continues to owe full fiduciary duties to the buyer.", "Disclosing the buyer's maximum price would breach confidentiality owed to the buyer."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["agency", "dual agency"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-06-015$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 6, $lp$agency$lp$, 1, $lp$To a third party (customer) in the transaction, a licensee owes:$lp$, $lp$["Loyalty and confidentiality", "Honesty, fair dealing, and disclosure of known material facts", "The same fiduciary duties owed to a principal", "No duties whatsoever"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Loyalty and confidentiality run to the principal only.", "Correct. Third parties are owed honesty, fair dealing, and disclosure of known material facts, but not fiduciary duties.", "Fiduciary duties are reserved for the principal.", "Licensees always owe third parties honesty and material-fact disclosure."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["third party (customer)", "material fact"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-06-016$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 6, $lp$agency$lp$, 2, $lp$California's agency disclosure process for residential 1–4 unit sales is best summarized as:$lp$, $lp$["Advertise, negotiate, close", "Disclose, elect, confirm", "Notify, record, verify", "Present, accept, ratify"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["These are transaction activities, not the statutory agency disclosure steps.", "Correct. The licensee delivers the agency disclosure form, elects in writing which party is represented, and confirms the relationships in writing with the purchase contract.", "Recording concerns documents affecting title, not agency disclosure.", "These describe contract formation, not agency disclosure."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["agency disclosure"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-06-017$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 6, $lp$agency$lp$, 2, $lp$An exclusive listing agreement that contains NO definite termination date is:$lp$, $lp$["Valid, because listings run until the property sells", "Grounds for disciplinary action against the licensee", "Automatically converted to an open listing", "Valid if the seller initials the omission"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Open-ended exclusive listings are exactly what the rule prohibits.", "Correct. An exclusive listing must specify a definite termination date; omitting one is grounds for discipline.", "No automatic conversion occurs.", "The seller cannot waive the requirement by initialing."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["exclusive right to sell listing"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-06-018$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 6, $lp$agency$lp$, 2, $lp$After a listing expires unsold, the former listing agent may:$lp$, $lp$["Freely tell prospective buyers the lowest price the seller would have accepted", "Not disclose the seller's confidential information, because confidentiality survives termination", "Disclose confidential information after six months", "Disclose it only to other licensees"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Termination of the agency does not release the agent to reveal what was learned in confidence.", "Correct. The duty of confidentiality survives the end of the agency relationship.", "No waiting period converts confidential information into public information.", "The identity of the recipient does not matter; the information remains protected."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["OLD CAR", "fiduciary"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-06-019$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 6, $lp$agency$lp$, 3, $lp$At an open house, a listing agent counsels a visiting unrepresented buyer on offer strategy, reveals the seller must close by September, and suggests offering below asking. The agent has MOST likely:$lp$, $lp$["Provided good customer service consistent with fair dealing", "Breached confidentiality and loyalty, and risked creating an undisclosed dual agency", "Created a valid buyer representation agreement", "Committed puffing"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Fair dealing requires honesty with customers, not advocacy against one's own client.", "Correct. Revealing the seller's timing motivation breaches confidentiality, advising a lower offer breaches loyalty, and the counseling conduct may create an implied agency producing undisclosed dual agency.", "A buyer representation agreement requires an agreement, not incidental advice.", "Puffing concerns exaggerated opinion about the property, which is not what occurred."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["implied agency", "dual agency", "OLD CAR"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-06-020$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 6, $lp$agency$lp$, 2, $lp$Which event does NOT terminate an agency relationship?$lp$, $lp$["Expiration of the listing term", "Destruction of the property", "The salesperson who took the listing leaving the brokerage", "Mutual agreement of the parties"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["A listing ends on its stated termination date.", "Destruction of the subject property makes performance impossible and ends the agency.", "Correct. The listing belongs to the broker, so the departure of the individual salesperson does not terminate the agency between the broker and the client.", "The parties may always agree to cancel."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["agency", "associate licensee"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-07-001$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 7, $lp$contracts$lp$, 1, $lp$Which of the following is NOT one of the four essential elements of a valid contract?$lp$, $lp$["Capable parties", "Mutual consent", "A written document", "Consideration"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["Capacity is required; parties must be legally able to bind themselves.", "Mutual consent — offer and acceptance freely given — is required.", "Correct. A writing is not an element of validity. Many contracts are enforceable orally; the statute of frauds separately requires writings for certain agreements, including real estate sales.", "Consideration — something of legal value from each side — is required."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["contract", "statute of frauds"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-07-002$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 7, $lp$contracts$lp$, 2, $lp$In California, a contract made by a 16-year-old to purchase real property is:$lp$, $lp$["Voidable at the minor's option", "Void", "Valid if a parent consents in writing", "Enforceable once the minor reaches 18"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Voidable is the general rule for many minors' contracts, but California treats real property contracts more strictly.", "Correct. A minor's contract concerning real property is void in California — it has no legal effect and cannot be ratified later.", "Parental consent cannot validate a void real property contract.", "Reaching majority does not revive a void contract; the parties must make a new one."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["void contract", "capacity"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-07-003$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 7, $lp$contracts$lp$, 3, $lp$A contract signed by a person who has been judicially declared incompetent is ______, while a contract signed by a person of unsound mind who has NOT been adjudicated is ______.$lp$, $lp$["voidable; void", "void; voidable", "void; void", "voidable; unenforceable"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["This reverses the rule — adjudication produces the stricter result.", "Correct. Judicial adjudication of incompetence removes the power to contract entirely (void); without adjudication, the contract is voidable and may be rescinded or affirmed.", "The unadjudicated person's contract is voidable, not void.", "Neither status turns on enforceability under the statute of frauds."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["capacity", "void contract", "voidable contract"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-07-004$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 7, $lp$contracts$lp$, 2, $lp$A seller crosses out the offered price, writes a higher figure, signs, and returns the document. The original offer is now:$lp$, $lp$["Still open for the buyer to accept", "Terminated, because a counteroffer rejects the original offer", "Binding at the original price", "Suspended until the buyer responds"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["A counteroffer extinguishes the original offer; it is no longer available to accept.", "Correct. A counteroffer both rejects the original offer and becomes a new offer from the counterofferor.", "No contract formed at the original price because acceptance was not unqualified.", "There is no suspended state — the original offer is dead unless the buyer renews it."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["counteroffer", "offer"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-07-005$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 7, $lp$contracts$lp$, 2, $lp$A seller signs a buyer's offer but locks it in a desk drawer and tells no one. At that moment:$lp$, $lp$["A binding contract exists because the seller signed", "No contract exists because acceptance was not communicated", "A voidable contract exists", "The offer automatically expires"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Signing alone does not form a contract; the offeror must receive the acceptance.", "Correct. Acceptance must be communicated to the offeror in the manner the offer requires before a contract is formed.", "Nothing about the facts creates a defect in consent producing voidability.", "The offer remains open until it lapses, is revoked, or is rejected."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["acceptance", "mutual consent"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-07-006$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 7, $lp$contracts$lp$, 2, $lp$Which agreement is NOT required to be in writing under California's statute of frauds?$lp$, $lp$["A contract to sell a single-family residence", "A lease for a term of 18 months", "A month-to-month residential rental agreement", "An agreement employing a broker to sell real property for compensation"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["Contracts for the sale of real property must be written and signed.", "Leases longer than one year fall within the statute.", "Correct. A month-to-month tenancy runs for a period of less than one year and may be created orally, though a writing is far better practice.", "A broker's employment agreement must be written and signed, which is why a broker cannot sue for a commission without one."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["statute of frauds"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-07-007$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 7, $lp$contracts$lp$, 2, $lp$An oral agreement to sell a home is best described as:$lp$, $lp$["Void", "Voidable", "Unenforceable", "Valid and enforceable"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["The agreement is not a nullity; if both parties perform voluntarily, the transaction stands.", "Voidable describes contracts with defective consent, such as those induced by fraud.", "Correct. The statute of frauds makes such an agreement unenforceable — a court will not compel performance, though voluntary performance is effective.", "Enforceability is exactly what the statute of frauds withholds."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["unenforceable contract", "statute of frauds"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-07-008$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 7, $lp$contracts$lp$, 2, $lp$An open listing, under which the seller promises to pay a commission to whichever broker produces a buyer, is an example of a:$lp$, $lp$["Bilateral contract", "Unilateral contract", "Executed contract", "Void contract"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["A bilateral contract exchanges promise for promise; here no broker promises to perform.", "Correct. A promise exchanged for an act creates a unilateral contract binding only the offeror until the act is performed.", "Executed means fully performed, which the listing is not until a buyer is produced and the sale closes.", "Nothing about the arrangement is illegal or otherwise void."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["unilateral contract", "open listing"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-07-009$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 7, $lp$contracts$lp$, 3, $lp$A buyer defaults on an $780,000 purchase of an owner-occupied home. The deposit is $35,000 and the liquidated damages clause was properly initialed. The seller may retain:$lp$, $lp$["$35,000", "$23,400", "$39,000", "$7,800"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["The full deposit exceeds the statutory cap and is presumed unreasonable.", "Correct. The cap is 3% of the purchase price: $780,000 × 0.03 = $23,400. The remaining $11,600 is refundable to the buyer.", "$39,000 is 5% of the price, above the permitted cap.", "$7,800 is 1% of the price, not the statutory limit."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["liquidated damages"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-07-010$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 7, $lp$contracts$lp$, 2, $lp$A seller refuses to convey after a valid contract. The buyer sues to force the sale of that particular property. The buyer is seeking:$lp$, $lp$["Liquidated damages", "Specific performance", "Rescission", "Novation"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Liquidated damages are a pre-agreed money remedy, not a compulsion to convey.", "Correct. Specific performance compels performance of the contract and is available in real estate because land is considered unique, making money damages inadequate.", "Rescission cancels the contract rather than enforcing it.", "Novation substitutes a new contract or party."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["specific performance"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-07-011$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 7, $lp$contracts$lp$, 3, $lp$A buyer under contract assigns her rights to an investor, who then fails to close. Absent a novation, the original buyer is:$lp$, $lp$["Fully released from liability", "Generally still secondarily liable to the seller", "Liable only for the deposit", "Liable only if the assignment was unrecorded"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Only a novation, agreed to by the seller, releases the original party.", "Correct. An assignment transfers rights but leaves the assignor secondarily liable; a novation would be required to obtain a release.", "Liability is not automatically limited to the deposit.", "Recording has no bearing on contractual liability between the parties."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["assignment", "novation"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-07-012$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 7, $lp$contracts$lp$, 2, $lp$A caretaker persuades a frail, isolated elderly owner to sell the home far below market value. The contract is most likely voidable for:$lp$, $lp$["Duress", "Menace", "Undue influence", "Mutual mistake"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["Duress requires unlawful force or confinement.", "Menace requires a threat of unlawful injury to person, property, or character.", "Correct. Undue influence is taking unfair advantage of another's weakness of mind, distress, or a relationship of trust and confidence.", "Mutual mistake requires both parties to be wrong about a material fact."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["undue influence", "voidable contract"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-07-013$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 7, $lp$contracts$lp$, 2, $lp$An owner sells a home to her sister for $1. As to consideration, the contract is:$lp$, $lp$["Void, because the consideration is inadequate", "Valid, because consideration need only be legally sufficient, not adequate", "Voidable by the seller's heirs", "Unenforceable without an appraisal"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Courts do not police the adequacy of a bargained exchange.", "Correct. Consideration must be legally sufficient, not economically adequate; the price is the parties' business, though gift-tax and creditor questions belong to a CPA and attorney.", "Heirs have no automatic right to undo a completed lifetime transfer for low consideration.", "No appraisal is required for contract validity."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["consideration"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-07-014$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 7, $lp$contracts$lp$, 2, $lp$A purchase agreement has been signed by both parties but escrow has not closed. The contract is:$lp$, $lp$["Executed", "Executory", "Unilateral", "Void"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Executed means fully performed; the parties still owe performance here. (Note that 'executed' is also used loosely to mean 'signed,' which is the trap.)", "Correct. A contract with performance still outstanding is executory — a purchase agreement is executory from acceptance until closing.", "A purchase agreement is bilateral: both parties made promises.", "The contract is valid and binding."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["executory / executed"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-07-015$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 7, $lp$contracts$lp$, 2, $lp$A contract to compensate an unlicensed person for performing licensed real estate activity is:$lp$, $lp$["Valid but unenforceable", "Voidable by the unlicensed person", "Void, because it lacks a lawful object", "Valid if the amount is reasonable"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["The defect goes deeper than enforceability; the object itself is unlawful.", "The unlicensed party has no election to make; the agreement has no legal effect.", "Correct. An illegal purpose destroys the lawful-object element, rendering the contract void, and courts generally leave the parties where they found them.", "Reasonableness of the fee cannot cure illegality."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["lawful object", "void contract"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-07-016$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 7, $lp$contracts$lp$, 3, $lp$Parties orally agree by phone to extend a written contract's closing date by four days. The extension is:$lp$, $lp$["Enforceable, because the parties mutually agreed", "Generally unenforceable, because a contract required to be written cannot be orally modified", "Enforceable if a witness heard the call", "Automatically valid for up to five days"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Mutual agreement is not enough when the statute of frauds governs the underlying contract.", "Correct. Modifications to contracts within the statute of frauds generally must themselves be written and signed — hence the practice of same-day written extension addenda.", "Witnesses do not satisfy the writing requirement.", "No grace period exists for oral extensions."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["statute of frauds"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-07-017$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 7, $lp$contracts$lp$, 2, $lp$An optionee pays $5,000 for the right to purchase a property at a set price within 90 days. During those 90 days, the optionor:$lp$, $lp$["May revoke the offer at any time", "Is bound to sell if the optionee elects to buy", "May sell to a higher bidder without liability", "May raise the price if market conditions change"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Consideration makes the option irrevocable for its term — that is the point of paying for it.", "Correct. An option is a unilateral contract binding the optionor to sell on the stated terms while leaving the optionee free to buy or not.", "Selling to another during the option period would breach the option.", "The price is fixed by the option agreement for its term."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["option", "unilateral contract"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-07-018$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 7, $lp$contracts$lp$, 2, $lp$Both buyer and seller mistakenly believe a parcel contains 2 usable acres when in fact a recorded easement leaves only 1. The contract is most likely:$lp$, $lp$["Valid and binding on both", "Voidable for mutual mistake of material fact", "Void for illegality", "Unenforceable under the statute of frauds"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["A shared error about a material fact undermines the meeting of the minds.", "Correct. Mutual mistake of a material fact — as opposed to a mistake about value — permits rescission by the affected party.", "Nothing illegal occurred.", "The agreement's form is not the problem; the parties' shared factual error is."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["mutual mistake", "voidable contract"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-07-019$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 7, $lp$contracts$lp$, 3, $lp$A broker sues a seller for an unpaid commission but has only an oral listing agreement. The broker will most likely:$lp$, $lp$["Recover the full commission, since the sale closed", "Recover a reasonable fee in quantum meruit", "Lose, because an agreement employing a broker to sell real property must be written and signed", "Recover if two witnesses confirm the terms"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["Closing the sale does not cure the missing written employment agreement.", "Courts generally do not award fallback recovery where the statute of frauds bars the claim.", "Correct. The statute of frauds requires a written, signed agreement authorizing an agent to sell real property for compensation, so the oral listing is unenforceable.", "Witness testimony does not substitute for the required writing."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["statute of frauds"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-07-020$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 7, $lp$contracts$lp$, 2, $lp$A licensee breaches a fiduciary duty and gains an advantage, but without deliberate intent to deceive. This is best characterized as:$lp$, $lp$["Actual fraud", "Constructive fraud", "Puffing", "Duress"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Actual fraud requires intentional misrepresentation or concealment.", "Correct. Constructive fraud is a breach of duty that gains an advantage without deliberate dishonesty — the doctrine that reaches fiduciaries who did not intend to lie.", "Puffing is exaggerated opinion about property.", "Duress involves unlawful force or confinement compelling consent."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["constructive fraud", "actual fraud"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-08-001$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 8, $lp$contracts$lp$, 1, $lp$Under which listing does the broker earn a commission even if the owner personally finds the buyer?$lp$, $lp$["Open listing", "Exclusive agency listing", "Exclusive right to sell listing", "Net listing"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["Under an open listing the owner may sell directly and owe nothing.", "Exclusive agency specifically permits the owner to sell without paying a commission.", "Correct. An exclusive right to sell entitles the broker to compensation no matter who produces the buyer, including the owner.", "A net listing describes a compensation structure, not who may sell without owing a commission."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["exclusive right to sell"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-08-002$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 8, $lp$contracts$lp$, 2, $lp$An exclusive listing agreement that omits a definite termination date is:$lp$, $lp$["Valid, running until the property sells", "Grounds for disciplinary action against the licensee", "Automatically limited to 90 days by law", "Converted to an open listing"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Open-ended exclusive listings are exactly what the rule forbids.", "Correct. An exclusive listing must contain a definite termination date; omitting one is grounds for discipline, as are automatic-extension provisions.", "No statutory default term fills the gap.", "There is no automatic conversion to another listing type."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["definite termination date", "listing agreement"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-08-003$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 8, $lp$contracts$lp$, 2, $lp$A safety (protection) clause typically requires the broker, to enforce it, to:$lp$, $lp$["Record a notice of interest against the property", "Deliver the seller a written list of protected prospects within the time the contract specifies", "Continue advertising the property after expiration", "Obtain the seller's verbal acknowledgment of each showing"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Brokers do not record interests against a seller's title to secure commissions.", "Correct. Enforceability normally depends on timely delivery of a written list identifying the prospects the broker introduced during the listing.", "Advertising after expiration is not the trigger and could mislead the public.", "Verbal acknowledgments do not satisfy the written-list requirement."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["safety clause (protection period)"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-08-004$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 8, $lp$contracts$lp$, 2, $lp$When two brokerages each claim the commission, the doctrine used to decide who earned it is:$lp$, $lp$["Procuring cause", "Novation", "Subrogation", "Ratification"]$lp$::jsonb, 0, $lp$["Correct. Procuring cause identifies the broker who set in motion the uninterrupted chain of events leading to the sale; such disputes are typically arbitrated through the association.", "Novation substitutes a contract or party.", "Subrogation substitutes one party for another regarding a claim, common in insurance.", "Ratification is after-the-fact approval of an unauthorized act."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["procuring cause"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-08-005$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 8, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$A broker receives a buyer's deposit check with instructions to hold it uncashed until acceptance. The seller accepts Wednesday. The broker must place the funds in escrow, a trust account, or the principal's hands:$lp$, $lp$["Immediately upon receiving the check", "Within three business days of acceptance", "Within ten calendar days of acceptance", "At the buyer's direction, any time before closing"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["A properly disclosed instruction to hold the check uncashed pending acceptance postpones the deposit obligation.", "Correct. Trust funds must be deposited within three business days; when the check is properly held pending acceptance, the clock runs from acceptance.", "Ten days exceeds the regulatory window.", "The deadline is fixed by regulation, not by the buyer's preference."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["earnest money deposit", "trust fund"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-08-006$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 8, $lp$contracts$lp$, 3, $lp$A buyer misses the written contingency removal deadline under a standard California residential purchase agreement using active removal. The seller may:$lp$, $lp$["Cancel immediately, because the deadline passed", "Deliver a notice to buyer to perform and cancel only after the cure period expires", "Keep the deposit automatically as liquidated damages", "Treat the contingency as waived and require the buyer to close"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Under active removal, a passed deadline does not by itself remove the contingency or permit immediate cancellation.", "Correct. The seller must deliver a written notice to buyer to perform, giving the buyer a cure period, before cancellation is permitted.", "Retaining the deposit requires a valid cancellation and an enforceable liquidated damages provision, subject to the 3% cap.", "Passive waiver is not the California residential default; the contingency remains until removed in writing."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["active removal", "notice to buyer to perform", "contingency"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-08-007$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 8, $lp$contracts$lp$, 3, $lp$A seller wants to net $500,000 after a $180,000 loan payoff, 5% commission, and 1.5% closing costs. The required sale price is approximately:$lp$, $lp$["$724,200", "$727,300", "$680,000", "$744,700"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["$724,200 comes from multiplying $680,000 by 1.065 — the classic error; you must divide, not multiply.", "Correct. (500,000 + 180,000) ÷ (1 − 0.065) = 680,000 ÷ 0.935 = $727,272.73, rounded to about $727,300.", "$680,000 is the net plus payoff before accounting for the percentage costs at all.", "$744,700 overstates the price, applying an incorrect divisor."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$[]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-08-008$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 8, $lp$contracts$lp$, 3, $lp$A home sells for $1,240,000 with a 4.5% total commission split 55% listing / 45% buyer side. The listing salesperson keeps 75% of her side. Before any fees, she receives:$lp$, $lp$["$55,800", "$30,690", "$23,017.50", "$25,110"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["$55,800 is the total commission for both sides.", "$30,690 is the listing side before the broker split.", "Correct. 1,240,000 × 0.045 = 55,800; × 0.55 = 30,690; × 0.75 = $23,017.50.", "$25,110 applies the 45% buyer-side share rather than the listing side."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$[]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-08-009$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 8, $lp$contracts$lp$, 2, $lp$A seller wishes to negotiate with several buyers simultaneously without accidentally forming two contracts. The seller should use:$lp$, $lp$["Separate ordinary counteroffers to each buyer", "A multiple counteroffer form stating that no contract forms until the seller signs a final acceptance", "A backup offer addendum with each buyer", "An option agreement with each buyer"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Ordinary counteroffers sent to multiple buyers risk binding the seller to more than one contract if several accept.", "Correct. A multiple counteroffer expressly provides that no contract arises until the seller signs a final acceptance of one buyer's response, preserving the seller's choice.", "Backup offers address what happens if the primary contract fails, not simultaneous negotiation.", "Options require consideration and bind the seller to sell, which is not the goal here."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["multiple counteroffer", "backup offer"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-08-010$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 8, $lp$contracts$lp$, 2, $lp$A backup offer is BEST described as one that:$lp$, $lp$["Automatically takes effect if the primary contract falls through", "Obligates the seller to cancel the primary contract", "Gives the backup buyer the right to match any other offer", "Converts to an option after 30 days"]$lp$::jsonb, 0, $lp$["Correct. A backup offer states its numbered position and springs into effect automatically if the primary contract is cancelled.", "A backup does not force cancellation of the primary contract.", "Matching rights are a right of first refusal, a different instrument.", "No automatic conversion to an option occurs."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["backup offer"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-08-011$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 8, $lp$contracts$lp$, 2, $lp$A seller sets a price she must receive and allows the broker to keep everything above it. This arrangement is a:$lp$, $lp$["Open listing", "Net listing", "Exclusive agency listing", "Option listing"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["An open listing describes non-exclusivity, not the compensation formula.", "Correct. A net listing lets the broker retain all proceeds above the seller's stated net — an arrangement that pits the broker's interest against the client's and is heavily restricted.", "Exclusive agency concerns who may sell without a commission.", "There is no recognized 'option listing' category."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["net listing"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-08-012$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 8, $lp$contracts$lp$, 2, $lp$An agreement allowing the seller to remain in the property for two weeks after closing is called a:$lp$, $lp$["Land contract", "Rent-back", "Backup occupancy", "Estate at sufferance"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["A land contract is a seller-financing device where the seller retains title while the buyer pays over time.", "Correct. A rent-back permits the seller to occupy for an agreed period after close of escrow, typically with terms addressing rent, insurance, and condition.", "This is not a recognized contract term.", "An estate at sufferance arises when someone holds over *without* agreement — the opposite of a negotiated rent-back."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["rent-back"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-08-013$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 8, $lp$contracts$lp$, 2, $lp$The clause that makes stated contract deadlines strict, so that late performance is a material breach, is:$lp$, $lp$["The safety clause", "The liquidated damages clause", "'Time is of the essence'", "The arbitration clause"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["A safety clause protects a broker's commission after listing expiration.", "Liquidated damages fix an amount payable on breach; they do not make deadlines strict.", "Correct. A 'time is of the essence' clause makes each stated deadline material, so missing one is a breach rather than a delay.", "Arbitration provisions govern how disputes are resolved, not deadline strictness."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["time is of the essence"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-08-014$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 8, $lp$contracts$lp$, 2, $lp$Which contingency protects a buyer whose lender's valuation comes in below the agreed price?$lp$, $lp$["Loan contingency", "Appraisal contingency", "Investigation contingency", "Title contingency"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["The loan contingency addresses obtaining financing, which can fail for many reasons unrelated to value.", "Correct. The appraisal contingency specifically protects the buyer when the appraised value falls below the contract price.", "The investigation contingency covers physical condition and general suitability.", "The title contingency covers review of the preliminary report and title matters."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["contingency"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-08-015$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 8, $lp$contracts$lp$, 2, $lp$Under a buyer representation agreement, if the seller's side contributes less compensation than the buyer agreed to pay the buyer's broker, typically:$lp$, $lp$["The broker must accept the lesser amount", "The buyer owes the difference under the agreement", "The seller must make up the difference", "The transaction cannot close"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["The written agreement between buyer and broker governs the broker's compensation.", "Correct. Buyer representation agreements generally provide that the buyer is responsible for any shortfall between the agreed compensation and what the seller's side contributes.", "A seller has no obligation beyond what the seller agreed to contribute.", "A compensation shortfall is a payment question, not an obstacle to closing."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["buyer representation agreement"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-08-016$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 8, $lp$contracts$lp$, 3, $lp$An offer is accepted Tuesday, September 8, with a 17-day investigation contingency counted from acceptance (day one being the day after). The buyer's written removal is due no later than:$lp$, $lp$["September 24", "September 25", "September 26", "September 29"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["September 24 counts only 16 days from September 9.", "Correct. Day one is September 9; 17 days runs through September 25 (September 9 + 16 days).", "September 26 counts 18 days.", "September 29 corresponds to a 21-day contingency, such as loan and appraisal."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["contingency"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-08-017$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 8, $lp$contracts$lp$, 2, $lp$A seller tells her listing agent she may sell to her cousin and refuses to pay a commission if she does. The BEST professional solution is to:$lp$, $lp$["Take an exclusive right to sell and say nothing about the cousin", "Take an exclusive right to sell with the cousin named as a written exclusion", "Take an oral side agreement excluding the cousin", "Refuse the listing entirely"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Allowing the seller to believe something the contract contradicts is a misrepresentation of your own agreement.", "Correct. A named written exclusion honors the seller's intent, protects the broker against every other buyer, and puts the understanding in the contract.", "Oral side agreements about commission are unenforceable and invite disputes.", "Declining is permissible but unnecessary when a clean written exclusion solves the problem."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["exclusive right to sell", "exclusive agency listing"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-08-018$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 8, $lp$contracts$lp$, 2, $lp$In a California residential purchase of an owner-occupied home, the liquidated damages and arbitration provisions are effective only if:$lp$, $lp$["The escrow holder approves them", "Both parties separately sign or initial them", "They are recorded with the county", "The broker signs as a witness"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Escrow has no approval role over the parties' contract terms.", "Correct. Both provisions require the parties' separate signatures or initials to be binding, ensuring they were specifically considered.", "Purchase agreements are not recorded.", "Broker witness signatures are not the requirement."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["liquidated damages"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-08-019$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 8, $lp$contracts$lp$, 2, $lp$A seller retains legal title while the buyer takes possession and pays in installments, receiving title only after the final payment. This is a:$lp$, $lp$["Lease with option to purchase", "Land contract (installment sale contract)", "Wraparound trust deed", "Assumption agreement"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["A lease-option combines a tenancy with a right to buy; title and possession are structured differently.", "Correct. Under a land contract the seller keeps legal title while the buyer possesses and pays over time — a risky arrangement for buyers and heavily regulated.", "A wraparound trust deed is junior financing that wraps an existing loan.", "An assumption transfers responsibility for an existing loan to a new borrower."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["land contract (installment sale contract)"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-08-020$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 8, $lp$contracts$lp$, 3, $lp$A buyer's agent emails a signed acceptance of the seller's counteroffer at 4:02 p.m. At 3:55 p.m. the seller had emailed a written revocation of that counteroffer, which the buyer's agent had not yet opened. Most likely:$lp$, $lp$["A binding contract formed at 4:02 p.m.", "No contract formed, because the revocation was communicated before acceptance", "The parties are bound because revocations must be delivered in person", "The seller must honor the counteroffer for a reasonable time regardless"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Acceptance came after the revocation was already communicated, so there was no live offer to accept.", "Correct. An offer or counteroffer may be revoked any time before acceptance is communicated; the revocation sent at 3:55 p.m. terminated the counteroffer, and whether the recipient had opened it does not revive it.", "Revocation need not be in person; written electronic delivery is effective.", "Without consideration keeping it open (an option), a counteroffer is freely revocable."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["counteroffer", "option"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-09-001$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 9, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 1, $lp$The Transfer Disclosure Statement is required in sales of residential property containing:$lp$, $lp$["One unit only", "One to four units", "One to eight units", "Any number of units"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["The requirement is not limited to single-family homes.", "Correct. The TDS applies to sales of residential property of one to four units and cannot be waived.", "Five or more units fall outside the residential TDS requirement.", "Larger apartment and commercial properties are not covered."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["TDS (Transfer Disclosure Statement)"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-09-002$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 9, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$A buyer and seller agree in writing to waive the Transfer Disclosure Statement to speed up an as-is sale. This waiver is:$lp$, $lp$["Valid if both parties initial it", "Valid because the sale is as-is", "Invalid, because the TDS requirement cannot be waived", "Valid only in cash transactions"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["Initialing does not create authority to waive a non-waivable statutory protection.", "An as-is sale changes repair expectations, not disclosure obligations.", "Correct. The TDS requirement for 1–4 unit residential property cannot be waived by agreement.", "The form of payment has no bearing on the disclosure requirement."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["TDS (Transfer Disclosure Statement)", "as-is"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-09-003$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 9, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 3, $lp$An executor selling a decedent's home through probate personally lived there and knows the north wall leaks. The sale is TDS-exempt. The executor must:$lp$, $lp$["Say nothing, because probate sales are exempt from disclosure", "Disclose the known leak, because exemption from the form does not excuse concealing known material facts", "Disclose only if the buyer submits a written request", "Disclose only after the inspection contingency expires"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["The exemption covers the statutory form, not the underlying duty regarding known material facts.", "Correct. A transferor with actual knowledge of a material defect must disclose it regardless of any form exemption; concealment invites rescission, damages, and discipline.", "The duty is affirmative and does not depend on a buyer's request.", "Delaying disclosure past the buyer's investigation period defeats its purpose and compounds liability."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["TDS exemptions", "material fact"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-09-004$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 9, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$Which transfer is generally EXEMPT from the TDS requirement?$lp$, $lp$["A 1955 bungalow sold as-is by its longtime owner", "A sale by a trustee following a foreclosure trustee's sale", "A condominium resale", "A duplex sold by an owner living out of state"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["As-is status and the home's age create no exemption.", "Correct. Transfers resulting from foreclosure — where the transferor typically has no personal knowledge of the property's condition — are exempt.", "Condominium resales of 1–4 residential units require the TDS.", "The seller's residence elsewhere does not create an exemption."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["TDS exemptions"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-09-005$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 9, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 3, $lp$A TDS is delivered by mail AFTER the buyer signed the offer. The buyer's statutory right to terminate generally lasts:$lp$, $lp$["Three days from delivery", "Five days from delivery", "Ten days from delivery", "There is no termination right once an offer is signed"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Three days applies to personal delivery.", "Correct. Delivery by mail generally gives the buyer five days to terminate; personal delivery gives three.", "Ten days is the federal lead-based paint inspection period, a different requirement.", "Late delivery of the TDS specifically creates a termination right."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["right to terminate (disclosure)", "TDS (Transfer Disclosure Statement)"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-09-006$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 9, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$Which is NOT one of the six zones addressed by the Natural Hazard Disclosure statement?$lp$, $lp$["Special flood hazard area", "Very high fire hazard severity zone", "Earthquake fault zone", "Airport noise impact zone"]$lp$::jsonb, 3, $lp$["FEMA special flood hazard areas are one of the six zones.", "Very high fire hazard severity zones are one of the six.", "Alquist-Priolo earthquake fault zones are one of the six.", "Correct. Airport noise is not among the six NHD zones, though it may still be a material fact requiring disclosure. The six are flood, dam inundation, very high fire severity, state responsibility area, earthquake fault, and seismic hazard."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["NHD (Natural Hazard Disclosure)"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-09-007$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 9, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$A death occurred on the property four years ago. A buyer asks the listing agent directly whether anyone has died in the home. The agent should:$lp$, $lp$["Deny it, since deaths older than three years need not be disclosed", "Answer truthfully, because a direct question must not be answered falsely", "Refuse to answer and cite privacy law", "Answer only in writing after consulting the seller's attorney"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["The three-year rule governs what must be volunteered; it never authorizes a false statement.", "Correct. Deaths older than three years need not be volunteered, but a direct question must be answered honestly — misrepresentation is never permitted.", "No privacy law shields the agent from answering honestly about a death on the property.", "Consulting the seller is prudent, but the obligation is to avoid a false answer, not to delay indefinitely."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["three-year death rule", "misrepresentation"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-09-008$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 9, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$Under California law, the fact that a former occupant died of or was afflicted with HIV/AIDS is:$lp$, $lp$["A material fact requiring disclosure", "Not a material fact requiring disclosure", "Disclosable only if the death occurred within three years", "Disclosable at the agent's discretion"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["California law expressly rejects treating this as a material fact.", "Correct. The statute provides that an occupant's HIV/AIDS status or death from it is not a material fact requiring disclosure, and disclosing it may raise fair housing concerns since disability is a protected class.", "The three-year rule applies to the fact of a death, not to the decedent's medical condition.", "This is not a discretionary judgment; the law addresses it directly."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["three-year death rule", "protected class"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-09-009$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 9, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 1, $lp$Federal lead-based paint disclosure requirements apply to residential housing built:$lp$, $lp$["Before 1950", "Before 1978", "Before 1985", "Before 1992"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["1950 is not the federal threshold.", "Correct. Pre-1978 housing triggers the federal lead-based paint disclosure requirements, including a 10-day inspection opportunity.", "1985 is not the threshold.", "1992 is the year of the underlying federal act, not the construction cutoff."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["lead-based paint disclosure"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-09-010$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 9, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$Under the federal lead-based paint rules, the buyer of a 1952 home must be given an opportunity to conduct a lead inspection or risk assessment lasting:$lp$, $lp$["3 days", "5 days", "10 days", "17 days"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["Three days is the personal-delivery TDS termination period.", "Five days is the mailed-delivery TDS termination period.", "Correct. Federal law provides a 10-day opportunity for a lead inspection or risk assessment, which the parties may modify or the buyer may waive in writing.", "Seventeen days is a common contractual investigation contingency, not the federal lead period."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["lead-based paint disclosure"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-09-011$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 9, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$Regarding the Megan's Law database, a licensee's obligation is to:$lp$, $lp$["Research the database and report findings to the buyer", "Provide the statutory notice and direct the buyer to the database", "Disclose the identity of any registrants living nearby", "Obtain a written waiver from the buyer"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Licensees are not required to research the database, and doing so invites liability for inaccuracy.", "Correct. The contract must contain the statutory notice, and the licensee directs the buyer to the Department of Justice database to conduct their own search.", "Characterizing neighbors creates significant liability and is not the licensee's role.", "No waiver is contemplated; the notice itself satisfies the requirement."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["Megan's Law notice"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-09-012$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 9, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$A seller knows the main sewer line has collapsed but sells the home strictly 'as-is' without mentioning it. The seller has:$lp$, $lp$["Acted properly, because as-is shifts all risk to the buyer", "Committed fraud by concealing a known material fact", "Acted properly if the buyer waived inspections", "Acted properly if the price reflected the condition"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["As-is means the seller will not repair or credit — it never authorizes concealment.", "Correct. Concealing a known material defect is fraud regardless of an as-is clause, exposing the seller and any complicit agent to rescission, damages, and discipline.", "A buyer's waiver of inspections does not excuse the seller's affirmative concealment.", "A discounted price does not substitute for disclosure of a known defect."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["as-is", "material fact", "concealment"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-09-013$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 9, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$For a condominium resale, the seller must deliver association documents INCLUDING disclosure of:$lp$, $lp$["The names and occupations of board members", "Pending litigation involving the association", "Each owner's mortgage balance", "The listing history of every unit"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Board member occupations are not a required disclosure item.", "Correct. Pending litigation involving the association must be disclosed, along with CC&Rs, bylaws, budget, reserves, assessments, and minutes — construction defect litigation can make units unfinanceable.", "Other owners' private financial information is neither available nor required.", "Listing histories of other units are not part of the required CID documents."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["CID documents"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-09-014$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 9, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$A newly built Inland Empire home carries an annual special tax funding local infrastructure. The seller must make a good-faith effort to obtain and deliver notice of this:$lp$, $lp$["Mello-Roos community facilities district lien", "Homeowner's exemption", "Documentary transfer tax", "Supplemental assessment"]$lp$::jsonb, 0, $lp$["Correct. Sellers must make a good-faith effort to obtain and deliver notice of a Mello-Roos community facilities district lien, which can materially affect the buyer's monthly cost.", "The homeowner's exemption reduces assessed value and is not a lien disclosure.", "Documentary transfer tax is a one-time transfer charge, not an ongoing district assessment.", "A supplemental assessment arises from reassessment on change of ownership, not from a financing district."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["Mello-Roos disclosure", "special assessment"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-09-015$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 9, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 3, $lp$After delivering the TDS, the seller discovers active termite damage in a wall opened during a repair. The seller must:$lp$, $lp$["Do nothing, because the TDS was already delivered", "Deliver an amended disclosure, which may restart the buyer's termination right", "Disclose only if the buyer's inspector failed to find it", "Wait until closing and credit the buyer"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["The duty to disclose continues as new material facts become known.", "Correct. A newly discovered material fact requires an amended disclosure, and amendment can restart a statutory termination right — another reason to disclose comprehensively at the outset.", "The duty does not depend on what the buyer's inspector did or did not find.", "Delaying disclosure to closing while offering money is concealment, not compliance."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["amended disclosure", "material fact"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-09-016$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 9, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$The agent's duty of visual inspection in a residential 1–4 unit sale requires the agent to:$lp$, $lp$["Inspect the roof, crawlspace, and inside wall cavities", "Conduct a reasonably competent and diligent visual inspection of accessible areas and disclose material facts revealed", "Hire a licensed inspector at the agent's expense", "Certify the property free of defects"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["The duty covers accessible areas visually; agents do not open walls or perform invasive inspection.", "Correct. The standard is a reasonably competent and diligent visual inspection of accessible areas, with written disclosure of what it reveals and referral to qualified inspectors.", "Agents recommend inspections; they are not required to purchase them.", "No agent certifies a property free of defects — that is precisely what the duty does not require."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["visual inspection duty"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-09-017$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 9, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$A city requires a sewer lateral inspection before any property transfer within its limits. This is an example of a:$lp$, $lp$["Federal disclosure requirement", "Point-of-sale ordinance", "Natural hazard zone", "CID governing document"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Sewer lateral requirements are local, not federal.", "Correct. Many California cities impose point-of-sale requirements such as retrofit, sewer lateral, or occupancy inspections that must be satisfied as a condition of transfer.", "Hazard zones are mapped areas disclosed through the NHD.", "CID documents govern common interest developments, not municipal transfer requirements."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["point-of-sale ordinance"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-09-018$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 9, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 3, $lp$A seller instructs the listing agent to omit a known recurring roof leak from the TDS. The agent should:$lp$, $lp$["Comply, since the TDS is the seller's own disclosure document", "Comply but note the disagreement in the file", "Refuse, disclose the known material fact, and withdraw from the listing if the seller persists", "Complete the TDS truthfully but keep it from the buyer until closing"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["The agent has an independent disclosure duty and cannot participate in concealment.", "A file note does not cure participation in fraud.", "Correct. The agent must refuse the unlawful instruction, disclose the known material fact, and withdraw if the seller insists on concealment.", "Withholding a completed disclosure defeats its purpose and is itself concealment."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["material fact", "concealment"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-09-019$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 9, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$The Natural Hazard Disclosure statement primarily tells the buyer:$lp$, $lp$["Whether the property will flood or burn", "Whether the property lies within specified mapped hazard zones", "The cost of hazard insurance for the property", "The structural condition of the improvements"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["The NHD makes no prediction about whether a specific event will occur.", "Correct. The NHD is map-based: it reports whether the parcel falls within any of the six designated zones, and it does not replace the seller's duty to disclose actual known conditions.", "Insurance pricing is obtained separately from carriers and should be explored early in the contingency period.", "Structural condition is addressed by the TDS and by inspections."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["NHD (Natural Hazard Disclosure)"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-09-020$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 9, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 3, $lp$A buyer discovers after closing that the listing agent knew of, and never disclosed, a serious foundation defect. The buyer's potential remedies include:$lp$, $lp$["Rescission and damages against the seller, plus claims against the brokerages and DRE discipline against the licensee", "Only a complaint to the DRE, since the sale already closed", "Only recovery limited to the earnest money deposit", "No remedy, because closing extinguishes disclosure claims"]$lp$::jsonb, 0, $lp$["Correct. Non-disclosure of a known material defect can support rescission and damages (and punitive damages for intentional conduct) against the seller and brokerages, with independent DRE discipline against the licensee.", "Civil remedies and regulatory discipline operate independently; closing does not limit the buyer to a complaint.", "Damages are measured by the loss, not capped at the deposit.", "Closing does not extinguish claims for fraudulent concealment."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["material fact", "concealment"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-10-001$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 10, $lp$transfer$lp$, 2, $lp$The two requirements for a valid escrow are:$lp$, $lp$["A licensed escrow officer and a recorded deed", "A binding contract between the parties and conditional delivery of instruments and funds to a neutral third party", "Mutual instructions and a title insurance policy", "An earnest money deposit and a preliminary report"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Neither a particular license nor recording is an element of a valid escrow.", "Correct. California requires a binding contract plus conditional delivery of transfer instruments and funds to a disinterested third party.", "Title insurance is customary but not an element of escrow validity.", "A deposit and prelim are typical features, not the legal requirements."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["escrow", "requirements for valid escrow"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-10-002$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 10, $lp$transfer$lp$, 2, $lp$Before closing, the escrow holder acts as:$lp$, $lp$["The agent of the seller only", "The agent of the buyer only", "A dual agent of both parties for carrying out the escrow instructions", "An independent contractor owing no duties to either party"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["Escrow favors neither party.", "Escrow is not the buyer's advocate.", "Correct. The escrow holder is a neutral depository and a dual agent of both parties for the limited purpose of carrying out the instructions; after closing it becomes each party's separate agent for delivery.", "Escrow owes real duties, including strict adherence to the mutual instructions."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["neutral depository", "escrow"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-10-003$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 10, $lp$transfer$lp$, 2, $lp$A buyer cancels and demands the deposit; the seller demands it as well. The escrow holder should:$lp$, $lp$["Release the funds to whichever party has the stronger claim", "Hold the funds pending mutual written cancellation instructions or a court order", "Split the deposit equally", "Return the funds to the buyer, since deposits are always refundable"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Escrow does not adjudicate the parties' rights.", "Correct. Escrow acts only on mutual written instructions; absent agreement it holds the funds and may file an interpleader action to let a court decide.", "Escrow has no authority to compromise the parties' claims.", "Deposits are not automatically refundable, and escrow cannot unilaterally release them."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["interpleader", "escrow instructions"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-10-004$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 10, $lp$transfer$lp$, 2, $lp$Which is NOT required for a deed to be valid between grantor and grantee?$lp$, $lp$["A competent grantor", "Delivery and acceptance", "Recording", "An adequate property description"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["A grantor lacking capacity cannot convey.", "Delivery with intent to pass title, and acceptance, are essential.", "Correct. Recording is not required for validity between the parties; it protects the grantee against subsequent bona fide purchasers.", "The property must be identifiable from the description."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["valid delivery", "recording"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-10-005$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 10, $lp$transfer$lp$, 3, $lp$An owner signs and notarizes a grant deed to her nephew, then locks it in her safe deposit box with a note to give it to him when she dies. After her death the nephew records it. The deed is:$lp$, $lp$["Valid, because it was signed, notarized, and recorded", "Invalid as a conveyance, because there was no delivery with present intent to pass title", "Valid, because recording completed the transfer", "Valid as a will substitute"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Signing, notarizing, and recording cannot supply the missing element of delivery.", "Correct. Delivery requires intent to pass title presently and irrevocably during the grantor's lifetime; retaining the deed for delivery at death defeats that intent.", "Recording never validates an otherwise invalid deed.", "A deed is not a substitute for a will; the property passes through her estate."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["valid delivery", "grant deed"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-10-006$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 10, $lp$transfer$lp$, 2, $lp$A California grant deed implies which warranty?$lp$, $lp$["That the grantor will defend title against all claims forever", "That the grantor has not previously conveyed the property to another", "That the property is free of all encumbrances of any kind", "That the property is fit for the buyer's intended use"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["That broad defense obligation belongs to a general warranty deed, rare in California.", "Correct. A grant deed implies that the grantor has not already conveyed the property and has disclosed encumbrances the grantor placed on it; it also conveys after-acquired title.", "The implied warranty covers encumbrances placed by the grantor, not all encumbrances.", "Deeds carry no fitness-for-use warranty."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["grant deed"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-10-007$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 10, $lp$transfer$lp$, 1, $lp$A deed that conveys whatever interest the grantor may have, with no warranties whatsoever, is a:$lp$, $lp$["Grant deed", "Warranty deed", "Quitclaim deed", "Trustee's deed"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["A grant deed carries two implied warranties.", "A warranty deed carries express warranties, the broadest protection.", "Correct. A quitclaim conveys only whatever interest the grantor holds, if any, and is commonly used to clear clouds or release interests.", "A trustee's deed is delivered to the purchaser at a foreclosure sale."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["quitclaim deed"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-10-008$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 10, $lp$transfer$lp$, 2, $lp$The preliminary report issued by a title company is best described as:$lp$, $lp$["A warranty that title is marketable", "An offer to issue a policy of title insurance on stated terms", "A guarantee of the property's boundaries", "A substitute for an owner's title policy"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["The prelim makes no warranty about the condition of title.", "Correct. The preliminary report is an offer to issue a policy subject to the stated exceptions — narrower legally than agents often assume, but the most useful document in the file.", "Boundaries are a survey matter, not covered by the prelim.", "It is not insurance; the policy issued at closing provides the coverage."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["preliminary report"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-10-009$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 10, $lp$transfer$lp$, 2, $lp$A statement of information is used primarily to:$lp$, $lp$["Verify the buyer's income for the lender", "Distinguish the parties from similarly named persons with recorded liens or judgments", "Disclose the property's physical condition", "Establish the property's legal description"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Income verification is the lender's underwriting function.", "Correct. The confidential statement of information lets the title company separate your parties from others with the same or similar names who have judgments or tax liens of record.", "Physical condition is addressed by the TDS and inspections.", "The legal description comes from the recorded documents and maps."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["statement of information"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-10-010$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 10, $lp$transfer$lp$, 3, $lp$A buyer closes but never records her deed and never occupies. The seller then deeds the property to a second buyer, who pays value, has no knowledge of the first sale, and records at once. Title belongs to:$lp$, $lp$["The first buyer, because her deed was delivered first", "The second buyer, as a bona fide purchaser who recorded first", "Neither; the property escheats", "Both, as tenants in common"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Delivery controls between the immediate parties, but an unrecorded deed loses to a subsequent bona fide purchaser.", "Correct. A purchaser who pays value, takes without notice, and records first prevails under the recording statute — leaving the first buyer with a fraud claim against the seller.", "Escheat applies only when an owner dies without a will or heirs.", "The recording contest has one winner, not shared ownership."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["bona fide purchaser (BFP)", "recording"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-10-011$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 10, $lp$transfer$lp$, 3, $lp$Same facts as the prior question, except the FIRST buyer has been living in the home since closing. Now the second buyer:$lp$, $lp$["Still prevails, because recording always controls", "Loses, because the first buyer's possession gives constructive notice defeating bona fide purchaser status", "Prevails only if he paid above market value", "Shares title with the first buyer"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Recording first is not enough; the purchaser must also take without notice.", "Correct. Possession of the property gives constructive (inquiry) notice of the occupant's claim, so the second buyer cannot qualify as a bona fide purchaser.", "The price paid does not cure notice.", "No co-ownership results from a failed bona fide purchaser claim."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["constructive notice", "bona fide purchaser (BFP)"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-10-012$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 10, $lp$transfer$lp$, 2, $lp$Which risk is typically EXCLUDED from a standard CLTA policy but covered by an ALTA extended policy?$lp$, $lp$["A forged deed in the chain of title", "An improperly delivered deed of record", "An encroachment that a survey would reveal", "A recorded judgment lien against the seller"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["Forgery is among the off-record risks standard coverage typically addresses.", "Improper delivery of a recorded deed is generally within standard coverage.", "Correct. Survey matters such as encroachments and boundary discrepancies are typically excluded from CLTA standard coverage and addressed by ALTA extended coverage, which requires a survey or inspection.", "Recorded liens are matters of public record covered by standard coverage."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["CLTA standard policy", "ALTA extended policy"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-10-013$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 10, $lp$transfer$lp$, 2, $lp$A lender's title insurance policy:$lp$, $lp$["Protects the buyer's equity in the property", "Protects the lender only, up to the loan balance, decreasing as the loan is repaid", "Protects both lender and buyer equally", "Covers zoning violations affecting the property"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["The buyer's equity is protected only by an owner's policy.", "Correct. A lender's policy insures the lender's security interest up to the loan amount and declines as the principal is paid; a buyer relying on it alone is uninsured.", "It does not protect the buyer at all.", "Government regulation, including zoning, is excluded from title insurance generally."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["lender's policy"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-10-014$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 10, $lp$transfer$lp$, 2, $lp$Title insurance differs from most other insurance because it:$lp$, $lp$["Is paid in monthly premiums", "Insures against defects that already exist when the policy is issued", "Covers future losses from natural disasters", "Must be renewed annually"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Title insurance is paid by a single premium at closing.", "Correct. Title insurance looks backward, covering defects existing at the time of issuance, unlike policies insuring against future events.", "Natural disaster losses are covered by hazard insurance.", "Coverage continues as long as the insured holds an interest, without annual renewal."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["CLTA standard policy"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-10-015$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 10, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 3, $lp$A sale closes October 16 with the buyer owning the day of closing. Annual taxes of $8,760 were prepaid by the seller for the fiscal year beginning July 1. Using a 360-day year and 30-day months, the proration is:$lp$, $lp$["$2,554.65 credit to the buyer", "$6,205.35 credit to the seller", "$6,205.35 credit to the buyer", "$8,760 credit to the seller"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["$2,554.65 is the seller's own share of taxes for the period he owned, not a buyer credit.", "Correct. Daily tax = 8,760 ÷ 360 = $24.33. Seller owned July 1–Oct 15 = 105 days = $2,554.65. The prepaid balance of $6,205.35 covers the buyer's period, so it is credited to the seller and debited to the buyer.", "The direction is reversed — a prepaid expense produces a seller credit, not a buyer credit.", "The seller is not reimbursed the full year; he consumed 105 days of it."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["proration"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-10-016$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 10, $lp$transfer$lp$, 3, $lp$A home sells for $918,000, and the buyer assumes an existing $300,000 loan. At $0.55 per $500 of consideration, the county documentary transfer tax is:$lp$, $lp$["$1,009.80", "$679.80", "$330.00", "$504.90"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["$1,009.80 taxes the full price, which would be correct only if the buyer obtained new financing rather than assuming the existing loan.", "Correct. Tax applies to consideration net of the assumed loan: (918,000 − 300,000) ÷ 500 = 1,236 increments × $0.55 = $679.80.", "$330.00 corresponds to taxing only the assumed loan amount.", "$504.90 corresponds to no standard calculation on these facts."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["documentary transfer tax"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-10-017$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 10, $lp$transfer$lp$, 2, $lp$In a California transaction, title passes to the buyer when:$lp$, $lp$["The purchase agreement is signed", "The buyer's funds arrive in escrow", "The deed is recorded", "The buyer takes possession"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["The purchase agreement creates contractual rights, not title.", "Funding is a condition of closing, not the transfer of title.", "Correct. Close of escrow occurs on recording, which is when title passes.", "Possession may occur before or after closing and does not determine title."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["close of escrow", "recording"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-10-018$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 10, $lp$transfer$lp$, 2, $lp$Before a deed may be recorded, it generally must be:$lp$, $lp$["Signed by the grantee", "Acknowledged before a notary", "Approved by the county assessor", "Accompanied by a title policy"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["The grantee does not sign the deed.", "Correct. Acknowledgment — the grantor's declaration before a notary that the signature is genuine and voluntary — is generally required for recording, though not for validity between the parties.", "The assessor does not approve deeds for recording.", "A title policy is not a prerequisite to recording."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["acknowledgment", "recording"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-10-019$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 10, $lp$transfer$lp$, 2, $lp$A buyer's agent asks the escrow officer whether the terms are 'a good deal' for her client. The escrow officer should:$lp$, $lp$["Give a candid opinion, since escrow sees many transactions", "Decline, because escrow is neutral and may not advise either party", "Advise only the party who selected the escrow company", "Refer the question to the seller's agent"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Offering an opinion on the merits would breach escrow's neutrality.", "Correct. As a neutral depository serving both parties, the escrow holder may not advise or advocate for either side.", "Who opened escrow does not create a right to advice.", "Redirecting the question to the opposing agent does not address the neutrality obligation."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["neutral depository"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-10-020$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 10, $lp$transfer$lp$, 2, $lp$Which is generally EXCLUDED from coverage under both CLTA and ALTA title policies?$lp$, $lp$["A forged deed in the chain of title", "A recorded easement not shown in the report", "Zoning and other government regulation", "An error in the public records index"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["Forgery is a classic covered off-record risk.", "A missed recorded easement is the sort of record defect title insurance addresses.", "Correct. Government regulation — zoning, building codes, and land use restrictions — is excluded from title insurance generally, as are eminent domain and defects the insured created or knew of and did not disclose.", "Indexing errors in the public records are the kind of risk title insurance exists to cover."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["CLTA standard policy", "ALTA extended policy"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-11-001$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 11, $lp$financing$lp$, 1, $lp$The three parties to a California deed of trust are the:$lp$, $lp$["Mortgagor, mortgagee, and escrow holder", "Trustor, trustee, and beneficiary", "Grantor, grantee, and trustee", "Vendor, vendee, and lender"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Mortgagor and mortgagee are the two parties to a mortgage; escrow is not a party to the security instrument.", "Correct. The trustor is the borrower, the beneficiary is the lender, and the trustee holds bare legal title with the power of sale.", "Grantor and grantee are deed terms, not the parties to a deed of trust.", "Vendor and vendee describe seller and buyer under a land contract."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["deed of trust", "trustor", "beneficiary"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-11-002$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 11, $lp$financing$lp$, 2, $lp$Which statement about the promissory note and the deed of trust is TRUE?$lp$, $lp$["The note secures the deed of trust", "The deed of trust is the evidence of the debt", "The security instrument follows the note when the note is sold", "Both must be recorded to be valid"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["The relationship runs the other way — the deed of trust secures the note.", "The note is the evidence of the debt; the deed of trust is the collateral.", "Correct. The security follows the note automatically, which is why a borrower's loan servicer can change without any new security instrument.", "The note is generally not recorded, and recording is not required for validity between the parties."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["promissory note", "security instrument"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-11-003$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 11, $lp$financing$lp$, 2, $lp$California is a lien theory state, which means that when a borrower signs a deed of trust, the lender:$lp$, $lp$["Receives legal title until the loan is repaid", "Receives a lien against the property while the borrower retains ownership", "Becomes a co-owner with the borrower", "Receives possession of the property"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["The trustee holds bare legal title as a technical device; the lender does not take title.", "Correct. In a lien theory state the lender holds a lien, the borrower retains ownership and possession, and a loan therefore does not sever a joint tenancy.", "No co-ownership arises from a loan.", "The borrower keeps possession throughout."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["lien theory", "deed of trust"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-11-004$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 11, $lp$financing$lp$, 2, $lp$The document recorded to clear a paid-off deed of trust from title is a:$lp$, $lp$["Satisfaction of mortgage", "Deed of reconveyance", "Trustee's deed", "Notice of completion"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["A satisfaction of mortgage clears a mortgage, the two-party instrument uncommon in California.", "Correct. Upon payoff the beneficiary requests, and the trustee records, a deed of reconveyance releasing the lien.", "A trustee's deed conveys title to the buyer at a foreclosure sale.", "A notice of completion relates to mechanics' lien deadlines."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["deed of reconveyance"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-11-005$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 11, $lp$financing$lp$, 2, $lp$The recorded document that begins a non-judicial foreclosure and starts the reinstatement period is the:$lp$, $lp$["Notice of Trustee's Sale", "Notice of Default", "Lis pendens", "Abstract of judgment"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["The Notice of Sale comes later, after the reinstatement period expires.", "Correct. Recording the Notice of Default starts the roughly three-month reinstatement period and places the default in the public record.", "A lis pendens gives notice of pending litigation affecting title.", "An abstract of judgment creates a judgment lien."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["Notice of Default (NOD)", "reinstatement"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-11-006$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 11, $lp$financing$lp$, 3, $lp$A borrower wants to stop foreclosure two months after the Notice of Default was recorded. To reinstate the loan, the borrower must pay:$lp$, $lp$["The entire outstanding loan balance", "The delinquent amounts plus costs and fees", "One year of future payments in advance", "The trustee's estimated sale price"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Paying the entire balance is redemption, required only after the reinstatement period expires.", "Correct. Reinstatement means curing the arrears plus costs and fees, after which the loan continues on its original terms.", "No advance-payment requirement exists.", "The sale price is irrelevant to reinstatement."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["reinstatement", "equitable right of redemption"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-11-007$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 11, $lp$financing$lp$, 2, $lp$Following a completed non-judicial trustee's sale in California, the former owner's statutory right of redemption is:$lp$, $lp$["One year", "Three months", "Ninety days", "Nonexistent"]$lp$::jsonb, 3, $lp$["One year is the judicial foreclosure redemption period where a deficiency was sought.", "Three months is the judicial redemption period where the sale satisfied the debt.", "Ninety days corresponds to no California post-trustee-sale right.", "Correct. There is no post-sale statutory redemption after a trustee's sale — once the sale concludes, the borrower's interest is extinguished."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["equitable right of redemption", "judicial foreclosure"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-11-008$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 11, $lp$financing$lp$, 3, $lp$A lender forecloses non-judicially on an investment fourplex and the sale leaves a $60,000 shortfall. The lender may:$lp$, $lp$["Obtain a deficiency judgment, because the property was not owner-occupied", "Not obtain a deficiency judgment, because non-judicial foreclosure bars it entirely", "Obtain a deficiency judgment within 90 days of the sale", "Obtain a deficiency judgment only with the Commissioner's approval"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Owner-occupancy matters to the separate purchase-money bar, but the trustee's-sale bar applies regardless of property type.", "Correct. No deficiency judgment is permitted after any non-judicial foreclosure — the lender traded that right for speed when it chose the trustee's sale.", "There is no 90-day deficiency window after a trustee's sale.", "The Commissioner has no role in deficiency actions."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["deficiency judgment", "power of sale"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-11-009$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 11, $lp$financing$lp$, 3, $lp$Which loan retains California's purchase-money anti-deficiency protection?$lp$, $lp$["A cash-out refinance on an owner-occupied home", "A loan used to buy an owner-occupied single-family home", "A commercial loan on a retail center", "A HELOC used to consolidate credit card debt"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["A cash-out refinance is generally not purchase-money and can lose the protection in a judicial foreclosure.", "Correct. A loan used to purchase a one-to-four unit owner-occupied dwelling — including seller carry-back financing — is protected from deficiency even in judicial foreclosure.", "Commercial property is outside the owner-occupied dwelling protection.", "A HELOC used for debt consolidation was not used to purchase the property."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["purchase-money loan", "deficiency judgment"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-11-010$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 11, $lp$financing$lp$, 3, $lp$A first trust deed forecloses. A second trust deed and a later judgment lien are recorded against the property. After the sale, the junior liens are:$lp$, $lp$["Extinguished from title by the senior foreclosure", "Assumed by the successful bidder", "Elevated to first position", "Unaffected and still enforceable against the property"]$lp$::jsonb, 0, $lp$["Correct. Foreclosure by a senior lienholder wipes junior liens from title, though the underlying debts may survive as unsecured claims subject to anti-deficiency rules.", "The bidder takes free of the wiped junior liens.", "Junior liens are eliminated, not promoted.", "The liens no longer attach to the property after the senior's sale."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["priority", "deficiency judgment"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-11-011$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 11, $lp$financing$lp$, 3, $lp$A trustee's sale by the FIRST trust deed holder nets $465,000. Delinquent taxes are $11,500, the foreclosing first is $402,000, a 2021 second is $70,000, and a 2023 judgment lien is $25,000. The second trust deed holder receives:$lp$, $lp$["$70,000", "$51,500", "$63,000", "$0"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["There are insufficient proceeds to pay the second in full.", "Correct. 465,000 − 11,500 (taxes) − 402,000 (first) = $51,500 available to the second, leaving it $18,500 short.", "$63,000 corresponds to no step in the priority distribution.", "Funds do remain for the second after taxes and the foreclosing first are paid."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["priority"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-11-012$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 11, $lp$financing$lp$, 2, $lp$An interest-only note of $420,000 bears 6.75% annual interest. The monthly interest payment is:$lp$, $lp$["$2,362.50", "$2,835.00", "$28,350.00", "$3,150.00"]$lp$::jsonb, 0, $lp$["Correct. $420,000 × 0.0675 = $28,350 per year; ÷ 12 = $2,362.50 per month.", "$2,835 divides the annual interest by 10 rather than 12.", "$28,350 is the annual interest, not the monthly payment.", "$3,150 corresponds to a 9% rate."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$[]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-11-013$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 11, $lp$financing$lp$, 3, $lp$Using a 360-day banker's year, interest accruing over 73 days on a $420,000 note at 6.75% is:$lp$, $lp$["$5,748.75", "$5,670.00", "$78.75", "$2,362.50"]$lp$::jsonb, 0, $lp$["Correct. Annual interest = $28,350; daily = 28,350 ÷ 360 = $78.75; × 73 days = $5,748.75.", "$5,670 uses a 365-day year daily rate applied incorrectly.", "$78.75 is the daily interest, not the 73-day total.", "$2,362.50 is one month's interest."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$[]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-11-014$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 11, $lp$financing$lp$, 2, $lp$The clause permitting a lender to declare the entire loan balance immediately due upon the borrower's default is the:$lp$, $lp$["Alienation clause", "Acceleration clause", "Subordination clause", "Release clause"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["An alienation clause is triggered by transfer of the property, not by default.", "Correct. The acceleration clause makes the whole balance due on default, and every foreclosure depends on it.", "A subordination clause changes lien priority.", "A release clause frees individual parcels from a blanket loan."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["acceleration clause"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-11-015$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 11, $lp$financing$lp$, 2, $lp$A lender may call a loan due when the borrower sells the property because of the:$lp$, $lp$["Acceleration clause", "Alienation (due-on-sale) clause", "Prepayment clause", "Defeasance clause"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Acceleration is triggered by default; here the borrower is current but transferring.", "Correct. The alienation, or due-on-sale, clause lets the lender call the balance due upon transfer, which is why most modern loans cannot simply be assumed without consent.", "A prepayment clause addresses early payoff, not transfer.", "A defeasance clause concerns defeating the security upon payment."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["alienation (due-on-sale) clause"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-11-016$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 11, $lp$financing$lp$, 3, $lp$A buyer takes title 'subject to' the seller's existing loan rather than assuming it. As to the note, the SELLER:$lp$, $lp$["Is released from all liability", "Remains liable on the note", "Becomes a guarantor only", "Is liable only for one year"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Only an assumption with novation releases the original borrower.", "Correct. Taking subject to leaves the original borrower fully liable on the note, and the loan's alienation clause may also let the lender accelerate upon the transfer.", "No guarantor status is created; the seller's original promise stands.", "Liability does not expire after a year."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["assumption vs. subject to", "alienation (due-on-sale) clause"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-11-017$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 11, $lp$financing$lp$, 3, $lp$A borrower with a $480,000 first and a $95,000 HELOC offers the first lender a deed in lieu of foreclosure. The lender will most likely refuse because:$lp$, $lp$["A deed in lieu requires court approval", "Accepting the deed means taking the property subject to the junior HELOC, whereas foreclosure would extinguish it", "Deeds in lieu are prohibited in California", "The borrower would retain a right of redemption"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["No court approval is required for a deed in lieu.", "Correct. A deed in lieu takes the property subject to junior liens, while foreclosure wipes them out — so a lender facing a second lien rationally chooses foreclosure.", "Deeds in lieu are lawful; lenders simply often decline them.", "A deed in lieu is a voluntary conveyance, not a foreclosure creating redemption rights."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["deed in lieu of foreclosure", "priority"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-11-018$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 11, $lp$financing$lp$, 2, $lp$At a trustee's sale, the foreclosing beneficiary may bid up to the amount owed without producing cash. This is called a:$lp$, $lp$["Credit bid", "Short bid", "Deficiency bid", "Subordinated bid"]$lp$::jsonb, 0, $lp$["Correct. A credit bid lets the beneficiary offset its bid against the debt owed rather than paying cash, up to the amount of the obligation.", "'Short bid' is not a recognized term.", "A deficiency is the shortfall after sale, not a bidding method.", "Subordination concerns lien priority, not bidding."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["credit bid", "trustee's deed"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-11-019$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 11, $lp$financing$lp$, 2, $lp$A lender chooses judicial foreclosure over a trustee's sale primarily to:$lp$, $lp$["Complete the process faster", "Reduce legal costs", "Preserve the possibility of a deficiency judgment", "Avoid giving the borrower any redemption rights"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["Judicial foreclosure is substantially slower, often taking a year or more.", "Litigation makes judicial foreclosure more expensive, not less.", "Correct. The sole practical advantage is preserving a possible deficiency judgment, which the non-judicial route forfeits entirely.", "Judicial foreclosure creates post-sale redemption rights; the trustee's sale is what eliminates them."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["judicial foreclosure", "deficiency judgment"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-11-020$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 11, $lp$financing$lp$, 2, $lp$A subdivision builder's blanket loan covers 40 lots. The provision allowing individual lots to be freed from the lien as they are sold and paid for is a:$lp$, $lp$["Subordination clause", "Release (partial reconveyance) clause", "Acceleration clause", "Alienation clause"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Subordination changes priority relative to another loan.", "Correct. A release clause permits partial reconveyance of individual parcels from a blanket loan so the builder can deliver clear title to each buyer.", "Acceleration makes the balance due on default.", "An alienation clause lets the lender call the loan on transfer — the opposite of what a builder needs."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["release (partial reconveyance) clause"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-12-001$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 12, $lp$financing$lp$, 1, $lp$The secondary mortgage market exists primarily to:$lp$, $lp$["Originate loans directly to consumers", "Buy existing loans so lenders can replenish capital and lend again", "Set interest rates for all residential mortgages", "Insure lenders against borrower default"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Origination happens in the primary market, between lender and borrower.", "Correct. The secondary market purchases existing loans, recycling lenders' capital and keeping mortgage money available — which is also why a borrower's loan is often sold shortly after closing.", "Rates are set by market forces and individual lenders, not by the secondary market directly.", "Insuring against default is the role of PMI, FHA, and VA guarantees."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["secondary mortgage market", "primary mortgage market"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-12-002$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 12, $lp$financing$lp$, 2, $lp$Which entity GUARANTEES securities backed by government loans rather than purchasing loans itself?$lp$, $lp$["Fannie Mae", "Freddie Mac", "Ginnie Mae", "The Federal Reserve"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["Fannie Mae purchases conventional loans and packages them into securities.", "Freddie Mac likewise purchases loans for securitization.", "Correct. Ginnie Mae is a government corporation that guarantees timely payment on securities backed by government loans such as FHA and VA — it guarantees rather than buys.", "The Federal Reserve conducts monetary policy and does not guarantee mortgage securities in this role."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["Ginnie Mae (GNMA)", "Fannie Mae (FNMA) / Freddie Mac (FHLMC)"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-12-003$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 12, $lp$financing$lp$, 2, $lp$A $1.6 million loan on a Pasadena home that exceeds Fannie Mae and Freddie Mac purchase limits is BEST described as:$lp$, $lp$["A government loan", "A conventional, non-conforming (jumbo) loan", "A conforming loan", "An FHA loan"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Government loans are FHA, VA, and similar insured or guaranteed products.", "Correct. It is conventional because no government agency insures or guarantees it, and non-conforming because it exceeds the conforming loan limit — the definition of a jumbo.", "Conforming means the loan meets Fannie/Freddie standards, including the size limit, which this loan exceeds.", "FHA loans are government-insured and subject to county loan limits."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["jumbo (non-conforming) loan", "conventional loan", "conforming loan"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-12-004$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 12, $lp$financing$lp$, 2, $lp$Private mortgage insurance (PMI) on a conventional loan protects:$lp$, $lp$["The borrower, if they lose their job", "The lender, against borrower default", "The seller, against a failed closing", "The property, against physical damage"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["PMI provides no benefit to the borrower despite being paid by the borrower.", "Correct. PMI protects the lender against loss from default and is generally required when the loan-to-value ratio exceeds 80%.", "Sellers are not protected by mortgage insurance.", "Physical damage is covered by hazard insurance."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["PMI (private mortgage insurance)"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-12-005$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 12, $lp$financing$lp$, 2, $lp$The Federal Housing Administration's role in an FHA loan is to:$lp$, $lp$["Lend the money directly to the borrower", "Insure the loan made by an approved lender", "Guarantee a portion of the loan for veterans", "Purchase the loan on the secondary market"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["FHA does not lend; approved private lenders make the loans.", "Correct. FHA insures loans made by approved lenders, which enables lower down payments and more flexible credit standards.", "Guaranteeing loans for veterans is the VA's function.", "Purchasing loans is the function of Fannie Mae and Freddie Mac."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["FHA loan", "MIP (mortgage insurance premium)"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-12-006$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 12, $lp$financing$lp$, 2, $lp$Which is a distinguishing feature of a VA loan compared with FHA and conventional financing?$lp$, $lp$["It requires a 20% down payment", "It typically requires no down payment and no monthly mortgage insurance", "It is available to any first-time buyer", "It carries no fees of any kind"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["VA loans generally require no down payment at all.", "Correct. Eligible veterans typically finance 100% with no monthly mortgage insurance, though a funding fee applies (waived for certain disabled veterans).", "Eligibility is limited to qualifying veterans, service members, and certain surviving spouses.", "A funding fee applies to most VA loans, though it may be financed."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["VA loan"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-12-007$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 12, $lp$financing$lp$, 3, $lp$Under the CalVet program, the state:$lp$, $lp$["Insures a loan made by a private lender", "Guarantees a portion of a conventional loan", "Purchases the property and sells it to the veteran under a contract of sale", "Provides a down payment grant to the veteran"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["Insuring private loans describes FHA, not CalVet.", "Guaranteeing a portion of a loan describes the VA program.", "Correct. CalVet buys the property and resells it to the veteran under a contract of sale, retaining legal title until the contract is paid — a structure unlike an ordinary loan.", "CalVet is a financing structure, not a grant program."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["CalVet", "land contract (installment sale contract)"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-12-008$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 12, $lp$financing$lp$, 2, $lp$A buyer purchases at $685,000 with $96,000 down. The loan-to-value ratio is approximately:$lp$, $lp$["80%", "86%", "14%", "90%"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["80% would require a $137,000 down payment on this price.", "Correct. Loan = 685,000 − 96,000 = 589,000; 589,000 ÷ 685,000 = 0.8598, or about 86% — above 80%, so PMI would be required on a conventional loan.", "14% is the down payment percentage, not the loan-to-value ratio.", "90% would correspond to a loan of $616,500."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["LTV (loan-to-value ratio)"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-12-009$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 12, $lp$financing$lp$, 3, $lp$A buyer takes a $540,000 loan and pays 2.5 discount points plus a 1 point origination fee. The total charged in points is:$lp$, $lp$["$13,500", "$5,400", "$18,900", "$21,600"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["$13,500 covers only the 2.5 discount points, omitting the origination fee.", "$5,400 is a single point.", "Correct. One point = 1% of the loan = $5,400. Discount: 2.5 × 5,400 = 13,500. Origination: 5,400. Total = $18,900.", "$21,600 corresponds to 4 points, one more than the 3.5 charged."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["point", "discount points"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-12-010$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 12, $lp$financing$lp$, 2, $lp$Points on a real estate loan are calculated as a percentage of the:$lp$, $lp$["Purchase price", "Loan amount", "Appraised value", "Down payment"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Using the purchase price is the most common error on this calculation.", "Correct. One point equals 1% of the loan amount, not of the price, value, or down payment.", "Appraised value affects LTV, not the computation of points.", "The down payment is unrelated to points."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["point"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-12-011$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 12, $lp$financing$lp$, 3, $lp$A loan factor table shows $6.32 per $1,000 for a 30-year fixed loan. On a $472,000 loan, monthly principal and interest is:$lp$, $lp$["$2,983.04", "$3,614.71", "$298.30", "$2,360.00"]$lp$::jsonb, 0, $lp$["Correct. 472,000 ÷ 1,000 = 472 thousands; 472 × $6.32 = $2,983.04 in monthly principal and interest.", "$3,614.71 is the full PITI once monthly taxes and insurance are added.", "$298.30 misplaces a decimal.", "$2,360.00 corresponds to a $5.00 factor."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["amortization", "PITI"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-12-012$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 12, $lp$financing$lp$, 2, $lp$A loan whose payments are less than the interest accruing, causing the balance to increase, has:$lp$, $lp$["Full amortization", "Negative amortization", "A prepayment penalty", "A balloon payment"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Full amortization retires the entire balance by maturity.", "Correct. In negative amortization the unpaid interest is added to principal and the balance grows — a structure that contributed to the 2008 crisis and is now sharply restricted.", "A prepayment penalty is a charge for paying early, unrelated to a growing balance.", "A balloon payment is a lump sum at maturity on a partially amortized loan."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["negative amortization", "amortization"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-12-013$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 12, $lp$financing$lp$, 3, $lp$An ARM has a 2.75% margin, a 2% periodic cap, and a current rate of 5.25%. At adjustment the index is 4.75%. The new rate will be:$lp$, $lp$["7.50%", "7.25%", "4.75%", "10.25%"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["$7.50% is the fully indexed rate (4.75 + 2.75) before applying the cap — the classic distractor.", "Correct. The fully indexed rate is 7.50%, but the 2% periodic cap limits the increase from 5.25% to a maximum of 7.25%, so the cap binds.", "4.75% is the index alone, without the margin.", "10.25% is the lifetime ceiling (5.25 + 5), not this adjustment."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["index / margin", "periodic cap / lifetime cap"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-12-014$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 12, $lp$financing$lp$, 2, $lp$In an adjustable-rate mortgage, the component the lender does NOT control is the:$lp$, $lp$["Margin", "Index", "Periodic cap", "Adjustment period"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["The margin is the fixed percentage the lender sets and adds to the index.", "Correct. The index is a published market rate outside the lender's control; index plus margin equals the fully indexed rate.", "Caps are negotiated loan terms set at origination.", "The adjustment period is a term of the loan agreement."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["index / margin"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-12-015$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 12, $lp$financing$lp$, 2, $lp$An agent's social media post reads: 'Own this Ontario home for just $2,100/month!' Under Regulation Z, stating that payment amount:$lp$, $lp$["Is exempt because social media is not regulated advertising", "Is a trigger term requiring disclosure of additional credit terms", "Requires only the APR to be stated", "Is permitted if the agent adds 'approximately'"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Regulation Z applies to advertising regardless of medium.", "Correct. A stated payment amount is a trigger term, requiring the ad to disclose the additional required credit terms — general claims like 'low payments' avoid the trigger.", "More than the APR alone must be disclosed once a trigger term appears.", "Softening the number with 'approximately' does not remove the trigger."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["trigger term", "APR (annual percentage rate)"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-12-016$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 12, $lp$financing$lp$, 3, $lp$The three-business-day right of rescission under Truth in Lending applies to:$lp$, $lp$["A purchase-money loan on a buyer's new home", "A refinance or equity loan secured by the borrower's principal residence", "Any loan on investment property", "All FHA and VA loans"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Purchase-money loans used to buy a home are specifically excluded from the rescission right.", "Correct. The right of rescission applies to certain refinances and home equity loans secured by the borrower's principal residence.", "Investment property loans are outside the principal-residence protection.", "The rescission right depends on the loan's purpose and the property's status, not the program."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["right of rescission"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-12-017$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 12, $lp$financing$lp$, 3, $lp$A lender offers an agent $500 for each buyer referred who closes a loan, calling it a 'marketing fee.' Accepting this payment would:$lp$, $lp$["Be lawful, because it is labeled a marketing fee", "Violate RESPA's prohibition on payment for referrals of settlement services", "Be lawful if disclosed to the buyer in writing", "Be lawful if the agent also posts about the lender online"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["The label does not control; the substance is compensation for referrals.", "Correct. RESPA prohibits giving or accepting anything of value for the referral of settlement service business; only payment for services actually performed at fair market value is permitted.", "Disclosure does not cure a prohibited referral fee.", "A token marketing activity does not convert a per-closed-loan payment into compensation for actual services."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["RESPA"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-12-018$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 12, $lp$financing$lp$, 2, $lp$The Equal Credit Opportunity Act prohibits a lender from denying credit because:$lp$, $lp$["The applicant's debt-to-income ratio is too high", "The applicant's income comes from public assistance", "The applicant has a recent bankruptcy", "The property fails to appraise"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Debt-to-income is a legitimate underwriting factor.", "Correct. ECOA prohibits discrimination based on race, color, religion, national origin, sex, marital status, age, or because income derives from public assistance.", "Credit history, including bankruptcy, is a permissible underwriting consideration.", "Insufficient collateral value is a legitimate basis for denial."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["ECOA"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-12-019$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 12, $lp$financing$lp$, 3, $lp$A seller carries back an all-inclusive trust deed that wraps an existing loan containing a due-on-sale clause. The PRIMARY risk is that:$lp$, $lp$["The buyer cannot record the AITD", "The underlying lender may accelerate the existing loan upon the transfer", "The seller loses the right to receive payments", "The AITD automatically becomes a first lien"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["An AITD can be recorded; recording is not the problem.", "Correct. The transfer can trigger the underlying loan's due-on-sale clause, and the buyer also risks losing the property if the seller stops paying the underlying loan despite receiving payments.", "The seller continues to receive payments under the wrap note.", "The AITD is junior financing; it does not displace the existing first lien."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["all-inclusive trust deed (AITD) / wraparound", "alienation (due-on-sale) clause"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-12-020$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 12, $lp$financing$lp$, 2, $lp$California's usury limits generally do NOT apply to a loan that is:$lp$, $lp$["Made between family members", "Arranged by a licensed real estate broker", "Secured by commercial property", "Under $50,000"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Family relationships create no usury exemption.", "Correct. Loans made or arranged by a licensed real estate broker are generally exempt from California's usury limits — one practical reason hard-money lending flows through brokers.", "Property type alone does not create the exemption.", "No general small-loan exemption based on that amount applies."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["usury"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-13-001$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 13, $lp$valuation$lp$, 1, $lp$The four elements that must all be present for a property to have value are:$lp$, $lp$["Demand, utility, scarcity, and transferability", "Location, condition, size, and age", "Cost, price, value, and assessment", "Land, labor, capital, and coordination"]$lp$::jsonb, 0, $lp$["Correct. DUST — demand, utility, scarcity, and transferability — must be present simultaneously; remove any one and value collapses.", "These are physical characteristics affecting value, not the elements that create it.", "These are different measures of worth, not the elements of value.", "These are the agents of production relevant to the principle of balance."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["DUST"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-13-002$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 13, $lp$valuation$lp$, 2, $lp$An owner spent $60,000 installing a pool that increases the home's value by $18,000. This gap illustrates the principle of:$lp$, $lp$["Anticipation", "Contribution", "Conformity", "Competition"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Anticipation concerns value derived from expected future benefits.", "Correct. Contribution holds that a component is worth what it adds to the value of the whole, not what it cost — the reason cost never equals value.", "Conformity concerns how a property relates to surrounding properties.", "Competition describes how excess profit attracts competitors."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["contribution", "price / cost / value"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-13-003$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 13, $lp$valuation$lp$, 2, $lp$A modest home located among substantially more expensive homes benefits from the principle of:$lp$, $lp$["Regression", "Progression", "Substitution", "Balance"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Regression is the reverse — a higher-value property dragged down by lower-value neighbors.", "Correct. Progression means a lower-value property gains value from surrounding higher-value properties — the basis of 'worst house on the best street.'", "Substitution caps value at the cost of an equally desirable alternative.", "Balance concerns the proper proportion of the agents of production."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["progression / regression", "conformity"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-13-004$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 13, $lp$valuation$lp$, 2, $lp$Highest and best use requires that the use be legally permissible, physically possible, financially feasible, and:$lp$, $lp$["Maximally productive", "Currently existing", "Approved by the assessor", "Consistent with the owner's intent"]$lp$::jsonb, 0, $lp$["Correct. The four tests are legally permissible, physically possible, financially feasible, and maximally productive — applied in that order.", "Highest and best use may differ from the current use; an old house on a commercial lot is the classic case.", "The assessor determines assessed value for taxation, not highest and best use.", "The owner's intent does not control an objective valuation analysis."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["highest and best use"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-13-005$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 13, $lp$valuation$lp$, 2, $lp$In the sales comparison approach, when the comparable property is SUPERIOR to the subject, the appraiser:$lp$, $lp$["Adds value to the subject", "Subtracts value from the comparable", "Adds value to the comparable", "Subtracts value from the subject"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["The subject is never adjusted; only comparables move.", "Correct. CBS — Comparable Better, Subtract. Adjustments are always applied to the comparable to make it resemble the subject.", "Adding to a superior comparable would move it further from the subject.", "Adjusting the subject is the single most common error in this approach."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["CBS / CIA", "sales comparison approach"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-13-006$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 13, $lp$valuation$lp$, 3, $lp$A comparable sold for $812,000. It has an extra bedroom worth $25,000 and a pool worth $18,000 that the subject lacks, but only a 1-car garage where the subject has a 2-car garage (a $12,000 difference). Ignoring time, the indicated value of the subject is:$lp$, $lp$["$781,000", "$867,000", "$757,000", "$805,000"]$lp$::jsonb, 0, $lp$["Correct. 812,000 − 25,000 (comp's extra bedroom) − 18,000 (comp's pool) + 12,000 (comp's inferior garage) = $781,000.", "$867,000 adds all three adjustments, reversing the direction on the superior features.", "$757,000 subtracts the garage adjustment instead of adding it.", "$805,000 would result only if a time adjustment were also applied, which this question excludes."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["CBS / CIA", "sales comparison approach"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-13-007$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 13, $lp$valuation$lp$, 2, $lp$The cost approach formula is:$lp$, $lp$["Replacement cost plus depreciation plus land value", "Land value plus replacement cost of improvements minus accrued depreciation", "Net operating income divided by the capitalization rate", "Gross rent multiplied by the gross rent multiplier"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Depreciation is subtracted, not added.", "Correct. Land is valued separately by sales comparison and is never depreciated; depreciated improvement value is added to it.", "That is the income approach formula.", "That is the gross rent multiplier method."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["cost approach"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-13-008$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 13, $lp$valuation$lp$, 3, $lp$A home has a replacement cost of $465,000, an effective age of 18 years, and a total economic life of 60 years. The land is worth $290,000. Using age-life depreciation, the indicated value is:$lp$, $lp$["$615,500", "$325,500", "$755,000", "$465,000"]$lp$::jsonb, 0, $lp$["Correct. Depreciation = 18 ÷ 60 = 30%; 465,000 × 0.30 = 139,500; 465,000 − 139,500 = 325,500 improvements; + 290,000 land = $615,500.", "$325,500 is the depreciated improvement value before adding land.", "$755,000 adds land to the undepreciated replacement cost.", "$465,000 is the replacement cost alone."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["cost approach", "effective age", "economic life"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-13-009$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 13, $lp$valuation$lp$, 2, $lp$A well-maintained home loses value because a large employer closed nearby. This loss is:$lp$, $lp$["Physical deterioration, and curable", "Functional obsolescence, and curable", "External obsolescence, and incurable", "Deferred maintenance, and curable"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["Nothing about the structure has worn out.", "The design and utility of the home are unaffected; the cause is off the property.", "Correct. Loss caused by factors outside the property lines is external (economic) obsolescence, and it is always incurable because the owner cannot fix what is off the property.", "Deferred maintenance is postponed repair work, a form of physical deterioration."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["external (economic) obsolescence"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-13-010$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 13, $lp$valuation$lp$, 2, $lp$A five-bedroom house with only one bathroom suffers from:$lp$, $lp$["Physical deterioration", "Functional obsolescence", "External obsolescence", "Economic life expiration"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["No wear or deterioration is described.", "Correct. A defect in the structure's own design or utility — here an inadequate bathroom count for the bedroom count — is functional obsolescence.", "External obsolescence arises from causes outside the property lines.", "Economic life is the period improvements contribute to value, not a form of obsolescence."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["functional obsolescence"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-13-011$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 13, $lp$valuation$lp$, 2, $lp$In calculating net operating income, which item is NOT deducted?$lp$, $lp$["Property taxes", "Property management fees", "Mortgage principal and interest payments", "Insurance premiums"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["Property taxes are an operating expense.", "Management fees are an operating expense.", "Correct. Debt service is excluded from NOI, along with depreciation and income taxes, because NOI measures what the property produces independent of how any owner financed it.", "Insurance is an operating expense."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["net operating income (NOI)"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-13-012$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 13, $lp$valuation$lp$, 3, $lp$A fourplex rents for $2,450 per unit monthly with 5% vacancy and $38,600 in annual operating expenses. At a 6.25% cap rate, the indicated value is approximately:$lp$, $lp$["$1,881,600", "$1,169,920", "$1,787,520", "$617,600"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["$1,881,600 capitalizes potential gross income without deducting vacancy or expenses.", "Correct. PGI = 2,450 × 4 × 12 = 117,600; less 5% vacancy = 111,720 EGI; less 38,600 expenses = 73,120 NOI; ÷ 0.0625 = $1,169,920.", "$1,787,520 capitalizes effective gross income without deducting operating expenses.", "$617,600 corresponds to no step in the calculation."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["net operating income (NOI)", "capitalization rate"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-13-013$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 13, $lp$valuation$lp$, 3, $lp$A property with $73,120 in net operating income is listed at $1,295,000. The capitalization rate the buyer would receive is approximately:$lp$, $lp$["6.25%", "5.65%", "17.7%", "4.20%"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["6.25% would correspond to a price near $1,169,900, not $1,295,000.", "Correct. R = I ÷ V = 73,120 ÷ 1,295,000 = 0.0565, or about 5.65% — a higher price yields a lower cap rate.", "17.7% inverts the calculation, dividing value by income.", "4.20% corresponds to no relationship between these figures."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["capitalization rate"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-13-014$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 13, $lp$valuation$lp$, 2, $lp$For a given net operating income, an increase in the capitalization rate will:$lp$, $lp$["Increase the value", "Decrease the value", "Leave value unchanged", "Increase value only if rents rise"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Value and cap rate move inversely, not together.", "Correct. Since V = I ÷ R, a larger denominator produces a smaller value — which is why investors say values fall when 'cap rates expand.'", "The relationship is mathematically direct and inverse; value must change.", "The inverse relationship holds regardless of rent changes."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["capitalization rate"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-13-015$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 13, $lp$valuation$lp$, 3, $lp$A duplex sold for $702,000 with monthly gross rent of $3,900. Its gross rent multiplier is:$lp$, $lp$["15.0", "180.0", "0.0056", "18.0"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["15.0 would result from using annual rent rather than monthly.", "Correct. GRM = price ÷ gross rent = 702,000 ÷ 3,900 = 180.0 using monthly rent, the residential convention.", "This inverts the calculation.", "18.0 misplaces a decimal."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["gross rent multiplier (GRM)"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-13-016$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 13, $lp$valuation$lp$, 2, $lp$The principal limitation of the gross rent multiplier is that it:$lp$, $lp$["Requires at least ten comparable sales", "Ignores operating expenses", "Cannot be used on residential property", "Requires an appraiser's license to calculate"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["No minimum number of comparables is inherent to the method.", "Correct. GRM uses gross rent and ignores expenses entirely, so two properties with identical rents but very different tax or HOA burdens produce the same GRM value — which is why it screens rather than decides.", "GRM is most commonly applied to residential rental property.", "Licensees routinely calculate GRM; it is not a licensed appraisal activity."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["gross rent multiplier (GRM)"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-13-017$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 13, $lp$valuation$lp$, 2, $lp$Which approach would an appraiser weight most heavily when valuing a newly constructed church?$lp$, $lp$["Sales comparison approach", "Income approach", "Cost approach", "Gross rent multiplier"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["Comparable sales of churches are scarce, making this approach unreliable here.", "A church generates no income stream to capitalize.", "Correct. The cost approach is most reliable for new construction and special-purpose properties where comparables are scarce and there is no income to capitalize.", "GRM requires rental income, which the property does not produce."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["cost approach", "reconciliation"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-13-018$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 13, $lp$valuation$lp$, 2, $lp$In reconciliation, the appraiser arrives at a final opinion of value by:$lp$, $lp$["Averaging the three approaches", "Always selecting the highest indication", "Weighing the approaches by reliability for the property type and assignment", "Selecting the value closest to the contract price"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["Averaging is expressly not how reconciliation works and is a classic wrong answer.", "Selecting the highest figure would abandon professional judgment.", "Correct. Reconciliation is judgment: the appraiser weights each approach by its reliability for this property type and the quality of its data.", "Anchoring to the contract price would compromise appraiser independence."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["reconciliation"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-13-019$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 13, $lp$valuation$lp$, 2, $lp$A meticulously maintained and fully updated 1955 home would be described by an appraiser as having a low:$lp$, $lp$["Actual age", "Effective age", "Economic life", "Physical life"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Actual age is fixed by the year built and cannot be low for a 1955 home.", "Correct. Effective age reflects apparent age based on condition and updating, and it — not actual age — drives age-life depreciation.", "A low economic life would mean the improvements contribute value for a shorter period, the opposite of what updating achieves.", "Physical life describes how long the structure will stand, not its apparent condition."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["effective age", "economic life"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-13-020$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 13, $lp$valuation$lp$, 2, $lp$Combining two adjoining parcels under one ownership so the whole is worth more than the sum of the parts creates value known as:$lp$, $lp$["Plottage", "Accretion", "Progression", "Contribution"]$lp$::jsonb, 0, $lp$["Correct. Assemblage is the act of combining adjacent parcels; plottage is the increment of value that combination produces.", "Accretion is the gradual addition of land by waterborne soil deposits.", "Progression concerns value gained from higher-value neighboring properties.", "Contribution measures what a component adds to the whole."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["assemblage / plottage"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-13-021$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 13, $lp$valuation$lp$, 2, $lp$A licensee prepares a pricing analysis using comparable sales for a listing presentation. This document is properly called:$lp$, $lp$["An appraisal", "A comparative market analysis", "A broker's opinion of USPAP value", "A certified valuation"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Only a licensed or certified appraiser may perform and label an appraisal.", "Correct. A CMA is a licensee's pricing analysis for listing or offer purposes; calling it an appraisal would misrepresent the licensee's authority.", "USPAP governs licensed appraisers, and this title misappropriates it.", "There is no such licensee product as a 'certified valuation.'"]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["CMA", "USPAP"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-13-022$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 13, $lp$valuation$lp$, 3, $lp$A listing agent emails the appraiser: 'We need at least $850,000 for this deal to close — please let me know if you can get there.' This communication is:$lp$, $lp$["Acceptable, because agents may provide comparable data", "Improper, because it pressures the appraiser toward a predetermined value", "Acceptable if the seller authorized it in writing", "Improper only if the appraiser agrees to the number"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Providing factual data and relevant comparables is legitimate; requesting a specific value is not.", "Correct. Appraiser independence rules prohibit pressuring an appraiser toward a target value; the agent may supply factual information but never request a number.", "The seller cannot authorize conduct that violates appraiser independence requirements.", "The impropriety lies in making the request, regardless of how the appraiser responds."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["USPAP", "CMA"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-14-001$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 14, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$A city downzones a parcel from 20 units per acre to 8, substantially reducing its value. The owner is entitled to:$lp$, $lp$["Just compensation, because value was taken", "No compensation, because zoning is an exercise of police power", "Severance damages", "An automatic variance"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Compensation is required for eminent domain takings, not ordinary regulation.", "Correct. Zoning rests on police power, which permits regulation for public health, safety, and welfare without compensation, even when value declines.", "Severance damages arise when part of a parcel is condemned, reducing the remainder's value.", "Variances are granted for parcel-specific hardship after application, never automatically."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["police power", "eminent domain"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-14-002$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 14, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$An owner's oddly shaped lot cannot accommodate a house within the required side setbacks. The appropriate relief is a:$lp$, $lp$["Conditional use permit", "Variance", "Rezoning", "Nonconforming use designation"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["A CUP authorizes a particular use the ordinance contemplates, not a dimensional deviation.", "Correct. A variance permits deviation from a dimensional standard because of a hardship unique to the parcel, without changing the permitted use.", "Rezoning changes the parcel's classification, which is not needed for a setback problem.", "Nonconforming status applies to uses that lawfully predate a zoning change."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["variance"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-14-003$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 14, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$A congregation wants to operate a church in a residential zone where the ordinance lists churches as permitted subject to approval. The congregation should apply for a:$lp$, $lp$["Variance", "Conditional use permit", "Zone change", "Amortization extension"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["A variance addresses dimensional hardship, not the type of use.", "Correct. When an ordinance contemplates a use in a zone but requires discretionary review, a conditional use permit is the proper mechanism, typically with conditions on parking, hours, and lighting.", "No reclassification is needed because the ordinance already allows the use conditionally.", "Amortization concerns phasing out an existing nonconforming use."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["conditional use permit (CUP)"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-14-004$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 14, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$A variance may NOT properly be granted on the basis that:$lp$, $lp$["The lot has an unusual triangular shape", "The parcel has extreme slope limiting the buildable area", "The owner needs additional square footage to afford the mortgage", "The lot is substantially smaller than others in the zone"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["Unusual shape is a classic parcel-specific hardship supporting a variance.", "Topographic constraints are a legitimate physical hardship.", "Correct. The hardship must attach to the land, not the owner's finances; personal or economic need is never a valid basis for a variance.", "Substandard lot size relative to the zone is a parcel-specific hardship."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["variance"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-14-005$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 14, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$A neighborhood market lawfully established in 1974 continued operating after the area was rezoned residential in 1991. The market is a:$lp$, $lp$["Conditional use", "Variance holder", "Nonconforming use", "Spot zone"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["A conditional use is approved under the current ordinance, not grandfathered from an earlier one.", "No dimensional variance is involved.", "Correct. A use lawful when established that no longer conforms after a zoning change is a nonconforming use, which generally cannot expand and may be lost by abandonment.", "Spot zoning is an invalid rezoning of a single parcel, not a grandfathered use."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["nonconforming use"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-14-006$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 14, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 3, $lp$A nonconforming corner market closed and sat vacant for three years. A buyer wants to reopen it. Most likely the:$lp$, $lp$["Nonconforming use continues indefinitely once established", "Nonconforming status was lost by abandonment, requiring a rezoning or CUP", "Buyer may reopen after notifying the assessor", "Use automatically converts to a conditional use"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Nonconforming rights are not perpetual; ordinances terminate them on discontinuance.", "Correct. Most ordinances end nonconforming status after a period of discontinuance — often six months to a year — so three years of vacancy almost certainly forfeited it, and discretionary approval would now be required.", "The assessor has no role in land use approvals.", "No automatic conversion to conditional use occurs."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["nonconforming use", "conditional use permit (CUP)"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-14-007$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 14, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$A local zoning ordinance must be:$lp$, $lp$["Approved by the DRE", "Consistent with the jurisdiction's general plan", "Identical to neighboring cities' ordinances", "Reviewed by the county assessor annually"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["The DRE regulates licensees and subdivision sales, not municipal zoning.", "Correct. The general plan is the community's blueprint and zoning implements it; an inconsistent ordinance is vulnerable to legal challenge.", "Each jurisdiction adopts its own ordinances.", "The assessor values property for taxation and has no zoning review role."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["general plan", "zoning ordinance"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-14-008$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 14, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$Rezoning a single parcel inconsistently with surrounding zoning, for the benefit of one owner, is called:$lp$, $lp$["Spot zoning", "Downzoning", "Cluster zoning", "Inclusionary zoning"]$lp$::jsonb, 0, $lp$["Correct. Spot zoning singles out one parcel inconsistently with the surrounding pattern and the general plan, and is frequently held invalid.", "Downzoning reduces permitted intensity across an area.", "Cluster zoning permits concentrated development with preserved open space.", "Inclusionary zoning requires affordable units within market-rate projects."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["spot zoning"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-14-009$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 14, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$When the government takes only part of a parcel and the remainder loses value, the owner may recover:$lp$, $lp$["Punitive damages", "Severance damages", "Liquidated damages", "Nothing beyond the value of the portion taken"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Punitive damages are not available in ordinary condemnation.", "Correct. Severance damages compensate for the reduction in value to the remaining property when part of a parcel is condemned.", "Liquidated damages are a contractual remedy, unrelated to condemnation.", "Just compensation extends to damage suffered by the remainder, not only the part taken."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["severance damages", "eminent domain"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-14-010$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 14, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 3, $lp$A public project permanently blocks all street access to a commercial parcel, destroying most of its value, but no land is formally taken. The owner's action is for:$lp$, $lp$["Inverse condemnation", "Quiet title", "Partition", "Declaratory relief on the general plan"]$lp$::jsonb, 0, $lp$["Correct. Inverse condemnation is the owner's remedy when government activity effectively takes or substantially damages property without formal condemnation proceedings.", "Quiet title resolves competing ownership claims, which are not in dispute.", "Partition divides co-owned property.", "A challenge to the general plan does not compensate the owner for lost access."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["inverse condemnation"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-14-011$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 14, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 3, $lp$The Subdivision Map Act is administered by ______ and primarily protects ______.$lp$, $lp$["the DRE; purchasers of subdivided lots", "local government; the community's orderly development", "the Bureau of Real Estate Appraisers; lenders", "the federal government; interstate land buyers"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["That describes the Subdivided Lands Act, the companion consumer protection statute.", "Correct. The Map Act is administered by cities and counties and governs the physical division of land — maps, dedications, and improvements — to ensure orderly community development.", "Appraiser regulation is unrelated to subdivision mapping.", "The Map Act is a California statute administered locally, not federally."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["Subdivision Map Act", "Subdivided Lands Act"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-14-012$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 14, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$The Subdivided Lands Act is administered by the:$lp$, $lp$["City planning commission", "Department of Real Estate", "Board of supervisors", "Department of Housing and Community Development"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Planning commissions administer local mapping and zoning under the Map Act.", "Correct. The DRE administers the Subdivided Lands Act, a consumer protection statute governing the marketing and sale of subdivided lots through the public report.", "Boards of supervisors act on local subdivision maps, not the state consumer statute.", "HCD regulates manufactured housing and housing programs, not subdivision public reports."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["Subdivided Lands Act", "public report"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-14-013$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 14, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 3, $lp$Holding only a PRELIMINARY public report, a subdivider may lawfully:$lp$, $lp$["Accept binding purchase contracts with non-refundable deposits", "Accept fully refundable, non-binding reservations", "Close escrow on completed homes", "Waive the public report requirement for cash buyers"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Binding contracts and non-refundable purchase money deposits require the final public report.", "Correct. A preliminary public report permits only reservations that are fully refundable and cancellable by either party.", "Closing sales requires the final public report and delivery to the buyer with a signed receipt.", "The requirement cannot be waived based on how the buyer pays."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["preliminary public report", "public report"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-14-014$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 14, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$Before accepting a purchase money deposit in a covered subdivision, the subdivider must give the buyer the public report and obtain:$lp$, $lp$["A recorded acknowledgment", "A signed receipt", "Approval from the county recorder", "A title insurance binder"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Public report delivery is not a recorded event.", "Correct. The subdivider must deliver the public report and obtain the prospective purchaser's signed receipt before taking a purchase money deposit — a document DRE audits examine closely.", "The recorder has no approval role in subdivision sales.", "Title insurance is separate from the public report requirement."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["public report"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-14-015$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 14, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 3, $lp$A rectangular parcel measures 330 feet by 264 feet. Its area in acres is:$lp$, $lp$["1.5 acres", "2.0 acres", "2.5 acres", "3.0 acres"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["1.5 acres would be about 65,340 square feet.", "Correct. 330 × 264 = 87,120 square feet; 87,120 ÷ 43,560 = exactly 2.0 acres.", "2.5 acres would be 108,900 square feet.", "3.0 acres would be 130,680 square feet."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["43,560"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-14-016$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 14, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 3, $lp$A 2.0-acre site must dedicate 18% for streets and drainage. Zoning permits 8 units per acre. The maximum number of units is:$lp$, $lp$["16", "13", "14", "12"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["16 units applies the density to gross acreage without deducting the dedication.", "Correct. Net area = 2.0 − (2.0 × 0.18) = 1.64 acres; 1.64 × 8 = 13.12 units, and density calculations round down to 13.", "14 rounds the 13.12 figure up, but a partial unit cannot be built.", "12 understates the permitted density."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["43,560", "zoning ordinance"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-14-017$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 14, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 3, $lp$A 4.75-acre parcel is listed at $1,235,000. The price per square foot is approximately:$lp$, $lp$["$5.97", "$260.00", "$0.26", "$28.35"]$lp$::jsonb, 0, $lp$["Correct. 4.75 × 43,560 = 206,910 square feet; 1,235,000 ÷ 206,910 = about $5.97 per square foot.", "$260,000 is the price per acre, not per square foot.", "$0.26 misplaces a decimal.", "$28.35 corresponds to no step in the calculation."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["43,560"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-14-018$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 14, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$Under CEQA, when an initial study finds a project may have significant environmental effects, the agency must prepare:$lp$, $lp$["A negative declaration", "An environmental impact report", "A public report", "A general plan amendment"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["A negative declaration is adopted when the project will NOT have significant effects.", "Correct. Significant effects require an EIR analyzing impacts, alternatives, and mitigation measures.", "A public report is a DRE subdivision sales disclosure, unrelated to CEQA.", "A general plan amendment changes the community blueprint and is not the CEQA response to identified impacts."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["CEQA", "environmental impact report (EIR)"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-14-019$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 14, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$CEQA is best described as a statute that:$lp$, $lp$["Prohibits projects causing any environmental impact", "Requires agencies to study, disclose, and mitigate environmental impacts of discretionary projects", "Sets air quality standards for new construction", "Transfers polluted property to state ownership"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["CEQA does not ban impacts; agencies may approve projects with unavoidable impacts upon making required findings.", "Correct. CEQA is a disclosure and process statute requiring environmental review, feasible mitigation, and public disclosure for discretionary approvals.", "Air quality standards come from separate air quality regulation.", "Contaminated property liability arises under CERCLA and related cleanup laws, not CEQA."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["CEQA"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-14-020$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 14, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$Where a city's zoning permits a 35-foot building but the subdivision's recorded CC&Rs limit height to 25 feet, an owner may build to:$lp$, $lp$["35 feet", "25 feet", "30 feet", "Any height, since public law preempts private restrictions"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Zoning sets a public ceiling but does not override a stricter private covenant.", "Correct. Where public zoning and private CC&Rs differ, the more restrictive governs — private restrictions may be stricter than public law, never looser.", "No averaging rule exists between public and private limits.", "Private recorded restrictions bind those who took title subject to them and are not preempted."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["CC&Rs", "zoning ordinance"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-15-001$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 15, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 1, $lp$Which is NOT one of the seven federal protected classes under the Fair Housing Act?$lp$, $lp$["Familial status", "National origin", "Source of income", "Disability"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["Familial status was added in 1988 and is federally protected.", "National origin is one of the original protected classes.", "Correct. Source of income is protected in California under FEHA but is not a federal protected class — a distinction the exam tests directly.", "Disability was added in 1988 and is federally protected."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["federal protected classes", "source of income"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-15-002$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 15, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$A California landlord tells an applicant, 'We don't accept Section 8 vouchers.' This statement:$lp$, $lp$["Is lawful, because vouchers are a financing method", "Violates California's source of income protection", "Is lawful if applied to all applicants equally", "Is lawful for buildings with four or fewer units"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["California treats source of income as a protected characteristic, not a neutral financing preference.", "Correct. Refusing to rent because an applicant will pay with a housing voucher violates FEHA; landlords may apply uniform financial criteria but must count the voucher as income.", "Applying an unlawful criterion uniformly does not make it lawful.", "Small-building exemptions do not authorize source of income discrimination in California."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["source of income", "FEHA"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-15-003$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 15, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 3, $lp$A buyer mentions her religion, and the agent responds, 'There's a wonderful community near the mosque in Rowland Heights — you'd feel right at home,' then focuses showings there. The agent has committed:$lp$, $lp$["No violation, because the agent was being helpful", "Steering", "Blockbusting", "Redlining"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Good intentions are irrelevant; steering violations are typically well-meant, which is precisely why they are common.", "Correct. Directing a buyer toward a neighborhood based on a protected characteristic and narrowing her options accordingly is steering, regardless of intent.", "Blockbusting involves inducing owners to sell by exploiting fear of neighborhood change.", "Redlining is area-based denial of loans, insurance, or services."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["steering"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-15-004$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 15, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$An agent tells homeowners that families of a particular ethnicity are moving in and values will fall, urging them to list now. This is:$lp$, $lp$["Steering", "Blockbusting", "Puffing", "Lawful market commentary if statistically accurate"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Steering directs buyers or renters toward or away from areas.", "Correct. Inducing owners to sell by representing that members of a protected class are entering the neighborhood is blockbusting, also called panic selling, and it is illegal whether or not the statements are true.", "Puffing is exaggerated opinion about a property's qualities.", "Accuracy is no defense; the conduct is prohibited regardless."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["blockbusting"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-15-005$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 15, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$A lender refuses to make loans in a specific neighborhood because of its racial composition, regardless of individual applicant qualifications. This violates the Fair Housing Act and California's:$lp$, $lp$["Subdivided Lands Act", "Holden Act (Housing Financial Discrimination Act)", "Davis-Stirling Act", "Subdivision Map Act"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["The Subdivided Lands Act governs subdivision marketing and public reports.", "Correct. The Holden Act prohibits lending discrimination based on the characteristics of the borrower or the neighborhood — California's direct statutory attack on redlining.", "Davis-Stirling governs common interest developments.", "The Map Act governs the physical division of land."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["redlining", "Holden Act"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-15-006$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 15, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$A tenant with a disability asks the landlord to waive the no-pets policy for her assistance animal. This request is for a:$lp$, $lp$["Reasonable modification, at the tenant's expense", "Reasonable accommodation, at the housing provider's expense", "Variance", "Conditional use permit"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["A modification is a physical change to the premises; waiving a policy is not physical.", "Correct. A change in rules, policies, practices, or services is a reasonable accommodation, and because it costs nothing to implement, the housing provider bears it.", "A variance is zoning relief, unrelated to tenancy.", "A CUP is a land use approval, unrelated to tenancy."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["reasonable accommodation", "assistance animal"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-15-007$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 15, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$A tenant with a disability wants to install grab bars in the bathroom. This is a reasonable modification, and generally:$lp$, $lp$["The landlord must pay for it", "The tenant pays for it", "The cost is split equally", "It may be refused as an alteration"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Providers bear the cost of policy accommodations, not physical modifications.", "Correct. Reasonable modifications are physical changes generally made at the tenant's expense, with restoration on move-out required only where reasonable.", "No cost-splitting rule applies.", "A landlord may not refuse a reasonable modification needed because of a disability."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["reasonable modification"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-15-008$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 15, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 3, $lp$An applicant discloses an emotional support animal. The housing provider may lawfully:$lp$, $lp$["Demand the applicant's medical records", "Request documentation of the disability-related need when the disability is not obvious", "Charge pet rent and a pet deposit", "Apply the building's breed and weight restrictions"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Medical records demands are prohibited; the provider may not inquire into diagnosis or severity.", "Correct. When the disability is not obvious, a provider may request documentation of the disability-related need for the animal — not diagnosis, records, or a purchased 'registration.'", "An assistance animal is not a pet, so pet rent and pet deposits do not apply.", "Breed and weight restrictions generally do not apply to assistance animals."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["assistance animal", "reasonable accommodation"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-15-009$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 15, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$Which advertising phrase most clearly violates fair housing law?$lp$, $lp$["Three-bedroom home with updated kitchen", "Quiet cul-de-sac location", "Perfect for a young family", "Detached two-car garage"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["This describes the property objectively.", "This describes location, not occupants.", "Correct. Referencing buyer characteristics — age and familial status — states a preference. Advertising must describe the property, never the people.", "This describes a physical feature."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["discriminatory advertising"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-15-010$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 15, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$A seller instructs the listing agent not to present offers from buyers who do not speak English. The agent should:$lp$, $lp$["Comply, because the seller owns the property", "Comply if the instruction is documented in the file", "Refuse, explain the law, and withdraw from the listing if the seller persists", "Present all offers but disclose the seller's preference to buyers"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["Ownership creates no exemption, and any theoretical exemption disappears once a licensee is involved.", "Documenting an unlawful instruction does not make following it lawful.", "Correct. Screening by primary language and national origin is unlawful; the agent must refuse, explain the obligation, and withdraw rather than participate.", "Communicating the discriminatory preference to buyers would itself violate fair housing law."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["FEHA", "federal protected classes"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-15-011$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 15, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$The Unruh Civil Rights Act prohibits discrimination by:$lp$, $lp$["Federal agencies only", "All business establishments in California, including brokerages and landlords", "Employers only", "Lenders only"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Unruh is a California statute governing private business conduct.", "Correct. The Unruh Act reaches all business establishments in California, and courts have applied it broadly to arbitrary discrimination beyond an exhaustive list.", "Employment discrimination is addressed by other provisions of FEHA.", "Lending discrimination is addressed specifically by the Holden Act."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["Unruh Civil Rights Act"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-15-012$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 15, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$Familial status protects households that include:$lp$, $lp$["Any two or more related adults", "Children under 18, pregnant persons, and those securing legal custody", "Married couples only", "Any household with a dependent of any age"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Related adults without minor children are not the protected category.", "Correct. Familial status covers households with children under 18, pregnant persons, and persons securing legal custody, with a narrow exception for qualified housing for older persons.", "Marital status is a separate California protection; familial status is about children.", "Adult dependents do not trigger familial status protection."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["familial status", "housing for older persons (HOPA)"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-15-013$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 15, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 3, $lp$A condominium association wishes to advertise itself as 'adults only' to exclude children. This is:$lp$, $lp$["Lawful, because associations may set occupancy rules", "Unlawful unless the community satisfies the strict criteria for housing for older persons", "Lawful if a majority of owners vote for it", "Lawful if children may still visit"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Association authority does not extend to violating familial status protections.", "Correct. Excluding children requires qualification as housing for older persons under strict statutory criteria; a community cannot simply declare itself adults only.", "An owner vote cannot authorize unlawful discrimination.", "Permitting visits does not cure an unlawful exclusion of families from residency."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["housing for older persons (HOPA)", "familial status"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-15-014$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 15, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$An individual who poses as a prospective buyer or renter to detect discrimination is known as a:$lp$, $lp$["Tester", "Straw buyer", "Subagent", "Principal"]$lp$::jsonb, 0, $lp$["Correct. Testers pose as prospects to detect discriminatory treatment; they have standing to sue and the evidence they gather is admissible.", "A straw buyer conceals the true purchaser's identity, often in mortgage fraud.", "A subagent is an agent of an agent.", "A principal is the party an agent represents."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["tester", "paired testing"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-15-015$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 15, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 3, $lp$An agent's records show she gives clients with certain surnames fewer showings and slower response times, though she intended no discrimination. This pattern:$lp$, $lp$["Is legally irrelevant without discriminatory intent", "Can constitute discrimination because fair housing enforcement examines outcomes, not only intent", "Is protected as a business judgment", "Matters only if a client files a complaint"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Intent is not required; differential treatment producing disparate outcomes is actionable.", "Correct. Fair housing enforcement examines outcomes and differential service, which is exactly what paired testing is designed to reveal — the reason implicit bias matters legally, not just ethically.", "Differential service correlated with protected characteristics is not a protected business judgment.", "Liability does not depend on a complaint being filed, and self-auditing to correct a pattern is far better than being investigated."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["implicit bias", "paired testing"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-15-016$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 15, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$A buyer asks the agent, 'Is this a good neighborhood?' The BEST response is to:$lp$, $lp$["Give an honest personal assessment of the area's residents", "Provide objective data sources and let the buyer evaluate them", "Describe the demographic makeup accurately", "Decline to discuss the neighborhood at all"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Characterizing residents invites steering liability.", "Correct. Direct the buyer to objective published sources — crime statistics, school ratings, census data — so the buyer evaluates the area themselves without the agent characterizing it.", "Describing demographics is precisely what an agent must not do.", "Refusing to engage is unhelpful; providing neutral factual resources is both compliant and better service."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["steering"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-15-017$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 15, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$California protects several characteristics beyond the federal seven, INCLUDING:$lp$, $lp$["Credit score", "Marital status and immigration status", "Employment tenure", "Debt-to-income ratio"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Credit score is a legitimate, uniformly applied financial criterion.", "Correct. California adds marital status, ancestry, source of income, sexual orientation, gender identity and expression, genetic information, citizenship and immigration status, primary language, military status, and age, among others.", "Length of employment is a financial qualification factor, not a protected characteristic.", "Debt-to-income ratio is a legitimate underwriting measure."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["FEHA"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-15-018$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 15, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 3, $lp$A fair housing violation by a licensee can result in:$lp$, $lp$["Civil liability only", "DRE discipline only", "Civil liability, agency enforcement, and DRE license discipline", "A warning letter for a first offense"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["Civil damages are one exposure among several.", "License discipline is independent of, but not exclusive of, other remedies.", "Correct. Three consequences stack independently: civil liability with damages and fees, administrative fair housing enforcement, and DRE suspension or revocation — settling one does not resolve the others.", "No first-offense warning system protects licensees from fair housing consequences."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["FEHA", "Unruh Civil Rights Act"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-15-019$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 15, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$The federal exemption for certain owner-occupied buildings of four or fewer units does NOT apply when:$lp$, $lp$["The owner lives in one of the units", "A real estate licensee is involved in the transaction", "The building was constructed before 1968", "The tenants share a common entrance"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Owner occupancy is a condition of the exemption, not something that defeats it.", "Correct. The exemptions never apply when a licensee participates, and they never permit discriminatory advertising — and California's broader statutes largely eliminate them in practice.", "The construction date does not control the exemption.", "Shared entrances are not the determining factor."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["Fair Housing Act", "FEHA"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-15-020$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 15, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$Which listing phrase is MOST likely to raise a fair housing concern?$lp$, $lp$["Walking distance to St. Rita's parish", "Newly remodeled kitchen with quartz counters", "Close to public transit and shopping", "Covered patio and mature landscaping"]$lp$::jsonb, 0, $lp$["Correct. Naming a religious institution as a landmark can signal a religious preference; a neutral proximity reference is the safer construction.", "This describes a physical feature of the property.", "This describes neutral, objective proximity to amenities.", "This describes physical features."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["discriminatory advertising"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-15-021$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 15, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$Implicit bias is best defined as:$lp$, $lp$["Deliberate discrimination concealed from regulators", "Unconscious association that influences judgment without conscious intent", "A lawful business preference among clients", "A defense to a fair housing claim"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Deliberate concealed discrimination is intentional, not implicit.", "Correct. Implicit bias operates unconsciously, which is why standardizing processes is more effective than relying on good intentions.", "Preferences among clients based on protected characteristics are never lawful.", "Lack of conscious intent is not a defense; enforcement examines conduct and outcomes."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["implicit bias"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-15-022$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 15, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 3, $lp$A seller says she prefers owner-occupant buyers over investors. The agent may:$lp$, $lp$["Not honor the preference, because it is discriminatory", "Honor it, because investor status is not a protected characteristic", "Honor it only if the buyer pool is diverse", "Honor it only with written DRE approval"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Preferring owner-occupants does not implicate any protected characteristic.", "Correct. Investor status is not protected, so a seller may lawfully prefer owner-occupant buyers — unlike preferences based on familial status, national origin, or language, which are unlawful.", "The composition of the buyer pool does not determine the legality of a non-protected preference.", "No DRE approval process exists or is needed for a lawful buyer preference."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["federal protected classes", "FEHA"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-16-001$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 16, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 1, $lp$Under Proposition 13, the basic property tax rate is limited to:$lp$, $lp$["1% of assessed value", "2% of assessed value", "1% of market value each year", "2% of the purchase price"]$lp$::jsonb, 0, $lp$["Correct. Proposition 13 caps the basic rate at 1% of assessed value; bonded indebtedness and direct assessments are added on top.", "2% is the annual cap on assessment increases, not the tax rate.", "Assessed value, not current market value, is the tax base — that is the entire point of Prop 13.", "The purchase price sets the base year value; it is not itself the rate."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["Proposition 13", "ad valorem"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-16-002$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 16, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$Under Proposition 13, a property's assessed value is generally reset to current market value upon:$lp$, $lp$["Refinancing the existing loan", "A change of ownership or completion of new construction", "Every fifth anniversary of purchase", "Any increase in neighborhood values"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Refinancing involves no change of ownership and does not trigger reassessment.", "Correct. Reassessment to market value occurs on a change of ownership or new construction; otherwise increases are capped at 2% per year.", "There is no periodic reassessment cycle under Prop 13.", "Rising neighborhood values do not reassess a property absent a triggering event."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["Proposition 13", "base year value"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-16-003$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 16, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 3, $lp$A buyer purchases for $1,180,000. The sellers, who bought in 1996, currently pay $4,900 annually. The buyer's first full-year tax bill will be approximately:$lp$, $lp$["$4,900, because the assessment carries over", "$13,900 at a typical effective rate near 1.18%", "$2,360, at 0.2% of the price", "$23,600, at 2% of the price"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["The sellers' assessment does not carry over; the base year value resets to the purchase price on change of ownership.", "Correct. The base year value resets to $1,180,000; at roughly 1.18% including bonds and direct assessments, the bill is about $13,900 — nearly $9,000 more than the sellers paid.", "0.2% is far below even the base 1% rate.", "2% is the annual assessment increase cap, not the tax rate."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["Proposition 13", "base year value"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-16-004$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 16, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$The California property tax lien date is:$lp$, $lp$["July 1", "January 1", "November 1", "December 10"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["July 1 begins the fiscal year, but the lien attaches earlier.", "Correct. Taxes attach as a lien on January 1 preceding the fiscal year that begins July 1.", "November 1 is when the first installment is due.", "December 10 is the first installment delinquency date."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["lien date"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-16-005$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 16, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$The second installment of California property taxes becomes delinquent on:$lp$, $lp$["February 1", "April 10", "December 10", "June 30"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["February 1 is the due date for the second installment, not the delinquency date.", "Correct. 'No Darn Fooling Around': November 1 due, December 10 delinquent, February 1 due, April 10 delinquent.", "December 10 is the first installment's delinquency date.", "June 30 ends the fiscal year."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["No Darn Fooling Around"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-16-006$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 16, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$After closing, a buyer receives an unexpected separate tax bill covering the difference between the old and new assessed values. This is a:$lp$, $lp$["Supplemental assessment", "Special assessment", "Documentary transfer tax", "Escape assessment penalty"]$lp$::jsonb, 0, $lp$["Correct. A supplemental assessment bills the difference between old and new assessed values prorated for the remainder of the fiscal year — it arrives separately, often months later, and is not covered by the impound account.", "A special assessment funds local improvements such as Mello-Roos districts.", "The documentary transfer tax is a one-time charge at closing on the transfer itself.", "This is not a penalty; it is the ordinary consequence of reassessment."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["supplemental assessment"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-16-007$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 16, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$Which transfer does NOT trigger reassessment for property tax purposes?$lp$, $lp$["A sale to an unrelated buyer", "A transfer between spouses", "A sale of a rental property to an investor", "A sale of a second home to an adult child who will rent it out"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["An arm's-length sale is the classic change of ownership triggering reassessment.", "Correct. Interspousal transfers, including on divorce or death, are excluded from reassessment.", "A sale to an investor is a change of ownership.", "Proposition 19 narrowed the parent–child exclusion to a principal residence the transferee also occupies as a principal residence, subject to a value cap — a rental would not qualify."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["Proposition 19", "base year value"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-16-008$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 16, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 3, $lp$A 68-year-old homeowner wants to sell and buy a smaller home elsewhere in California without losing her low assessed value. Under Proposition 19 she may:$lp$, $lp$["Transfer her base year value to a replacement principal residence, subject to limits", "Keep her old assessment permanently on any new property", "Do nothing; base year values are never transferable", "Transfer her assessment only within the same county"]$lp$::jsonb, 0, $lp$["Correct. Proposition 19 permits homeowners 55 or older, severely disabled persons, and disaster victims to transfer a base year value to a replacement principal residence anywhere in California, with adjustments if the replacement costs more.", "The benefit applies to a replacement principal residence with limits, not to any property permanently.", "Base year value portability exists and was expanded by Proposition 19.", "Proposition 19 expanded portability statewide, removing the former county restrictions."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["Proposition 19", "base year value"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-16-009$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 16, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$A recurring assessment funding infrastructure in a newer Inland Empire subdivision, which must be disclosed to buyers, is a:$lp$, $lp$["Homeowners' exemption", "Mello-Roos community facilities district assessment", "Supplemental assessment", "Documentary transfer tax"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["The homeowners' exemption reduces assessed value rather than adding a charge.", "Correct. Mello-Roos CFD assessments fund infrastructure in newer developments, can add hundreds of dollars monthly, and carry a specific disclosure requirement.", "A supplemental assessment is a one-time reassessment bill, not a recurring district charge.", "The transfer tax applies once at transfer."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["Mello-Roos (CFD)", "special assessment"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-16-010$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 16, $lp$transfer$lp$, 3, $lp$A property sells for $918,000 and the buyer assumes an existing $300,000 loan. At $0.55 per $500, the county documentary transfer tax is:$lp$, $lp$["$1,009.80", "$679.80", "$330.00", "$505.00"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["$1,009.80 taxes the full price, correct only if the buyer obtained new financing instead of assuming the loan.", "Correct. Tax applies to consideration net of the assumed loan: (918,000 − 300,000) ÷ 500 = 1,236 × $0.55 = $679.80.", "$330.00 taxes only the assumed loan amount.", "$505.00 corresponds to no step in the calculation."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["documentary transfer tax"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-16-011$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 16, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$Which expenditure ADDS to a homeowner's adjusted basis?$lp$, $lp$["Repainting the interior", "Repairing a leaking faucet", "Adding a bedroom", "Annual pest control service"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["Repainting is a repair that maintains rather than improves the property.", "Fixing a leak is a repair, not a capital improvement.", "Correct. A capital improvement such as an addition increases adjusted basis, which reduces taxable gain on sale — the reason to keep receipts from day one.", "Routine service is a maintenance expense."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["basis / adjusted basis", "capital improvement vs. repair"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-16-012$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 16, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$To claim the principal residence gain exclusion, a taxpayer must generally have owned and used the home as a principal residence for at least:$lp$, $lp$["1 of the last 3 years", "2 of the last 5 years", "3 of the last 7 years", "5 of the last 10 years"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["This is not the statutory test.", "Correct. The exclusion requires ownership and use as a principal residence for at least 2 of the 5 years preceding the sale, and is generally available once every two years.", "This is not the statutory test.", "This is not the statutory test."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["principal residence exclusion (§121)"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-16-013$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 16, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 3, $lp$A married couple bought for $385,000, added $90,000 in capital improvements, and sold for $1,090,000 with $70,850 in selling costs. Their taxable gain after the §121 exclusion is:$lp$, $lp$["$0", "$44,150", "$544,150", "$134,150"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["The gain exceeds the $500,000 exclusion, so some remains taxable.", "Correct. Adjusted basis = 475,000; amount realized = 1,090,000 − 70,850 = 1,019,150; gain = 544,150; less the $500,000 married exclusion = $44,150 taxable.", "$544,150 is the gain before applying the exclusion.", "$134,150 would be the taxable gain if the $90,000 of improvements had not been added to basis — illustrating why records matter."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["principal residence exclusion (§121)", "amount realized"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-16-014$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 16, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$In a 1031 exchange, the taxpayer must identify replacement property in writing within ______ and complete the acquisition within ______ of the relinquished property's closing.$lp$, $lp$["30 days; 90 days", "45 days; 180 days", "60 days; 180 days", "45 days; 365 days"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["These are not the statutory periods.", "Correct. 45 days to identify in writing and 180 days to close, both running concurrently from the relinquished closing and generally not extendable.", "The identification period is 45 days, not 60.", "The exchange period is 180 days, not a full year."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["1031 exchange", "45-day / 180-day rules"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-16-015$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 16, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 3, $lp$An investor closes her relinquished property and has the $552,000 in net proceeds wired to her personal account for two days before buying the replacement. The exchange:$lp$, $lp$["Remains valid because she reinvested the full amount", "Fails, because a qualified intermediary must hold the proceeds", "Remains valid if she documents the transfer", "Remains valid because the 45-day rule was met"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Reinvesting the money does not cure constructive receipt of the proceeds.", "Correct. A qualified intermediary must hold the funds; if the taxpayer receives the proceeds, the exchange fails entirely and the full realized gain becomes taxable.", "Documentation cannot undo actual receipt of the funds.", "Meeting the deadlines is necessary but not sufficient — the intermediary requirement is independent."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["qualified intermediary", "1031 exchange"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-16-016$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 16, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 3, $lp$In an exchange, the relinquished property carried $310,000 of debt and the replacement carries $260,000. Realized gain is $432,000 and no cash was received. The recognized taxable gain is:$lp$, $lp$["$0", "$50,000", "$432,000", "$382,000"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Debt relief creates boot even when no cash changes hands.", "Correct. Debt relief of $50,000 is mortgage boot, and recognized gain equals the lesser of boot or realized gain — so $50,000 is taxable and $382,000 is deferred.", "$432,000 would be recognized only if the exchange failed entirely.", "$382,000 is the deferred portion, not the taxable portion."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["boot", "1031 exchange"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-16-017$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 16, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$Which property qualifies for a 1031 exchange?$lp$, $lp$["The taxpayer's principal residence", "A rental fourplex held for investment", "A vacation home used exclusively for personal enjoyment", "Shares of a real estate investment trust"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["A personal residence does not qualify; the §121 exclusion addresses it instead.", "Correct. Section 1031 applies to real property held for investment or productive use in a trade or business.", "Purely personal-use property does not qualify.", "Securities are excluded, and since 2018 §1031 applies to real property only."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["1031 exchange"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-16-018$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 16, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$For depreciation purposes on an investment property, the investor may depreciate:$lp$, $lp$["The land only", "The improvements only", "Both land and improvements", "Neither, until the property is sold"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Land is never depreciated, in taxation or appraisal.", "Correct. Only improvements are depreciated over the applicable recovery period; depreciation reduces basis and therefore increases gain on sale.", "Including land would overstate the deduction; land is excluded.", "Depreciation is taken annually during ownership, not deferred to sale."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["depreciation (cost recovery)"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-16-019$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 16, $lp$transfer$lp$, 2, $lp$FIRPTA requires withholding at closing when the seller is:$lp$, $lp$["A first-time seller", "A foreign person", "Any seller with a capital gain", "An out-of-state California resident"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Selling for the first time has no withholding consequence.", "Correct. FIRPTA is the federal withholding regime applying to dispositions by foreign persons, with exemptions for certain lower-priced residences the buyer will occupy.", "Realizing a gain does not by itself trigger federal withholding.", "Out-of-state sellers may trigger California withholding, which is a separate state requirement."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["FIRPTA", "California withholding"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-16-020$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 16, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$A client asks the agent to calculate her capital gains tax liability on a pending sale. The agent should:$lp$, $lp$["Perform the calculation using the §121 rules", "Explain that the exclusion exists and refer her to a CPA", "Refuse to discuss taxes in any form", "Provide the calculation with a written disclaimer"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Computing a client's tax liability exceeds a licensee's authority and risks unlicensed practice.", "Correct. A licensee may explain that provisions such as the §121 exclusion exist and should be discussed with a CPA, but must not calculate liability or advise on tax strategy.", "Refusing to mention the subject is unhelpful; flagging the issue and referring is the professional standard.", "A disclaimer does not convert prohibited tax advice into permitted activity."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["principal residence exclusion (§121)"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-17-001$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 17, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$A residential lease contains a clause stating the tenant waives the warranty of habitability. This clause is:$lp$, $lp$["Enforceable if the tenant initialed it", "Void, because the warranty of habitability cannot be waived", "Enforceable if rent is below market", "Enforceable in month-to-month tenancies only"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Initialing cannot validate a waiver the law forbids.", "Correct. California implies a non-waivable warranty of habitability in every residential lease; a clause purporting to waive it is void.", "Reduced rent does not purchase a waiver of habitability.", "The warranty applies regardless of tenancy type."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["warranty of habitability"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-17-002$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 17, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$Which deduction from a residential security deposit is NOT permitted?$lp$, $lp$["Unpaid rent", "Cleaning to the level of cleanliness at the start of the tenancy", "Repainting due to normal fading after a two-year tenancy", "Repairing a large hole punched in a wall"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["Unpaid rent is expressly deductible.", "Cleaning to restore the original level of cleanliness is deductible.", "Correct. Ordinary wear and tear — including paint that has simply aged through normal occupancy — may never be deducted.", "Damage beyond ordinary wear and tear is deductible."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["ordinary wear and tear", "security deposit"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-17-003$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 17, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$After a residential tenant vacates, the landlord must return the security deposit with an itemized statement within:$lp$, $lp$["14 days", "21 days", "30 days", "45 days"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["14 days is not the California deadline.", "Correct. California requires return of the deposit with an itemized statement, and supporting receipts or invoices for deductions, within 21 days after the tenant vacates.", "30 days is a common figure in other states but not California's rule.", "45 days is not the California deadline."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["21-day rule", "security deposit"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-17-004$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 17, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$A California residential landlord charges a $400 'non-refundable cleaning fee' at move-in. This charge is:$lp$, $lp$["Lawful if disclosed in the lease", "Unlawful, because California residential deposits must be refundable", "Lawful if the unit is furnished", "Lawful up to one month's rent"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Disclosure does not convert a prohibited non-refundable charge into a lawful one.", "Correct. All residential security deposits in California are refundable; labeling a charge non-refundable does not make it so, though actual cleaning costs may be deducted at move-out.", "Furnishing status affects deposit caps historically, not refundability.", "The cap governs the amount, not whether a charge may be non-refundable."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["security deposit"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-17-005$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 17, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$A tenant has not paid rent. The landlord's first step toward eviction is to serve a:$lp$, $lp$["Three-day notice to pay rent or quit", "Thirty-day notice to terminate", "Writ of possession", "Notice of default"]$lp$::jsonb, 0, $lp$["Correct. Nonpayment requires a three-day notice to pay rent or quit before an unlawful detainer may be filed.", "A 30-day notice terminates a month-to-month tenancy without cause; it is not the nonpayment remedy.", "A writ of possession issues after judgment, at the end of the process.", "A notice of default begins foreclosure on a loan, not an eviction."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["three-day notice", "unlawful detainer"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-17-006$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 17, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$After obtaining an unlawful detainer judgment and a writ of possession, the tenant is physically removed by:$lp$, $lp$["The landlord", "The property manager", "The sheriff", "A licensed private security firm"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["Landlord self-help removal is illegal and exposes the landlord to significant damages.", "A property manager has no more authority to remove a tenant than the owner does.", "Correct. Only the sheriff, acting under a writ of possession, may physically remove a tenant.", "Private parties may not execute a lockout regardless of licensing."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["writ of possession", "unlawful detainer"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-17-007$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 17, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 3, $lp$Frustrated by a holdover tenant, a landlord shuts off the water and removes the front door. The landlord has:$lp$, $lp$["Lawfully exercised the right of reentry", "Committed an illegal self-help eviction exposing him to damages", "Constructively evicted the tenant, ending all obligations", "Properly mitigated damages"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["No right of reentry authorizes utility shutoffs or removing doors.", "Correct. Self-help evictions — lockouts, utility shutoffs, removing doors or belongings — are illegal in California and expose the landlord to actual damages, statutory penalties, and attorney's fees.", "Constructive eviction is a tenant's remedy against a landlord, not a lawful landlord tactic.", "Illegal conduct is not damage mitigation."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["unlawful detainer", "constructive eviction"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-17-008$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 17, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$A landlord wishes to enter a tenant's unit to show it to a prospective buyer. Generally the landlord must give:$lp$, $lp$["No notice, since the landlord owns the property", "Reasonable written notice, with 24 hours presumed reasonable", "One week's written notice", "Notice only if the tenant objects"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Ownership does not eliminate the tenant's right to possession and quiet enjoyment.", "Correct. Entry for repairs, inspections, or showings requires reasonable written notice, with 24 hours presumed reasonable, during normal business hours.", "A week exceeds the statutory expectation.", "The notice obligation is affirmative and does not depend on the tenant objecting."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["right of entry"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-17-009$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 17, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$A tenant complains to the city about a code violation, and two weeks later the landlord serves a rent increase and termination notice. This is likely:$lp$, $lp$["Lawful, because landlords may raise rent at will", "A prohibited retaliatory eviction", "Constructive eviction", "A permitted no-fault termination"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Rent increases are not unlimited, and motive matters when it follows protected activity.", "Correct. Retaliation for complaining to a code enforcement agency or exercising repair rights is prohibited, and California presumes retaliation when adverse action follows protected activity within a statutory window.", "Constructive eviction describes conditions making premises unusable.", "A no-fault termination must have a legitimate statutory basis and cannot mask retaliation."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["retaliatory eviction"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-17-010$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 17, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 3, $lp$Under the Tenant Protection Act, an annual rent increase on covered property is generally limited to:$lp$, $lp$["3% flat", "5% plus local CPI, not to exceed 10%", "10% plus CPI", "Whatever the market supports"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["No flat 3% statewide cap applies under AB 1482.", "Correct. The statewide cap is 5% plus local CPI, with a hard ceiling of 10%, whichever is lower — and local ordinances may be stricter.", "This inverts the formula and exceeds the statutory ceiling.", "Covered property is subject to the statutory cap regardless of market conditions."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["Tenant Protection Act (AB 1482)"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-17-011$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 17, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 3, $lp$A tenant in a covered 1985 building pays $1,850. Local CPI is 3.2%. The maximum lawful new rent is approximately:$lp$, $lp$["$1,942.50", "$2,001.70", "$2,035.00", "$2,200.00"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["$1,942.50 applies only a 5% increase, omitting the CPI component.", "Correct. 5% + 3.2% = 8.2%, below the 10% ceiling; $1,850 × 1.082 = $2,001.70.", "$2,035.00 corresponds to a 10% increase, which exceeds the applicable formula here.", "$2,200 is an 18.9% increase and is unlawful under the Act."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["Tenant Protection Act (AB 1482)"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-17-012$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 17, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$Under the Tenant Protection Act, just cause is required to terminate a tenancy once the tenant has occupied the unit for:$lp$, $lp$["30 days", "6 months", "12 months", "24 months"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["No just cause protection attaches that quickly.", "Six months is not the statutory threshold.", "Correct. After 12 months of occupancy, a covered tenancy may be terminated only for a stated just cause, either at-fault or no-fault.", "24 months exceeds the statutory threshold."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["just cause", "Tenant Protection Act (AB 1482)"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-17-013$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 17, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$A landlord terminates a covered tenancy so the owner's daughter can move in. This no-fault just cause generally requires:$lp$, $lp$["Nothing beyond proper notice", "Relocation assistance to the tenant", "A court order", "Payment of the tenant's moving company invoice in full"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Notice alone is insufficient for a no-fault termination under the Act.", "Correct. No-fault just cause terminations require relocation assistance, typically one month's rent or an equivalent rent waiver.", "No court order is required to serve a proper just cause notice.", "The obligation is a statutory relocation payment, not reimbursement of actual moving invoices."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["relocation assistance", "just cause"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-17-014$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 17, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 3, $lp$A tenant transfers her ENTIRE remaining lease term to a new tenant. Absent a novation, the original tenant:$lp$, $lp$["Is fully released from the lease", "Remains secondarily liable to the landlord", "Becomes a sublessor", "Must vacate within three days"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Only a novation, with the landlord's agreement, releases the original tenant.", "Correct. An assignment transfers the entire remaining interest, but the assignor remains secondarily liable unless the landlord grants a release — the reason a clean exit requires an assignment plus a written release.", "Transferring the entire interest is an assignment; a sublease transfers less than the whole.", "No vacate deadline arises from an assignment."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["assignment", "sublease"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-17-015$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 17, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$In a sublease, the subtenant's contractual relationship is with the:$lp$, $lp$["Original landlord", "Original tenant", "Property manager", "Lender"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["A sublease creates no direct privity between the subtenant and the landlord.", "Correct. In a sublease the subtenant answers to the original tenant, who becomes a sublessor and remains fully liable on the master lease.", "The manager acts for the owner but is not a party to the sublease.", "A lender has no role in the sublease relationship."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["sublease", "assignment"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-17-016$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 17, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$A retail lease providing base rent plus a percentage of the tenant's gross sales is a:$lp$, $lp$["Gross lease", "Percentage lease", "Ground lease", "Index lease"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["A gross lease has fixed rent with the landlord paying operating expenses.", "Correct. A percentage lease combines base rent with a share of the tenant's gross sales, aligning landlord and tenant interests; it is standard in shopping centers.", "A ground lease is a long-term lease of land on which the tenant builds.", "An index lease adjusts rent by reference to a published index such as CPI."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["percentage lease", "gross lease"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-17-017$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 17, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$In a triple net (NNN) lease, the tenant typically pays rent plus:$lp$, $lp$["Nothing additional", "Taxes, insurance, and maintenance", "A percentage of gross sales", "The landlord's mortgage payment"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["That describes a gross lease.", "Correct. Triple net shifts taxes, insurance, and maintenance to the tenant in addition to base rent; it is standard in commercial and retail leasing.", "That describes a percentage lease.", "Debt service is the owner's obligation and is not a tenant expense category."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["net lease / triple net (NNN)"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-17-018$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 17, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$A property manager engaged by an owner under a management agreement acts as a:$lp$, $lp$["Special agent", "General agent", "Universal agent", "Subagent of the tenants"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["A special agent has authority for one specific transaction, like a listing broker.", "Correct. A property manager has ongoing authority over a range of matters for the owner, making the relationship a general agency — broader than a listing broker's special agency.", "A universal agent may act in all matters under a broad power of attorney.", "The manager represents the owner, not the tenants."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["management agreement", "general agent"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-17-019$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 17, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 3, $lp$A property manager deposits collected rents into the brokerage's general operating account for a week before transferring them to the trust account. This is:$lp$, $lp$["Acceptable if the funds are fully transferred", "Commingling, a disciplinary violation even without loss", "Acceptable if the owner consents", "Conversion, but only if funds are spent"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Restoring the funds does not cure the mixing.", "Correct. Rents are trust funds belonging to the owner; placing them in a general business account is commingling, which is disciplinable even when no one suffers a loss.", "An owner cannot consent to a violation of trust fund handling rules.", "Conversion is the more serious offense of actually using the funds; commingling is already a violation here."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["trust account", "commingling"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-17-020$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 17, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 3, $lp$A manager collects rent from 7 of 8 units at $2,150 each and charges a 7% fee on collected rents. Expenses are $1,340 maintenance and $620 utilities. The owner's distribution is:$lp$, $lp$["$12,036.50", "$13,090.00", "$11,832.00", "$15,050.00"]$lp$::jsonb, 0, $lp$["Correct. Collected rent = 7 × 2,150 = 15,050; fee = 15,050 × 0.07 = 1,053.50; distribution = 15,050 − 1,053.50 − 1,340 − 620 = $12,036.50.", "$13,090.00 omits the management fee.", "$11,832.00 applies the fee to scheduled rent for all eight units rather than collected rent.", "$15,050.00 is gross collected rent before any disbursements."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["management agreement"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-18-001$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 18, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 1, $lp$Trust funds received by a broker must be placed into escrow, a trust account, or the hands of the principal not later than:$lp$, $lp$["The end of the business day received", "Three business days after receipt", "Five business days after receipt", "Ten calendar days after receipt"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Same-day deposit is best practice but is not the regulatory deadline.", "Correct. The three-business-day rule governs deposits of trust funds by a broker or the broker's salesperson.", "Five days exceeds the regulatory window.", "Ten days exceeds the regulatory window."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["trust funds", "three-business-day rule"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-18-002$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 18, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 3, $lp$A buyer's $25,000 deposit reaches the brokerage Friday at 4 p.m. with written instructions to hold the check uncashed until acceptance. The seller accepts Monday. The deposit deadline runs from:$lp$, $lp$["Friday, when the check was received", "Monday, when the offer was accepted", "The date escrow opens", "The date the buyer's contingencies are removed"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["A properly disclosed instruction to hold the check pending acceptance postpones the obligation past receipt.", "Correct. When the offer directs the check be held uncashed and the seller is informed of that instruction, the three-business-day clock runs from acceptance.", "The regulatory deadline is not tied to escrow's opening.", "Contingency removal occurs well after the deposit obligation attaches."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["uncashed-check exception", "three-business-day rule"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-18-003$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 18, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$A broker deposits a client's earnest money into the brokerage's general operating account over a weekend, then transfers it to the trust account Monday with no loss to anyone. The broker has committed:$lp$, $lp$["No violation, because the funds were restored", "Commingling", "Conversion", "A permissible temporary transfer"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Restoration does not cure the violation; the mixing itself is the offense.", "Correct. Placing trust funds in the broker's own account is commingling, disciplinable even when no client suffers a loss and the funds are promptly restored.", "Conversion requires actually using the funds for another purpose, which did not occur here.", "No allowance permits routing trust funds through a general business account."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["commingling", "conversion"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-18-004$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 18, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$A broker uses a client's trust funds to cover the brokerage payroll, intending to replace the money next month. This is:$lp$, $lp$["Commingling only", "Conversion, supporting revocation and criminal charges", "A permitted short-term loan", "Acceptable if repaid with interest"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Commingling is mixing; here the funds were actually spent, which is the more serious offense.", "Correct. Using trust funds for another purpose is conversion, grounds for license revocation and criminal prosecution.", "Trust funds may never be borrowed by the broker.", "Repayment with interest does not cure misappropriation."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["conversion", "commingling"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-18-005$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 18, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$A broker's monthly trust account reconciliation must show agreement among:$lp$, $lp$["The bank statement and the broker's tax return", "The control record, the sum of all beneficiary ledgers, and the bank statement", "The listing agreements and the escrow instructions", "The commission ledger and the operating account"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Tax filings are unrelated to trust fund reconciliation.", "Correct. All three must agree, and the reconciliation must be performed monthly in writing and retained — a leading DRE audit finding when it is missing.", "Contracts are transaction records, not trust accounting records.", "The operating account is separate from trust funds by definition."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["monthly reconciliation", "control record", "beneficiary ledger"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-18-006$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 18, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 3, $lp$A trust account holds funds for eight beneficiaries. The broker disburses more than one beneficiary's balance, causing that ledger to go negative while total account funds remain positive. This is:$lp$, $lp$["Acceptable, because the account overall is positive", "Conversion, because one beneficiary's funds were used for another", "Commingling only", "A bookkeeping error with no regulatory consequence"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["A positive overall balance does not excuse a negative individual ledger.", "Correct. Using one beneficiary's funds to cover another's disbursement is conversion; no beneficiary's balance may ever go negative.", "The violation goes beyond mixing — client funds were actually applied to another's obligation.", "Negative beneficiary balances are a serious violation, not a harmless error."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["conversion", "beneficiary ledger"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-18-007$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 18, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$Brokerage transaction records must generally be retained for:$lp$, $lp$["One year", "Three years", "Seven years", "Indefinitely"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["One year is insufficient under the Real Estate Law.", "Correct. Brokers must retain transaction records for three years, running from closing or from the listing date where no closing occurred.", "Seven years is a common tax retention period, not the brokerage requirement.", "Indefinite retention is prudent for some records but is not the regulatory standard."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["record retention"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-18-008$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 18, $lp$transfer$lp$, 2, $lp$On a settlement statement, the purchase price appears as a:$lp$, $lp$["Debit to the buyer and a credit to the seller", "Credit to the buyer and a debit to the seller", "Debit to both parties", "Credit to both parties"]$lp$::jsonb, 0, $lp$["Correct. The buyer owes the price (debit) and the seller receives it (credit).", "This reverses the entries.", "The price cannot be an obligation of both parties.", "The price cannot be a benefit to both parties."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["debit / credit", "settlement statement"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-18-009$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 18, $lp$transfer$lp$, 3, $lp$At closing on a rental property, tenant security deposits held by the seller are:$lp$, $lp$["Prorated as of the closing date", "Transferred in full as a debit to the seller and credit to the buyer", "Retained by the seller", "Paid directly to the tenants at closing"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Security deposits are never prorated; they are the tenants' money in full.", "Correct. Deposits transfer in their entirety, debited to the seller and credited to the buyer, who assumes the obligation to account to the tenants.", "The seller cannot keep funds belonging to tenants.", "Deposits are not refunded at sale; the obligation passes to the new owner."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["security deposit transfer", "proration"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-18-010$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 18, $lp$transfer$lp$, 3, $lp$A sale closes June 20 with the buyer owning the day of closing. The seller prepaid $9,180 in annual taxes for the fiscal year beginning July 1. Using a 360-day year, the proration is:$lp$, $lp$["$280.50 credit to the seller", "$8,899.50 credit to the seller", "$280.50 credit to the buyer", "$4,590.00 credit to the buyer"]$lp$::jsonb, 0, $lp$["Correct. Daily = 9,180 ÷ 360 = 25.50. Seller owned July 1 through June 19 = 349 days = $8,899.50 consumed; the prepaid remainder of $280.50 benefits the buyer and is credited to the seller.", "$8,899.50 is the seller's own consumed share, not the amount reimbursed.", "The direction is reversed — a prepaid expense produces a seller credit.", "$4,590 is half the annual bill and corresponds to no step here."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["proration", "debit / credit"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-18-011$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 18, $lp$transfer$lp$, 2, $lp$Unpaid property taxes that accrued during the seller's ownership appear at closing as a:$lp$, $lp$["Credit to the seller and debit to the buyer", "Debit to the seller and credit to the buyer", "Debit to both parties", "Item excluded from proration"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Prepaid taxes produce a seller credit; these taxes are unpaid.", "Correct. An accrued but unpaid expense is charged to the seller and credited to the buyer, who will pay the bill when it comes due.", "Only one party bears the accrued obligation.", "Taxes are the most commonly prorated closing item."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["proration", "debit / credit"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-18-012$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 18, $lp$transfer$lp$, 2, $lp$In a California transaction, title passes to the buyer upon:$lp$, $lp$["Signing the purchase agreement", "Signing the loan documents", "Recording", "Delivery of the keys"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["The purchase agreement creates contract rights, not title.", "Loan signing is a step toward funding, not the transfer of title.", "Correct. Close of escrow occurs at recording, which is when title passes and after which escrow disburses.", "Possession may be delivered before or after closing and does not determine title."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["debit / credit"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-18-013$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 18, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 3, $lp$A trustee of a family trust sells a home she personally occupied for six years and knows the roof leaks. The sale is TDS-exempt as a fiduciary transfer. She must:$lp$, $lp$["Say nothing, since the exemption applies", "Disclose the known leak, because the form exemption does not excuse concealing known material facts", "Disclose only if the buyer requests a TDS", "Disclose only to the buyer's agent"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["A form exemption is never a knowledge exemption.", "Correct. A transferor with actual knowledge of a material defect must disclose it regardless of any exemption from the statutory form.", "The duty is affirmative and does not depend on a buyer's request.", "Disclosure must reach the buyer, not merely the buyer's agent informally."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["record retention"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-18-014$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 18, $lp$contracts$lp$, 3, $lp$A seller wants to negotiate with two buyers at once without risk of forming two binding contracts. She should use:$lp$, $lp$["Two ordinary counteroffers", "A multiple counteroffer form requiring her final acceptance", "A backup offer addendum with each", "An option agreement with each"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Sending ordinary counteroffers to two buyers risks binding her twice if both accept.", "Correct. A multiple counteroffer provides that no contract forms until the seller signs a final acceptance of one buyer's response, preserving her choice.", "Backup offers address what happens after a primary contract fails.", "Options require consideration and would bind her to sell."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["settlement statement"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-18-015$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 18, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$A salesperson closes a transaction. Escrow should disburse the commission to:$lp$, $lp$["The salesperson directly", "The salesperson's employing broker", "The salesperson and broker in their agreed shares", "Whichever party the salesperson designates"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["A salesperson may not be paid directly for licensed activity by escrow.", "Correct. Escrow pays the brokerages; the broker then compensates the salesperson under their written agreement — a salesperson may accept compensation only from their employing broker.", "Escrow does not split commissions inside a brokerage.", "The salesperson has no authority to redirect commission payments."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["responsible broker"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-18-016$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 18, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$Which is the BEST practice for a broker holding a small amount of personal funds in the trust account?$lp$, $lp$["Keep several months of operating capital there for convenience", "Keep only a limited amount to cover bank service charges, as regulations permit", "Keep earned commissions there indefinitely", "Keep personal funds equal to the largest client balance"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Holding operating capital in the trust account is commingling.", "Correct. Regulations permit only a limited amount of the broker's own funds to cover bank service charges; that narrow allowance is the sole intentional mixing permitted.", "Leaving earned commissions in the trust account beyond a limited period is commingling.", "No provision ties permitted personal funds to client balances."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["commingling", "trust funds"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-18-017$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 18, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$During a DRE audit, the MOST common trust fund findings involve:$lp$, $lp$["Deliberate theft of client funds", "Missing monthly reconciliations, absent per-beneficiary ledgers, and late deposits", "Failure to carry E&O insurance", "Improper advertising language"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Outright theft is rare compared with recordkeeping failures.", "Correct. Audit findings are typically mundane: reconciliations not performed, ledgers not maintained per beneficiary, deposits made late, and personal funds left in the account.", "E&O coverage is a business and brokerage matter, not a trust fund audit finding.", "Advertising issues arise under separate rules, not trust fund audits."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["monthly reconciliation", "beneficiary ledger"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-18-018$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 18, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$For a newly licensed salesperson choosing a first brokerage, the factor that generally matters MOST is:$lp$, $lp$["The highest available commission split", "Training, mentorship, and the quality of broker supervision", "The size of the national brand", "The lowest desk fee"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["A high split with no support is worth less than a lower split with real guidance, especially before the first closings.", "Correct. For a new licensee, training and accessible supervision outweigh split, because the responsible broker's oversight both develops competence and protects against liability.", "Brand recognition does not substitute for supervision and training.", "Low fees do not help an agent who has no transactions and no one to consult."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["responsible broker", "E&O insurance"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-18-019$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 18, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 3, $lp$A capstone transaction closes at $845,000 with no assumed loan. At $0.55 per $500, the county documentary transfer tax is:$lp$, $lp$["$464.75", "$929.50", "$1,859.00", "$4,225.00"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["$464.75 applies $0.55 per $1,000 rather than per $500.", "Correct. 845,000 ÷ 500 = 1,690 increments; 1,690 × $0.55 = $929.50. With no assumed loan, the full price is taxed.", "$1,859.00 doubles the correct figure, which would reflect an added city tax at the same rate.", "$4,225.00 is 0.5% of the price and corresponds to no standard rate."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["settlement statement"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRIN-18-020$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$principles$lp$, 18, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 3, $lp$A seller instructs her agent to prefer 'a nice family, not investors.' The agent should understand that:$lp$, $lp$["Both preferences are lawful because the seller owns the property", "Preferring owner-occupants over investors is lawful, but preferring a 'family' implicates familial status and cannot be honored", "Both preferences are unlawful", "Either preference is lawful if documented in the file"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Ownership creates no exemption from fair housing law for a protected characteristic.", "Correct. Investor status is not a protected characteristic, so an owner-occupant preference is lawful; familial status is protected, so the 'family' preference must be refused.", "The investor preference is lawful; only the familial status preference is not.", "Documenting an unlawful instruction does not authorize following it."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["responsible broker"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-01-001$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 1, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$A salesperson receives 1099 income from her brokerage. For purposes of the Real Estate Law's supervision requirements, she is treated as:$lp$, $lp$["An independent principal responsible only to herself", "The employee of her responsible broker", "A co-broker with shared liability", "An unsupervised contractor"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Tax classification does not relieve the broker of supervision duties or the salesperson of working under them.", "Correct. A salesperson may be an independent contractor for tax purposes while being treated as the broker's employee for supervision and accountability — both are true simultaneously.", "Salespersons do not hold co-broker status.", "All licensed activity by a salesperson is subject to broker supervision."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["independent contractor", "responsible broker"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-01-002$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 1, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$In a cap-based commission model, the 'cap' refers to:$lp$, $lp$["The maximum commission a client may be charged", "A cumulative amount paid to the brokerage in a year, after which the agent's split improves", "The largest transaction size the brokerage will accept", "A limit on the agent's annual earnings"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Commissions charged to clients are always negotiable and are not capped by brokerage models.", "Correct. Once the agent has paid the brokerage the cap amount within the anniversary year, the split typically improves, often to 100%.", "Caps concern compensation structure, not transaction size limits.", "A cap raises an agent's earnings after it is met; it does not limit them."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["cap", "commission split"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-01-003$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 1, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 3, $lp$An agent expects 6 transactions at $19,500 gross commission per side. Brokerage A offers a 60/40 split with a $395 per-transaction fee and no desk fee. The agent's annual net from Brokerage A is:$lp$, $lp$["$117,000", "$70,200", "$67,830", "$63,180"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["$117,000 is total gross commission for six sides before any split.", "$70,200 applies the 60% split but omits the per-transaction fees.", "Correct. $19,500 × 0.60 = $11,700 per deal; less $395 = $11,305; × 6 = $67,830.", "$63,180 applies a 54% split, which is not the stated arrangement."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["commission split"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-01-004$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 1, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 3, $lp$A fixed monthly desk fee is MOST damaging to an agent's economics when:$lp$, $lp$["Transaction volume is high", "Transaction volume is low", "The market is appreciating", "The agent works on a team"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["At high volume the fixed fee is spread across many closings and its per-transaction impact shrinks.", "Correct. A fixed fee is owed whether or not the agent closes anything, so it hurts most at low volume — exactly when a new agent can least absorb it.", "Market direction does not change the fixed nature of the fee.", "Team membership does not alter how a fixed fee interacts with volume."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["desk fee", "cap"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-01-005$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 1, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$A California salesperson team wishes to advertise under a team name. The name must generally:$lp$, $lp$["Be registered as a fictitious business name by the salesperson", "Include a team member's surname plus a term such as 'team,' 'group,' or 'associates'", "Be approved by the local association of Realtors", "Include the word 'Realty' to signal professionalism"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Salespersons do not obtain their own fictitious business name licensing; that is a broker function.", "Correct. A team name must generally include a team member's surname together with a term like team, group, or associates, must not imply an independent brokerage, and advertising must identify the responsible broker.", "Association approval is not the legal requirement.", "Terms suggesting an independent brokerage — like 'Realty' — are precisely what the rule guards against."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["team"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-01-006$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 1, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$A team sign displays only the team name and a phone number, with no mention of the brokerage. This creates a risk of:$lp$, $lp$["A blind ad, because it conceals that the advertiser is a licensee acting through a broker", "A RESPA violation", "A trust fund violation", "An antitrust violation"]$lp$::jsonb, 0, $lp$["Correct. Advertising that hides the licensee's status and the responsible broker is a blind ad and is prohibited; team advertising must identify the broker.", "RESPA governs settlement service referrals and kickbacks, not signage.", "Trust fund rules govern client money, not advertising.", "Antitrust concerns price-fixing and market allocation, not sign content."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["team", "responsible broker"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-01-007$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 1, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 3, $lp$An agent needs to earn $60,000. Median price is $540,000, she earns a 2.5% side, and her split is 70/30 in her favor. She needs approximately:$lp$, $lp$["5 transactions", "7 transactions", "9 transactions", "4 transactions"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["5 transactions would yield about $47,250, short of the goal.", "Correct. $540,000 × 0.025 = $13,500 gross per side; × 0.70 = $9,450 to the agent; $60,000 ÷ $9,450 = 6.35, so 7 transactions.", "9 transactions would substantially exceed the target.", "4 transactions would yield about $37,800."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["gross commission income (GCI)"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-01-008$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 1, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$Because a salesperson receives 1099 income with no withholding, the most important financial habit in year one is to:$lp$, $lp$["Maximize deductions by delaying expense tracking until tax season", "Set aside a portion of every commission for taxes and pay quarterly estimates", "Wait until the first tax return to determine liability", "Treat all commission income as spendable"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Delaying expense tracking loses deductions and makes reconstruction difficult.", "Correct. Nothing is withheld from commission checks, so the agent must reserve roughly 25–35% for income and self-employment tax and make quarterly estimated payments.", "Waiting until filing produces a liability the agent has not reserved for.", "Treating gross commission as spendable income is the classic first-year mistake."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["independent contractor"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-01-009$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 1, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$Errors and omissions insurance generally does NOT cover:$lp$, $lp$["An alleged failure to disclose a known material fact", "A clerical error in preparing a contract", "Commingling or misappropriation of trust funds", "A negligence claim arising from a showing"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["Disclosure claims are a core reason E&O exists.", "Errors in document preparation are within typical coverage.", "Correct. E&O excludes intentional wrongdoing, fraud, and trust fund misappropriation — there is no insurance product for taking client money.", "Negligence claims arising from professional services are typically covered."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["E&O insurance"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-01-010$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 1, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$For a brand-new licensee, the factor most predictive of remaining in the business past year two is:$lp$, $lp$["Securing the highest available commission split", "Having six to twelve months of living expenses in reserve", "Joining the largest national brand", "Buying online portal leads immediately"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["A high split on zero closings is worth nothing.", "Correct. Commissions arrive months after work begins, and running out of money before the pipeline matures is the most common reason new agents leave.", "Brand size does not address the cash flow gap.", "Purchased leads add cost during the period when cash is scarcest."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["gross commission income (GCI)"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-01-011$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 1, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$When interviewing brokerages, the question most valuable to a new licensee is:$lp$, $lp$["What is your commission split?", "Who reviews my first purchase contract, and who do I call on a Sunday?", "How many agents work here?", "What is your market share?"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Split matters, but it is secondary to competence and liability protection in year one.", "Correct. Supervision quality is both the fastest route to competence and a liability shield, and it is the question new agents most often fail to ask.", "Headcount says little about the support an individual agent receives.", "Brokerage market share does not translate into support for a new licensee."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["responsible broker"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-01-012$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 1, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$A franchise fee in a national brand brokerage is typically taken:$lp$, $lp$["From the agent's share after the broker split", "Off the top of the commission before the broker/agent split", "Only from the broker's share", "Annually as a flat membership charge"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Taking it after the split would understate its effect on the agent.", "Correct. A franchise fee is commonly deducted off the top before the split, which is why agents must model it explicitly when comparing offers.", "The fee reduces the pool both parties share, not only the broker's portion.", "Franchise fees are typically transaction-based rather than a flat annual charge."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["franchise fee", "commission split"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-01-013$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 1, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 3, $lp$A new agent joins a team that supplies leads and takes 50% of the agent's side on team-generated business. The primary trade-off being accepted is:$lp$, $lp$["Higher compensation for less work", "Lower per-transaction income in exchange for lead flow, systems, and supervision", "Freedom from broker supervision", "Exemption from team advertising rules"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Team splits reduce per-transaction income; the benefit lies elsewhere.", "Correct. The agent trades a smaller share for leads before they can generate their own, established systems, and experienced review of their work — often the fastest route to competence.", "Team members remain fully subject to the responsible broker's supervision.", "Team advertising rules apply regardless of the compensation arrangement."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["team", "commission split"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-01-014$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 1, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$Which expense is most commonly omitted from a new agent's first-year budget?$lp$, $lp$["MLS and association dues", "Six to twelve months of personal living expenses", "Business cards", "A lockbox key"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Dues are a known, recurring line most agents anticipate.", "Correct. New agents routinely budget business costs and omit the personal runway needed to survive the four to six month gap before the first commission arrives.", "Marketing materials are typically anticipated.", "Lockbox access is a small, expected cost."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["gross commission income (GCI)"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-01-015$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 1, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$A new agent is unsure how to complete an unfamiliar contract provision. The correct action is to:$lp$, $lp$["Draft language that captures the parties' intent", "Ask the responsible broker before completing it", "Copy language from a contract in another transaction", "Leave it blank and address it at closing"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Drafting custom legal language is the unauthorized practice of law.", "Correct. Broker supervision exists for exactly this, and asking is both the competent and the protected course.", "Borrowing language from an unrelated transaction risks importing terms that do not fit and still amounts to drafting.", "Leaving material terms blank creates ambiguity and potential unenforceability."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["responsible broker"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-01-016$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 1, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 3, $lp$An agent closes 2 transactions in year one at $19,500 per side. Brokerage A: 60/40 split, $395 per transaction, no desk fee. Brokerage B: 80/20 split, $295 per transaction, $800 monthly desk fee. Which nets more, and why?$lp$, $lp$["Brokerage A, because $9,600 in annual desk fees outweighs B's better split at this volume", "Brokerage B, because a higher split always wins", "The two are identical at every volume", "Brokerage B, because per-transaction fees matter more than desk fees"]$lp$::jsonb, 0, $lp$["Correct. A: ($19,500 × 0.60 − $395) × 2 = $22,610. B: ($19,500 × 0.80 − $295) × 2 − $9,600 = $30,610 − $9,600 = $21,010. A nets $1,600 more, because fixed fees are owed regardless of production and dominate at low volume.", "A higher split only wins once volume is sufficient to absorb the fixed costs; at two transactions it does not.", "The structures diverge at every volume; which one leads depends on transaction count.", "Here the $9,600 in desk fees dwarfs the $200 per-transaction difference — the fixed cost is what decides it."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["desk fee", "commission split"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-01-017$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 1, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$Working backward from an income goal, the most useful final output of a business plan is:$lp$, $lp$["A target gross commission income figure", "A daily or weekly activity count the agent can schedule", "A list of desired brokerages", "A projected market appreciation rate"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["An income target is the starting input, not something the agent can act on directly.", "Correct. Converting the income goal into transactions, then into conversations, produces a schedulable daily activity — and consistent activity, not knowledge, is what new agents most often lack.", "Brokerage selection is a separate decision.", "Appreciation is outside the agent's control and does not drive daily behavior."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["conversion ratio", "gross commission income (GCI)"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-01-018$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 1, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$The habit that most reduces an agent's long-term liability exposure is:$lp$, $lp$["Carrying higher E&O limits", "Confirming every material conversation in writing the same day", "Working only with referred clients", "Avoiding older properties"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Higher limits pay claims; they do not prevent them.", "Correct. A written record created contemporaneously is the single most effective protection, because most disputes turn on what was said and when.", "Referred clients sue too, and limiting the client base is not a risk strategy.", "Property age does not determine liability; disclosure practice does."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["E&O insurance"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-02-001$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 2, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$Ranked by conversion rate rather than volume, the highest-performing source of business for most agents is:$lp$, $lp$["Purchased online portal leads", "Repeat clients and direct referrals", "Cold calling expired listings", "Paid social media advertising"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Portal leads deliver volume at low conversion and immediate cost.", "Correct. Repeat clients and referrals convert at the highest rate and cost the least, though they lag — which is why new agents must build the sphere early.", "Cold outreach has the lowest conversion and highest rejection of the common sources.", "Paid social generates volume but converts well below relationship-based sources."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["sphere of influence (SOI)"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-02-002$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 2, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 3, $lp$A farm area contains 420 homes with 27 sales in the past twelve months. Its turnover rate is approximately:$lp$, $lp$["3.2%", "6.4%", "15.6%", "0.64%"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["3.2% would correspond to about 13 sales in this area.", "Correct. Turnover = 27 ÷ 420 = 0.064, or about 6.4% — comfortably above the roughly 4–5% threshold below which farming becomes slow going.", "15.6% inverts the calculation.", "0.64% misplaces a decimal."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["turnover rate", "geographic farm"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-02-003$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 2, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 3, $lp$Two farms are under consideration. Farm A: 6.4% turnover, dominant agent holds 12%. Farm B: 3.2% turnover, dominant agent holds 38%. The better choice is:$lp$, $lp$["Farm B, because less turnover means less competition", "Farm A, because it has higher turnover and a fragmented competitive field", "Farm B, because an established agent proves the area is valuable", "Either; farm selection is a matter of personal preference"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Low turnover means fewer transactions available, not less competition.", "Correct. Farm A offers double the sales opportunity against a fragmented field, while Farm B pairs half the opportunity with an entrenched 38% competitor.", "A dominant competitor makes market entry harder, not more attractive.", "Farm selection is arithmetic before it is preference — many agents farm where they live rather than where the numbers work."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["turnover rate", "geographic farm"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-02-004$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 2, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$Before cold calling homeowners, a licensee must:$lp$, $lp$["Obtain written consent from each household", "Scrub the numbers against the National Do Not Call Registry and maintain an internal do-not-call list", "Notify the DRE of the campaign", "Register the campaign with the local association"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Written consent is not required for all calls, but DNC compliance is.", "Correct. Numbers must be scrubbed against the DNC registry and the caller must maintain an internal do-not-call list, with limited exceptions for established business relationships and inquiries.", "The DRE does not register telemarketing campaigns.", "Association registration is not a legal requirement for calling."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["Do Not Call Registry"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-02-005$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 2, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 3, $lp$An agent purchases 5,000 phone numbers and sends a mass marketing text to all of them. The principal legal exposure is:$lp$, $lp$["A DRE advertising violation only", "Statutory damages assessed per message under the rules governing autodialed texts to cell phones", "A RESPA violation", "No exposure, since texts are not calls"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Advertising rules are implicated, but the ruinous exposure is elsewhere.", "Correct. Texts are treated as calls, mass messaging to cell numbers without prior express consent implicates the autodialer rules, and damages are assessed per message — which is how a 5,000-message campaign becomes a catastrophic claim.", "RESPA governs settlement service referrals, not text marketing.", "Text messages are treated as calls for these purposes."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["Do Not Call Registry"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-02-006$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 2, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$Under CAN-SPAM, a commercial marketing email must include:$lp$, $lp$["The recipient's prior written consent", "A valid physical postal address and a working opt-out mechanism", "The broker's license number in the subject line", "A read receipt request"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["CAN-SPAM operates on an opt-out rather than opt-in model.", "Correct. Commercial email requires honest headers and subject lines, a valid physical postal address, and a functioning opt-out that is honored promptly.", "License numbers belong in advertising per DRE rules but are not a CAN-SPAM subject-line requirement.", "Read receipts have no bearing on compliance."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["CAN-SPAM"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-02-007$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 2, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$Contacting for-sale-by-owner sellers is:$lp$, $lp$["Prohibited, because they have not requested representation", "Lawful, subject to Do Not Call and other solicitation rules", "Lawful only after the property has been listed and expired", "Prohibited unless the seller initiates contact"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["FSBOs may lawfully be contacted; no prohibition arises from their unrepresented status.", "Correct. FSBO outreach is lawful but remains subject to DNC scrubbing, calling-hour restrictions, and the other solicitation rules.", "No such sequencing requirement exists.", "Agents may initiate contact within the applicable solicitation rules."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["FSBO", "Do Not Call Registry"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-02-008$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 2, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 3, $lp$An unlicensed friend refers a buyer who closes, and asks the agent for $300 as thanks. The agent may:$lp$, $lp$["Pay it, because the amount is nominal", "Not pay it, because compensating an unlicensed person for referring real estate business is prohibited", "Pay it through the brokerage to make it lawful", "Pay it if the buyer consents in writing"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["The prohibition does not turn on the size of the payment.", "Correct. Paying an unlicensed person for a real estate referral is prohibited, and the violation belongs to the payer — routing it through a broker does not cure it.", "The brokerage cannot lawfully make a payment the licensee could not make.", "Client consent is irrelevant to the licensing prohibition."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["referral fee"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-02-009$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 2, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$A broker may lawfully pay a referral fee to:$lp$, $lp$["An unlicensed past client", "Another licensed broker, including one licensed in another state", "A lender who sends buyer referrals", "Any person who introduces a client"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Unlicensed persons may not be compensated for real estate referrals.", "Correct. Broker-to-broker referral fees are permitted, including across state lines.", "Paying a lender for referrals — or accepting payment from one — implicates RESPA's prohibition on referral compensation for settlement services.", "Compensation depends on licensure, not on who made the introduction."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["referral fee"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-02-010$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 2, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 3, $lp$Portal leads: $9,600 spent, 3 closings averaging $8,900 commission. Farm mail: $4,200 spent, 2 closings at the same average. Comparing return on investment:$lp$, $lp$["Portal leads, at about 178% versus the farm's 324%", "Farm mail, at about 324% versus the portal's 178%", "They are equivalent", "Portal leads, because they produced more closings"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["This reverses which source produced the higher return.", "Correct. Portal: (3 × 8,900 − 9,600) ÷ 9,600 = 178%. Farm: (2 × 8,900 − 4,200) ÷ 4,200 = 324%. The farm generated far fewer leads at a much higher cost per lead, yet returned nearly double.", "The returns differ substantially.", "Closing count alone does not measure return; the farm produced fewer closings at far lower cost."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["cost per closing", "cost per lead (CPL)"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-02-011$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 2, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$Judging lead sources by cost per lead alone is misleading because:$lp$, $lp$["Cost per lead is impossible to calculate accurately", "Sources with high cost per lead often convert far better, producing a lower cost per closing", "Lead volume is the only meaningful metric", "Marketing spend is not deductible"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Cost per lead is straightforward to compute; the issue is what it omits.", "Correct. A source can look expensive per lead and still be the best investment, because conversion determines cost per closing and return — which is why relationship-based sources outperform cheap volume.", "Volume without conversion produces activity, not income.", "Marketing expenses are generally deductible; that is unrelated to source evaluation."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["cost per lead (CPL)", "cost per closing"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-02-012$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 2, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 3, $lp$An agent configures a digital housing ad to exclude certain ZIP codes with predominantly minority populations. This is:$lp$, $lp$["Lawful, because geographic targeting is a business decision", "Potentially unlawful discrimination, because geographic exclusion can serve as a proxy for protected characteristics", "Lawful if the agent does not live in those areas", "Lawful if the excluded areas are outside the agent's farm"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Geographic choices are not automatically neutral when they track protected characteristics.", "Correct. Targeting or excluding audiences by proxies for protected characteristics can produce unlawful discrimination, and enforcement actions over discriminatory housing ad targeting are why platforms now restrict housing ads.", "Where the agent lives has no bearing on the legality of the targeting.", "Farm boundaries do not immunize exclusions that correlate with protected characteristics."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["geographic farm"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-02-013$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 2, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$The professionally sound approach to an expired listing is to:$lp$, $lp$["Explain why the previous agent was incompetent", "Diagnose the likely cause — usually price, condition, or exposure — without disparaging the prior licensee", "Offer to list below market to guarantee a sale", "Contact the seller repeatedly until they respond"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Disparaging another licensee is unprofessional and conflicts with ethical obligations toward other agents.", "Correct. Expired sellers have just had a disappointing experience and will hear from many agents at once; honest diagnosis differentiates without criticizing a colleague.", "Deliberately underpricing to win a listing does not serve the client's interest.", "Repeated unwanted contact raises solicitation compliance problems and damages credibility."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["expired listing"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-02-014$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 2, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$A 400-home farm turns over at 5% annually. Capturing 15% of that market would produce approximately:$lp$, $lp$["20 transactions per year", "3 transactions per year", "6 transactions per year", "60 transactions per year"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["20 is the total number of sales in the farm, not the agent's share.", "Correct. 400 × 0.05 = 20 sales annually; 20 × 0.15 = 3 transactions — meaningful, and realistic only after sustained presence.", "6 would represent a 30% capture rate.", "60 exceeds the total sales occurring in the farm."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["geographic farm", "turnover rate"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-02-015$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 2, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$The most important discipline in working a sphere of influence is:$lp$, $lp$["Sending highly creative campaigns", "Consistent, useful contact sustained over years", "Contacting everyone weekly", "Limiting outreach to people likely to move soon"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Creativity without consistency produces short bursts and no compounding.", "Correct. An agent who contacts 200 people four times a year, every year, will outperform one who runs brilliant campaigns for three months and stops.", "Weekly contact is excessive and produces unsubscribes rather than relationships.", "Predicting who will move is unreliable; broad consistent contact captures referrals as well as direct business."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["sphere of influence (SOI)"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-02-016$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 2, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 3, $lp$A lender offers an agent $500 for each buyer referral that closes a loan. Accepting this would violate:$lp$, $lp$["Only the brokerage's internal policy", "RESPA's prohibition on compensation for referrals of settlement services", "The statute of frauds", "Nothing, if disclosed to the buyer"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["The violation is statutory, not merely a policy matter.", "Correct. RESPA prohibits giving or accepting anything of value for referring settlement service business; only payment for services actually performed at fair market value is permitted, and labeling it a marketing fee changes nothing.", "The statute of frauds concerns which contracts must be written.", "Disclosure does not cure a prohibited referral fee."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["referral fee"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-02-017$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 2, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$Geographic farming typically produces meaningful return after:$lp$, $lp$["The first mailing", "Roughly 6 to 18 months of consistent presence", "One week of door knocking", "It never produces measurable return"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["A single mailing produces recognition at best, not transactions.", "Correct. Farming is a multi-year investment; expect 6 to 18 months before meaningful return, which is why abandoning a farm early wastes the entire prior spend.", "Sustained presence over months is what builds the recognition that generates listings.", "Farming produces durable return once established, as the ROI comparison in this unit shows."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["geographic farm"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-02-018$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 2, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$A new licensee's most cost-effective first prospecting action is to:$lp$, $lp$["Purchase a portal lead package", "Contact everyone already in their personal network and tell them they are licensed", "Begin cold calling expired listings", "Launch a paid social advertising campaign"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Purchased leads cost money during the period when cash is scarcest and convert poorly.", "Correct. The sphere costs nothing, converts best, and telling forty people you know will outperform a month of purchased leads.", "Cold outreach has the lowest conversion and is heavily regulated.", "Paid advertising spends scarce capital on the lowest-converting channel."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["sphere of influence (SOI)"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-03-001$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 3, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$A seller says at the listing appointment, 'I'd rather not sell to a young family — the street is quiet.' The agent should:$lp$, $lp$["Note the preference and screen offers accordingly", "Decline the instruction, explain that familial status is protected, and state what the agent will do instead", "Agree verbally but present all offers anyway", "Ask the seller to put the instruction in writing"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Screening offers by familial status is unlawful discrimination.", "Correct. The agent must decline clearly, explain the legal obligation without lecturing, and redirect the seller to lawful decision criteria such as price, financing, and timeline.", "Agreeing verbally while doing otherwise leaves the client believing the instruction was accepted and does not correct the underlying problem.", "Documenting an unlawful instruction does not make following it lawful and creates written evidence of the intent."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["differential service", "steering"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-03-002$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 3, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 3, $lp$Paired testers with identical finances contact the same agent. One is shown 11 homes across six neighborhoods; the other is shown 4 homes in two neighborhoods and asked twice about affordability. The agent sincerely believes she treated both professionally. This is:$lp$, $lp$["Not a violation, because there was no discriminatory intent", "Unlawful differential service, because fair housing enforcement examines conduct and outcomes rather than motive", "Lawful, because agents may exercise judgment about client readiness", "A violation only if a tester files a complaint"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Intent is not an element; sincere belief in one's own fairness is common among agents whose conduct violates the law.", "Correct. Differential service in showings, information provided, and qualification assumptions is actionable regardless of intent — which is precisely why implicit bias matters legally.", "Judgment about readiness applied unequally by protected characteristic is the violation itself.", "Liability does not depend on a complaint being filed, and testers have standing to sue."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["differential service", "paired testing", "implicit bias"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-03-003$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 3, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$A buyer asks, 'What kind of people live in this neighborhood?' The best response is to:$lp$, $lp$["Describe the demographics accurately", "Decline to characterize residents and direct the buyer to objective published data", "Say the neighborhood is 'very diverse'", "Say you are not allowed to answer and change the subject"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Describing residents by protected characteristics is steering, however accurate.", "Correct. Refuse to characterize residents, then redirect to census data, school reports, and crime statistics the buyer can evaluate independently — compliant and more genuinely useful.", "Characterizing an area as 'diverse' still describes the people rather than the property and can itself signal a preference.", "A bare refusal without a useful alternative serves the client poorly; the redirect is what makes the response work."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["steering"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-03-004$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 3, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$A property management client instructs the agent to 'screen out the voucher applicants.' In California this instruction is:$lp$, $lp$["Lawful, because vouchers involve additional paperwork", "Unlawful, because source of income is a protected characteristic", "Lawful if applied to every applicant equally", "Lawful for buildings with fewer than five units"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Administrative inconvenience is not a lawful basis for refusing a protected source of income.", "Correct. Source of income is protected under California law; landlords may apply uniform financial criteria but must count the voucher as income.", "Applying an unlawful criterion uniformly does not make it lawful.", "No small-building exemption authorizes source of income discrimination in California."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["source of income"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-03-005$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 3, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$Implicit bias is best understood as:$lp$, $lp$["Deliberate discrimination that the actor conceals", "Unconscious association that shapes judgment without conscious intent", "A defense available when no discriminatory motive existed", "A concern only for agents who lack fair housing training"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Concealed deliberate discrimination is intentional conduct, not implicit bias.", "Correct. Implicit bias operates unconsciously and affects everyone, including people sincerely committed to fairness — which is why process rather than resolve is the countermeasure.", "Absence of conscious motive is not a defense; enforcement examines conduct and outcomes.", "Training alone does not eliminate implicit bias; standardized process does more."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["implicit bias"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-03-006$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 3, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 3, $lp$The most effective practical countermeasure to implicit bias in an agent's practice is:$lp$, $lp$["Committing personally to treat everyone fairly", "Standardizing intake, response times, and follow-up so every client receives an identical process", "Avoiding clients from unfamiliar backgrounds", "Relying on the brokerage's annual training"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Good intentions do not reliably change behavior under time pressure, which is when bias operates fastest.", "Correct. Uniform process removes the discretionary moments where bias enters and produces data the agent can audit — consistency defeats bias more reliably than resolve.", "Avoiding certain clients is itself discriminatory and worsens the problem.", "Annual training raises awareness but does not change the daily decisions where bias operates."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["practice standard", "implicit bias"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-03-007$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 3, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 3, $lp$An agent's records show she responds to some inquiries within an hour and to others in three hours, and the pattern correlates with the apparent national origin of the inquirer. She intended no discrimination. This pattern:$lp$, $lp$["Is legally irrelevant absent intent", "Can constitute unlawful differential service and is exactly what paired testing is designed to reveal", "Is acceptable because response time is not a housing service", "Matters only if a client complains"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Intent is not required for a differential service claim.", "Correct. Unequal service quality correlated with a protected characteristic is actionable, and response-time and showing-count disparities are precisely what testing surfaces — which is why self-auditing is better than being audited.", "Response time is part of the service provided and counts.", "Liability does not depend on a complaint, and the agent who finds the pattern first can fix it."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["differential service", "paired testing"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-03-008$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 3, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$A tenant with a disability requests permission to install grab bars at her own expense. The landlord must:$lp$, $lp$["Deny it under a no-alterations policy", "Permit it as a reasonable modification, generally at the tenant's expense", "Permit it and pay for the installation", "Require a physician's letter describing the diagnosis"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["A no-alterations policy does not override the right to a reasonable modification.", "Correct. A physical change to the premises is a reasonable modification, generally at the tenant's cost, with restoration required only where reasonable — a landlord typically cannot require removal of grab bars.", "The provider bears the cost of accommodations (policy changes), not modifications (physical changes).", "Diagnosis and medical records may not be demanded; only documentation of disability-related need when the disability is not obvious."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["reasonable modification", "reasonable accommodation"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-03-009$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 3, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$An applicant discloses an emotional support animal that exceeds the building's weight limit. The housing provider may:$lp$, $lp$["Apply the weight limit and deny the animal", "Charge pet rent and a pet deposit", "Request documentation of the disability-related need if the disability is not obvious", "Require proof of registration from an online service"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["Breed and weight restrictions generally do not apply to assistance animals.", "An assistance animal is not a pet, so pet rent and pet deposits do not apply.", "Correct. Where the disability is not obvious, the provider may request documentation of the disability-related need — not diagnosis, records, or severity.", "Online 'registrations' have no legal force and may not be required."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["assistance animal", "reasonable accommodation"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-03-010$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 3, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 3, $lp$An agent shows only single-story homes to a buyer who uses a mobility device, without the buyer having asked for that limitation. This is:$lp$, $lp$["Considerate service tailored to the client", "Steering, because the agent narrowed options based on a protected characteristic rather than stated criteria", "Required under accessibility law", "Acceptable if the agent explains the reasoning"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Well-intended tailoring based on an assumption about disability is the classic steering pattern.", "Correct. The agent inferred criteria from a protected characteristic instead of asking; letting the client define their own criteria is the rule.", "No law requires narrowing a buyer's options by disability.", "Explaining the reasoning does not cure having narrowed the options without being asked."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["steering"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-03-011$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 3, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$Which listing phrase creates a fair housing problem AND a separate factual-representation risk?$lp$, $lp$["Updated kitchen with quartz counters", "Quiet, safe family neighborhood", "Detached two-car garage", "Corner lot with mature landscaping"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["This describes a property feature objectively.", "Correct. 'Family' implicates familial status, and 'safe' is an unverifiable factual claim about crime that creates misrepresentation exposure independent of fair housing.", "This describes a physical feature.", "This describes the lot objectively."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["differential service"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-03-012$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 3, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$An agent may lawfully disclose to a seller that the buyer:$lp$, $lp$["Uses a wheelchair", "Is offering all cash with a 10-day close", "Recently immigrated to the United States", "Has three young children"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Disability is protected and is never relevant to the seller's decision; volunteering it invites a claim.", "Correct. Offer terms — price, financing, timeline — are the lawful basis for a seller's decision and are properly communicated.", "Immigration status and national origin are protected characteristics.", "Familial status is protected and must not be communicated as a decision factor."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["differential service"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-03-013$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 3, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$A colleague mentions casually that he sent a family 'to the east side, where they'll be more comfortable.' This describes:$lp$, $lp$["Good client service", "Steering", "Blockbusting", "Redlining"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Directing clients to areas based on assumed comfort tied to a protected characteristic is not service.", "Correct. Channeling prospects toward particular neighborhoods based on a protected characteristic is steering, and it usually arrives phrased as helpfulness.", "Blockbusting involves inducing owners to sell by exploiting fear of neighborhood change.", "Redlining is area-based denial of loans, insurance, or services."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["steering"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-03-014$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 3, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 3, $lp$An agent replies to an inquiry four hours later and demands a preapproval before scheduling showings, though her standard practice is to reply within an hour and offer a consultation first. The inquiry had a name she could not pronounce and minor grammatical errors. The violation here is difficult to detect because:$lp$, $lp$["No protected characteristic was involved", "Nothing was explicitly said or refused, and the agent never revisits the interaction", "The buyer failed to follow up", "Response time is not a service"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["National origin and primary language are protected, and both are implicated.", "Correct. The differential treatment is invisible in any single interaction, the buyer simply disappears, and the agent never thinks about it again — which is why counting one's own behavior matters more than introspection.", "The buyer's non-response is a consequence of the treatment, not an excuse for it.", "Response time and the service offered are both part of the service provided."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["implicit bias", "differential service"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-03-015$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 3, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$The purpose of writing a personal practice standard is to:$lp$, $lp$["Satisfy a brokerage paperwork requirement", "Predetermine uniform responses and processes before the pressure of a live situation", "Create a marketing document for clients", "Substitute for fair housing continuing education"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["It is a professional tool, not a compliance formality.", "Correct. Fair housing failures happen in the seconds after an unexpected statement; deciding your response and process in advance is what makes the right action automatic.", "It is an internal operating standard, not marketing collateral.", "It complements required education rather than replacing it."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["practice standard"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-03-016$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 3, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$A seller states a preference for owner-occupant buyers over investors. The agent may:$lp$, $lp$["Not honor it, because all buyer preferences are discriminatory", "Honor it, because investor status is not a protected characteristic", "Honor it only if the buyer pool is diverse", "Honor it only with written DRE approval"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Only preferences based on protected characteristics are unlawful.", "Correct. Investor status is not protected, so an owner-occupant preference is lawful — unlike preferences based on familial status, national origin, or language.", "The composition of the buyer pool does not determine whether a non-protected preference is lawful.", "No DRE approval process exists or is needed for a lawful preference."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["differential service"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-03-017$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 3, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$Before a showing, the best way to address a client's possible accessibility needs is to:$lp$, $lp$["Assume needs based on observation and plan accordingly", "Ask whether there is anything about the property or showing you should plan for", "Avoid the topic entirely to prevent a discrimination claim", "Ask the client to describe their medical condition"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Assuming needs based on appearance leads directly to steering.", "Correct. An open question lets the client state what they need without the agent presuming a limitation or ignoring a real one.", "Avoiding the topic can leave a genuine access barrier unaddressed and serves the client poorly.", "Asking about a medical condition is inappropriate and unnecessary."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["reasonable accommodation", "steering"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-03-018$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 3, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 3, $lp$A seller insists on an unlawful screening instruction after the agent explains the law. The agent should:$lp$, $lp$["Continue the listing and quietly disregard the instruction", "Withdraw from the listing", "Follow the instruction since the seller was warned", "Ask another agent in the office to take the listing and follow it"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Quietly disregarding leaves the agent representing a client actively directing unlawful conduct and does not resolve the exposure.", "Correct. Obedience extends only to lawful instructions; when a client insists on unlawful screening, withdrawal is the appropriate course — no commission is worth the license.", "A warning does not transfer responsibility for unlawful conduct to the client alone.", "Passing the listing to a colleague transfers the violation rather than preventing it and implicates the brokerage."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["practice standard"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-03-019$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 3, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$Federal fair housing exemptions for certain owner-occupied small buildings do NOT apply when:$lp$, $lp$["The owner occupies one of the units", "A real estate licensee participates in the transaction", "The building predates 1968", "The units share a common entrance"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Owner occupancy is a condition of the exemption rather than something defeating it.", "Correct. The exemptions never apply when a licensee is involved and never permit discriminatory advertising — and California's broader statutes eliminate them in most practical situations.", "Construction date does not control the exemption.", "Shared entrances are not the determining factor."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["source of income"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-03-020$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 3, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 3, $lp$A single fair housing violation by a licensee can produce:$lp$, $lp$["Civil damages only", "Civil damages and attorney's fees, administrative enforcement, and DRE license discipline, each independently", "DRE discipline only", "A warning for a first offense"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Civil damages are one of several independent exposures.", "Correct. The three consequences stack and proceed independently — settling a civil case does not resolve an administrative complaint or prevent license discipline.", "License discipline is independent of, but not exclusive of, the other remedies.", "No first-offense warning system shields licensees from fair housing consequences."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["practice standard"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-04-001$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 4, $lp$agency$lp$, 2, $lp$At a listing appointment, the correct sequence is to deliver the agency disclosure:$lp$, $lp$["After the seller signs the listing agreement", "Before the seller signs the listing agreement", "At the same time as the first offer is presented", "Only if the seller asks about representation"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Delivering it afterward defeats its purpose and creates a technical violation.", "Correct. The agency disclosure must be delivered and the relationship elected in writing before the listing is signed, so the seller understands whom the agent represents before committing.", "Confirmation occurs with the purchase contract, but initial disclosure comes far earlier.", "The obligation is affirmative and does not depend on the client raising it."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["agency disclosure"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-04-002$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 4, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$The most useful document an agent can bring to a listing appointment, from the seller's perspective, is:$lp$, $lp$["A brokerage marketing brochure", "A written seller's net sheet", "A list of the agent's designations", "A printout of active listings only"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Brokerage materials speak to the agent's story rather than the seller's decision.", "Correct. Sellers decide in net proceeds, not gross price; a written estimate of proceeds after payoff, commissions, costs, and prorations addresses the question they actually have.", "Designations rarely influence a seller's decision and do not answer their questions.", "Actives show competition but omit the closed sales that constitute evidence of value."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["seller's net sheet"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-04-003$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 4, $lp$contracts$lp$, 2, $lp$An exclusive listing agreement that omits a definite termination date is:$lp$, $lp$["Enforceable until the property sells", "Grounds for disciplinary action against the licensee", "Automatically limited to six months", "Valid if the seller initials the omission"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Open-ended exclusive listings are what the rule prohibits.", "Correct. A definite termination date is required in an exclusive listing, and omitting it — or including an automatic extension — is grounds for discipline.", "No statutory default term fills the gap.", "The requirement cannot be waived by initialing."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["definite termination date"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-04-004$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 4, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 3, $lp$Before taking a listing, public records show title vested as 'Rosa M., a life estate; remainder to David M. and Ana M.' To deliver insurable fee title, the listing agreement should be signed by:$lp$, $lp$["Rosa alone, as the party in possession", "Rosa, David, and Ana", "David and Ana alone, as the ultimate owners", "Rosa, with the remaindermen notified at closing"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Rosa can convey only her life estate, which ends at her death.", "Correct. Combining the life estate and the remainder produces fee simple absolute, so all three must be parties — a threshold question to resolve at the appointment rather than in escrow.", "The remaindermen hold no present possessory interest and cannot convey the life estate.", "Notification at closing is far too late; the remaindermen must be signatories."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["estoppel certificate"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-04-005$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 4, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 3, $lp$A home is expected to sell for $845,000. Payoff is $412,000, total commission 5%, and other closing costs total $9,804.50. Ignoring prorations, estimated seller net is approximately:$lp$, $lp$["$433,000", "$380,945", "$390,750", "$423,196"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["$433,000 subtracts only the loan payoff, omitting commission and costs.", "Correct. $845,000 − $412,000 payoff − $42,250 commission − $9,804.50 costs = $380,945.50.", "$390,750 omits part of the closing costs.", "$423,196 omits the commission."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["seller's net sheet"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-04-006$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 4, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$The most persuasive pricing exhibit when counseling a seller against overpricing is:$lp$, $lp$["Active listings currently on the market", "Expired and withdrawn listings", "The agent's personal opinion of value", "The seller's original purchase price"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Actives show what sellers hope for, not what buyers pay.", "Correct. Expireds are evidence of what the market refused, which demonstrates the cost of testing a high price better than any argument.", "Opinion invites debate; evidence does not.", "What the seller paid has no bearing on current market value."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["days on market (DOM)"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-04-007$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 4, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$The best time to agree on a price reduction schedule is:$lp$, $lp$["After the first thirty days without an offer", "At the listing appointment, before the property goes on market", "When the seller raises the subject", "Only if the property fails to sell within the listing term"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["By then the seller is frustrated and emotion has attached to the price.", "Correct. Agreeing the review schedule before marketing begins converts a difficult negotiation into a plan the seller already accepted.", "Waiting for the seller to raise it means it usually never gets raised.", "A reduction discussion after the term expires is too late to help."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["price reduction schedule"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-04-008$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 4, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 3, $lp$A seller says another agent quoted a lower commission. The licensee may:$lp$, $lp$["Explain that commission rates are set by the local association", "Negotiate the rate freely, but never claim any rate is standard or fixed", "Match the rate only with DRE approval", "Refuse to discuss commission before the listing is signed"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Associations do not and cannot set commission rates; claiming so risks an antitrust problem.", "Correct. Commissions are always negotiable, and a licensee may negotiate — but representing a rate as standard, customary, or fixed by law is prohibited.", "The DRE does not approve commission rates.", "Compensation is a core listing term and must be discussed openly."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["definite termination date"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-04-009$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 4, $lp$contracts$lp$, 2, $lp$A seller wants to reserve the right to sell to her cousin without paying a commission, but otherwise wants full representation. The best solution is:$lp$, $lp$["An open listing", "An exclusive right to sell with the cousin as a written named exclusion", "An oral side agreement", "A net listing"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["An open listing gives up the protection the seller says she wants.", "Correct. A named written exclusion honors the seller's actual intent while protecting the broker as to every other buyer, and it puts the understanding in the contract.", "Oral commission agreements are unenforceable and invite disputes.", "A net listing is a conflict-prone compensation structure and does not address the exclusion question."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["named exclusion"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-04-010$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 4, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$Completing the seller's disclosures BEFORE the property is marketed primarily:$lp$, $lp$["Increases the sale price", "Eliminates the buyer's statutory termination right that arises from post-offer delivery", "Satisfies the agent's visual inspection duty", "Removes the need for a natural hazard report"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Early disclosure does not itself raise price.", "Correct. When disclosures are delivered after a buyer signs an offer, a statutory right to terminate arises; delivering beforehand eliminates that risk entirely.", "The agent's visual inspection duty is separate and must still be performed.", "The NHD remains required regardless of timing."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["seller's net sheet"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-04-011$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 4, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$When listing an occupied rental property, the agent should obtain from the seller:$lp$, $lp$["Only the current rent amount", "The leases, rent roll, security deposit accounting, and tenant estoppel certificates", "The tenants' credit reports", "Written tenant consent to the sale"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Rent alone tells a buyer nothing about the obligations they are inheriting.", "Correct. A buyer takes subject to existing leases, so leases, rent roll, deposit accounting, and estoppel certificates confirming terms are essential.", "Tenant credit reports are private and not the seller's to distribute.", "Tenants have no consent right over the owner's sale."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["estoppel certificate"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-04-012$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 4, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 3, $lp$A seller directs the agent to omit a known recurring roof leak from the disclosures and refuses to reconsider. The agent should:$lp$, $lp$["Take the listing and complete the disclosure accurately without telling the seller", "Decline or withdraw from the listing", "Take the listing and note the disagreement in the file", "Take the listing and disclose only if a buyer asks"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Completing the seller's own disclosure form contrary to their instruction without addressing it does not resolve a client actively directing concealment.", "Correct. The agent cannot participate in concealing a known material fact; when the seller persists after being informed, declining or withdrawing is the appropriate course.", "A file note does not cure participation in concealment.", "Waiting to be asked is concealment."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["seller's net sheet"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-04-013$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 4, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$A new licensee with no closed transactions is asked about experience at a listing appointment. The best response is to:$lp$, $lp$["Describe team transactions as though they were personally closed", "State the situation honestly and describe the broker's oversight and the attention the agent can give", "Avoid the question and redirect to marketing", "Claim more experience than the agent has to win the listing"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Presenting others' transactions as your own is a misrepresentation and easily discovered.", "Correct. A new agent's honest position — the broker's experience behind them and more available time than a busy top producer — is a genuine argument sellers respond to.", "Evading a direct question damages credibility more than the honest answer would.", "Inflating a track record is a misrepresentation and grounds for discipline."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["marketing plan"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-04-014$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 4, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$Researching a property's vesting BEFORE the listing appointment matters most because it determines:$lp$, $lp$["The likely sale price", "Who must sign to convey marketable title", "The commission rate", "The marketing strategy"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Vesting does not affect market value.", "Correct. Trusts, life estates, community property, and co-ownership each change who must sign — a threshold question best resolved before marketing rather than in escrow.", "Compensation is negotiated independently of how title is held.", "Marketing strategy flows from the property and market, not the vesting."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["estoppel certificate"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-04-015$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 4, $lp$contracts$lp$, 2, $lp$A safety (protection) clause typically requires the broker to deliver, within a stated period after expiration:$lp$, $lp$["A final marketing report", "A written list of protected prospects", "A recorded notice of the broker's interest", "An accounting of marketing expenses"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["A marketing report is not what triggers the clause.", "Correct. Enforceability generally depends on timely delivery of a written list identifying prospects the broker introduced during the listing — calendar it the day the listing expires.", "Brokers do not record interests against a seller's title to secure commissions.", "Expense accounting is unrelated to the protection period."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["named exclusion"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-04-016$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 4, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$A seller's net sheet should always be:$lp$, $lp$["Presented as a guaranteed figure", "Labeled an estimate, with conservative assumptions", "Withheld until an offer is received", "Prepared only by the escrow officer"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Guaranteeing proceeds is impossible and creates liability when figures shift.", "Correct. Label it an estimate and be conservative — sellers remember the number they were shown, and optimistic figures create disappointment and claims.", "Its greatest value is at the listing appointment, when the seller is deciding.", "Agents routinely prepare estimates; escrow prepares the final settlement statement."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["seller's net sheet"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-04-017$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 4, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 3, $lp$A seller insists on a list price well above what the data supports and refuses any review schedule. The agent's best course is to:$lp$, $lp$["Take the listing, since any listing generates exposure", "Decline plainly, explain why, and leave the door open", "Take the listing and reduce the price unilaterally later", "Take the listing at the seller's price and market it minimally"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["An overpriced listing consumes months of effort, goes stale, and often sells below what correct pricing would have achieved.", "Correct. Declining without insult preserves the relationship — many such sellers call back within sixty days — and avoids committing to a price the agent cannot defend.", "An agent cannot change the list price without the seller's authorization.", "Accepting a listing while intending to underserve it breaches the duty of reasonable care."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["price reduction schedule"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-04-018$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 4, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$Settling which fixtures and personal property are included or excluded is best done:$lp$, $lp$["At the final walk-through", "In writing at the listing appointment", "By relying on the MARIA tests if a dispute arises", "Verbally with the buyer's agent"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["The walk-through is where unresolved fixture questions become disputes, not where they should first be raised.", "Correct. Documenting inclusions and exclusions in the listing prevents the single most common closing argument, and written agreement controls over the fixture tests.", "MARIA resolves disputes expensively; a written term prevents them entirely.", "Verbal understandings about fixtures are unenforceable and routinely disputed."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["marketing plan"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-05-001$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 5, $lp$valuation$lp$, 1, $lp$The only category of market data that constitutes evidence of what buyers actually paid is:$lp$, $lp$["Active listings", "Closed sales", "Pending sales", "Expired listings"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Actives show what sellers hope to receive, not what buyers paid.", "Correct. Closed sales are the only confirmed evidence of value; everything else is context.", "Pending sales indicate direction but the price is unconfirmed until closing.", "Expireds show what the market refused, which is useful context but not evidence of value."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["comparable (comp)", "CMA"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-05-002$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 5, $lp$valuation$lp$, 2, $lp$A comparable has a feature the subject lacks. The appraiser or agent should:$lp$, $lp$["Add value to the subject", "Subtract value from the comparable", "Add value to the comparable", "Subtract value from the subject"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["The subject is never adjusted; only comparables move.", "Correct. Comparable Better, Subtract — adjustments make the comparable resemble the subject.", "Adding to a superior comparable moves it further from the subject.", "Adjusting the subject is the most common error in this method."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["CBS / CIA"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-05-003$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 5, $lp$valuation$lp$, 3, $lp$In the same tract, a 3-bedroom sold for $712,000 and a comparable 4-bedroom sold for $741,000. Using paired sales analysis, the market adjustment for the additional bedroom is:$lp$, $lp$["$50,000", "$29,000", "$14,500", "$71,200"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["$50,000 is a guess rather than a derived figure; the market says otherwise.", "Correct. $741,000 − $712,000 = $29,000, derived from two sales alike except for the one feature — the cleanest method of establishing an adjustment.", "$14,500 halves the difference for no stated reason.", "$71,200 is 10% of one sale price and bears no relation to the paired difference."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["paired sales analysis"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-05-004$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 5, $lp$valuation$lp$, 3, $lp$Four tract sales: no pool at $698,000 and $705,000; with pool at $719,000 and $722,000. The indicated pool adjustment is:$lp$, $lp$["$19,000", "$60,000", "$24,000", "$9,500"]$lp$::jsonb, 0, $lp$["Correct. Average without pool = $701,500; average with pool = $720,500; difference = $19,000 — roughly a third of what an in-ground pool costs to build, illustrating the principle of contribution.", "$60,000 is a typical construction cost, not the market's contribution to value.", "$24,000 does not follow from these figures.", "$9,500 halves the paired difference without basis."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["paired sales analysis"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-05-005$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 5, $lp$valuation$lp$, 3, $lp$A comparable sold 8 months ago for $812,000 in a market that appreciated 3% over that period. The time adjustment is:$lp$, $lp$["−$24,360", "+$24,360", "+$2,436", "No adjustment; time is not adjusted"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["A negative adjustment would apply in a declining market, not an appreciating one.", "Correct. The comparable sold before the market rose, making it inferior in market conditions: $812,000 × 0.03 = $24,360 added to the comparable.", "$2,436 reflects 0.3% rather than 3%.", "Market conditions is a standard adjustment category and is applied before physical adjustments."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["time (market conditions) adjustment"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-05-006$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 5, $lp$valuation$lp$, 2, $lp$Gross adjustment measures:$lp$, $lp$["The net effect of all adjustments accounting for direction", "The absolute sum of all adjustments regardless of direction", "The largest single adjustment applied", "The adjustment for market conditions only"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["That describes net adjustment.", "Correct. Gross adjustment sums the magnitude of every adjustment regardless of sign, which is why it is the better test of whether a property is genuinely comparable.", "A single adjustment does not capture cumulative dissimilarity.", "Time is one component among several."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["gross adjustment", "net adjustment"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-05-007$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 5, $lp$valuation$lp$, 3, $lp$A comparable requires gross adjustments totaling 40% of its sale price. This indicates that:$lp$, $lp$["The adjustments were calculated incorrectly", "The property is not genuinely comparable and a better one should be found", "The subject is overpriced", "A time adjustment was omitted"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["The arithmetic may be perfectly correct; the problem is the choice of comparable.", "Correct. Heavy gross adjustment signals dissimilarity — the comparable is telling you it is not comparable, and the conclusion drawn from it will be weak.", "Adjustment magnitude says nothing about the subject's pricing.", "A missing time adjustment would generally reduce, not inflate, the gross figure."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["gross adjustment"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-05-008$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 5, $lp$valuation$lp$, 3, $lp$Three adjusted comparables indicate $799,960, $795,643, and $813,275. The comparable at $795,643 required only 2.1% gross adjustment; the others required 10.4% and 6.8%. The best conclusion is:$lp$, $lp$["Average the three: $802,959", "Weight the least-adjusted comparable most heavily and conclude near $799,000", "Use the highest indication, $813,275", "Use the lowest indication, $795,643, exclusively"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Averaging treats a weak comparable as equal to a strong one and cannot be defended when a seller asks why.", "Correct. Reconciliation is judgment: the comparable requiring the smallest adjustment is the most reliable, and the others bracket it — supporting a range with a recommendation near $799,000.", "Selecting the highest indication abandons analysis in favor of the most flattering number.", "Exclusive reliance on one comparable discards corroborating evidence that supports the range."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["gross adjustment", "CMA"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-05-009$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 5, $lp$valuation$lp$, 3, $lp$A nearby 1,240 sq ft home sold at $560 per square foot. Applying that figure to a 2,900 sq ft home is unreliable because:$lp$, $lp$["Price per square foot cannot be calculated for larger homes", "Cost per square foot generally declines as home size increases", "Square footage is never a valuation factor", "The two homes are in different tax districts"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["The calculation is possible; the extrapolation is what fails.", "Correct. Land, kitchen, systems, and site costs do not scale linearly with living area, so larger homes sell at lower per-square-foot figures — extrapolating a small home's rate overstates value badly.", "Living area is a significant factor; the issue is how it is applied.", "Tax districts do not explain the per-square-foot relationship."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["price per square foot"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-05-010$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 5, $lp$valuation$lp$, 3, $lp$A submarket has 148 active listings and averages 41 closed sales per month. Months of inventory is approximately:$lp$, $lp$["3.6 months", "27.7 months", "0.28 months", "6.1 months"]$lp$::jsonb, 0, $lp$["Correct. 148 ÷ 41 = 3.6 months, indicating a modestly seller-favorable to balanced market.", "27.7 is the absorption percentage per month, not months of inventory.", "This inverts the calculation.", "6.1 months would require about 24 sales per month at this inventory level."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["months of inventory", "absorption rate"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-05-011$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 5, $lp$valuation$lp$, 3, $lp$Homes selling within 14 days averaged 99.2% of original list; homes taking over 60 days averaged 92.8%. On an $800,000 home, the difference in proceeds is approximately:$lp$, $lp$["$6,400", "$51,200", "$12,800", "$25,600"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["$6,400 reflects a 0.8% difference rather than the 6.4-point spread.", "Correct. $800,000 × 0.992 = $793,600; × 0.928 = $742,400; the difference is $51,200 — before counting two extra months of carrying costs.", "$12,800 reflects a 1.6% difference.", "$25,600 reflects a 3.2% difference."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["sale-to-list ratio"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-05-012$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 5, $lp$valuation$lp$, 2, $lp$Which sale should be EXCLUDED from a CMA as evidence of market value?$lp$, $lp$["An arm's-length sale that closed three months ago", "A sale between siblings at a below-market price", "A sale of a similar home two streets away", "A sale that closed after a normal 30-day escrow"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["A recent arm's-length sale is exactly what a CMA should use.", "Correct. Sales between relatives, distress sales, and transactions with unusual concessions are prices but not evidence of market value, because they fail the arm's-length condition.", "Nearby similar sales are strong comparables.", "A normal escrow period does not disqualify a sale."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["comparable (comp)"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-05-013$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 5, $lp$valuation$lp$, 2, $lp$The correct order for applying adjustments places market conditions (time):$lp$, $lp$["After all physical adjustments", "Before location and physical characteristics", "Only if the sale is more than a year old", "At the very end, as a final reconciliation"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Applying time last distorts the physical adjustments, which are expressed in current dollars.", "Correct. The sequence runs property rights, financing, conditions of sale, expenditures after purchase, then market conditions, then location, then physical characteristics.", "Time adjustments apply whenever the market has moved, regardless of a one-year threshold.", "Reconciliation weighs completed comparables; it is not where time is applied."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["time (market conditions) adjustment"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-05-014$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 5, $lp$valuation$lp$, 2, $lp$A licensee may NOT:$lp$, $lp$["Prepare a CMA for a listing presentation", "Accept a fee contingent on the CMA reaching a particular value", "Give a seller a price range with stated assumptions", "Use closed sales to support a pricing recommendation"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Preparing a CMA is normal, expected licensed activity.", "Correct. Compensation contingent on reaching a specified value compromises objectivity and is prohibited — the same principle that governs appraiser independence.", "A range with stated assumptions is the professionally sound way to present a conclusion.", "Closed sales are the proper evidentiary basis for a CMA."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["CMA"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-05-015$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 5, $lp$valuation$lp$, 2, $lp$How many strong comparables does a well-constructed CMA typically use?$lp$, $lp$["One, if it is nearly identical", "Three to five", "At least ten, for statistical reliability", "As many as are available in the ZIP code"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["A single comparable provides no corroboration and no way to test the conclusion.", "Correct. Three to five strong comparables outperform a larger set of weak ones; the fewer and smaller the adjustments, the more reliable the conclusion.", "Volume does not substitute for comparability, and weak comparables degrade the analysis.", "ZIP codes routinely span multiple distinct submarkets."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["comparable (comp)", "CMA"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-05-016$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 5, $lp$valuation$lp$, 2, $lp$Presenting a CMA conclusion as a range rather than a single figure is preferable because:$lp$, $lp$["It avoids committing to a recommendation", "It allows the assumptions behind each end of the range to be stated explicitly", "Ranges are required by California law", "It prevents the seller from disagreeing"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["A good range is paired with a recommendation, not a substitute for one.", "Correct. A range lets the agent tie each end to conditions — as-is versus prepared and staged, for instance — which is more honest and more useful than a single number.", "No statute mandates a range.", "Sellers may still disagree; the range makes the reasoning easier to examine."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["CMA"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-05-017$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 5, $lp$valuation$lp$, 3, $lp$A comparable sold for $845,000 and is 180 sq ft larger than the subject. At a derived rate of $180 per square foot, the living area adjustment is:$lp$, $lp$["+$32,400 to the comparable", "−$32,400 to the comparable", "+$32,400 to the subject", "−$32,400 to the subject"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Adding would treat the larger comparable as inferior.", "Correct. The comparable is superior in living area, so subtract: 180 × $180 = $32,400 deducted from the comparable.", "The subject is never adjusted.", "The subject is never adjusted."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["CBS / CIA"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-05-018$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 5, $lp$valuation$lp$, 2, $lp$When a paired sales analysis is unavailable, an acceptable basis for an adjustment value is:$lp$, $lp$["The agent's intuition about buyer preferences", "Cost with depreciation applied, disclosed as the method used", "The seller's stated opinion of the feature's worth", "The full replacement cost of the feature"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Invented adjustment values cannot be defended when questioned.", "Correct. Cost less depreciation is a legitimate fallback, provided the method is disclosed in the presentation so the seller can evaluate it.", "A seller's opinion of value is the thing being tested, not evidence.", "Full cost overstates contribution — improvements rarely add their full cost to value."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["paired sales analysis"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-05-019$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 5, $lp$valuation$lp$, 2, $lp$Expired and withdrawn listings are most useful in a CMA as evidence of:$lp$, $lp$["Current market value", "What the market refused to pay", "Future appreciation", "The seller's motivation"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Expireds never sold and therefore establish no value.", "Correct. Expireds show the prices at which the market declined to transact — the clearest available evidence of the cost of overpricing.", "They say nothing about future direction.", "They reflect pricing outcomes rather than any individual seller's motivation."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["days on market (DOM)"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-05-020$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 5, $lp$valuation$lp$, 3, $lp$An agent's adjusted-comparable analysis indicates about $800,000, but a price-per-square-foot estimate suggests $1,000,000. The agent should:$lp$, $lp$["Average the two and present $900,000", "Re-examine the comparables and the per-square-foot basis to find the source of the divergence", "Present the higher figure, since sellers prefer it", "Present the lower figure without investigating"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Averaging two methods, one of which is likely misapplied, produces a number supported by nothing.", "Correct. A large divergence is a signal that comparables were poorly chosen or the per-square-foot figure was extrapolated across dissimilar sizes — investigate rather than reconcile arithmetically.", "Presenting the flattering figure to win a listing is exactly the overpricing trap.", "Ignoring a signal that something is wrong forfeits the chance to correct the analysis."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["price per square foot", "CMA"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-06-001$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 6, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$An agent posts a listing photo, price, and her personal cell number to her Instagram account, with no brokerage identification anywhere in the post. This is most likely:$lp$, $lp$["Acceptable, because social media is personal rather than commercial", "A blind ad, because it fails to reveal the advertiser is a licensee acting through a broker", "A RESPA violation", "Acceptable if her bio mentions she is an agent"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["The DRE treats social media as solicitation material; the platform's informality is irrelevant.", "Correct. Advertising property without identifying the licensee's status and responsible broker is a blind ad and is prohibited.", "RESPA governs settlement service referrals, not advertising identification.", "A bio mention does not cure identification missing from the material advertising the property."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["blind ad", "solicitation materials"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-06-002$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 6, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$Which listing phrase presents BOTH a fair housing problem and a separate factual-representation risk?$lp$, $lp$["Updated kitchen with quartz counters", "Quiet, safe family neighborhood", "Detached two-car garage", "Corner lot with mature landscaping"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["This describes a property feature objectively.", "Correct. 'Family' implicates familial status, while 'safe' is an unverifiable claim about crime that creates misrepresentation exposure independently.", "This describes a physical feature.", "This describes the lot objectively."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["puffing"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-06-003$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 6, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$Virtually staged listing photographs are:$lp$, $lp$["Prohibited in California", "Permitted, but must be disclosed as virtually staged", "Permitted without disclosure if the staging is realistic", "Permitted only for vacant land"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Virtual staging is widely used and lawful.", "Correct. Virtual staging must be clearly labeled; an unlabeled staged image misrepresents the property's actual condition and scale.", "Realism makes an undisclosed alteration more misleading, not less.", "Virtual staging is used for improved property, not land."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["virtual staging"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-06-004$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 6, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 3, $lp$An agent virtually stages a room, placing a rug over an area of damaged flooring, and does not label the photo. The most serious problem is that the image:$lp$, $lp$["Used furniture the seller does not own", "Concealed a property condition a buyer would want to see", "Was taken with a wide-angle lens", "Was not credited to the photographer"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Depicting furniture the seller does not own is ordinary staging and is not itself the problem.", "Correct. Beyond the labeling failure, placing a virtual element over a defect moves the conduct toward active concealment of a material condition.", "Lens choice raises separate scale concerns but is not what happened here.", "Attribution is a copyright matter, unrelated to concealment."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["virtual staging"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-06-005$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 6, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$Which statement in listing copy is lawful puffing rather than a representation of fact?$lp$, $lp$["Approximately 2,180 square feet", "Permitted bonus room", "Charming curb appeal", "Zoned for commercial use"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["Square footage is verifiable fact and a frequent source of misrepresentation claims.", "Permit status is verifiable and must never be stated without seeing the permit.", "Correct. 'Charming' is subjective opinion no reasonable buyer relies on as fact.", "Zoning is verifiable fact."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["puffing"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-06-006$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 6, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 3, $lp$An agent repeats a 2,450 sq ft figure from a prior listing; the assessor shows 2,180, and part of the difference is an unpermitted patio enclosure. The agent's exposure is:$lp$, $lp$["None, because the figure came from a prior listing", "Misrepresentation, because innocent misstatement of a material fact can support rescission or damages", "Limited to a DRE warning letter", "None, if the buyer had the opportunity to measure"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Repeating an unverified figure is not verification and provides no defense.", "Correct. Even innocent misrepresentation of a material fact can support rescission or damages, and the unpermitted enclosure is separately material and should have been disclosed.", "Civil liability and license discipline both remain available; a warning is not the ceiling.", "A buyer's opportunity to verify does not excuse an affirmative misstatement by the agent."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["puffing"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-06-007$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 6, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$The safest way to advertise square footage is to:$lp$, $lp$["State the figure without qualification", "Cite the source and recommend buyer verification", "Round up to the nearest hundred", "Omit square footage entirely"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["An unqualified figure is an affirmative representation the agent must be able to support.", "Correct. Attributing the figure to its source — assessor, appraisal, or builder — and recommending buyer verification is the standard protective practice.", "Rounding up overstates the property and compounds the risk.", "Omitting a material marketing detail disserves the seller when a qualified statement is available."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["solicitation materials"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-06-008$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 6, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$Which advertising phrase constitutes source of income discrimination in California?$lp$, $lp$["Cash offers preferred", "No Section 8", "Conventional financing welcome", "Seller will consider all offers"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Preferring cash concerns transaction certainty, not a protected characteristic.", "Correct. Source of income is protected under California law, and refusing housing vouchers in advertising is a clear violation.", "Noting acceptable financing types does not exclude a protected source of income.", "This states openness and excludes no one."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["Equal Housing Opportunity"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-06-009$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 6, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$Listing photographs are typically owned by:$lp$, $lp$["The listing agent automatically", "The photographer, unless the agreement transfers rights", "The seller, as owner of the property", "The MLS"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Paying for photos does not by itself transfer copyright.", "Correct. Photographs are copyrighted works owned by the photographer absent a written transfer or license — which is why agents need a written license covering their intended uses.", "Owning the property does not create copyright in images of it.", "MLS submission grants usage rights within the MLS framework but does not transfer ownership."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["attribution"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-06-010$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 6, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 3, $lp$An agent who represented the buyer places a 'SOLD' rider bearing her name on a property she did not list. This is:$lp$, $lp$["Standard practice for the buyer's agent", "Misleading, because it implies a listing role she did not have", "Permitted with the buyer's consent", "Permitted if the sign is removed within a week"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Sign riders on a listing belong to the listing broker.", "Correct. Advertising a sale in a way that implies a role the agent did not have is misleading; 'Just sold — represented the buyer' would be accurate.", "The buyer cannot authorize a misleading representation about the agent's role.", "Duration does not cure a misleading implication."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["attribution"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-06-011$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 6, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$Displaying another broker's listings on an agent's website is generally permitted through IDX, provided the agent:$lp$, $lp$["Removes the listing broker's name to avoid confusion", "Complies with MLS attribution requirements", "Obtains the seller's written consent", "Pays the listing broker a display fee"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Removing the listing broker's identification is precisely the violation attribution rules prevent.", "Correct. IDX permits display of other participants' listings subject to attribution requirements identifying the listing broker.", "Seller consent flows through the listing broker's MLS participation, not individual permission.", "IDX display does not involve a per-listing payment to the listing broker."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["IDX", "attribution"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-06-012$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 6, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 3, $lp$An agent digitally removes overhead power lines from a listing photograph. This is:$lp$, $lp$["Acceptable image enhancement", "A misrepresentation of the property's actual condition", "Acceptable if disclosed only to the buyer's agent", "Acceptable because power lines are outside the property line"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Removing a real feature goes beyond enhancement into altering what the property is.", "Correct. Editing out power lines, neighboring structures, or other real conditions misrepresents the property, distinct from disclosed virtual staging.", "Selective disclosure to one party does not cure a misleading public advertisement.", "A condition visible from and affecting the property is material regardless of where the poles sit."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["virtual staging"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-06-013$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 6, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$AI-generated or AI-enhanced listing imagery should be treated:$lp$, $lp$["As ordinary photography requiring no special handling", "The same as virtual staging — permitted, clearly labeled, and never used to conceal a condition", "As prohibited under all circumstances", "As acceptable only for exterior shots"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Imagery that does not depict the property as it is cannot be presented as ordinary photography.", "Correct. If an image does not show the property as it actually exists, it must be disclosed as an alteration and must never mask a defect.", "Enhancement is not categorically prohibited; undisclosed misrepresentation is.", "The concern is accuracy, not which elevation is depicted."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["virtual staging"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-06-014$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 6, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$A licensee's website must display licensee and broker identification:$lp$, $lp$["Only on the contact page", "On pages a consumer may land on, not buried in a single interior page", "Only if the site advertises specific listings", "Only in the site's terms of use"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Consumers arrive on many pages directly from search and social links.", "Correct. Identification must be present where a consumer encounters the solicitation, not hidden on one interior page.", "The identification obligation attaches to solicitation generally, not only to listing pages.", "Legal boilerplate is not where required identification belongs."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["license identification requirement"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-06-015$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 6, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 3, $lp$An agent advertises 'Sold 12 homes last year,' but 12 was the team's total and the agent personally represented 4. The claim is:$lp$, $lp$["Acceptable industry shorthand", "Misleading, because production claims must accurately reflect the agent's own role", "Acceptable if the team name appears nearby", "Acceptable because team production is shared"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Ambiguity that inflates an agent's record is not shorthand; it is a misrepresentation.", "Correct. Advertised production statistics must be accurate and not misleading about the agent's personal involvement.", "Proximity of a team name does not clarify that the agent personally handled four transactions.", "Shared team effort does not permit an individual to claim the team's total as personal production."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["solicitation materials"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-06-016$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 6, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$Marketing imagery that consistently depicts only one demographic group can:$lp$, $lp$["Never raise legal concerns, since images make no statements", "Signal an unlawful preference under fair housing law", "Be cured by adding the Equal Housing Opportunity logo", "Be justified as reflecting the neighborhood's composition"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Images communicate preference as effectively as words and are treated accordingly.", "Correct. Advertising imagery indicating a preference for or against protected groups is a documented enforcement theory.", "The logo is required but does not cure imagery that signals a preference.", "Reflecting neighborhood composition is not a defense to advertising that signals who is welcome."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["Equal Housing Opportunity"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-06-017$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 6, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$Describing a property as 'walking distance to St. Anne's' raises a concern under:$lp$, $lp$["Truth in Lending", "Fair housing law, because it may signal a religious preference", "RESPA", "The statute of frauds"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["TILA governs credit cost disclosure.", "Correct. Naming a religious institution as a landmark can indicate a religious preference; a neutral proximity reference is the safer construction.", "RESPA governs settlement service referrals.", "The statute of frauds concerns which contracts must be written."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["Equal Housing Opportunity"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-06-018$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 6, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$The single best test to apply before publishing any listing copy or social post is:$lp$, $lp$["Does it sound professional?", "Does it describe the property rather than the people?", "Is it under the character limit?", "Does it mention the price?"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Tone does not detect fair housing problems.", "Correct. Nearly every advertising fair housing violation fails this one test, and applying it consistently also produces better marketing copy.", "Length has no bearing on compliance.", "Price disclosure is unrelated to the fair housing risk in copy."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["solicitation materials", "Equal Housing Opportunity"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-07-001$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 7, $lp$agency$lp$, 2, $lp$The buyer consultation should occur:$lp$, $lp$["After the buyer has seen several properties", "Before showing any property", "Only when the buyer is ready to write an offer", "At the closing table"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Showing first means agency, financing, and criteria are all unresolved while the agent is already working.", "Correct. The consultation establishes agency in writing, verifies financing, lets the buyer define criteria, and sets expectations — all of which must precede showings.", "Waiting until offer time wastes months and leaves the relationship undefined.", "The consultation is the first meeting, not the last."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["buyer consultation", "buyer representation agreement"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-07-002$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 7, $lp$financing$lp$, 2, $lp$A lender letter based on income and assets the borrower stated but the lender never verified is a:$lp$, $lp$["Preapproval", "Prequalification", "Loan commitment", "Clear to close"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Preapproval requires the lender to verify income, assets, and credit.", "Correct. A prequalification rests on unverified stated information and is weak evidence of ability to close, whatever the letter's title says.", "A loan commitment is final underwriting approval near the end of escrow.", "Clear to close is the final step before funding."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["prequalification", "preapproval"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-07-003$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 7, $lp$financing$lp$, 3, $lp$A buyer's agent should advise removing the loan contingency when:$lp$, $lp$["The lender says the file 'looks good'", "Underwriting has approved the file in writing", "The appraisal has been ordered", "The contingency deadline arrives"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Informal reassurance is not approval, and files die after it constantly.", "Correct. Removal should follow written underwriting approval — if financing fails after removal, the buyer is in default and faces liquidated damages up to 3% of the price.", "Ordering an appraisal says nothing about the borrower's underwriting status.", "A deadline arriving is a reason to seek an extension or deliver a removal, not a reason to remove without approval."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["loan commitment", "preapproval"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-07-004$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 7, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 3, $lp$During a showing an agent notices ceiling staining with fresh paint around it. The buyer says it does not matter. The agent must:$lp$, $lp$["Accept the buyer's decision and say nothing", "Disclose the observation in writing and recommend a qualified inspection", "Diagnose the likely cause for the buyer", "Report the seller to the DRE"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["The visual inspection duty is the agent's own and is not waived by the buyer's enthusiasm.", "Correct. Observe, disclose, refer — put the observation in writing the same day and recommend inspection of that area, then document if the buyer declines.", "Agents are not inspectors and must not diagnose cause or estimate cost.", "The immediate duty is disclosure to the buyer, not a regulatory complaint."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["visual inspection duty", "observe, disclose, refer"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-07-005$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 7, $lp$agency$lp$, 3, $lp$At an open house, the listing agent asks a buyer's agent about her clients' timeline and approval amount. Sharing that information would:$lp$, $lp$["Build useful rapport between agents", "Breach the duty of confidentiality owed to the buyer", "Be required by the duty of honesty to third parties", "Be permissible if the buyer never finds out"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Rapport is useful, but not at the cost of the client's negotiating position.", "Correct. Urgency and a maximum approval amount are confidential client information; disclosing them hands the seller leverage and is actionable if it costs the buyer money.", "Honesty to third parties does not require volunteering the client's confidential information.", "The breach occurs regardless of whether the client learns of it."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["buyer representation agreement"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-07-006$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 7, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$A listing is tenant-occupied with a 24-hour notice requirement. The seller tells the agent to show it immediately. The agent should:$lp$, $lp$["Show it, because the owner consented", "Wait for proper notice, because the seller cannot waive the tenant's entry rights", "Show it if the tenant appears to be away", "Show it and leave a note"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Ownership does not override the tenant's right of possession.", "Correct. Entry generally requires reasonable written notice with 24 hours presumed reasonable; the seller cannot waive the tenant's rights, and the buyer is about to inherit this relationship.", "Absence does not create a right of entry.", "A note after the fact does not satisfy an advance notice requirement."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["visual inspection duty"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-07-007$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 7, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$A buyer asks whether they can add a second unit to a property. The agent should:$lp$, $lp$["Give an opinion based on the lot size", "Refer the buyer to the city planning department", "State that state law now permits it everywhere", "Confirm it is allowed if neighbors have done it"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Lot size is one of many factors and an agent opinion here invites reliance the agent cannot support.", "Correct. Zoning, permitting, and feasibility questions belong to the planning department; the agent's role is to route the buyer to the authority that can actually answer.", "State housing law has expanded unit allowances but application varies by parcel and jurisdiction; a blanket claim is a misrepresentation.", "A neighbor's structure may be nonconforming, unpermitted, or approved under different rules."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["observe, disclose, refer"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-07-008$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 7, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 3, $lp$A lender offers a buyer's agent a $400 payment for each client referred who closes a loan. Accepting it would:$lp$, $lp$["Be lawful if disclosed to the buyer", "Violate RESPA's prohibition on compensation for referrals of settlement services", "Be lawful because the agent is not a lender", "Be lawful if the buyer chose the lender independently"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Disclosure does not cure a prohibited referral fee.", "Correct. RESPA prohibits giving or accepting anything of value for referring settlement service business; only payment for services actually performed at fair market value is permitted.", "The prohibition applies to anyone giving or receiving the payment, not only to lenders.", "The payment is compensation for the referral regardless of how the buyer made the choice."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["buyer consultation"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-07-009$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 7, $lp$agency$lp$, 2, $lp$During the consultation, the agent should establish the buyer's search criteria by:$lp$, $lp$["Recommending neighborhoods that suit the buyer's background", "Asking the buyer to define their own criteria", "Selecting areas the agent knows best", "Narrowing to areas where similar buyers have purchased"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Inferring where a buyer belongs from their background is steering.", "Correct. Letting clients define their own criteria is simultaneously better service and the agent's fair housing protection.", "Agent convenience is not a legitimate basis for limiting a buyer's options.", "Steering buyers toward areas where 'similar' buyers bought uses protected characteristics as a proxy."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["buyer consultation"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-07-010$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 7, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$A buyer asks whether a neighborhood is safe. The agent should:$lp$, $lp$["Give an honest personal assessment", "Provide published crime data sources for the buyer to evaluate", "Say the area is safe if the agent believes it is", "Refuse to discuss the neighborhood in any way"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["A personal assessment about safety is both an unverifiable factual claim and a steering risk.", "Correct. Direct the buyer to objective published sources so they evaluate the area themselves — compliant and more genuinely useful.", "Characterizing an area as safe is a representation the agent cannot support.", "A bare refusal disserves the client; the redirect to data is what makes the response work."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["observe, disclose, refer"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-07-011$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 7, $lp$agency$lp$, 2, $lp$A buyer instructs the agent to waive the inspection contingency against the agent's advice. The agent should:$lp$, $lp$["Refuse to write the offer", "Write it as instructed and document the advice given in writing the same day", "Write it and say nothing further", "Write it but secretly retain the contingency"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Waiving inspections is lawful; the buyer is entitled to make that choice.", "Correct. Obedience covers lawful instructions, and a same-day email confirming what was advised and that the buyer elected otherwise is the agent's file if a defect surfaces later.", "Failing to document contrary advice leaves the agent exposed when the buyer later says they were never warned.", "Altering the buyer's instructions without authorization breaches the duty of obedience."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["visual inspection duty"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-07-012$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 7, $lp$financing$lp$, 2, $lp$The strongest evidence a buyer can attach to an offer regarding financing is a letter showing the lender has:$lp$, $lp$["Spoken with the buyer by phone", "Verified income, assets, and credit", "Estimated a payment amount", "Received the purchase contract"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["A conversation produces a prequalification at best.", "Correct. Verification of income, assets, and credit is what distinguishes a preapproval from a prequalification and what a seller is actually evaluating.", "A payment estimate reflects no underwriting review.", "Receiving the contract happens after the offer and says nothing about the borrower."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["preapproval", "prequalification"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-07-013$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 7, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$Agents should assume that occupied listings may have:$lp$, $lp$["No recording devices, since that would be illegal", "Audio and video recording devices in use", "Recording only in common areas", "Recording only with posted notice"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Video recording of one's own property is common and often lawful; audio raises separate legal questions but devices are frequently present.", "Correct. Assume every occupied property may be recording, and never discuss a buyer's budget, enthusiasm, or strategy inside it.", "Devices are commonly placed throughout a home, not only in common areas.", "Notice is not always posted, and the practical rule is to assume recording regardless."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["buyer consultation"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-07-014$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 7, $lp$agency$lp$, 3, $lp$A buyer asks the agent to omit from the offer that the buyer is an investor who intends to rent the property. The agent should:$lp$, $lp$["Comply, because investor status is not protected", "Decline, because concealing a material fact from the other party is fraudulent", "Comply if the seller does not ask", "Comply and note it in the file"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Investor status is not a protected characteristic, but that is not the issue — active concealment is.", "Correct. Misrepresenting or concealing the buyer's identity or intent from the seller is fraud, not a negotiating tactic, and the agent owes third parties honesty.", "The absence of a question does not license concealment of a material fact.", "Documenting participation in concealment does not cure it."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["buyer representation agreement"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-07-015$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 7, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$A buyer asks how they should hold title. The agent should:$lp$, $lp$["Recommend joint tenancy for its probate avoidance", "Explain that options exist and refer the buyer to an attorney or CPA", "Instruct escrow to select the most common local vesting", "Recommend a trust for asset protection"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Recommending a specific vesting is giving legal advice beyond a licensee's authority.", "Correct. A licensee may describe the options generally but must refer the choice to an attorney or CPA, because vesting carries legal, estate, and tax consequences.", "Delegating a client's legal decision to escrow based on local habit does not cure the advice problem.", "Recommending a trust is legal advice and may also implicate tax planning."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["buyer consultation"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-07-016$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 7, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$In a slow market, a hesitant buyer asks whether prices will fall further. The agent should:$lp$, $lp$["Predict the direction of the market confidently", "Present inventory, days on market, and sale-to-list data and let the buyer decide", "Urge the buyer to act before prices rise", "Decline to provide any market information"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Market predictions cannot be supported and clients remember them.", "Correct. Present what the data shows and let the buyer decide; pressuring a hesitant buyer into a purchase they regret costs a career of referrals for one commission.", "Urgency claims the agent cannot substantiate are misrepresentations.", "Withholding market data disserves the client — the issue is forecasting, not informing."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["buyer consultation"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-07-017$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 7, $lp$agency$lp$, 2, $lp$Under a buyer representation agreement, if the seller's side contributes less compensation than the buyer agreed to pay the buyer's broker, typically:$lp$, $lp$["The broker absorbs the shortfall", "The buyer owes the difference under the agreement", "The seller must increase the contribution", "The transaction cannot close"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["The written agreement between buyer and broker governs the broker's compensation.", "Correct. Buyer representation agreements generally make the buyer responsible for any shortfall, which is why the agent must explain the compensation structure clearly at consultation.", "The seller has no obligation beyond what the seller agreed to contribute.", "A compensation shortfall is a payment question, not an obstacle to closing."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["buyer representation agreement"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-07-018$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 7, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$The professional posture summarized as 'observe, disclose, refer' means the agent should:$lp$, $lp$["Diagnose the problem, estimate the cost, and recommend a contractor", "State what was observed, put it in writing, and recommend a qualified expert", "Note concerns verbally and wait for the buyer's inspector to find them", "Avoid commenting on condition to limit liability"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Diagnosis and cost estimation exceed a licensee's expertise and create liability.", "Correct. The agent reports the observation in writing and routes the client to someone qualified to evaluate it — the standard that satisfies the visual inspection duty without overstepping.", "Relying on the inspector to find it does not satisfy the agent's independent disclosure duty.", "Silence about an observed material fact is the violation, not a liability strategy."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["observe, disclose, refer", "visual inspection duty"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-08-001$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 8, $lp$contracts$lp$, 3, $lp$A seller receives three offers. Offer A: $862,000 with a $12,000 credit requested. Offer B: $850,000, no credit. Commission is 5% and other costs are $9,800 on each. Comparing net proceeds:$lp$, $lp$["A nets more, because its price is higher", "B nets more, by about $600", "They net identically", "A nets more, by about $12,000"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["The higher gross price is reduced by both a larger commission and the credit.", "Correct. A: 862,000 − 43,100 − 9,800 − 12,000 = $797,100. B: 850,000 − 42,500 − 9,800 = $797,700. B nets about $600 more while also carrying less risk.", "The nets differ, though only slightly — which is exactly why a net comparison is necessary.", "The credit reduces A's net but does not create a $12,000 advantage for A."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["net comparison"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-08-002$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 8, $lp$contracts$lp$, 2, $lp$When countering more than one buyer simultaneously, the seller should use:$lp$, $lp$["A separate ordinary counteroffer to each buyer", "A multiple counteroffer form providing that no contract forms until the seller signs a final acceptance", "A backup offer addendum with each", "A verbal counter to all parties"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Sending ordinary counteroffers to several buyers risks binding the seller to two contracts if more than one accepts.", "Correct. The multiple counteroffer form preserves the seller's choice by deferring contract formation until the seller signs a final acceptance of one response.", "Backup offers govern what happens after a primary contract fails, not simultaneous negotiation.", "Verbal counters on real property terms are unenforceable and create ambiguity."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["multiple counteroffer"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-08-003$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 8, $lp$contracts$lp$, 3, $lp$A seller's agent sends identical ordinary counteroffers to two buyers and both sign and return. The likely consequence is:$lp$, $lp$["The seller may choose whichever they prefer", "The seller may be bound to two contracts on one property", "Both counteroffers automatically become backup offers", "The first to deliver acceptance wins and the other is void"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Once both buyers accepted, the seller no longer has an unencumbered choice.", "Correct. Two accepted counteroffers can create two binding contracts on one property, and specific performance is available in real estate because land is unique — an avoidable crisis the multiple counteroffer form exists to prevent.", "Nothing converts an accepted counteroffer into a backup position automatically.", "Both acceptances may be effective; the seller cannot simply disregard one."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["multiple counteroffer", "backup offer"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-08-004$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 8, $lp$contracts$lp$, 3, $lp$A buyer offers $1,240,000. The property appraises at $1,190,000 and the lender will lend 80% of the lesser of price or value. The buyer's required cash is:$lp$, $lp$["$248,000", "$288,000", "$238,000", "$50,000"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["$248,000 is 20% of the purchase price, which is what the buyer planned on before the low appraisal.", "Correct. The loan is 80% of $1,190,000 = $952,000; the buyer must bring $1,240,000 − $952,000 = $288,000, or $40,000 more than planned.", "$238,000 corresponds to 20% of the appraised value, not the cash actually required.", "$50,000 is the appraisal gap alone, not total cash required."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["appraisal gap"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-08-005$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 8, $lp$contracts$lp$, 3, $lp$The contingency whose waiver exposes a buyer to liquidated damages of up to 3% of the price if financing later fails is the:$lp$, $lp$["Investigation contingency", "Loan contingency", "Appraisal contingency", "Title contingency"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Waiving inspection accepts condition risk but does not itself create financing default.", "Correct. If financing fails after the loan contingency is removed, the buyer is in default and the seller may pursue liquidated damages capped at 3% of the price on owner-occupied 1–4 units — $24,000 on an $800,000 home.", "The appraisal contingency addresses value shortfalls, which the buyer covers in cash.", "The title contingency covers review of the preliminary report."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["appraisal gap"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-08-006$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 8, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 3, $lp$Buyers ask their agent to submit a personal letter describing their young family, their church, and a family photo. The agent should:$lp$, $lp$["Submit it, since personal appeals often help", "Decline to transmit it and explain the fair housing exposure it creates", "Submit it after removing the photo", "Submit it only if the listing agent agrees"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["The letter injects familial status, religion, and — via the photo — race or national origin into the seller's decision.", "Correct. Buyer letters create fair housing exposure for the seller and both brokerages; declining and explaining, then strengthening the offer on lawful terms, is the defensible practice.", "Removing the photo does not remove the familial status and religious content.", "The listing agent's agreement does not cure the exposure to either side."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["buyer letter"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-08-007$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 8, $lp$contracts$lp$, 2, $lp$Liquidated damages and arbitration provisions in a California residential purchase agreement are effective only if:$lp$, $lp$["The broker signs as a witness", "Both parties separately sign or initial them", "They are recorded with the county", "Escrow approves them"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Broker witnessing is not the requirement.", "Correct. Both provisions require the parties' separate signatures or initials, ensuring each was specifically considered — and arbitration in particular waives a jury trial, which buyers should understand before initialing.", "Purchase agreements are not recorded.", "Escrow has no approval role over the parties' contract terms."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["net comparison"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-08-008$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 8, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 3, $lp$A listing agent tells a buyer's agent there are three competing offers when in fact there are none. This is:$lp$, $lp$["Acceptable negotiation strategy", "Fraud, because it is a false statement of material fact intended to induce reliance", "Acceptable if the seller authorized it", "Acceptable if offers are expected soon"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Misstating the competitive landscape is not strategy; it is a false factual representation.", "Correct. Fabricating competing offers to induce a higher bid is fraud, exposing the agent to civil liability and license discipline — a practice agents rationalize but cannot defend.", "A seller cannot authorize the agent to defraud a third party.", "Anticipated offers are not existing offers, and representing them as such is false."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["highest and best"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-08-009$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 8, $lp$contracts$lp$, 2, $lp$An escalation clause automatically raises a buyer's offer above competing bids up to a cap. Its principal drawback is that it:$lp$, $lp$["Is prohibited in California", "Reveals the buyer's maximum price to the seller", "Waives the appraisal contingency", "Cannot be used with financed offers"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Escalation clauses are not prohibited, though acceptance varies by market and brokerage policy.", "Correct. The cap discloses the buyer's ceiling, and disputes can arise over proof of the competing offer — which is why many brokerages discourage them.", "An escalation clause addresses price, not appraisal protection.", "They may be used with financed offers, subject to appraisal and lending realities."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["escalation clause"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-08-010$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 8, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$When presenting multiple offers to a seller, the agent should describe each offer's:$lp$, $lp$["Buyers' family circumstances and background", "Price, net proceeds, financing strength, contingencies, and timeline", "Buyers' apparent motivation and personal story", "Buyers' occupations and community ties"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Describing buyers' family circumstances injects protected characteristics into the decision.", "Correct. Terms and certainty of closing are the lawful bases for the seller's decision, best presented as a net comparison rather than a stack of documents.", "Personal narratives about buyers create the same exposure as buyer letters.", "Occupation and community ties can function as proxies for protected characteristics."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["net comparison", "buyer letter"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-08-011$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 8, $lp$contracts$lp$, 2, $lp$A backup offer is one that:$lp$, $lp$["Automatically takes effect if the primary contract falls through", "Requires the seller to cancel the primary contract", "Gives the backup buyer a right to match other offers", "Replaces the primary offer after 30 days"]$lp$::jsonb, 0, $lp$["Correct. A backup offer states its numbered position and springs into effect automatically if the primary contract is cancelled.", "A backup gives the seller security; it does not force cancellation of the primary.", "Matching rights describe a right of first refusal, a different instrument.", "No automatic replacement occurs on a time schedule."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["backup offer"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-08-012$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 8, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$A buyer's deposit check accompanies an offer with instructions to hold it uncashed until acceptance. The seller accepts Monday. The broker must place the funds appropriately:$lp$, $lp$["The day the check was received", "Within three business days of acceptance", "Within ten calendar days of acceptance", "Whenever escrow requests them"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["A properly disclosed instruction to hold the check pending acceptance postpones the deposit obligation.", "Correct. Trust funds go to escrow, a trust account, or the principal within three business days, and when the check is properly held pending acceptance the clock runs from acceptance.", "Ten days exceeds the regulatory window.", "The deadline is set by regulation, not by escrow's timing."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["proof of funds"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-08-013$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 8, $lp$contracts$lp$, 3, $lp$A cash offer nets a seller $4,750 less than the highest-netting financed offer but closes in 14 days with no contingencies. The agent should:$lp$, $lp$["Recommend the cash offer, since certainty is always worth more", "Present the comparison and let the seller weigh certainty against the $4,750", "Recommend the financed offer, since it nets more", "Decline to present the cash offer"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Certainty has value, but how much is a judgment belonging to the seller, not the agent.", "Correct. The agent's job is to present net proceeds, contingency exposure, financing strength, and timeline clearly; whether $4,750 is worth the certainty is the seller's decision.", "Net proceeds alone ignore the risk each offer carries.", "All offers must be presented."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["net comparison"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-08-014$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 8, $lp$contracts$lp$, 2, $lp$Beyond price, the factor sellers most often weigh when comparing offers is:$lp$, $lp$["The buyer's personal likability", "Certainty of closing", "The buyer's agent's brokerage", "The number of pages in the offer"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Evaluating buyers personally invites fair housing exposure and is not the seller's legitimate concern.", "Correct. A seller comparing offers is comparing certainty of closing at a given number — which is why financing strength, deposit size, contingency structure, and timeline matter alongside price.", "Brokerage identity is not a meaningful measure of an offer's strength.", "Length has no bearing on an offer's quality."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["net comparison", "proof of funds"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-08-015$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 8, $lp$contracts$lp$, 2, $lp$A seller with a contingent contract wants to keep marketing and require the buyer to perform or release if a better offer arrives. The provision that permits this is a:$lp$, $lp$["Kick-out clause", "Escalation clause", "Safety clause", "Subordination clause"]$lp$::jsonb, 0, $lp$["Correct. A kick-out clause lets the seller continue marketing and require a contingent buyer to remove their contingency or release the contract.", "An escalation clause automatically raises a buyer's price above competing bids.", "A safety clause protects a broker's commission after a listing expires.", "Subordination changes lien priority."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["kick-out clause"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-08-016$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 8, $lp$contracts$lp$, 2, $lp$Asking all offerors for 'highest and best' carries the risk that:$lp$, $lp$["It is prohibited in California", "Some strong buyers withdraw rather than bid against themselves", "It automatically binds the seller to the highest response", "It voids all existing offers"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["The practice is lawful and common.", "Correct. Some well-qualified buyers decline to participate, so the seller should be advised of that trade-off before the request goes out.", "The seller remains free to accept, counter, or reject any response.", "Existing offers remain outstanding unless withdrawn or expired."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["highest and best"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-08-017$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 8, $lp$contracts$lp$, 2, $lp$A buyer's agent should advise a buyer considering waiving the appraisal contingency to first:$lp$, $lp$["Increase the offer price further", "Calculate the cash required if the property appraises below the offer", "Request a longer escrow", "Switch to an FHA loan"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Raising the price increases the potential gap rather than addressing it.", "Correct. Quantify the exposure — if it appraises $50,000 low, the buyer covers that in cash — and confirm the buyer both has the funds and is willing to spend them.", "Escrow length does not address a valuation shortfall.", "Loan type does not eliminate the requirement to cover a gap between price and value."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["appraisal gap"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-08-018$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 8, $lp$contracts$lp$, 2, $lp$The clause making every stated deadline in the purchase agreement material is:$lp$, $lp$["The liquidated damages clause", "'Time is of the essence'", "The arbitration clause", "The escalation clause"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Liquidated damages fix an amount payable on breach; they do not make deadlines strict.", "Correct. 'Time is of the essence' makes each stated date material, so late performance is a breach rather than a delay — the reason every deadline must be calendared at acceptance.", "Arbitration governs dispute resolution.", "An escalation clause governs price adjustment among competing offers."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["net comparison"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-08-019$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 8, $lp$contracts$lp$, 2, $lp$When a buyer waives a contingency against the agent's recommendation, the agent should:$lp$, $lp$["Refuse to proceed with the transaction", "Confirm the advice given, and the buyer's contrary election, in writing the same day", "Note it verbally to the listing agent", "Retain the contingency without telling the buyer"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["The buyer is entitled to make this lawful choice; refusing to proceed abandons the client.", "Correct. A same-day written confirmation of what was advised and what the buyer elected is the agent's file when a problem surfaces after closing.", "Informing the opposing agent neither documents the advice nor protects the buyer.", "Altering the buyer's instructions without authorization breaches the duty of obedience."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["appraisal gap"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-08-020$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 8, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$A listing agent may disclose the terms of one buyer's offer to a competing buyer:$lp$, $lp$["Freely, since offers are not confidential", "Only with the seller's authorization", "Never, under any circumstances", "Only after the seller accepts another offer"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Offer terms belong to the seller's confidential negotiating position, not to the agent's discretion.", "Correct. Disclosure of offer terms requires the seller's authorization; the seller decides whether revealing terms serves their interest.", "With the seller's authorization such disclosure is permitted and is sometimes strategically useful.", "Post-acceptance timing does not determine whether disclosure was authorized."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["highest and best"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-09-001$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 9, $lp$contracts$lp$, 3, $lp$Under California's active removal regime, when a buyer's contingency deadline passes without a written removal:$lp$, $lp$["The contingency is automatically removed", "The contingency remains in effect and the seller may deliver a Notice to Buyer to Perform", "The contract is automatically cancelled", "The seller may immediately keep the deposit"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Passive waiver is not the California residential default; contingencies persist until removed in writing.", "Correct. The contingency survives the deadline, and the seller's remedy is a written Notice to Buyer to Perform that starts a cure period before cancellation is permitted.", "No automatic cancellation occurs, and a seller who cancels on the deadline may be in breach.", "Deposit release requires a valid cancellation and mutual written instructions."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["active removal", "Notice to Buyer to Perform"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-09-002$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 9, $lp$contracts$lp$, 3, $lp$A listing agent advises the seller to cancel immediately when the buyer misses the investigation deadline, with no notice delivered. This advice is:$lp$, $lp$["Correct, since time is of the essence", "Incorrect, and acting on it could place the seller in breach", "Correct if a backup offer exists", "Correct if the buyer was reminded verbally"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Time is of the essence makes deadlines material but does not eliminate the notice-and-cure procedure.", "Correct. Under active removal the seller must deliver a Notice to Buyer to Perform and let the cure period run; unilateral cancellation on the deadline is a wrongful cancellation.", "The presence of a backup offer does not change the required procedure.", "A verbal reminder is not the written notice the contract requires."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["Notice to Buyer to Perform", "active removal"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-09-003$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 9, $lp$transfer$lp$, 2, $lp$Both parties claim the deposit after a failed transaction. Escrow will:$lp$, $lp$["Release it to the party with the stronger claim", "Hold it until both parties sign mutual cancellation instructions or a court orders release", "Split it evenly", "Return it to the buyer automatically"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Escrow does not adjudicate the parties' rights.", "Correct. Escrow acts only on mutual written instructions; absent agreement it holds the funds and may interplead them — which is why an agent should never promise a client an automatic refund.", "Escrow has no authority to compromise the parties' claims.", "Deposits are not automatically refundable."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["mutual cancellation instructions"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-09-004$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 9, $lp$contracts$lp$, 3, $lp$An offer is accepted Tuesday, September 8, with a 17-day investigation contingency counted from the day after acceptance. Written removal is due no later than:$lp$, $lp$["September 24", "September 25", "September 26", "September 29"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["September 24 counts only 16 days from September 9.", "Correct. Day one is September 9; 17 days runs through September 25 (September 9 plus 16 additional days).", "September 26 counts 18 days.", "September 29 corresponds to a 21-day contingency such as loan or appraisal."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["time is of the essence"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-09-005$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 9, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$Inspections should be scheduled:$lp$, $lp$["In the final days of the investigation period", "Within the first few days after acceptance", "After the appraisal is completed", "Only if the buyer requests them"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Scheduling late leaves no room to review reports or negotiate repairs before the deadline.", "Correct. Inspectors book out and the period is short, so scheduling in the first days leaves time to review findings and negotiate before the contingency expires.", "The appraisal is a lender function on a separate track and does not gate inspections.", "The agent should recommend inspections proactively rather than waiting to be asked."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["repair request / request for repairs"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-09-006$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 9, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 3, $lp$A buyer's inspection reveals a cracked sewer lateral and the buyer cancels within their contingency. When the seller relists, the seller:$lp$, $lp$["Has no disclosure obligation because that escrow was cancelled", "Must disclose the defect to subsequent buyers, because the seller now has actual knowledge", "Must disclose only if the next buyer asks", "May wait until an offer is accepted to disclose"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Cancellation does not erase the seller's knowledge.", "Correct. The seller now has actual knowledge of a material defect and must disclose it to every subsequent buyer — which is why agents should warn sellers of this before inspections occur.", "The disclosure duty is affirmative and does not depend on being asked.", "Delaying disclosure until after acceptance defeats its purpose and creates a termination right."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["sewer lateral inspection"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-09-007$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 9, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 3, $lp$Inspections reveal three material items totaling about $11,500 and 31 cosmetic items. The strongest repair request strategy is to:$lp$, $lp$["Submit all 34 items so nothing is waived", "Request the three material items, ideally as a credit, and waive the cosmetic list in writing", "Request only the least expensive item to seem reasonable", "Request a price reduction equal to all 34 items"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["A 34-item list invites the seller to reject the whole request as unserious, which is usually what happens.", "Correct. Focusing on material items — health, safety, structure, and systems — and expressly waiving the cosmetic list signals seriousness and makes the remaining request hard to dismiss. A credit also lets the buyer control contractor quality.", "Understating the request forfeits value the buyer is entitled to negotiate.", "Valuing cosmetic items into a price reduction produces a number the seller will not accept."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["repair request / request for repairs", "seller credit"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-09-008$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 9, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$An 'as-is' sale means the seller:$lp$, $lp$["Has no obligation to disclose known defects", "Has not agreed in advance to make repairs, though the buyer may still request them", "May refuse to allow inspections", "Is released from all liability after closing"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["As-is never waives the duty to disclose known material facts.", "Correct. As-is sets repair expectations; the buyer may still request repairs or credits, the seller may decline, and the buyer may still cancel within their contingency.", "Buyers retain their investigation rights in an as-is sale.", "Liability for concealment or misrepresentation survives closing."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["repair request / request for repairs"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-09-009$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 9, $lp$transfer$lp$, 2, $lp$A judgment lien appearing on the preliminary report against someone with the seller's name should be addressed:$lp$, $lp$["In the final week before closing", "On day one, because curative work takes time", "Only if the buyer's lender objects", "By ignoring it, since name variances are common"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Waiting until the final week converts a solvable issue into a missed closing.", "Correct. Read the preliminary report immediately and start curative work — a name variance resolved by a statement of information takes days if begun early and kills the closing if begun late.", "The lender will object, but the agent should not wait for that to act.", "Name variances are common and routinely resolved, but only if someone begins the process."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["mutual cancellation instructions"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-09-010$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 9, $lp$contracts$lp$, 2, $lp$The clause that makes each stated deadline material, so that late performance is a breach rather than a delay, is:$lp$, $lp$["The liquidated damages clause", "'Time is of the essence'", "The active removal provision", "The arbitration clause"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Liquidated damages fix an amount payable on breach rather than making deadlines strict.", "Correct. 'Time is of the essence' makes every stated date material, which is why the calendar built on the day of acceptance is the highest-value hour in the transaction.", "Active removal governs how contingencies are removed, not the materiality of dates.", "Arbitration governs dispute resolution."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["time is of the essence"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-09-011$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 9, $lp$transfer$lp$, 2, $lp$Escrow may act on instructions that are:$lp$, $lp$["Given verbally by either party", "Mutual and in writing", "Given by the listing agent alone", "Implied by the purchase contract"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Verbal instructions are not effective; escrow requires writing.", "Correct. As a neutral depository serving both parties, escrow acts only on mutual written instructions and cannot advise or favor either side.", "An agent cannot instruct escrow on behalf of both parties.", "The contract informs escrow's instructions but does not replace signed mutual instructions."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["mutual cancellation instructions"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-09-012$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 9, $lp$financing$lp$, 2, $lp$The most common cause of a delayed closing attributable to the buyer is:$lp$, $lp$["Choosing the wrong escrow company", "Slow delivery of documents the lender requested", "Scheduling the final walk-through late", "Requesting a different closing date"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Escrow selection rarely drives timing problems.", "Correct. A buyer who takes days to send a bank statement or tax return is the single most common source of delay, which is why agents should monitor underwriting status weekly.", "The walk-through occurs near closing and is easily scheduled.", "A requested date change is a negotiated term rather than an unplanned delay."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["active removal"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-09-013$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 9, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$A buyer purchasing a home in a very high fire hazard severity zone should obtain written insurance quotes:$lp$, $lp$["After the loan is approved", "In the first days of the contingency period", "At the final walk-through", "After closing"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Loan approval depends in part on insurability, so waiting inverts the sequence.", "Correct. Insurance availability and cost in mapped fire zones can prevent closing entirely, so quotes belong at the start of the contingency period rather than the end.", "The walk-through is days before closing — far too late to discover a coverage problem.", "A buyer who cannot obtain coverage cannot close, so this must be resolved beforehand."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["repair request / request for repairs"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-09-014$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 9, $lp$contracts$lp$, 2, $lp$The final walk-through is properly used to:$lp$, $lp$["Renegotiate price based on new observations", "Verify the property's condition and that agreed repairs were completed", "Conduct the buyer's first inspection", "Perform the appraisal"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["The walk-through is a verification step, not a renegotiation opportunity.", "Correct. Its purpose is confirming the property is in the agreed condition and that negotiated repairs were performed.", "Inspections belong in the investigation period, not days before closing.", "The appraisal is a lender-ordered valuation performed by an appraiser."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["final walk-through"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-09-015$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 9, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 3, $lp$A seller credit in lieu of repairs is often preferable to seller-performed repairs because:$lp$, $lp$["Credits are unlimited in amount", "The buyer controls contractor selection and work quality, and the seller avoids managing the work", "Credits do not need to be disclosed to the lender", "Credits eliminate the seller's disclosure obligations"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Lenders limit how much credit may be applied, which must be checked before structuring one.", "Correct. The buyer chooses the contractor and controls quality while the seller avoids coordinating repairs — usually a better outcome for both.", "Credits appear on the settlement statement and are visible to the lender.", "Disclosure obligations regarding known defects are unaffected by how repairs are handled."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["seller credit", "repair request / request for repairs"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-09-016$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 9, $lp$contracts$lp$, 2, $lp$A buyer's agent realizing a contingency deadline has just passed should:$lp$, $lp$["Say nothing and hope the seller does not notice", "Contact the listing agent immediately and either deliver the removal or request a written extension", "Deliver a Notice to Seller to Perform", "Instruct escrow to extend the date"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Silence is what converts an administrative slip into a cancellation.", "Correct. Prompt communication with either a written removal or a requested extension resolves the exposure before the seller starts the notice clock.", "A Notice to Seller to Perform addresses the seller's failures, not the buyer's.", "Escrow cannot amend contract deadlines; only the parties can, in writing."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["Notice to Buyer to Perform", "active removal"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-09-017$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 9, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$The most useful early warning that an escrow is failing is typically:$lp$, $lp$["A missed closing date", "A client who stops responding to communication", "The appraisal being ordered", "The preliminary report arriving"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["By the closing date the failure has already occurred.", "Correct. Almost every escrow that dies signals a week or more in advance, and communication going quiet is the most reliable early indicator — call rather than text.", "Ordering the appraisal is a routine step.", "The preliminary report arriving is expected and routine."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["active removal"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-09-018$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 9, $lp$contracts$lp$, 2, $lp$Building the transaction calendar backward from contingency deadlines is valuable primarily because it:$lp$, $lp$["Shortens the escrow period", "Reveals when inspections and orders must be initiated for the deadlines to be achievable", "Eliminates the need for a Notice to Perform", "Guarantees the loan will fund on time"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Backward planning does not change the contractual escrow length.", "Correct. The deadline dates are arithmetic; determining that inspections must be scheduled in the first three days for a day-25 deadline to be met is the judgment that makes the timeline real.", "Notices remain available regardless of planning quality.", "No planning guarantees lender performance, though it surfaces problems earlier."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["time is of the essence"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-10-001$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 10, $lp$financing$lp$, 2, $lp$Which milestone most strongly indicates that lender conditions have been completed and the loan is ready for closing steps?$lp$, $lp$["Prequalification", "Preapproval", "Clear to close", "A buyer's verbal statement"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["A prequalification is an early estimate and is not a completed loan review.", "A preapproval can still contain borrower and property conditions.", "Correct. A clear to close follows satisfaction of required underwriting conditions, although final process steps still remain.", "A buyer cannot independently determine that a lender has cleared a file."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["clear to close", "preapproval"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-10-002$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 10, $lp$financing$lp$, 2, $lp$A $640,000 loan on an $800,000 purchase price has an LTV of:$lp$, $lp$["20%", "64%", "80%", "125%"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["Twenty percent is the down-payment percentage in this example, not the LTV.", "Dividing 640,000 by one million would produce 64%, but one million is not the value here.", "Correct. $640,000 ÷ $800,000 = 0.80, or 80%.", "An LTV above 100% would mean the loan exceeds the stated value."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["loan-to-value ratio (LTV)"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-10-003$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 10, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 3, $lp$A buyer asks the agent whether a particular rate is the best loan for them. The agent should first:$lp$, $lp$["Promise the rate is excellent because the lender is preferred", "Explain that the lender should compare terms and costs with the buyer", "Tell the buyer to waive the loan contingency", "Choose the loan product based on the listing agent's preference"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["An agent should not make an unsupported loan recommendation or guarantee.", "Correct. The agent may help the buyer frame questions, but the lender must explain product terms and the buyer makes the financing decision.", "A contingency decision should follow informed analysis, not replace it.", "The listing side does not choose the buyer's financing."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["Loan Estimate", "preapproval"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-10-004$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 10, $lp$financing$lp$, 3, $lp$A home is under contract for $900,000. The appraisal is $875,000 and the lender will lend 80% of appraised value. What is the maximum stated loan amount?$lp$, $lp$["$700,000", "$720,000", "$875,000", "$900,000"]$lp$::jsonb, 0, $lp$["Correct. 80% × $875,000 equals $700,000.", "$720,000 is 80% of the contract price, not the lower appraised value.", "The appraisal is not itself the loan amount.", "The contract price is not the lender's 80% maximum in this fact pattern."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["appraisal gap", "loan-to-value ratio (LTV)"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-10-005$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 10, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$Which buyer action should prompt an immediate conversation with the lender before closing?$lp$, $lp$["Opening a new auto loan", "Reviewing the preliminary report", "Scheduling a final walk-through", "Asking escrow for signing times"]$lp$::jsonb, 0, $lp$["Correct. New debt can affect credit and debt-to-income underwriting; the lender should assess it before the buyer acts.", "Reviewing title is important but does not itself change borrower qualification.", "A final walk-through is a normal transaction activity.", "Signing logistics do not normally affect loan qualification."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["underwriting"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-10-006$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 10, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 3, $lp$A lender tells an agent that underwriting needs another document. The most appropriate update to the listing side is:$lp$, $lp$["The buyer will certainly close on time", "The lender needs an additional document and an updated status will follow by a stated time", "The buyer's income and account balances in detail", "Nothing; status updates are never appropriate"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["No one should promise an outcome before the lender confirms it.", "Correct. This is factual, protects private information, and gives the other side a reliable next update.", "Detailed financial information is private and unnecessary for transaction coordination.", "Appropriate milestone communication can prevent avoidable escalation."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["underwriting"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-10-007$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 10, $lp$financing$lp$, 2, $lp$A seller credit is best described as:$lp$, $lp$["Cash the buyer may always receive after closing", "A contribution that can apply only as permitted by the contract and loan program", "A replacement for buyer qualification", "A guarantee that an appraisal will meet contract price"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Unused credit does not automatically become cash to the buyer.", "Correct. Allowable use and limits depend on the contract and loan program, which the lender confirms.", "A credit does not replace income, assets, credit, or underwriting.", "An appraisal is independent of a seller-credit request."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["seller credit"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-10-008$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 10, $lp$financing$lp$, 2, $lp$Which form is intended to help a borrower understand estimated loan terms and costs after application in a covered consumer mortgage transaction?$lp$, $lp$["Transfer Disclosure Statement", "Loan Estimate", "Preliminary report", "Grant deed"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["A TDS concerns property condition disclosures, not loan terms.", "Correct. The Loan Estimate summarizes estimated loan terms and costs.", "A preliminary report identifies title matters rather than lender pricing.", "A grant deed transfers a real-property interest."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["Loan Estimate"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-10-009$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 10, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 3, $lp$A buyer is purchasing a condominium. Which financing issue should the agent raise early with the lender?$lp$, $lp$["Whether project review or property eligibility requirements apply", "Whether the buyer should ignore HOA documents", "Whether the appraiser can be pressured to support value", "Whether title is unnecessary because the buyer has a loan"]$lp$::jsonb, 0, $lp$["Correct. Condominium projects can involve lender review and should be discussed before a deadline creates pressure.", "HOA documents can be material to both buyer due diligence and lender review.", "Appraiser independence is important; pressure is improper.", "Title review remains important in financed transactions."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["underwriting"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-10-010$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 10, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 3, $lp$An email says escrow's wiring instructions have changed and asks the buyer to send funds immediately. The safest response is to:$lp$, $lp$["Use the new instructions because the email looks professional", "Reply to the email asking for confirmation", "Verify instructions through a known independently confirmed escrow phone number", "Send half the funds first"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["Email appearance is not reliable protection against wire fraud.", "Replying can still communicate with a compromised account.", "Correct. Independently verifying through a known phone number is the appropriate fraud-control step.", "Sending a partial wire still risks loss and does not verify instructions."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["wire fraud"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-10-011$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 10, $lp$financing$lp$, 2, $lp$A buyer plans to borrow $500,000 on a $625,000 purchase. Before closing costs, the planned down payment is:$lp$, $lp$["$100,000", "$125,000", "$500,000", "$625,000"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["$100,000 would leave a $525,000 loan.", "Correct. $625,000 − $500,000 = $125,000.", "$500,000 is the planned loan amount.", "$625,000 is the full purchase price."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["down payment"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-10-012$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 10, $lp$financing$lp$, 3, $lp$Which statement about a preapproval is most accurate?$lp$, $lp$["It always means all borrower and property conditions have been satisfied", "It has the identical scope at every lender", "It can reduce uncertainty but should be read for remaining conditions and verification", "It permits the agent to guarantee closing"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["Property, income, asset, appraisal, and other conditions may remain.", "Lenders do not necessarily use the label for the same review depth.", "Correct. Ask what was verified, what remains, and whether property type or timing conditions apply.", "Only the lender can determine loan approval; an agent cannot guarantee closing."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["preapproval", "underwriting"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-10-013$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 10, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 3, $lp$Which referral practice is most consistent with fair and consumer-focused financing service?$lp$, $lp$["Directing buyers to a lender based on a protected characteristic", "Giving every buyer the same neutral referral criteria and preserving lender choice", "Requiring use of the brokerage's preferred lender", "Withholding lender options until after offer acceptance"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Protected characteristics are not a lawful basis for financing referrals.", "Correct. Neutral criteria, consistent service, and buyer choice reduce discrimination and conflict risk.", "A preferred lender list is not a mandate.", "Buyers benefit from understanding financing options before they write an offer."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["fair lending"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-10-014$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 10, $lp$financing$lp$, 2, $lp$Which item is not necessarily included in a simple PITI estimate?$lp$, $lp$["Principal", "Interest", "Homeowners-association dues", "Property taxes"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["The P in PITI includes principal.", "The I in PITI includes interest.", "Correct. HOA dues may be a real housing cost, but they are not one of the four PITI components.", "The T in PITI includes property taxes."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["PITI"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-10-015$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 10, $lp$contracts$lp$, 3, $lp$A low appraisal occurs while the buyer's appraisal contingency remains in effect. Which is the best agent action?$lp$, $lp$["Tell the buyer the seller must reduce price", "Review the contract, confirm lender options, and explain the buyer's available transaction paths", "Assure the seller that the appraisal can be changed by request", "Ignore the appraisal until closing"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["The seller's response depends on negotiation and contract terms; it is not automatic.", "Correct. Contract language, lender options, buyer funds, and timing determine the practical paths.", "An appraisal reconsideration has a lender process and is not guaranteed.", "Ignoring the issue can cause a preventable closing failure."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["appraisal gap", "contingency"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-10-016$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 10, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$Why should insurance be investigated early for a property in a higher-risk fire area?$lp$, $lp$["Insurance is unrelated to financing", "Availability and cost can affect closing and buyer qualification", "The seller's agent can bind the buyer's policy", "It removes the need for a home inspection"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Lenders commonly require acceptable insurance as a closing condition.", "Correct. Insurance availability, terms, and cost can affect whether and how the buyer closes.", "An agent generally cannot bind an insurance policy for a buyer.", "Insurance and inspections serve different purposes."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["underwriting"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-10-017$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 10, $lp$financing$lp$, 2, $lp$The Closing Disclosure is designed primarily to show:$lp$, $lp$["The seller's marketing plan", "Final loan terms and costs", "The property's legal description only", "The buyer's inspection findings"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Marketing is unrelated to mortgage disclosures.", "Correct. The Closing Disclosure presents final loan terms and costs for covered transactions.", "A legal description may appear in other transaction documents but is not the form's primary purpose.", "Inspection findings are not the purpose of a Closing Disclosure."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["Closing Disclosure"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-10-018$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 10, $lp$financing$lp$, 3, $lp$A buyer makes a large cash deposit shortly before underwriting. The agent should advise the buyer to:$lp$, $lp$["Hide the deposit by moving it again", "Promptly ask the lender what source documentation is needed", "Cancel the transaction immediately", "Tell the seller all details of the deposit"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Moving or hiding funds can worsen underwriting and fraud concerns.", "Correct. The lender can explain documentation requirements; prompt, accurate response protects the timeline.", "The issue may be resolvable with documentation and does not automatically require cancellation.", "The buyer's financial detail is private and generally unnecessary for the seller."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["underwriting"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-11-001$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 11, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$A licensee sees a broad ceiling stain during a listing walk-through. The most appropriate first response is to:$lp$, $lp$["Assure buyers it is an old repaired leak", "Describe the visible condition, ask for relevant facts, and recommend appropriate investigation", "Remove it from the seller's disclosure", "Diagnose the roof as defective"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["An unsupported assurance can mislead a buyer.", "Correct. The licensee should observe, seek accurate information, disclose material facts, and recommend qualified evaluation rather than diagnose.", "A licensee should not conceal a material observation.", "Diagnosis exceeds the ordinary agent role."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["visual inspection", "material fact"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-11-002$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 11, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 3, $lp$Which statement best describes a licensee's visual inspection duty?$lp$, $lp$["It requires a technical home inspection of every system", "It applies only when the seller asks for it", "It is a reasonably competent and diligent observation of accessible areas, not a diagnosis", "It permits the agent to open sealed walls if a defect is suspected"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["A specialist home inspection is beyond the ordinary visual-inspection scope.", "The duty is not optional merely because a seller prefers less scrutiny.", "Correct. The duty concerns accessible areas and observable material facts; it does not make the agent a diagnostician.", "Opening sealed areas would exceed ordinary visual observation and can cause damage."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["visual inspection"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-11-003$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 11, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$A seller asks the listing agent not to mention a known cracked sewer lateral. The agent should:$lp$, $lp$["Follow the seller's instruction because the seller owns the property", "Promise to keep it confidential", "Escalate the issue and not participate in concealment of a material fact", "Wait until a buyer asks directly"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["Ownership does not authorize concealment of a material fact in a transaction.", "Confidentiality does not protect a client instruction to conceal material information.", "Correct. The broker should be involved and the agent cannot participate in concealment.", "A disclosure duty is not limited to direct buyer questions."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["material fact", "escalation"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-11-004$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 11, $lp$agency$lp$, 3, $lp$Which transaction note is most useful for a later file review?$lp$, $lp$["Buyer understands everything", "Agent handled it", "June 3: sent buyer inspection report; recommended roofing evaluation; buyer acknowledged receipt", "No issues"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["This conclusion is vague and difficult to prove.", "This statement does not identify facts, actions, or timing.", "Correct. It identifies the material document, specific recommendation, date, and acknowledgment.", "A bare conclusion is not a useful transaction record."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["risk management"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-11-005$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 11, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 3, $lp$An email says escrow's wire instructions changed. What should the agent advise?$lp$, $lp$["Use the instructions because the email contains the escrow logo", "Reply to the sender for confirmation", "Verify through a known independently confirmed escrow telephone number", "Send a test wire first"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["Logos can be copied in fraudulent messages.", "A compromised account can answer the reply.", "Correct. Independent verification through a known number is the core wire-fraud control.", "A test wire can still be stolen and does not validate the instructions."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["wire fraud"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-11-006$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 11, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$An agent observes a horizontal crack in a garage wall. The safest statement is:$lp$, $lp$["It is definitely foundation failure", "It is normal settlement and nothing to worry about", "There is a visible crack; a qualified professional should evaluate cause and significance", "The buyer cannot inspect it"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["The agent cannot diagnose without appropriate expertise.", "A reassuring diagnosis is still an unsupported diagnosis.", "Correct. It accurately states the observation and recommends appropriate expert evaluation.", "A buyer may investigate property conditions within applicable contract rights."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["scope of competence"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-11-007$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 11, $lp$agency$lp$, 2, $lp$Which information is normally confidential client information?$lp$, $lp$["A buyer's maximum price they have not authorized sharing", "A recorded property easement", "The listing price shown in MLS", "A public tax assessment"]$lp$::jsonb, 0, $lp$["Correct. A buyer's undisclosed maximum price is confidential and must not be shared without authorization.", "Recorded easements are generally public title information.", "A public listing price is not confidential client information.", "Tax assessments are generally public records."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["confidential information"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-11-008$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 11, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 3, $lp$A buyer's inspection finds a material defect, then the buyer cancels. On relisting, the seller should:$lp$, $lp$["Treat the prior inspection as irrelevant because the transaction ended", "Disclose known material information to later buyers as appropriate", "Delete every record of the finding", "Tell later buyers only if they hire the same inspector"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Cancellation does not erase a seller's actual knowledge.", "Correct. A known material condition remains relevant to subsequent transaction disclosures.", "Deleting records does not eliminate knowledge or duty.", "Disclosure is not limited to buyers using a particular inspector."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["material fact"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-11-009$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 11, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$Which advertising statement is riskiest without verification?$lp$, $lp$["A bright living room", "New roof", "A welcoming entry", "A charming patio"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["This is generally subjective promotional language.", "Correct. “New roof” is a verifiable factual claim and should be confirmed before advertising.", "This is generally subjective promotional language.", "This is generally subjective promotional language."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["puffery"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-11-010$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 11, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 3, $lp$A client asks an agent to interpret a disputed contract clause. The agent should first:$lp$, $lp$["Give a legal opinion based on prior transactions", "Refer the issue to the broker and appropriate legal counsel", "Rewrite the clause after acceptance", "Tell the client contract language never matters"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Prior experience does not authorize legal interpretation.", "Correct. Ambiguous or disputed contract interpretation should be escalated to the broker and, where appropriate, counsel.", "Changing a contract requires authorized agreement and may itself create risk.", "Contract language can materially determine rights and obligations."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["escalation", "scope of competence"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-11-011$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 11, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$Risk management is best described as:$lp$, $lp$["Avoiding all difficult conversations", "Identifying, communicating, documenting, and mitigating transaction risk", "Selling only newly built homes", "Buying insurance instead of using judgment"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Avoidance can create greater harm.", "Correct. Good risk management is a disciplined process that protects consumers and the transaction.", "Every property type involves risk management.", "Insurance may be part of a plan but does not replace professional conduct."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["risk management"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-11-012$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 11, $lp$agency$lp$, 3, $lp$An open-house visitor tells the listing agent their top price. The agent should:$lp$, $lp$["Immediately tell the seller the number", "Treat it as confidential if an agency relationship or confidentiality duty applies and clarify representation", "Post it in the team chat", "Promise the visitor representation without disclosure"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Disclosure depends on the actual agency relationship and confidentiality duties; it is not automatic.", "Correct. Clarifying representation and preserving any duty of confidentiality is the professional response.", "A team chat can improperly spread private information.", "Agency should be disclosed and documented, not casually promised."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["confidential information", "agency"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-11-013$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 11, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$Why is an “as-is” sale not a defense to concealment?$lp$, $lp$["As-is eliminates every buyer investigation right", "As-is does not eliminate duties to disclose known material facts", "As-is makes all statements true", "As-is transfers the broker's license to the buyer"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Buyers may still retain investigation and contractual rights.", "Correct. Property is sold in its present condition, but known material facts may still require disclosure.", "The phrase cannot cure a false statement.", "A real estate license is unrelated to property condition."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["material fact"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-11-014$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 11, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$Which action best protects client privacy?$lp$, $lp$["Sending tax returns in a group text", "Sharing only necessary information through approved transaction channels", "Discussing loan balances at an open house", "Using a personal disappearing-message app for disclosures"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Group texts are not appropriate for sensitive financial documents.", "Correct. Need-to-know information through approved secure channels reduces privacy and recordkeeping risk.", "Open houses are public settings.", "Disappearing messages impair the transaction record."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["privacy"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-11-015$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 11, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 3, $lp$A listing uses AI-generated virtual staging that hides a large wall crack. This is risky because it:$lp$, $lp$["Can conceal a material property condition", "Always improves disclosure quality", "Eliminates the need for photographs", "Makes the agent an appraiser"]$lp$::jsonb, 0, $lp$["Correct. Altered imagery that conceals a material condition can mislead consumers.", "Improvement is not guaranteed and concealment is contrary to accurate marketing.", "Photographs and disclosures remain separate obligations.", "Virtual staging does not make an agent an appraiser."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["material fact", "advertising"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-11-016$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 11, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 3, $lp$A lender delay threatens a contingency deadline. The agent should:$lp$, $lp$["Promise the loan will close anyway", "Communicate factual status, coordinate with buyer and lender, and address the deadline promptly", "Wait until the deadline expires", "Tell the seller the buyer's full financial history"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["An agent should not guarantee lender performance.", "Correct. Accurate status, prompt coordination, and attention to the contract deadline are appropriate.", "Waiting can destroy available options.", "Financial history is private and unnecessary for a status update."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["risk management", "contingency"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-12-001$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 12, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$A buyer gives a salesperson a deposit check at an open house. The safest next action is to:$lp$, $lp$["Keep it in a desk", "Follow the broker's approved receipt and prompt-delivery procedure", "Deposit it personally", "Promise it has cleared"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["A desk is not an approved controlled holding process.", "Correct. Client funds require the responsible broker's approved receipt and delivery workflow.", "A salesperson must not place client funds in a personal account.", "Receipt is not the same as clearance."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["trust fund", "chain of custody"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-12-002$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 12, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$Commingling is:$lp$, $lp$["Neutral escrow following instructions", "Improperly mixing trust money with other funds", "A beneficiary ledger", "A deposit receipt"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Neutral handling is distinct from commingling.", "Correct. Commingling improperly mixes funds that must remain segregated.", "A ledger is a control.", "A receipt documents receipt."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["commingling"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-12-003$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 12, $lp$contracts$lp$, 3, $lp$A buyer cancels and asks the agent to release a disputed deposit. The seller disagrees. The agent should:$lp$, $lp$["Tell escrow to pay buyer", "Tell escrow to pay seller", "Preserve communications, escalate, and use the authorized release process", "Split the funds"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["Cancellation alone does not authorize an agent to direct release.", "Ownership does not resolve a disputed instruction.", "Correct. A dispute requires the authorized holder and proper written authority or other lawful direction.", "An agent cannot invent a disbursement."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["neutral escrow holder", "trust fund"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-12-004$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 12, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$Why is a beneficiary ledger valuable?$lp$, $lp$["It identifies funds held for each transaction", "It replaces a bank statement", "It pays office expenses", "It eliminates instructions"]$lp$::jsonb, 0, $lp$["Correct. It connects the total balance to people and transactions entitled to funds.", "Bank statements and detailed records serve different purposes.", "Client funds cannot pay office expenses.", "Instructions remain important."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["beneficiary ledger"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-12-005$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 12, $lp$agency$lp$, 2, $lp$A client asks a salesperson to release escrow funds. The best response is:$lp$, $lp$["I will release them", "I cannot authorize release; I will send your written request to broker and escrow", "Writing is never needed", "Escrow always pays the buyer"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["A salesperson does not personally authorize disbursement.", "Correct. It accurately states the boundary and routes the request properly.", "Written authority is often essential.", "The outcome depends on authority and instructions."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["escalation", "neutral escrow holder"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-12-006$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 12, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 3, $lp$An email says escrow's wire destination changed. What is the best control?$lp$, $lp$["Trust the logo", "Use the number in the email", "Verify through a known independently confirmed escrow contact", "Send a test wire"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["Branding can be copied.", "A compromised account can answer.", "Correct. Independent verification through a trusted contact path reduces fraud risk.", "A test wire can still be lost."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["wire fraud"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-12-007$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 12, $lp$contracts$lp$, 2, $lp$Receiving a check and having it clear are:$lp$, $lp$["The same event", "Distinct events", "Proof the seller earned it", "Irrelevant"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Receipt and bank clearance happen at different times.", "Correct. Each event has different facts and records.", "Neither event alone determines entitlement.", "Both can be material facts."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["trust fund"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-12-008$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 12, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 3, $lp$Conversion of trust funds means:$lp$, $lp$["Authorized escrow deposit", "Unauthorized use of another's money", "Monthly reconciliation", "Office expense"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Authorized handling is not conversion.", "Correct. Conversion is unauthorized use of money held for another.", "Reconciliation is a control.", "Operating expenses should not use client funds."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["conversion"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-12-009$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 12, $lp$agency$lp$, 2, $lp$A missing deposit receipt should be handled by:$lp$, $lp$["Creating one from memory", "Ignoring it", "Promptly reporting and investigating under broker procedure", "Guaranteeing the money is safe"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["Recreating facts can worsen the record problem.", "Assumptions do not resolve it.", "Correct. Prompt escalation allows transparent confirmation and correction.", "The agent cannot make that guarantee."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["escalation", "chain of custody"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-12-010$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 12, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$Brokerage office-expense money belongs in:$lp$, $lp$["A client trust account", "The brokerage operating account", "A beneficiary ledger", "A salesperson account"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Trust accounts are for funds held for others.", "Correct. Earned business funds and expenses belong in the operating account.", "A ledger is not an account.", "Personal accounts are not brokerage accounts."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["trust account", "commingling"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-12-011$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 12, $lp$contracts$lp$, 3, $lp$A buyer's electronic deposit is rejected. The agent should:$lp$, $lp$["Assume it will process", "Communicate factual status, use verified instructions, and alert broker", "Waive the deadline", "Use a personal payment app"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["A rejection should be addressed.", "Correct. The agent should report the fact and use an approved process.", "An agent cannot waive a deadline alone.", "Personal apps create control risks."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["escalation", "trust fund"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-12-012$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 12, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$Reconciliation is:$lp$, $lp$["Comparing records to locate differences", "Writing offers", "Choosing an appraiser", "Advertising"]$lp$::jsonb, 0, $lp$["Correct. Reconciliation checks whether bank, trust, and detailed records agree.", "Offer drafting is unrelated.", "Appraisal is unrelated.", "Marketing is unrelated."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["reconciliation"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-12-013$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 12, $lp$agency$lp$, 3, $lp$Why avoid saying one way of holding funds is always safest?$lp$, $lp$["Explain verified roles and procedures instead", "Escrow cannot receive funds", "Trust accounts have no records", "Clients cannot ask"]$lp$::jsonb, 0, $lp$["Correct. Safety depends on verified roles, procedures, and facts.", "Escrow holders commonly receive funds.", "Trust accounts require records.", "Clients can ask for clear information."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["neutral escrow holder"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-12-014$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 12, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$Which record best shows a deposit handoff?$lp$, $lp$["A vague text", "A dated receipt and delivery or escrow confirmation", "A social post", "An unsigned promise"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Vague texts lack detail.", "Correct. Specific evidence creates a traceable chain of custody.", "Social posts are not transaction records.", "An unsigned promise may not establish an event."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["chain of custody"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-12-015$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 12, $lp$contracts$lp$, 3, $lp$A seller asks to use one buyer's deposit for another listing's repair. The agent should:$lp$, $lp$["Agree if repaid", "Refuse and escalate because funds cannot be repurposed without authority", "Transfer part", "Ask buyer later"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["A repayment promise is not authority.", "Correct. The request suggests conversion and requires immediate broker involvement.", "Partial unauthorized use remains unauthorized.", "Later consent cannot justify interim use."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["conversion", "trust fund"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-12-016$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 12, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$For exact trust-fund timing rules, rely on:$lp$, $lp$["Social media", "Current law and responsible broker procedure", "Old class notes alone", "Client preference"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Social summaries are not authoritative.", "Correct. Exact requirements must be verified against current authority and broker policy.", "Older notes may be outdated.", "Preference cannot override duties."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["trust account", "escalation"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-13-001$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 13, $lp$transfer$lp$, 2, $lp$A preliminary title report is best understood as:$lp$, $lp$["A guarantee that title has no issues", "A document identifying proposed coverage, requirements, and exceptions", "A legal opinion from the agent", "A substitute for escrow instructions"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["A report may list matters requiring attention.", "Correct. It identifies proposed coverage and matters relevant before a policy issues.", "Agents should not provide title legal opinions.", "Escrow instructions remain separate authority."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["preliminary title report"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-13-002$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 13, $lp$agency$lp$, 2, $lp$A buyer asks whether an easement means they can build a gate. The agent should:$lp$, $lp$["Promise it will be allowed", "Explain the legal effect personally", "Refer the question to title or qualified counsel", "Ignore the report"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["An unsupported promise can mislead.", "Legal interpretation exceeds the ordinary agent role.", "Correct. The agent can identify and route the issue without diagnosing legal rights.", "The report should be reviewed."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["easement", "escalation"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-13-003$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 13, $lp$contracts$lp$, 3, $lp$A party calls escrow and verbally directs release of disputed funds. Escrow should act based on:$lp$, $lp$["The loudest caller", "Authorized instructions and applicable requirements", "The agent's personal view", "The listing price"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Volume is not authority.", "Correct. Escrow operates under authorized instructions and applicable requirements.", "An agent cannot substitute personal judgment for authority.", "Price is unrelated."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["escrow"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-13-004$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 13, $lp$transfer$lp$, 2, $lp$Recording is:$lp$, $lp$["A buyer signing loan documents", "A lender issuing approval", "Acceptance of an instrument for public county records", "Giving keys to buyer"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["Signing is a different step.", "Approval is not recording.", "Correct. Recording concerns public county record entry.", "Possession is separate."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["recording"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-13-005$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 13, $lp$contracts$lp$, 2, $lp$Which closing statement is safest?$lp$, $lp$["We are definitely closing tomorrow", "Loan documents are ready; I will confirm remaining escrow, funding, and recording steps", "Keys can be handed over now", "Nothing can delay a funded loan"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["The agent cannot guarantee closing.", "Correct. It communicates known facts without promising an outcome.", "Possession must be authorized.", "Other required steps may remain."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["funding", "recording"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-13-006$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 13, $lp$transfer$lp$, 3, $lp$A seller's payoff exceeds projected proceeds. The agent should:$lp$, $lp$["Use buyer deposit to cover it", "Promptly escalate and let authorized parties determine a solution", "Assure seller it does not matter", "Alter the settlement statement"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Buyer funds cannot be repurposed.", "Correct. A shortfall requires prompt broker and escrow coordination, not an agent-created solution.", "It may prevent closing.", "An agent cannot alter authorized settlement information."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["payoff demand", "escalation"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-13-007$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 13, $lp$agency$lp$, 2, $lp$The proper agent role in title review is to:$lp$, $lp$["Give legal conclusions", "Identify questions and route them to qualified professionals", "Guarantee insurability", "Delete exceptions"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Legal conclusions may exceed license scope.", "Correct. Agents surface issues and coordinate qualified review.", "Agents cannot guarantee a title product.", "Exceptions are addressed through authorized processes."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["preliminary title report"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-13-008$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 13, $lp$contracts$lp$, 3, $lp$A buyer wants keys before recording is confirmed. The best next step is to:$lp$, $lp$["Give keys because movers are waiting", "Confirm written authority, escrow status, and broker procedure first", "Ask neighbor to unlock property", "Promise recording will occur"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Convenience is not authority.", "Correct. Early access creates risk and requires verified authority.", "A neighbor cannot authorize possession.", "The agent cannot guarantee recording."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["possession", "recording"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-13-009$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 13, $lp$transfer$lp$, 2, $lp$Vesting refers to:$lp$, $lp$["A property marketing plan", "How legal title is held", "A lender's rate lock", "An inspection method"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Marketing is unrelated.", "Correct. Vesting identifies the manner legal title is held.", "Rate locks are loan matters.", "Inspection is separate."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["vesting"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-13-010$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 13, $lp$agency$lp$, 2, $lp$Which buyer information is usually unnecessary in a factual status update to a seller?$lp$, $lp$["That loan conditions remain", "The expected appraisal date", "The buyer's full tax returns", "That an extension request was sent"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["Factual status may be relevant.", "A schedule status can be relevant.", "Correct. Full tax returns are private and normally unnecessary.", "The request's existence may be relevant."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["privacy"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-13-011$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 13, $lp$contracts$lp$, 3, $lp$A contingency should be marked removed when:$lp$, $lp$["The agent feels optimistic", "Applicable written action supports that status", "The lender sounds positive", "The listing agent asks"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Optimism is not evidence.", "Correct. Use the applicable written transaction evidence.", "Positive conversation may not remove a contingency.", "A request does not change a contract."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["contingency"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-13-012$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 13, $lp$transfer$lp$, 2, $lp$A title exception is:$lp$, $lp$["Always a defect that kills the sale", "A stated matter outside or limited under coverage", "A commission agreement", "A loan approval"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Its effect must be evaluated, not assumed.", "Correct. Exceptions identify matters outside or limited under stated coverage.", "Commission is separate.", "Loans are separate."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["exception"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-13-013$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 13, $lp$agency$lp$, 3, $lp$Why keep a detailed post-close file?$lp$, $lp$["To discard documents faster", "To answer later questions with a factual record", "To avoid sending authorized documents", "To replace title records"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["A file should be preserved under policy.", "Correct. Later questions may arise about disclosures, payments, possession, or recording.", "Authorized client delivery remains important.", "The file does not replace public title records."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["recordkeeping"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-13-014$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 13, $lp$contracts$lp$, 2, $lp$Funding is:$lp$, $lp$["County recording a deed", "A lender disbursing proceeds under its process", "Buyer receiving keys", "Seller signing listing agreement"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Recording is separate.", "Correct. Funding is lender disbursement under its process.", "Possession is separate.", "Listing is unrelated."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["funding"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-13-015$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 13, $lp$transfer$lp$, 3, $lp$A report names a seller whose name differs from the purchase agreement. The agent should:$lp$, $lp$["Ignore it", "Promptly flag it to title, broker, and appropriate parties", "Change the report", "Assure buyer it is a typo"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Name mismatch can affect authority or vesting.", "Correct. Prompt verification prevents a late closing problem.", "Only authorized professionals can correct title documents.", "The agent should not assume the cause."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["vesting", "escalation"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-13-016$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 13, $lp$agency$lp$, 2, $lp$An agent's transaction milestone board should use:$lp$, $lp$["Vague labels only", "Actual dates, responsible parties, and supporting evidence", "Personal memory", "Social-media messages"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Vagueness creates avoidable mistakes.", "Correct. Specific milestones support clear coordination and review.", "Memory is not a durable record.", "Social messages are not a complete transaction system."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["recordkeeping"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-14-001$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 14, $lp$agency$lp$, 2, $lp$A property manager normally acts as agent for the:$lp$, $lp$["Owner", "Resident only", "Vendor", "County recorder"]$lp$::jsonb, 0, $lp$["Correct. The management agreement ordinarily establishes agency with the owner.", "A resident is not automatically the manager's client.", "A vendor has a different role.", "Recording is unrelated."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["property management agreement"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-14-002$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 14, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 3, $lp$An owner says they do not want families with children upstairs. The manager should:$lp$, $lp$["Add the rule to screening", "Refuse the instruction and escalate the fair-housing concern", "Mention it only by phone", "Charge families more"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["This can be discriminatory.", "Correct. The manager cannot implement an unlawful discriminatory instruction.", "Changing the channel does not cure the problem.", "Different treatment creates further risk."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["fair housing", "escalation"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-14-003$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 14, $lp$agency$lp$, 2, $lp$Neutral rental criteria should be:$lp$, $lp$["Changed for favored applicants", "Written and consistently applied", "Based on photographs", "Kept secret from the broker"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Selective changes may create discrimination risk.", "Correct. Consistency supports lawful, fair operations.", "Photos should not drive screening decisions.", "The broker must supervise the process."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["neutral criteria"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-14-004$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 14, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 3, $lp$A resident reports water flowing from a ceiling light fixture. The manager should first:$lp$, $lp$["Wait until Monday", "Follow emergency protocol and arrange urgent qualified response", "Tell resident it is harmless", "Diagnose the wiring"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Delay can worsen immediate danger.", "Correct. Potential electrical/water hazards require prompt emergency procedures.", "The manager lacks basis to make that assurance.", "Diagnosis is outside the ordinary management role."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["work order", "habitability"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-14-005$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 14, $lp$contracts$lp$, 2, $lp$The best record for a maintenance complaint is a:$lp$, $lp$["Vague verbal promise", "Timestamped work order with communications and outcome", "Social-media message", "Memory after repair"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["A verbal promise may be untraceable.", "Correct. A detailed work order creates an auditable factual record.", "Social media is not a complete file.", "Memory is not a reliable record."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["work order"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-14-006$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 14, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$Rent and security deposits should be handled through:$lp$, $lp$["Personal payment apps", "Approved brokerage accounts and records", "A manager's cash drawer", "Another property's ledger"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Personal accounts create control risk.", "Correct. Client funds need approved handling and accounting.", "Cash drawers are not appropriate controls.", "Funds must not be mixed between properties."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["security deposit", "trust fund"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-14-007$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 14, $lp$agency$lp$, 3, $lp$A prospect asks for a disability-related exception to a policy. The manager should:$lp$, $lp$["Deny it as inconvenient", "Route it through the approved accommodation process", "Ask for medical details in public", "Ignore the request"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Inconvenience alone is not the proper analysis.", "Correct. Accommodation requests require the broker's approved fair-housing procedure.", "Sensitive information must be handled carefully.", "Ignoring it creates risk."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["reasonable accommodation"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-14-008$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 14, $lp$contracts$lp$, 2, $lp$A rent ledger tracks:$lp$, $lp$["Marketing impressions", "Charges, payments, credits, and balances", "Title exceptions", "Loan underwriting"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Marketing is unrelated.", "Correct. The ledger documents the resident's account activity.", "Title is unrelated.", "Lending is unrelated."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["rent ledger"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$PRAC-14-009$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$practice$lp$, 14, $lp$agency$lp$, 3, $lp$An owner wants buyers shown an occupied unit whenever convenient. The manager should:$lp$, $lp$["Give keys to any buyer", "Use approved notice/access process and document it", "Enter without record", "Tell resident to leave permanently"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Keys require controlled access.", "Correct. Privacy and entry require the authorized process.", "Undocumented entry is risky.", "The manager cannot impose that demand."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["privacy"]$lp$::jsonb),
