  ($lp$LEGAL-02-009$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 2, $lp$agency$lp$, 3, $lp$Ratification occurs when a principal:$lp$, $lp$["Later approves an unauthorized act with material knowledge", "Never learns of an act", "Changes brokers", "Rejects an offer"]$lp$::jsonb, 0, $lp$["Correct. Ratification is later approval with knowledge.", "Knowledge is important.", "Changing brokers is different.", "Offer rejection is different."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["ratification"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-02-010$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 2, $lp$agency$lp$, 3, $lp$A broker's conduct makes a third party reasonably believe an agent has authority. This describes:$lp$, $lp$["Apparent authority", "A title exception", "A contingency", "A deed"]$lp$::jsonb, 0, $lp$["Correct. Apparent authority arises from reasonable appearance based on principal conduct.", "Title is unrelated.", "Contracts are unrelated.", "Deeds are unrelated."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["apparent authority"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-02-011$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 2, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$Agency disclosure should occur:$lp$, $lp$["Early enough to be meaningful", "Only after closing", "Only if client asks", "After negotiation is complete"]$lp$::jsonb, 0, $lp$["Correct. Disclosure should support informed choice.", "Closing is too late.", "Required disclosure is not merely optional.", "Late disclosure defeats its purpose."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["informed consent"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-02-012$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 2, $lp$agency$lp$, 2, $lp$A conflict of interest should be handled by:$lp$, $lp$["Silence", "Early identification, disclosure, and broker process", "Private side deal", "Deleting messages"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Silence is not consent.", "Correct. Conflicts require transparent authorized management.", "Side deals add risk.", "Records must be preserved."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["conflict of interest"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-02-013$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 2, $lp$agency$lp$, 3, $lp$An agent wants to buy their client's listing through a friend. The agent should first:$lp$, $lp$["Hide their interest", "Notify broker and use required conflict disclosure", "Tell seller it is best offer", "Use seller's confidential facts"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Concealment breaches trust.", "Correct. Personal interest requires early broker review and disclosure.", "Price opinion does not cure conflict.", "Confidential facts cannot be exploited."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["conflict of interest", "informed consent"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-02-014$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 2, $lp$contracts$lp$, 2, $lp$A material client instruction should be preserved in:$lp$, $lp$["Approved transaction record", "Only personal memory", "A disappearing message", "A social post"]$lp$::jsonb, 0, $lp$["Correct. Material instructions require durable records.", "Memory is not a record.", "Disappearing messages impair the file.", "Social posts are not secure files."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["recordkeeping"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-02-015$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 2, $lp$agency$lp$, 2, $lp$Confidentiality may continue after:$lp$, $lp$["Closing", "A text message", "A showing only", "A price change"]$lp$::jsonb, 0, $lp$["Correct. Closing does not automatically erase confidentiality.", "Texts are not the relevant event.", "A showing is not necessarily relationship end.", "Price change is unrelated."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["confidentiality"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-02-016$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 2, $lp$agency$lp$, 3, $lp$When representation is uncertain, the best agent action is to:$lp$, $lp$["Give individual advice anyway", "Clarify relationship, use approved disclosures, and consult broker", "Promise confidentiality without analysis", "Avoid all documentation"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Unclear representation creates risk.", "Correct. Clarification and supervision protect all parties.", "Promises can create duties unexpectedly.", "Documentation is essential."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["agency", "escalation"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-03-001$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 3, $lp$contracts$lp$, 2, $lp$A void contract is best described as one that:$lp$, $lp$["Never had legal effect and cannot be ratified", "Is valid until one party elects to rescind", "Is valid but a court will not enforce it", "Requires attorney review before signing"]$lp$::jsonb, 0, $lp$["Correct. A void contract never had legal effect and nothing can affirm it.", "That describes a voidable contract.", "That describes an unenforceable contract.", "Attorney review is not what makes a contract void."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["void contract"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-03-002$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 3, $lp$contracts$lp$, 2, $lp$A contract induced by fraud is generally:$lp$, $lp$["Void", "Voidable at the defrauded party's election", "Automatically rescinded", "Unenforceable only under the Statute of Frauds"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Fraud does not make a contract void from the start.", "Correct. The defrauded party may elect to disaffirm or may instead affirm and sue for damages.", "Rescission is not automatic; the injured party must elect it.", "This is unrelated to the Statute of Frauds."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["actual fraud", "voidable contract"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-03-003$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 3, $lp$contracts$lp$, 3, $lp$A seller tells a buyer a roof was 'fully replaced last year' when only a small section was patched, and the buyer relies on that statement and waives the roof inspection. This is most accurately described as:$lp$, $lp$["Constructive fraud", "Actual fraud", "Mutual mistake", "Unconscionability"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Constructive fraud does not require an intentional false statement like this one.", "Correct. An intentional material misrepresentation made to induce reliance is actual fraud.", "Only one party was mistaken, and the seller knew the truth.", "This is not about contract terms being one-sided."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["actual fraud"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-03-004$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 3, $lp$contracts$lp$, 2, $lp$Constructive fraud differs from actual fraud because constructive fraud:$lp$, $lp$["Requires intent to deceive", "Can arise from breach of a duty without intent to deceive", "Is never actionable", "Only applies to written contracts"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Intent is the element constructive fraud does not require.", "Correct. It can arise from a breach of duty, such as a fiduciary's disclosure duty, even without intent to deceive.", "It is actionable.", "It is not limited to written contracts."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["constructive fraud"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-03-005$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 3, $lp$contracts$lp$, 2, $lp$Duress in contract law requires:$lp$, $lp$["Hard bargaining over price", "An unlawful threat overcoming free will", "A low appraisal", "A missed deadline"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Ordinary hard bargaining is not duress.", "Correct. Duress requires an unlawful threat that overcomes the will of the other party.", "Appraisal outcomes are unrelated.", "A missed deadline is unrelated to duress."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["duress"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-03-006$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 3, $lp$contracts$lp$, 3, $lp$An isolated, dependent elderly seller is persuaded by a trusted relative to sell a home well below market value without independent advice. This fact pattern most strongly suggests:$lp$, $lp$["Mutual mistake", "Undue influence", "Unconscionability only", "Parol evidence violation"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Both parties did not share a mistaken belief here.", "Correct. A position of trust combined with vulnerability and an unfair result is a classic undue-influence pattern.", "Unconscionability is a separate, narrower doctrine focused on contract terms.", "The parol evidence rule is unrelated."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["undue influence"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-03-007$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 3, $lp$contracts$lp$, 2, $lp$A mutual mistake that can make a contract voidable involves:$lp$, $lp$["Only one party being wrong about an unimportant detail", "Both parties sharing an untrue belief about a material fact", "A party changing their mind after signing", "A party failing to read the contract"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["An unimportant, one-sided error does not qualify.", "Correct. A shared, material misunderstanding of fact can make a contract voidable.", "Buyer's remorse is not mistake.", "Failing to read is not mistake in the legal sense."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["mutual mistake"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-03-008$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 3, $lp$contracts$lp$, 2, $lp$A unilateral mistake generally excuses performance only when:$lp$, $lp$["The other party knew or should have known of the error and tried to exploit it", "Any party regrets the deal", "The mistake involves the address only", "The error is discovered after closing"]$lp$::jsonb, 0, $lp$["Correct. A unilateral mistake typically excuses performance only if the other side knew or should have known and took advantage.", "Regret alone does not excuse performance.", "Address errors are not automatically excusing.", "Timing after closing does not itself create the excuse."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["mistake"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-03-009$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 3, $lp$contracts$lp$, 3, $lp$The parol evidence rule generally:$lp$, $lp$["Requires every contract to be notarized", "Bars evidence of prior statements that contradict a final written agreement", "Prevents any contract modification", "Only applies to commercial leases"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Notarization is unrelated to this rule.", "Correct. It bars evidence contradicting or adding to an integrated written agreement.", "Later written modifications can still be shown; the rule targets prior/contemporaneous statements.", "It is not limited to commercial leases."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["parol evidence rule"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-03-010$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 3, $lp$contracts$lp$, 2, $lp$A buyer and seller verbally agree the seller will leave the washer and dryer, but the written contract is silent. The safest professional practice is to:$lp$, $lp$["Rely on the verbal agreement", "Put the agreement in writing in the contract or an addendum", "Assume it is automatically included", "Wait until closing to mention it"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Verbal side promises are difficult to enforce under the parol evidence rule.", "Correct. Material terms should be written into the contract itself.", "Nothing is automatically included without being written.", "Waiting risks a dispute with no written basis."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["parol evidence rule"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-03-011$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 3, $lp$contracts$lp$, 3, $lp$A liquidated damages clause on a $650,000 purchase capped at 3% limits the seller's recovery, upon buyer default, to approximately:$lp$, $lp$["$6,500", "$19,500", "$65,000", "$195,000"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["That is 1%, not 3%.", "Correct. $650,000 × 0.03 = $19,500.", "That is 10%, not 3%.", "That figure is far too high."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["liquidated damages"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-03-012$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 3, $lp$contracts$lp$, 2, $lp$A liquidated damages clause will likely be struck down as unenforceable if it:$lp$, $lp$["Reasonably estimates anticipated harm", "Is initialed by both parties", "Operates as a penalty far exceeding likely harm", "Applies only to buyer default"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["A reasonable estimate is enforceable.", "Initials are a formality requirement, not a defect.", "Correct. An amount so large it functions as a penalty risks being unenforceable.", "Applying to buyer default alone is not itself a defect."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["liquidated damages"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-03-013$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 3, $lp$contracts$lp$, 3, $lp$A seller who signs a valid purchase agreement then refuses to close because a better off-market offer appeared may face a buyer's remedy of:$lp$, $lp$["Specific performance", "Automatic rescission for both parties", "Nothing, since sellers may always cancel", "Liquidated damages against the buyer"]$lp$::jsonb, 0, $lp$["Correct. Because real property is unique, courts more readily order specific performance to compel the seller to convey.", "Rescission is not automatic and does not fit this fact pattern.", "A seller cannot lawfully cancel simply because a better offer appeared.", "Liquidated damages against the buyer make no sense; the seller is the breaching party here."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["specific performance"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-03-014$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 3, $lp$contracts$lp$, 2, $lp$Specific performance is more readily available in real estate contracts than for ordinary goods contracts because:$lp$, $lp$["Real estate contracts are always written", "Real property is considered legally unique", "Escrow requires it", "Buyers always prefer it"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Being written is not the reason.", "Correct. Land's legal uniqueness makes money damages an inadequate substitute.", "Escrow does not require this remedy.", "Buyer preference does not determine legal availability."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["specific performance"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-03-015$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 3, $lp$contracts$lp$, 2, $lp$Rescission as a remedy is best described as:$lp$, $lp$["Compelling performance of the contract", "Awarding money damages only", "Unwinding the contract to restore pre-contract positions", "Modifying contract terms going forward"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["That describes specific performance.", "Damages are a separate remedy.", "Correct. Rescission restores both parties to their pre-contract position.", "Rescission unwinds rather than modifies."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["rescission"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-03-016$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 3, $lp$contracts$lp$, 2, $lp$An adhesion contract is best described as:$lp$, $lp$["Always unenforceable", "A standardized, non-negotiable form offered by the stronger party", "A contract requiring notarization", "A verbal agreement only"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Adhesion contracts are routinely enforced.", "Correct. It is a take-it-or-leave-it standardized form from the party with greater bargaining power.", "Notarization is unrelated.", "Adhesion contracts are typically written, standardized forms."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["adhesion contract"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-03-017$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 3, $lp$contracts$lp$, 3, $lp$Establishing unconscionability generally requires showing:$lp$, $lp$["Only that one party had less bargaining power", "Only that the contract was in writing", "Both procedural and substantive unfairness", "That the contract used standard association forms"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["Unequal bargaining power alone is not enough.", "Being written is not the standard.", "Correct. Courts typically require both procedural unfairness and substantively one-sided terms.", "Using standard forms does not itself establish unconscionability."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["unconscionability"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-03-018$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 3, $lp$contracts$lp$, 2, $lp$When an agent suspects a contract dispute involves fraud, undue influence, or a formation defense, the correct professional step is to:$lp$, $lp$["Resolve it personally to protect the deal", "Draft custom contract language fixing the issue", "Recognize the pattern, document facts, and refer to an attorney", "Ignore it if the client does not raise it"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["Licensees may not practice law by resolving legal defenses.", "Drafting custom legal remedies is practicing law.", "Correct. Recognize, document, and refer — this is the boundary of the licensee's role.", "Ignoring a recognized legal issue does not meet fiduciary or ethical obligations."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["escalation"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-04-001$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 4, $lp$transfer$lp$, 2, $lp$Which is NOT required for a deed to be valid between the original parties?$lp$, $lp$["Writing", "A competent grantor", "Recording", "Delivery and acceptance"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["Writing is required under the Statute of Frauds.", "A competent grantor is required.", "Correct. Recording is not required for validity between the parties; it affects priority and notice.", "Delivery and acceptance are required."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["deed"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-04-002$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 4, $lp$transfer$lp$, 2, $lp$Consideration is:$lp$, $lp$["Required for a valid deed", "Not required for a valid deed; a gift deed can be valid", "Required only for quitclaim deeds", "Required only for trust deeds"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Consideration is not a deed-validity requirement.", "Correct. A deed can be valid without consideration, as in a gift deed.", "This is not specific to quitclaims.", "This is not specific to trust deeds."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["deed"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-04-003$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 4, $lp$transfer$lp$, 3, $lp$Delivery of a deed requires:$lp$, $lp$["Physical handover of the paper only", "The grantor's present intent to immediately convey, plus acceptance", "Recording within 30 days", "The grantee's signature"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Physical handover alone is neither necessary nor sufficient.", "Correct. Present intent to convey immediately, plus acceptance, is the legal test.", "Recording is unrelated to delivery.", "Grantees are not required to sign deeds."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["delivery"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-04-004$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 4, $lp$transfer$lp$, 3, $lp$A grantor signs a deed 'to my daughter' and keeps it in a locked box with intent that it take effect only at death. This deed is most likely:$lp$, $lp$["A valid present conveyance", "Invalid for lack of present intent to convey and delivery during life", "Valid once recorded posthumously", "Valid because it is in writing and signed"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["There was no present intent to convey during the grantor's lifetime.", "Correct. Without present intent and delivery during life, this functions as an invalid attempted testamentary transfer.", "Recording after death does not cure the defect.", "Writing and signature alone are not sufficient without delivery and present intent."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["delivery"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-04-005$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 4, $lp$transfer$lp$, 2, $lp$A grant deed's implied warranties generally do NOT cover:$lp$, $lp$["Encumbrances made by the grantor", "Prior conveyance by the grantor to someone else", "Defects arising before the grantor's ownership", "Undisclosed liens created by the grantor"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["This is covered by implied warranty.", "This is covered by implied warranty.", "Correct. Grant deed warranties do not reach back before the grantor's own ownership.", "This is covered by implied warranty."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["grant deed"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-04-006$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 4, $lp$transfer$lp$, 2, $lp$A quitclaim deed:$lp$, $lp$["Warrants clear title against all claims", "Conveys whatever interest the grantor has, with no warranties", "Cannot be used between family members", "Automatically transfers ownership even if the grantor has no interest"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["That describes a warranty deed, not a quitclaim.", "Correct. A quitclaim conveys only whatever interest exists, with no warranties at all.", "Quitclaims are commonly used between family members and ex-spouses.", "A quitclaim cannot convey an interest the grantor does not have."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["quitclaim deed"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-04-007$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 4, $lp$transfer$lp$, 2, $lp$An ex-spouse signs a document conveying whatever community-property interest he may have in the family home to his former wife as part of a divorce settlement. The appropriate instrument is most likely a:$lp$, $lp$["Warranty deed", "Trust deed", "Quitclaim deed", "Reconveyance deed"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["Warranty deeds are rare in California and unnecessary here.", "A trust deed is a security instrument, not applicable here.", "Correct. A quitclaim is the customary tool for clearing a specific person's potential claim on title.", "A reconveyance deed releases a trust deed lien, which is not the situation here."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["quitclaim deed"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-04-008$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 4, $lp$transfer$lp$, 2, $lp$A trust deed (deed of trust) functions primarily as:$lp$, $lp$["A conveyance of full ownership", "A security instrument conveying bare legal title to a trustee", "A quitclaim between spouses", "A government taking instrument"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["It is not a conveyance of full beneficial ownership.", "Correct. It secures a debt by conveying bare legal title to a trustee, reconveyed upon payoff.", "It is unrelated to interspousal quitclaims.", "It is unrelated to eminent domain."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["trust deed"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-04-009$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 4, $lp$transfer$lp$, 3, $lp$Recording a deed primarily:$lp$, $lp$["Transfers title", "Establishes constructive notice and priority", "Is required for the deed to be valid between the parties", "Eliminates the need for title insurance"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Title transfers upon valid execution and delivery, not recording.", "Correct. Recording gives constructive notice and helps establish priority among competing claims.", "Recording is not required for validity between the original parties.", "Recording does not eliminate the value of title insurance."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["recording", "constructive notice"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-04-010$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 4, $lp$transfer$lp$, 3, $lp$A distressed seller sells the same property to Buyer A and Buyer B. Buyer A's deed is delivered first but not recorded for two weeks. Buyer B, unaware of Buyer A's purchase, pays value and records immediately. Under California recording principles, priority most likely favors:$lp$, $lp$["Buyer A, because delivery occurred first", "Buyer B, because Buyer B paid value and recorded first without notice", "Whichever buyer has physical possession", "The seller, who may resell to a third buyer"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Earlier delivery alone does not guarantee priority against a later good-faith recorder.", "Correct. A subsequent purchaser for value who records first without notice of the prior unrecorded interest can gain priority.", "Possession is not the controlling factor in this scenario.", "The seller has no continuing right to resell after conveying to Buyer A."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["recording", "priority"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-04-011$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 4, $lp$transfer$lp$, 2, $lp$Constructive notice means:$lp$, $lp$["Someone actually knew a fact", "The law treats a recorded document as known regardless of actual knowledge", "A verbal warning was given", "A notice was posted on the property only"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["That describes actual notice.", "Correct. Constructive notice is imputed by recording, whether or not someone actually looked.", "Verbal warnings are not constructive notice.", "Physical posting alone is not the legal mechanism described."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["constructive notice"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-04-012$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 4, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$A legal description referencing directions, distances, and monuments from a fixed point of beginning is called:$lp$, $lp$["Government survey", "Metes and bounds", "Lot, block, and tract", "Assessor's parcel number"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Government survey uses township, range, and section.", "Correct. Metes and bounds describes property by directions, distances, and monuments.", "Lot, block, and tract references a recorded subdivision map.", "An APN is an assessment identifier, not a full legal description."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["metes and bounds"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-04-013$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 4, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$A street address alone is generally:$lp$, $lp$["A sufficient legal description for a deed", "Not treated as a sufficient legal description for a deed", "Required in addition to metes and bounds", "Only used for commercial property"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["A street address alone is not treated as sufficient.", "Correct. Deeds require a legal description sufficient to identify the property with reasonable certainty.", "It is not a formal requirement alongside metes and bounds.", "This is not limited to commercial property."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["legal description"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-04-014$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 4, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 3, $lp$An ambiguous legal description that conflicts with the assessor's parcel number is best understood as:$lp$, $lp$["An irrelevant clerical detail", "A title problem that may require a surveyor or quiet title action to resolve", "Automatically cured by escrow", "Something only the buyer's lender needs to worry about"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["It is not irrelevant; it can cloud title.", "Correct. Ambiguous descriptions are title problems requiring resolution before a clean closing.", "Escrow does not automatically cure description defects.", "It affects buyer, seller, and lender alike."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["legal description"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-04-015$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 4, $lp$transfer$lp$, 2, $lp$Acquiring title through open, notorious, hostile, and continuous possession under claim of right, combined with tax payment for the statutory period, describes:$lp$, $lp$["Eminent domain", "Adverse possession", "Devise", "Intestate succession"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Eminent domain is a government taking with compensation.", "Correct. This describes adverse possession.", "Devise is transfer under a valid will.", "Intestate succession applies when there is no valid will."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["adverse possession"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-04-016$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 4, $lp$transfer$lp$, 2, $lp$When a person dies without a valid will, real property passes by:$lp$, $lp$["Devise", "Intestate succession", "Adverse possession", "Eminent domain"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Devise requires a valid will.", "Correct. Intestate succession governs distribution when there is no valid will.", "Adverse possession requires possession over time, unrelated to death.", "Eminent domain is a government taking."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["intestate succession"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-04-017$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 4, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$Escrow delivery of a deed, where a grantor deposits a signed deed with instructions to deliver upon satisfying conditions, is generally treated as:$lp$, $lp$["Invalid until the grantor personally hands over the deed", "Valid delivery once conditions are met, sometimes relating back to the deposit date", "Only valid for commercial transactions", "The same as a testamentary transfer"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Escrow delivery is a recognized valid delivery method.", "Correct. This is valid delivery once conditions are satisfied, and can relate back under the relation-back doctrine.", "It is common in both residential and commercial transactions.", "It is distinct from a testamentary transfer, which requires will formalities."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["relation-back doctrine"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-04-018$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 4, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$A licensee cannot advise a client on which vesting to choose because:$lp$, $lp$["Vesting never matters to the client", "That is a legal/tax determination outside license scope", "Only lenders may choose vesting", "Vesting is fixed by the assessor"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Vesting has real legal and financial consequences for the client.", "Correct. Vesting choice implicates legal and tax consequences beyond license scope; refer to an attorney or CPA.", "Lenders do not choose vesting for the buyer.", "The assessor does not determine vesting."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["vesting"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-05-001$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 5, $lp$transfer$lp$, 2, $lp$The chain of title is best described as:$lp$, $lp$["The current owner's mailing address history", "The sequential recorded history of ownership and encumbrances affecting a parcel", "A list of past real estate agents", "The property's tax assessment history only"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Mailing address history is unrelated.", "Correct. It traces recorded documents affecting the parcel from the earliest record forward.", "Agent history is unrelated to chain of title.", "Tax assessment is a separate record."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["chain of title"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-05-002$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 5, $lp$transfer$lp$, 3, $lp$Record title, marketable title, and insurable title differ because:$lp$, $lp$["They are three names for the same thing", "Record title is what the public record shows, which may not reflect true ownership", "Insurable title always equals marketable title", "Marketable title is decided only by the assessor"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["They are distinct concepts.", "Correct. Record title reflects the public record, which can include invalid instruments like forgeries.", "A title company can insure over exceptions a buyer might still reasonably object to.", "The assessor does not determine marketability."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["record title", "marketable title", "insurable title"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-05-003$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 5, $lp$transfer$lp$, 2, $lp$Marketable title is best described as:$lp$, $lp$["Perfect title with zero exceptions", "Title reasonably free from doubt that a prudent buyer would accept", "Title with an ALTA policy only", "Title that has never been sold before"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Perfection is not the standard.", "Correct. Marketable title is reasonably free from doubt.", "Policy type does not define marketability.", "Prior sales history is unrelated."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["marketable title"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-05-004$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 5, $lp$transfer$lp$, 2, $lp$A preliminary title report is best understood as:$lp$, $lp$["A finalized title insurance policy", "A guarantee of clear title", "An offer to insure on stated terms", "A recorded deed"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["It is not a finalized policy.", "It is not a guarantee.", "Correct. It states the conditions under which the company will insure.", "It is not itself a deed."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["preliminary report"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-05-005$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 5, $lp$transfer$lp$, 2, $lp$The best practice for reviewing a preliminary report's specific exceptions is to:$lp$, $lp$["Wait until the week of closing", "Read them on day one of escrow", "Let escrow read them silently", "Only read them if the buyer asks"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Waiting compresses the time available to clear problems.", "Correct. Early review leaves time for curative work.", "Agents should personally review, not delegate blindly.", "Review should happen regardless of whether asked."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["preliminary report"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-05-006$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 5, $lp$transfer$lp$, 3, $lp$A prelim lists a judgment lien against a name matching the seller's but with a different middle initial and no matching prior address. This is most likely:$lp$, $lp$["A confirmed lien against the seller", "A wild document requiring an affidavit of identity to clear", "Proof the seller has bad credit", "Irrelevant and safely ignored"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["The mismatched details suggest it may not belong to the seller.", "Correct. This is a classic wild document, typically cleared with an affidavit of identity/non-identity.", "Credit history is a different matter entirely.", "It should not be ignored; it must be reviewed and cleared."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["wild document"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-05-007$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 5, $lp$transfer$lp$, 2, $lp$A CLTA policy compared to an ALTA policy typically:$lp$, $lp$["Provides broader coverage at a higher premium", "Provides narrower, standard coverage at a lower premium", "Is required by all lenders", "Only applies to commercial property"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["That describes ALTA relative to CLTA.", "Correct. CLTA is the more limited, standard-coverage baseline, commonly used for owner's policies.", "Lenders typically require ALTA-type coverage.", "CLTA is common in residential transactions."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["CLTA policy"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-05-008$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 5, $lp$transfer$lp$, 2, $lp$ALTA coverage is often required by:$lp$, $lp$["Buyers only, never lenders", "Lenders, and available as extended owner's coverage", "The county recorder", "The listing agent"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Lenders commonly require it, not just buyers.", "Correct. ALTA is often lender-required and available as enhanced owner's coverage.", "The recorder does not require policy type.", "Agents do not require policy type."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["ALTA policy"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-05-009$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 5, $lp$transfer$lp$, 3, $lp$A forged deed in the chain of title is:$lp$, $lp$["Voidable by the innocent purchaser", "Void, conveying nothing even to a later good-faith purchaser", "Valid once recorded", "Cured automatically after ten years"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["It is void, not merely voidable.", "Correct. A forged deed conveys nothing, regardless of subsequent good-faith reliance.", "Recording does not validate a forged instrument.", "There is no automatic ten-year cure described here."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["forged deed"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-05-010$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 5, $lp$transfer$lp$, 3, $lp$Because a forged deed conveys nothing, the practical protection for later innocent purchasers most often comes from:$lp$, $lp$["The forger's future cooperation", "Title insurance absorbing the loss subject to policy terms", "Automatic government compensation", "The county recorder's guarantee"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["The forger is unlikely to cooperate.", "Correct. Title insurance is priced to absorb exactly this kind of catastrophic, low-frequency risk.", "No such automatic government compensation exists here.", "The recorder does not guarantee title validity."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["forged deed", "title insurance"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-05-011$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 5, $lp$transfer$lp$, 2, $lp$An unreleased lien from a mortgage that was actually paid off years ago is an example of:$lp$, $lp$["A properly cleared title", "A title defect requiring a recorded satisfaction or reconveyance", "A boundary dispute", "A quiet title judgment"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["It is not properly cleared until released of record.", "Correct. The paid lien remains a visible defect until its satisfaction or reconveyance is recorded.", "This is not a boundary issue.", "No judgment has occurred yet."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["unreleased lien"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-05-012$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 5, $lp$transfer$lp$, 2, $lp$An undisclosed heir surfacing years after an improperly probated estate can:$lp$, $lp$["Never affect a later buyer's title", "Cloud title even after multiple subsequent sales", "Only affect the original decedent's spouse", "Be ignored if the property has changed hands"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["It can affect later title.", "Correct. An omitted heir's interest can surface and cloud title well after subsequent transfers.", "It can affect any heir, not only a spouse.", "Subsequent sales do not erase the underlying defect."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["undisclosed heirs"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-05-013$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 5, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$A quiet title action is:$lp$, $lp$["An administrative form filed with the title company", "A lawsuit asking a court to determine true ownership", "A type of deed", "A recording fee waiver"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["It is a judicial action, not an administrative form.", "Correct. It is a lawsuit resolving disputed or clouded ownership.", "It is not a deed type.", "It is unrelated to recording fees."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["quiet title action"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-05-014$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 5, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$A quiet title action is typically appropriate when:$lp$, $lp$["A defect can be cleared with a simple affidavit", "A defect cannot be cleared administratively, such as an heir who will not sign a release", "The buyer wants a faster closing", "The seller wants a lower price"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Simple defects are usually cleared administratively, not through litigation.", "Correct. Quiet title is reserved for defects that cannot be resolved without a court.", "Quiet title actions take months and slow closings, not speed them.", "Price preference is unrelated."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["quiet title action"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-05-015$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 5, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$When an agent identifies an unexpected exception on a preliminary report, the correct first step is to:$lp$, $lp$["Wait to see if it resolves itself", "Route it to the title company's curative department and escrow immediately", "Advise the client on the legal remedy personally", "Remove it from the file"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Waiting wastes valuable curative time.", "Correct. Prompt routing to the professionals who can clear it protects the timeline.", "Giving legal advice on remedies exceeds license scope.", "Ignoring or removing it from the file does not resolve the defect."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["preliminary report"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-05-016$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 5, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$General/standard exceptions in a preliminary report typically include matters that:$lp$, $lp$["Are unique to one specific property only", "An accurate survey would reveal, or rights of parties in possession", "Only apply to commercial property", "Never appear in residential transactions"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Specific exceptions, not general ones, are unique to the property.", "Correct. Standard exceptions commonly include survey-related and possession-related matters.", "They are not limited to commercial property.", "They routinely appear in residential preliminary reports."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["preliminary report"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-05-017$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 5, $lp$transfer$lp$, 2, $lp$Title insurance is unusual compared to most insurance because:$lp$, $lp$["It insures against future events only", "It is priced around a one-time premium protecting against past, existing defects", "It requires monthly premiums", "It only covers lenders, never owners"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["It primarily protects against defects that already exist in the historical record.", "Correct. A one-time premium covers risk arising from the property's past.", "Title insurance is typically a one-time premium, not a recurring monthly payment.", "Owner's policies exist alongside lender's policies."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["title insurance"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-05-018$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 5, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$If a preliminary report reveals a serious defect such as a suspected forged instrument in the chain, the licensee should:$lp$, $lp$["Resolve it personally to keep the deal moving", "Tell the client the closing date is realistically at risk and an attorney may be needed", "Hide the issue from the client until it worsens", "Recommend the buyer waive title review"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Resolving serious title defects is outside license scope.", "Correct. The client needs an honest timeline assessment and, likely, legal involvement.", "Concealing a known issue breaches disclosure obligations.", "Waiving title review on a serious defect would be poor advice."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["quiet title action"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-06-001$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 6, $lp$transfer$lp$, 2, $lp$Escrow is best legally characterized as:$lp$, $lp$["An agent solely for the buyer", "An agent solely for the seller", "A dual agency of limited purpose bound by mutual instructions", "A government office"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["Escrow does not represent only the buyer.", "Escrow does not represent only the seller.", "Correct. Escrow acts as agent for both parties but only within the scope of mutual written instructions.", "Escrow is typically a private, licensed entity, not a government office."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["escrow"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-06-002$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 6, $lp$transfer$lp$, 2, $lp$An escrow holder may:$lp$, $lp$["Give legal advice to either party", "Give real estate advice to either party", "Follow mutual written instructions neutrally", "Take the side of whichever party pays the fee"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["Escrow cannot give legal advice.", "Escrow cannot give real estate advice.", "Correct. Escrow's role is to neutrally execute mutual written instructions.", "Escrow must remain neutral regardless of who pays fees."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["escrow"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-06-003$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 6, $lp$transfer$lp$, 3, $lp$A valid escrow requires:$lp$, $lp$["A binding contract, conditional delivery of instruments, and instructions", "Only a signed check", "Only a verbal agreement", "A court order"]$lp$::jsonb, 0, $lp$["Correct. These three elements establish a valid escrow.", "A check alone does not create escrow.", "Escrow requires more than a verbal agreement.", "No court order is needed to open escrow."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["escrow"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-06-004$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 6, $lp$transfer$lp$, 2, $lp$Escrow instructions that conflict with the underlying purchase agreement should be treated as:$lp$, $lp$["Irrelevant since escrow controls", "A real problem requiring resolution and documentation", "Automatically resolved in favor of escrow", "Something only the lender needs to address"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Escrow does not automatically override the contract.", "Correct. A conflict must be resolved and documented, not ignored.", "There is no automatic resolution rule favoring escrow instructions.", "This concerns all parties, not only the lender."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["escrow"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-06-005$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 6, $lp$transfer$lp$, 2, $lp$Independent escrow companies in California are generally regulated by:$lp$, $lp$["The DRE only", "DFPI (Department of Financial Protection and Innovation)", "The IRS", "The county assessor"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["The DRE regulates real estate licensees, not independent escrow companies directly.", "Correct. DFPI regulates and licenses independent escrow companies.", "The IRS is unrelated to escrow licensing.", "The assessor is unrelated to escrow licensing."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["DFPI"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-06-006$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 6, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$RESPA is best described as:$lp$, $lp$["A California-only statute", "A federal law regulating settlement services and prohibiting kickbacks", "A form used only by appraisers", "A state licensing exam"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["RESPA is federal, not California-only.", "Correct. RESPA governs settlement-service disclosures and prohibits referral kickbacks.", "It is not an appraisal form.", "It is not a licensing exam."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["RESPA"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-06-007$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 6, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$RESPA Section 8 prohibits:$lp$, $lp$["Any referral between licensees", "Fees or kickbacks for referrals and unearned fee splitting", "All affiliated business arrangements", "Charging any settlement fee"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Ordinary professional referrals without payment are not prohibited.", "Correct. Section 8 targets kickbacks for referrals and splitting unearned fees.", "Disclosed, compliant affiliated business arrangements are permitted.", "Fair, earned settlement fees are not prohibited."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["Section 8 (RESPA)"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-06-008$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 6, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 3, $lp$A title company pays an agent's team a flat monthly fee for 'marketing services' with no tracked deliverables, in exchange for escrow referrals. This is most likely:$lp$, $lp$["A lawful marketing arrangement", "A disguised kickback for referrals", "Required by RESPA", "Only a tax issue"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Without genuine services at fair value, it is not lawful.", "Correct. Substance controls over the contract's label; untracked payment for referrals is a disguised kickback.", "RESPA does not require such payments; it prohibits this conduct.", "It is a RESPA compliance issue, not merely a tax issue."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["Section 8 (RESPA)"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-06-009$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 6, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$An Affiliated Business Arrangement is lawful under RESPA when:$lp$, $lp$["It is concealed from the client", "The client is disclosed the affiliation, has genuine choice, and pays market-rate fees", "The client is required to use the affiliated company", "Fees are set above market rate"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Concealment defeats the disclosure requirement.", "Correct. Disclosure, genuine choice, and fair pricing make an AfBA lawful.", "Requiring use without choice violates RESPA's intent.", "Above-market fees undermine the arrangement's legitimacy."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["Affiliated Business Arrangement (AfBA)"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-06-010$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 6, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$A brokerage with an ownership stake in an escrow company refers a buyer to it, provides a written disclosure of the ownership interest, and confirms the buyer is not required to use it. This arrangement is:$lp$, $lp$["A RESPA violation regardless of disclosure", "Compliant if disclosure, choice, and fair pricing are present", "Illegal because affiliated businesses are always banned", "Only a problem if the buyer complains"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Proper disclosure and choice make this compliant, not a violation.", "Correct. This is the compliant structure for an affiliated business arrangement.", "Affiliated businesses are not categorically banned.", "Compliance does not depend on whether the buyer complains."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["Affiliated Business Arrangement (AfBA)"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-06-011$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 6, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$RESPA's disclosure requirements primarily concern:$lp$, $lp$["Fair housing training", "Estimated and actual settlement costs disclosed to loan applicants", "Property condition disclosures", "Agency relationship disclosures"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Fair housing training is a separate topic.", "Correct. RESPA-related disclosure requirements inform borrowers of settlement costs.", "Property condition disclosures are governed by separate California law.", "Agency disclosure is a separate California requirement."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["RESPA"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-06-012$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 6, $lp$transfer$lp$, 2, $lp$Which statement about RESPA and California escrow law is most accurate?$lp$, $lp$["They are identical bodies of law", "A transaction can be RESPA-compliant yet violate a California-specific escrow rule, or vice versa", "California escrow law is superseded entirely by RESPA", "RESPA governs all California disclosure requirements"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["They are not identical; they overlap but are distinct.", "Correct. Compliance with one does not guarantee compliance with the other.", "California escrow law is not entirely superseded by RESPA.", "RESPA does not govern all state-mandated disclosures, such as NHD."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["RESPA"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-06-013$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 6, $lp$transfer$lp$, 2, $lp$California's good funds requirements primarily address:$lp$, $lp$["When escrow may disburse funds based on the type of funding instrument", "Which agent gets paid first", "Whether a listing agreement is exclusive", "Fair housing testing"]$lp$::jsonb, 0, $lp$["Correct. Good funds rules limit disbursement timing based on instrument type, such as wire versus personal check.", "Commission order is unrelated.", "Listing agreement type is unrelated.", "Fair housing testing is unrelated to good funds rules."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["good funds requirement"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-06-014$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 6, $lp$transfer$lp$, 3, $lp$A buyer receives an email near closing, appearing to be from escrow, with new wiring instructions. The safest response is to:$lp$, $lp$["Wire funds immediately to avoid delay", "Independently verify by phone using a previously known number, not one from the email", "Reply to the email asking for confirmation", "Wire a smaller test amount first"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Immediate wiring risks sending funds to a fraudulent account.", "Correct. Independent verification using an already-known number is the standard protection against wire fraud.", "Replying to the same compromised email thread does not verify anything.", "A test wire still risks loss and does not verify legitimacy."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["wire fraud (business email compromise)"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-06-015$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 6, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$Warning buyers about wire fraud risk is best practice because:$lp$, $lp$["It is legally required only of lenders", "It protects the client and reduces the agent's own liability exposure", "It is unnecessary if escrow is licensed", "Only cash buyers are at risk"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["It is a shared professional responsibility, not solely a lender obligation.", "Correct. Early written warnings protect the client and the agent.", "Escrow being licensed does not eliminate email interception risk.", "Any buyer wiring funds, not just cash buyers, can be targeted."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["wire fraud (business email compromise)"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-06-016$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 6, $lp$transfer$lp$, 2, $lp$Steering a client to an unlicensed entity to informally hold transaction funds is problematic primarily because:$lp$, $lp$["It is faster than licensed escrow", "It bypasses bonding, trust accounting, and regulatory protections", "It is always cheaper", "It is required when the buyer pays cash"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Speed does not justify bypassing required protections.", "Correct. Licensed escrow provides bonding, trust accounting, and regulatory oversight that protect the funds.", "Cost savings do not offset the loss of regulatory protection.", "Cash purchases do not require bypassing licensed escrow."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["DFPI"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-07-001$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 7, $lp$financing$lp$, 2, $lp$The promissory note and the deed of trust differ because:$lp$, $lp$["They are the same document under two names", "The note creates the debt obligation; the deed of trust pledges the property as security", "The deed of trust creates the debt; the note pledges the property", "Only the deed of trust is legally binding"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["They are legally distinct instruments.", "Correct. The note is the personal promise to repay; the deed of trust secures it with the property.", "This reverses their actual roles.", "Both are legally binding, serving different functions."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["promissory note", "deed of trust"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-07-002$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 7, $lp$financing$lp$, 2, $lp$In a California deed of trust, the party holding bare legal title as security with power to conduct a sale on default is the:$lp$, $lp$["Trustor", "Beneficiary", "Trustee", "Grantee"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["The trustor is the borrower.", "The beneficiary is the lender.", "Correct. The trustee holds bare legal title as security and can conduct the sale.", "Grantee is not the term used in this three-party structure."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["deed of trust"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-07-003$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 7, $lp$financing$lp$, 2, $lp$The dominant foreclosure method in California is:$lp$, $lp$["Judicial foreclosure", "Non-judicial foreclosure under the power of sale", "Strict foreclosure", "Eminent domain"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Judicial foreclosure is used less often due to cost and time.", "Correct. Non-judicial foreclosure under the power of sale is the overwhelming default method.", "Strict foreclosure is not the California residential default.", "Eminent domain is unrelated to private loan default."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["non-judicial foreclosure"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-07-004$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 7, $lp$financing$lp$, 3, $lp$Compared to non-judicial foreclosure, judicial foreclosure:$lp$, $lp$["Is faster and cheaper", "Allows a possible deficiency judgment and a post-sale redemption right", "Never involves a court", "Automatically forfeits the lender's security"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Judicial foreclosure is slower and more expensive.", "Correct. It preserves a possible deficiency remedy and a statutory redemption right.", "Judicial foreclosure is, by definition, a court action.", "The lender does not forfeit its security by choosing judicial foreclosure."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["judicial foreclosure"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-07-005$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 7, $lp$financing$lp$, 3, $lp$A lender that forecloses non-judicially on a debt generally:$lp$, $lp$["Preserves full rights to sue for any deficiency afterward", "Gives up the right to sue for a deficiency on that debt", "Must also file a lawsuit simultaneously", "Is guaranteed a deficiency judgment"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Non-judicial foreclosure generally forecloses the deficiency remedy on that debt.", "Correct. Choosing the fast non-judicial route generally waives the deficiency judgment option.", "Non-judicial foreclosure does not require a simultaneous lawsuit.", "No deficiency judgment follows from a non-judicial sale in this context."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["deficiency"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-07-006$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 7, $lp$financing$lp$, 2, $lp$A deficiency is:$lp$, $lp$["The buyer's down payment", "The shortfall between foreclosure sale proceeds and the debt owed", "A type of security instrument", "An appraisal shortfall"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Down payment is unrelated.", "Correct. It is the gap between what the sale recovered and what remains owed.", "It is not an instrument type.", "It is not specifically an appraisal concept."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["deficiency"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-07-007$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 7, $lp$financing$lp$, 3, $lp$A purchase-money loan used to buy a qualifying owner-occupied residence, upon foreclosure, generally:$lp$, $lp$["Exposes the borrower to a deficiency judgment regardless of method", "Protects the borrower from a deficiency judgment", "Only applies if the lender is the seller", "Requires judicial foreclosure to be valid"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["This is the opposite of the anti-deficiency protection.", "Correct. Purchase-money loans on a qualifying residence are generally protected from deficiency judgments.", "The protection can apply to institutional purchase-money loans, not only seller carrybacks.", "The protection is not conditioned on using judicial foreclosure."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["purchase-money loan"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-07-008$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 7, $lp$financing$lp$, 3, $lp$A homeowner refinances an original purchase-money loan into a new loan with cash out. After a later default and foreclosure, the deficiency analysis for the new loan is:$lp$, $lp$["Identical to the original purchase-money protection", "Likely different, since the refinance is generally not treated as purchase-money", "Irrelevant because refinances are never enforceable", "Automatically fully protected due to the original purchase"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Refinancing generally changes the loan's protected status.", "Correct. A cash-out refinance is generally not treated as purchase-money, changing the deficiency analysis.", "Refinances are enforceable loans.", "The original purchase-money status does not automatically carry over."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["purchase-money loan"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-07-009$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 7, $lp$financing$lp$, 3, $lp$The one-action rule primarily:$lp$, $lp$["Requires a lender to exhaust its security before or as its sole method of pursuing the debt", "Allows unlimited simultaneous lawsuits on the same debt", "Applies only to unsecured debt", "Requires two separate foreclosure actions"]$lp$::jsonb, 0, $lp$["Correct. It limits the lender to one action, generally requiring the security to be pursued.", "This is the opposite of the rule's effect.", "It applies to secured real estate debt, not unsecured debt generally.", "It does not require duplicate foreclosure actions."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["one-action rule"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-07-010$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 7, $lp$financing$lp$, 2, $lp$The right of redemption after a foreclosure sale is available:$lp$, $lp$["After any foreclosure, judicial or non-judicial", "Only after a judicial foreclosure sale", "Only after a non-judicial trustee's sale", "Never in California"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Non-judicial sales do not carry a post-sale redemption right.", "Correct. Redemption rights attach only to judicial foreclosure sales.", "Non-judicial trustee's sales are final with no redemption right.", "Redemption exists in the judicial context."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["right of redemption"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-07-011$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 7, $lp$financing$lp$, 2, $lp$A completed non-judicial trustee's sale is generally:$lp$, $lp$["Reversible by the former owner at any time", "Final, with no statutory post-sale redemption right", "Subject to a mandatory one-year redemption period", "Automatically voided if the owner objects"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["It is not freely reversible by the former owner.", "Correct. Non-judicial sales are final with no post-sale redemption right.", "No such mandatory redemption period applies to non-judicial sales.", "An objection alone does not void a completed sale."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["non-judicial foreclosure"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-07-012$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 7, $lp$financing$lp$, 3, $lp$A borrower challenges whether the foreclosing party actually held the right to enforce the note. This is best described as a:$lp$, $lp$["Anti-deficiency claim", "Standing-to-foreclose challenge", "Redemption claim", "Quiet title claim only"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["This is not about deficiency exposure.", "Correct. This challenges whether the foreclosing party had the legal right to enforce the note.", "Redemption is a separate post-sale right, not this challenge.", "It is not specifically framed as quiet title, though related litigation could arise."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["wrongful foreclosure"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-07-013$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 7, $lp$financing$lp$, 2, $lp$A successful wrongful foreclosure claim can:$lp$, $lp$["Never affect a completed sale", "Potentially unwind a completed trustee's sale", "Only reduce the borrower's credit score", "Only apply to judicial foreclosures"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["It can affect a completed sale in appropriate circumstances.", "Correct. As a remedy of last resort, it can unwind a completed sale.", "Credit score is not the legal remedy at issue.", "Wrongful foreclosure claims can arise from non-judicial sales as well."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["wrongful foreclosure"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-07-014$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 7, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$When a licensee works with a homeowner facing foreclosure, the licensee may:$lp$, $lp$["Negotiate legal terms with the lender's legal department as legal representation", "Promise a specific deficiency outcome", "Discuss listing the property and explain the general timeline", "Advise on the homeowner's legal rights"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["Legal representation is outside license scope.", "Promising legal outcomes is outside license scope and misleading.", "Correct. Discussing listing options and general timelines is within the licensee's role.", "Advising on legal rights is outside license scope."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["distressed homeowner"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-07-015$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 7, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$The most valuable thing an agent can do for a homeowner facing foreclosure is:$lp$, $lp$["Wait until the trustee's sale is scheduled to act", "Refer them to a housing counselor or attorney early", "Discourage them from seeking outside help", "Handle all legal negotiations personally"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Waiting narrows the homeowner's available options.", "Correct. Early referral preserves the widest range of options.", "Discouraging outside help is poor and potentially harmful advice.", "Handling legal negotiations personally exceeds license scope."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["distressed homeowner"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-07-016$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 7, $lp$financing$lp$, 2, $lp$Non-judicial foreclosure is attractive to institutional lenders primarily because it is:$lp$, $lp$["Slower but more thorough", "Faster and cheaper, without requiring a lawsuit", "The only method that allows a deficiency judgment", "Required by federal law for all loans"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["It is faster, not slower.", "Correct. Speed and lower cost, without litigation, are the main advantages for lenders.", "Judicial foreclosure, not non-judicial, preserves the deficiency option.", "It is not a universal federal mandate."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["non-judicial foreclosure"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-07-017$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 7, $lp$financing$lp$, 2, $lp$A home equity line of credit (HELOC) taken out after the original purchase generally:$lp$, $lp$["Automatically carries the same purchase-money deficiency protection", "Is not automatically protected the same way as the original purchase-money loan", "Cannot be foreclosed upon", "Eliminates the original loan's protection retroactively"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["HELOCs are not automatically purchase-money loans.", "Correct. Non-purchase-money loans like HELOCs do not automatically carry the same protection.", "HELOCs secured by the property can be subject to foreclosure.", "It does not retroactively strip the original loan's own protection if that loan still exists separately."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["purchase-money loan"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-07-018$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 7, $lp$financing$lp$, 2, $lp$A trustee in a California deed of trust is best described as:$lp$, $lp$["The borrower", "The lender", "A neutral third party holding bare legal title as security", "A government regulator"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["The borrower is the trustor.", "The lender is the beneficiary.", "Correct. The trustee is a neutral party holding bare legal title with power of sale on default.", "The trustee is not a government regulator."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["deed of trust"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-08-001$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 8, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$The implied warranty of habitability:$lp$, $lp$["Can be waived if both parties agree in writing", "Cannot be waived by the lease", "Only applies to commercial leases", "Applies only in the first year of tenancy"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["It cannot be waived even by written agreement.", "Correct. It is non-waivable as a matter of public policy.", "It applies to residential leases, not commercial.", "It applies throughout the tenancy, not only the first year."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["implied warranty of habitability"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-08-002$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 8, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$The implied warranty of habitability requires that a unit be:$lp$, $lp$["Luxuriously finished", "Fit for human habitation and free of health/safety hazards", "Rent-controlled", "Owned outright by the landlord"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Luxury finishes are not required.", "Correct. Basic livability and safety are the standard.", "Rent control status is unrelated.", "Ownership structure is unrelated to habitability."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["implied warranty of habitability"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-08-003$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 8, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 3, $lp$California's Tenant Protection Act (AB 1482) generally imposes:$lp$, $lp$["No limits on rent increases", "A statewide rent cap and just-cause eviction requirement for most covered tenancies", "A ban on all evictions", "A requirement that all units be rent-controlled locally"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["It does impose limits for covered tenancies.", "Correct. It caps annual rent increases and requires just cause for eviction in most covered cases.", "It does not ban all evictions; it requires just cause.", "Local rent control is separate and additional, not required by AB 1482 itself."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["Tenant Protection Act (AB 1482)"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-08-004$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 8, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$Just cause for eviction generally falls into which two categories?$lp$, $lp$["Civil and criminal", "At-fault and no-fault", "Voluntary and involuntary", "Written and verbal"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["This is not the standard categorization.", "Correct. Just cause is generally categorized as at-fault or no-fault.", "This is not the standard categorization used.", "This is not the standard categorization used."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["just cause"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-08-005$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 8, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$An owner moving into the unit is generally an example of:$lp$, $lp$["At-fault just cause", "No-fault just cause", "Retaliatory eviction", "Constructive eviction"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["The tenant is not at fault in this scenario.", "Correct. Owner move-in is a no-fault just cause category, often triggering relocation assistance.", "This is not retaliation against the tenant.", "This is not about habitability failure."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["just cause"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-08-006$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 8, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 3, $lp$Self-help eviction, such as changing locks or shutting off utilities without a court judgment, is:$lp$, $lp$["Legal if the tenant is clearly behind on rent", "Illegal regardless of how clearly the tenant is in default", "Legal only for commercial tenants", "Legal with 24 hours' notice"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Clear tenant default does not legalize self-help eviction.", "Correct. Self-help eviction is illegal regardless of the merits of the underlying claim.", "This applies broadly, not only to commercial tenants in a way that changes the rule here.", "Notice alone does not make self-help eviction legal."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["self-help eviction"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-08-007$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 8, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$The court process required to lawfully remove a tenant is called:$lp$, $lp$["A quiet title action", "An unlawful detainer action", "A probate proceeding", "A workers' compensation claim"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Quiet title concerns ownership disputes, not eviction.", "Correct. Unlawful detainer is the required court process for eviction.", "Probate concerns estates, not eviction.", "Workers' compensation is unrelated."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["unlawful detainer (UD)"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-08-008$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 8, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$After a landlord wins an unlawful detainer judgment, physical removal of the tenant may be carried out by:$lp$, $lp$["The landlord personally", "The landlord's property manager", "The sheriff under a writ of possession", "Any licensed locksmith"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["Landlords may not personally remove tenants.", "Property managers also may not personally remove tenants.", "Correct. Only the sheriff, under a writ of possession, may carry out physical removal.", "A locksmith alone does not have authority to remove a tenant."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["unlawful detainer (UD)"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-08-009$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 8, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 3, $lp$Constructive eviction requires that the tenant:$lp$, $lp$["Stay in the unit and stop paying rent", "Give notice, allow a reasonable cure period, and actually vacate", "Sue for damages without moving out", "File for bankruptcy"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Staying and withholding rent is a weaker legal position than constructive eviction requires.", "Correct. Notice, a cure opportunity, and actual vacatur are generally required.", "A damages suit without vacating does not establish constructive eviction.", "Bankruptcy is unrelated to this doctrine."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["constructive eviction"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-08-010$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 8, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$A tenant who reports a serious mold problem, waits a reasonable time for repair despite repeated requests, then vacates after continued inaction has a strong claim of:$lp$, $lp$["Retaliatory eviction", "Constructive eviction", "Self-help eviction", "Adverse possession"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Retaliation is not the fact pattern here.", "Correct. Unremedied serious habitability failure plus proper notice and vacatur is constructive eviction.", "Self-help eviction describes illegal landlord lockout conduct, not this scenario.", "Adverse possession is unrelated to this scenario."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["constructive eviction"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-08-011$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 8, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 3, $lp$A landlord raises rent shortly after a tenant files a code enforcement complaint about habitability. This can trigger:$lp$, $lp$["Automatic dismissal of the complaint", "A rebuttable presumption of retaliatory eviction", "Immediate unlawful detainer against the landlord", "No legal consequence"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["The complaint is not automatically dismissed by this conduct.", "Correct. California law creates a rebuttable presumption of retaliation when adverse action follows protected activity within a statutory window.", "Unlawful detainer applies to removing tenants, not this scenario.", "There can be real legal consequence for the landlord."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["retaliatory eviction"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-08-012$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 8, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$Normal wear and tear, such as faded paint or worn carpet from ordinary use, is:$lp$, $lp$["Chargeable against the security deposit", "Not chargeable against the security deposit", "Only chargeable in commercial leases", "Chargeable only if the tenant stayed over a year"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["It cannot be charged to the tenant.", "Correct. Normal wear and tear is an ownership cost, not tenant damage.", "This distinction applies broadly, not only commercial leases.", "Tenancy length does not change this rule."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["normal wear and tear"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-08-013$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 8, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$When deducting from a security deposit, a landlord must generally:$lp$, $lp$["Provide no explanation", "Provide an itemized statement and, for larger deductions, documentation", "Keep the entire deposit regardless of condition", "Wait indefinitely to return the balance"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["An explanation is required.", "Correct. Itemization and documentation for larger deductions are required, with the balance returned within a statutory period.", "The landlord may not simply keep the full deposit without basis.", "There is a statutory time limit to return the balance."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["security deposit"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-08-014$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 8, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 3, $lp$Repair-and-deduct as a tenant remedy generally:$lp$, $lp$["Allows unlimited repairs deducted at any time", "Has statutory dollar and frequency limits with procedural notice requirements", "Requires no prior notice to the landlord", "Is available only to commercial tenants"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["It is not unlimited; statutory limits apply.", "Correct. Statutory limits and required notice/procedure apply before a tenant may use this remedy.", "Prior notice is generally required.", "It is a residential tenant remedy in this context."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["repair-and-deduct"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-08-015$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 8, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$Rent withholding as a habitability remedy:$lp$, $lp$["Carries no risk to the tenant", "Carries real risk of eviction if the justification is later found insufficient", "Is always the safest tenant option", "Requires no documentation"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["It carries real legal risk.", "Correct. If a court finds the withholding unjustified, the tenant risks an eviction action.", "It is a genuinely risky remedy, not the safest option by default.", "Careful documentation is essential to support the claim."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["rent withholding"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-08-016$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 8, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$A landlord frustrated with the unlawful detainer timeline changes the locks while the tenant is at work. Even if the tenant genuinely owes back rent, the landlord:$lp$, $lp$["Has acted lawfully because the tenant was in default", "Has committed an independently illegal act exposing them to statutory damages", "Has no liability since it saved court costs", "Must only pay a small administrative fine"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Tenant default does not excuse self-help eviction.", "Correct. Self-help eviction is illegal on its own terms, regardless of the underlying claim's merit.", "Liability is real and can be significant, not avoided by cost savings.", "The exposure is a real damages claim, not merely a small fine."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["self-help eviction"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-08-017$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 8, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$A licensee involved in property management who encounters a genuine just-cause compliance question should:$lp$, $lp$["Guess based on general knowledge", "Recognize the issue, document it, and refer to an attorney or specialized expertise", "Ignore it since it is the owner's problem", "Tell the tenant the eviction is definitely valid"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Guessing on a legal compliance question risks real harm.", "Correct. Recognize, document, and refer is the consistent professional standard.", "Property managers have real duties here; it is not simply the owner's problem alone.", "Definitively confirming validity is a legal determination outside license scope."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["just cause"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-08-018$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 8, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$Exemptions from all or part of California's statewide rent cap and just-cause framework can include:$lp$, $lp$["No properties are ever exempt", "Certain single-family homes and condos with proper notice, and some newer construction", "Only properties owned by corporations", "Only properties outside California"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Exemptions do exist for certain property types.", "Correct. Certain single-family/condo properties with proper notice and newer construction can be exempt, subject to specific requirements.", "Ownership structure alone does not determine exemption.", "The framework is a California state law; this option is not the relevant exemption category."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["Tenant Protection Act (AB 1482)"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-09-001$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 9, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$Zoning regulation is grounded in:$lp$, $lp$["Eminent domain", "Police power", "Inverse condemnation", "Adverse possession"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Eminent domain involves a formal taking with compensation.", "Correct. Zoning rests on police power, regulating without compensation.", "Inverse condemnation is a claim against uncompensated government action.", "Adverse possession is unrelated to zoning authority."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["police power"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-09-002$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 9, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$Eminent domain differs from police power because eminent domain:$lp$, $lp$["Requires just compensation for a taking", "Never requires compensation", "Only applies to zoning", "Is the same legal doctrine as police power"]$lp$::jsonb, 0, $lp$["Correct. Eminent domain requires just compensation for a formal taking.", "Police power, not eminent domain, generally does not require compensation.", "Eminent domain is broader than zoning specifically.", "They are distinct doctrines."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["police power"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-09-003$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 9, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 3, $lp$Inverse condemnation is best described as:$lp$, $lp$["A government lawsuit to acquire property", "A property owner's claim that uncompensated government action effectively took or damaged property", "A tax assessment appeal", "A type of zoning variance"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["It is the owner's claim, not the government's acquisition action.", "Correct. It is the owner's remedy when government action effectively takes or damages property without formal compensation.", "It is not a tax appeal.", "It is not a zoning variance."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["inverse condemnation"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-09-004$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 9, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 3, $lp$A variance is best described as:$lp$, $lp$["A grandfathered pre-existing use", "Prospective permission to deviate from zoning for a specific parcel due to unique hardship", "A federal environmental permit", "Automatic upon request"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["That describes a nonconforming use.", "Correct. A variance is forward-looking relief granted for a specific parcel's unique hardship.", "It is a local land-use approval, not a federal environmental permit.", "It requires an application and hearing process, not automatic grant."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["variance"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-09-005$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 9, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$A legal nonconforming use is:$lp$, $lp$["A newly requested exception to current zoning", "A use lawful when established that no longer conforms to later-adopted zoning", "Always illegal and must be terminated immediately", "The same thing as a variance"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["It is not a newly requested exception.", "Correct. It is a grandfathered pre-existing use under later zoning changes.", "It is generally allowed to continue, not automatically illegal.", "It is a distinct concept from a variance."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["legal nonconforming use"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-09-006$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 9, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 3, $lp$An older structure built before a setback rule existed and continuously used since then most likely qualifies as:$lp$, $lp$["A variance", "A legal nonconforming use", "An eminent domain taking", "An unlawful detainer"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["It was not granted prospectively for a new deviation; it predates the rule.", "Correct. Continuous pre-existing use under an older rule is a nonconforming use.", "This is unrelated to eminent domain.", "Unlawful detainer is unrelated to zoning."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["legal nonconforming use"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-09-007$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 9, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$A legal nonconforming use is generally lost if:$lp$, $lp$["The property is sold to a new owner", "It is abandoned for a specified period or the structure is destroyed beyond a threshold and not rebuilt", "The city changes its general plan", "The owner paints the building"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["A sale alone does not eliminate a nonconforming use.", "Correct. Abandonment or significant destruction without timely rebuilding can end the grandfathered right.", "A general plan change alone does not automatically strip existing nonconforming status.", "Painting is unrelated."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["legal nonconforming use"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-09-008$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 9, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$CEQA primarily requires:$lp$, $lp$["Government agencies to evaluate and disclose environmental impacts of discretionary projects", "Private sellers to remediate all contamination before listing", "Buyers to waive environmental review", "Only federal agencies to conduct review"]$lp$::jsonb, 0, $lp$["Correct. CEQA requires state/local agency evaluation and disclosure of significant environmental impacts.", "CEQA does not impose a private seller remediation mandate by itself.", "Buyers are not required to waive review under CEQA.", "CEQA is a California state law, not limited to federal agencies."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["CEQA"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-09-009$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 9, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 3, $lp$Under CEQA, if an Initial Study finds a project may have significant environmental effects that cannot be avoided, the likely next step is:$lp$, $lp$["Automatic project denial", "A Negative Declaration or a full Environmental Impact Report", "Immediate approval with no further review", "A quiet title action"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Significant effects do not automatically mean denial.", "Correct. Depending on whether impacts can be mitigated, a Negative Declaration or EIR follows.", "Further review, not immediate approval, follows a finding of significant effects.", "Quiet title is unrelated to CEQA review."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["Environmental Impact Report (EIR)"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-09-010$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 9, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$An Environmental Impact Report (EIR) is generally required when:$lp$, $lp$["A project is fully exempt from CEQA", "A project may have significant environmental effects not avoidable through minor changes", "Every routine building permit is issued", "Never for residential development"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Exempt projects do not require an EIR.", "Correct. An EIR is used for significant, unavoidable environmental effects.", "Routine permits typically do not require a full EIR.", "EIRs can apply to residential development under the right circumstances."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["Environmental Impact Report (EIR)"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-09-011$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 9, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$California's Housing Accountability Act framework generally:$lp$, $lp$["Gives local governments unlimited discretion to deny housing", "Limits local denial of qualifying housing developments meeting objective standards", "Bans all new housing development", "Applies only to commercial projects"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["It limits, rather than expands, unrestricted local denial power.", "Correct. It restricts denial or unreasonable conditioning of qualifying, objective-standard-compliant housing projects.", "It is designed to increase, not ban, housing development.", "It concerns housing development, not primarily commercial projects."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["Housing Accountability Act"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-09-012$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 9, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$Recent California state law has generally made ADU (accessory dwelling unit) approval:$lp$, $lp$["More restrictive than before", "More permissive, often allowed by right in many single-family zones subject to state standards", "Illegal statewide", "Only available to commercial developers"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["The trend has been toward permissiveness, not restriction.", "Correct. State law has expanded ADU rights, limiting how restrictive local rules can be.", "ADUs are not illegal; state law has expanded their availability.", "ADUs primarily concern single-family and residential property owners, not commercial developers specifically."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["Housing Accountability Act"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-09-013$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 9, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$Under federal and California hazardous substance cleanup law, liability for contamination cleanup can attach to:$lp$, $lp$["Only the party who originally caused the contamination", "Current and prior owners, in some cases regardless of fault", "No one if the contamination is old enough", "Only government agencies"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Liability can reach beyond only the original polluter.", "Correct. Current and prior owners can face cleanup liability regardless of fault in some circumstances.", "Age of contamination does not eliminate liability exposure.", "Private owners, not only government agencies, can face this liability."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["Superfund/state cleanup liability"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-09-014$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 9, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$A buyer considering a commercial parcel with a decades-long history as a dry-cleaning business should be advised to consider:$lp$, $lp$["Skipping environmental due diligence to save time", "A Phase I Environmental Site Assessment before removing contingencies", "Relying solely on the seller's verbal assurance", "Waiting until after closing to investigate"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Skipping due diligence on a red-flag site risks major exposure.", "Correct. A Phase I assessment is the standard first step given the contamination-risk profile.", "A verbal assurance is not adequate due diligence.", "Waiting until after closing removes the ability to negotiate or walk away."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["Phase I Environmental Site Assessment"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-09-015$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 9, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$A Phase II environmental assessment typically follows a Phase I when:$lp$, $lp$["The Phase I found no concerns at all", "The Phase I flagged potential contamination risk warranting actual testing", "The buyer wants a lower price only", "The seller requests it regardless of findings"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["No further testing is typically needed if Phase I found no concerns.", "Correct. Phase II involves actual soil/groundwater testing triggered by Phase I findings.", "Price negotiation alone does not drive the Phase I/Phase II sequence.", "Phase II follows from Phase I findings, not simply seller preference."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["Phase I Environmental Site Assessment"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-09-016$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 9, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$Lead-based paint disclosure under federal law is specifically required for:$lp$, $lp$["All commercial property", "Housing built before 1978", "New construction only", "Rental property only, never sales"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["It is a residential requirement tied to housing age, not all commercial property.", "Correct. Federal law requires lead-based paint disclosure for pre-1978 housing.", "New construction after 1978 does not carry this same requirement.", "It applies to both sales and rentals of qualifying pre-1978 housing."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["lead-based paint disclosure"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-09-017$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 9, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$A property using a well and septic system rather than municipal utilities:$lp$, $lp$["Requires no additional disclosure consideration", "Carries additional environmental and disclosure considerations beyond a standard municipal-utility transaction", "Cannot legally be sold", "Is automatically exempt from NHD requirements"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Additional considerations do apply.", "Correct. Wells and septic systems raise distinct environmental and disclosure issues.", "Such properties can be lawfully sold with proper disclosure.", "NHD requirements are separate and not automatically waived by utility type."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["wells and septic systems"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-09-018$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 9, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$When a licensee spots a likely CEQA trigger, a possible zoning nonconformity, or an environmental red flag, the correct action is to:$lp$, $lp$["Resolve it personally using general real estate knowledge", "Recognize it and route it to the appropriate specialist before the client is contractually committed", "Ignore it unless the client specifically asks", "Wait until after closing to raise it"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["These are specialist determinations outside general license scope.", "Correct. Early recognition and referral is the professional standard.", "Proactive recognition, not waiting to be asked, is the standard expected of a licensee.", "Waiting until after closing removes the client's ability to act on the information."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["CEQA"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-10-001$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 10, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$When federal and California fair housing protections differ, California real estate practice generally follows:$lp$, $lp$["Only the federal standard", "The more protective standard, typically California's broader list", "Whichever standard the agent prefers", "Only local city ordinances"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Federal law alone would omit California-specific protections.", "Correct. The more protective standard generally controls in California.", "Agent preference does not determine which law applies.", "Local ordinances may add further protections but do not replace this rule."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["Civil Rights Department (CRD)"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-10-002$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 10, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$Which is a California-specific protected class not guaranteed the same way under the federal FHA?$lp$, $lp$["Race", "Source of income", "National origin", "Disability"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Race is a federal protected class.", "Correct. Source of income, including housing vouchers, is a notable California-specific expansion.", "National origin is a federal protected class.", "Disability is a federal protected class."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["source of income protection"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-10-003$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 10, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$Refusing to rent to a qualified applicant because they intend to pay with a housing voucher is:$lp$, $lp$["Permitted, since vouchers are not guaranteed income", "Discrimination under California's source-of-income protection", "Only a federal violation", "Allowed if disclosed in advance"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Voucher-based refusal is not permitted in California.", "Correct. Treating voucher income differently from other lawful income is discriminatory under California law.", "This is specifically a California-law issue as much as federal.", "Advance disclosure of an intent to discriminate does not make it lawful."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["source of income protection"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-10-004$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 10, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 3, $lp$Disparate treatment requires:$lp$, $lp$["A neutral policy with unequal statistical effect", "Intentional differential treatment because of a protected characteristic", "No proof of intent whatsoever", "Only a written policy"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["That describes disparate impact, not treatment.", "Correct. Disparate treatment is intentional discrimination.", "Disparate treatment requires intent, unlike disparate impact.", "It can occur through unwritten conduct, not only written policy."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["disparate treatment"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-10-005$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 10, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 3, $lp$Disparate impact liability can arise from:$lp$, $lp$["Only explicit statements naming a protected class", "A facially neutral policy with a statistically disproportionate discriminatory effect and no defensible business necessity", "Only intentional acts", "Advertising alone, never screening policies"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Explicit statements are more typical of disparate treatment.", "Correct. Disparate impact targets neutral policies with discriminatory effect, absent a valid business necessity.", "Intent is not required for disparate impact.", "Screening policies, not just advertising, can trigger disparate impact analysis."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["disparate impact"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-10-006$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 10, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 3, $lp$A property manager sets a minimum credit score requirement well above what is needed to ensure ability to pay rent, with no alternative proof-of-payment option. This practice most directly raises a concern under:$lp$, $lp$["Disparate treatment theory only", "Disparate impact theory", "The parol evidence rule", "The Statute of Frauds"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["There is no stated intent to discriminate, so disparate treatment is not the primary theory here.", "Correct. A facially neutral criterion set well above business necessity can have a disparate statistical effect.", "The parol evidence rule is unrelated.", "The Statute of Frauds is unrelated."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["disparate impact"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-10-007$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 10, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$Steering is best described as:$lp$, $lp$["Denying loans based on neighborhood racial composition", "Directing prospects toward or away from neighborhoods based on a protected characteristic", "Inducing panic selling based on demographic change", "Requiring a credit check"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["That describes redlining.", "Correct. Steering directs prospects based on a protected characteristic.", "That describes blockbuking.", "Credit checks alone are not steering."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["steering"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-10-008$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 10, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$Redlining is best described as:$lp$, $lp$["Directing buyers to specific listings", "Denying or limiting financial services based on an area's racial or ethnic composition", "A property line dispute", "A type of zoning variance"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["That describes steering, not redlining.", "Correct. Redlining denies or limits services based on area demographics rather than individual creditworthiness.", "It is unrelated to boundary disputes.", "It is unrelated to zoning variances."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["redlining"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-10-009$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 10, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$Blockbuking involves:$lp$, $lp$["Inducing panic selling by suggesting protected-class residents will lower property values", "A method of legal description", "A type of foreclosure", "A form of dual agency"]$lp$::jsonb, 0, $lp$["Correct. Blockbuking exploits fear of demographic change to induce panic sales.", "It is unrelated to legal descriptions.", "It is unrelated to foreclosure.", "It is unrelated to dual agency."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["blockbuking"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-10-010$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 10, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 3, $lp$An agent tells a family, based on assumptions about the neighborhood's demographics, 'I think you'd be happier somewhere else.' This is most accurately an example of:$lp$, $lp$["Legitimate market advice", "Steering", "A reasonable accommodation", "Blockbuking"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["This is not legitimate neutral advice; it is based on protected-class assumptions.", "Correct. Directing prospects based on assumed characteristics is steering, even when framed helpfully.", "This is unrelated to disability accommodation.", "This does not involve inducing panic selling."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["steering"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-10-011$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 10, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$A reasonable accommodation is:$lp$, $lp$["A required physical structural change", "A required change to a rule, policy, or practice", "Always paid for by the tenant", "Optional even for legitimate disability needs"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["That describes a reasonable modification.", "Correct. Accommodation involves policy/rule changes, generally at the landlord's expense.", "Accommodations are generally provided at the landlord's expense, not the tenant's.", "It is a required obligation, not optional, when legitimate and reasonable."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["reasonable accommodation"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-10-012$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 10, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$A reasonable modification is:$lp$, $lp$["A change to a rule or policy", "A physical structural change, often at the tenant's expense", "Never permitted in rental housing", "The same thing as a reasonable accommodation"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["That describes a reasonable accommodation.", "Correct. Modifications are physical changes, and the tenant often bears the cost though the landlord must permit it.", "Landlords generally must permit reasonable modifications.", "They are legally distinct concepts, though related."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["reasonable modification"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-10-013$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 10, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 3, $lp$A tenant with legitimate documentation requests an emotional support animal in a building with a strict no-pets policy and breed restrictions. The landlord should generally:$lp$, $lp$["Apply the no-pets policy and breed restriction as written", "Treat this as a reasonable accommodation request and grant it absent a direct threat or fundamental alteration", "Charge a pet deposit and pet rent", "Deny it automatically since it is a dog"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["The no-pets policy and breed restrictions do not apply to a qualifying assistance animal.", "Correct. This is an accommodation request, generally required to be granted when legitimate.", "Assistance animals cannot be charged pet deposits or pet rent.", "Automatic denial based on species or breed is not the correct standard."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["reasonable accommodation"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-10-014$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 10, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$An assistance/support animal for fair housing purposes is treated as:$lp$, $lp$["A pet subject to standard pet policies", "Not a pet, and not subject to pet deposits or breed restrictions", "Only allowed with special written permission from HUD", "Prohibited in all rental housing"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["It is not treated as an ordinary pet.", "Correct. Fair housing law excludes qualifying assistance animals from pet policies, deposits, and breed restrictions.", "No separate HUD permission process is required for this protection to apply.", "It is not prohibited; it must generally be accommodated."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["reasonable accommodation"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-10-015$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 10, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$Federal fair housing complaints are investigated primarily by:$lp$, $lp$["The IRS", "HUD", "The county assessor", "The DRE only"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["The IRS is unrelated to fair housing enforcement.", "Correct. HUD investigates federal Fair Housing Act complaints.", "The assessor is unrelated to fair housing enforcement.", "The DRE handles licensee discipline but is not the primary federal enforcement body."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["Civil Rights Department (CRD)"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-10-016$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 10, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$California's state agency enforcing FEHA and Unruh Act housing protections is:$lp$, $lp$["HUD", "The Civil Rights Department (CRD)", "DFPI", "The DRE"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["HUD is the federal enforcement agency.", "Correct. California's Civil Rights Department (formerly DFEH) enforces FEHA and Unruh Act housing protections.", "DFPI regulates financial services, not fair housing enforcement.", "The DRE handles real estate licensing, not the primary fair housing enforcement body."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["Civil Rights Department (CRD)"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-10-017$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 10, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 3, $lp$A fair housing tester who never genuinely intended to rent a unit but posed as a prospect to detect discrimination:$lp$, $lp$["Has no legal standing to sue", "Has legal standing to sue, and their evidence is generally admissible", "Can only report findings to the press", "Must disclose their tester status before visiting a property"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Testers do have recognized legal standing.", "Correct. Testers have standing to sue, and their evidence is generally admissible despite lacking genuine rental intent.", "Testers can pursue formal legal remedies, not only press reporting.", "Testers are not required to disclose their status in advance to be effective and have standing."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["fair housing testers"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-10-018$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 10, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$Marketing language describing a listing as 'perfect for a young family' raises a compliance concern because it:$lp$, $lp$["Is always illegal regardless of context", "Edges toward familial-status-coded language that should be avoided", "Is required for effective marketing", "Only matters for rental listings, never sales"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Context matters, but this phrasing carries real risk and should be avoided.", "Correct. It signals a preferred resident type tied to a protected characteristic.", "Effective marketing can and should avoid such language.", "Advertising rules apply to both sales and rental marketing."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["advertising compliance"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-10-019$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 10, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$Remedies for a fair housing violation can include:$lp$, $lp$["Only a verbal warning", "Actual damages, penalties, injunctive relief, and attorney's fees, plus possible license discipline for a real estate licensee", "No remedies unless HUD sues personally", "Only criminal prosecution"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["This undersells the range of available remedies.", "Correct. Multiple remedies exist, and licensees face independent license discipline risk.", "Private lawsuits are also available without requiring HUD to be the plaintiff.", "Criminal prosecution is not the typical primary remedy in most fair housing cases."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["Civil Rights Department (CRD)"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-10-020$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 10, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$Good intent is:$lp$, $lp$["A complete defense to both disparate treatment and disparate impact claims", "A defense to disparate treatment but not to disparate impact", "A defense to disparate impact but not disparate treatment", "Never relevant to either theory"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Good intent does not fully defeat disparate impact claims.", "Correct. Lack of intent can defeat disparate treatment but does not excuse a discriminatory statistical effect under disparate impact.", "This reverses the correct relationship.", "Intent is relevant, particularly to disparate treatment claims."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["disparate impact"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-11-001$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 11, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$An easement created by a written deed or specific agreement is an example of:$lp$, $lp$["Prescription", "Express grant", "Necessity", "Implication"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Prescription arises from use, not a writing.", "Correct. Express grant is created by a written instrument.", "Necessity arises from landlocked circumstances, not a writing alone.", "Implication arises from circumstances at severance, not a writing."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["express grant"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-11-002$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 11, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 3, $lp$An easement by implication typically requires that the use, at the time of severance, was:$lp$, $lp$["Secret and occasional", "Apparent, continuous, and reasonably necessary", "Created only by court order", "Paid for with property taxes"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Secret, occasional use does not support implication.", "Correct. Apparent, continuous, reasonably necessary use at severance supports an implied easement.", "Court order is not required for implication.", "Tax payment is unrelated to easement by implication."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["easement by implication"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-11-003$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 11, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 3, $lp$An easement by necessity generally requires:$lp$, $lp$["Mere convenience of access", "Strict necessity, such as a landlocked parcel from a prior division of commonly owned land", "A recorded survey only", "Continuous use for the statutory period"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Mere convenience is not enough; strict necessity is required.", "Correct. Easement by necessity requires strict necessity arising from a division of previously commonly owned land.", "A recorded survey alone does not create this easement.", "Statutory-period continuous use describes prescription, not necessity."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["easement by necessity"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-11-004$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 11, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$A prescriptive easement differs from adverse possession in ownership terms because a prescriptive easement:$lp$, $lp$["Conveys full ownership of the land", "Conveys only a right to use, not ownership", "Requires payment of property taxes just like adverse possession", "Cannot ever be established in California"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Full ownership describes adverse possession, not a prescriptive easement.", "Correct. A prescriptive easement grants only a right to use, not title.", "Unlike adverse possession, prescriptive easements do not require tax payment.", "Prescriptive easements can be established in California under the right facts."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["prescriptive easement"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-11-005$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 11, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$A key distinction between a prescriptive easement and adverse possession is that:$lp$, $lp$["Adverse possession does not require tax payment but prescription does", "Prescription does not require tax payment on the servient land, while adverse possession does", "Both require identical tax payment obligations", "Neither requires open and notorious use"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["This reverses the actual rule.", "Correct. Prescriptive easements do not require tax payment; adverse possession does.", "Their tax requirements differ, not match.", "Both generally require open and notorious use as an element."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["prescriptive easement"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-11-006$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 11, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$An easement appurtenant:$lp$, $lp$["Benefits a specific person, not land", "Benefits a specific parcel and runs automatically with the land", "Cannot be created by deed", "Always terminates upon sale of either parcel"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["That describes an easement in gross.", "Correct. It benefits a dominant parcel and runs with the land automatically.", "It can be created by deed, among other methods.", "It generally survives a sale of either parcel; it does not automatically terminate."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["easement appurtenant"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-11-007$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 11, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$A utility company's easement to run power lines across a property is typically an example of:$lp$, $lp$["Easement appurtenant", "Easement in gross", "Prescriptive easement only", "Easement by necessity only"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["It does not benefit a specific dominant parcel.", "Correct. It benefits a specific entity, characteristic of an easement in gross.", "It is typically created by grant, not solely by prescription.", "It is not created solely through necessity."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["easement in gross"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-11-008$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 11, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$Merger as a method of easement termination occurs when:$lp$, $lp$["The dominant tenement owner sells the property", "The same person acquires both the dominant and servient tenements", "The easement is used more frequently", "A survey is recorded"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["A sale to a different owner does not itself terminate the easement.", "Correct. Merger eliminates the two-separate-parcels requirement for an easement to exist.", "Increased use does not terminate an easement.", "Recording a survey does not itself terminate an easement."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["merger"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-11-009$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 11, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$Abandonment of an easement generally requires:$lp$, $lp$["Mere non-use for any period", "Conduct clearly showing intent to permanently stop using the easement", "A written release only", "Nothing; it is automatic after one year"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Non-use alone is generally insufficient.", "Correct. Clear conduct showing intent to permanently abandon is required.", "A written release is a separate termination method, not required for abandonment specifically.", "There is no automatic one-year abandonment rule described here."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["abandonment"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-11-010$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 11, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$An encroachment is best described as:$lp$, $lp$["A recorded easement", "An unauthorized physical intrusion of a structure onto neighboring land", "A type of nuisance lawsuit", "A boundary survey report"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["It is not itself a recorded easement.", "Correct. It is an unauthorized physical intrusion onto neighboring property.", "It is a physical condition, not itself a lawsuit type.", "It is not the survey report itself, though a survey can reveal one."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["encroachment"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-11-011$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 11, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 3, $lp$A survey reveals a wall has sat fourteen inches onto a neighbor's lot for over twenty years without objection. A practical, commonly used resolution is:$lp$, $lp$["Ignoring it since it has been that way for years", "A negotiated, recorded boundary line agreement between the neighbors", "Immediate forced demolition with no negotiation", "Assuming the issue disappears at closing"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Ignoring it leaves the issue unresolved and risks future disputes.", "Correct. A recorded boundary line agreement is often the most practical, cost-effective resolution.", "Forced demolition without negotiation is not the typical or most practical first step.", "The issue does not disappear automatically at closing and must be disclosed."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["boundary line agreement"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-11-012$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 11, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$Failing to object to a longstanding encroachment risks:$lp$, $lp$["Nothing at all", "The encroacher eventually establishing a prescriptive easement or similar claim", "Automatic transfer of full ownership within one year", "Immediate criminal liability for the property owner"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Real legal risk can develop over time.", "Correct. Undisturbed, continuous encroachment can support a prescriptive claim after the statutory period.", "There is no automatic one-year full ownership transfer.", "This is a civil property matter, not typically a criminal one."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["prescriptive easement"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-11-013$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 11, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$Private nuisance differs from public nuisance because private nuisance:$lp$, $lp$["Affects the broader community", "Affects a particular person's use and enjoyment of their specific property", "Can only be pursued by government agencies", "Requires proof of a recorded easement"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["That describes public nuisance.", "Correct. Private nuisance affects a specific owner's use and enjoyment.", "Private nuisance is generally pursued by the affected private party.", "Nuisance claims do not require a recorded easement."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["private nuisance"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-11-014$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 11, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$A public nuisance:$lp$, $lp$["Affects only one neighboring property", "Affects the broader community and can be addressed by government or private parties", "Is always resolved through a boundary survey", "Cannot ever result in legal action"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Public nuisance reaches beyond one property.", "Correct. It affects the community broadly and multiple parties can pursue action.", "A survey resolves boundary disputes, not nuisance claims generally.", "Legal action is available for a genuine public nuisance."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["public nuisance"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-11-015$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 11, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$A common defense to a nuisance claim is:$lp$, $lp$["That the interference is not substantial under a reasonableness standard", "That the defendant owns the property", "That the plaintiff is a real estate licensee", "That the county has issued a business license, which is always a complete defense"]$lp$::jsonb, 0, $lp$["Correct. Courts apply a reasonableness standard; minor interference is not automatically actionable.", "Property ownership alone is not a defense to causing a nuisance.", "The plaintiff's occupation is irrelevant to the nuisance analysis.", "A business license is a factor but not automatically a complete defense."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["private nuisance"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-11-016$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 11, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$A licensed land surveyor's boundary survey primarily:$lp$, $lp$["Insures title against defects", "Establishes the precise legal location of property lines", "Replaces the need for title insurance entirely", "Determines zoning classification"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Title insurance, not a survey, insures against title defects.", "Correct. A survey establishes precise legal boundary locations.", "A survey does not replace the separate function of title insurance.", "Zoning classification is a separate governmental determination."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["boundary survey"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-11-017$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 11, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$A preliminary title report differs from a boundary survey because the preliminary report:$lp$, $lp$["Physically locates the boundary on the ground", "Identifies recorded interests but does not resolve the physical boundary location", "Is prepared by a licensed surveyor", "Is unrelated to real estate transactions"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Physical location on the ground is the survey's job, not the prelim's.", "Correct. The prelim addresses recorded interests, not the physical boundary itself.", "A prelim is prepared by a title company, not a surveyor.", "It is directly related to real estate transactions."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["boundary survey"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-11-018$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 11, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$A known boundary dispute or encroachment on a listed property should be:$lp$, $lp$["Omitted if the neighbors have handled it informally for years", "Disclosed as a material fact", "Only disclosed if the buyer specifically asks", "Left for the buyer's attorney to discover independently"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Informal handling does not excuse disclosure of a known material fact.", "Correct. A known boundary issue is a material fact requiring disclosure.", "Disclosure duties do not depend on the buyer asking first.", "The seller/agent has an affirmative disclosure duty, not a passive one."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["encroachment"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-12-001$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 12, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$A mechanics' lien is best described as:$lp$, $lp$["A lien against the contractor's personal assets only", "A statutory lien against the improved property for unpaid labor or materials", "A type of title insurance policy", "A form of easement"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["It attaches to the property, not just the contractor's personal assets.", "Correct. It is a statutory lien against the improved property.", "It is unrelated to title insurance.", "It is not an easement."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["mechanics' lien"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-12-002$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 12, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$Parties who may file a mechanics' lien generally include:$lp$, $lp$["Only the general contractor", "General contractors, subcontractors, laborers, and material suppliers", "Only licensed architects", "Only the property's title company"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Lien rights extend beyond only the general contractor.", "Correct. Multiple tiers of contractors, laborers, and suppliers may qualify.", "Architects are not the only eligible category.", "Title companies do not file mechanics' liens."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["mechanics' lien"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-12-003$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 12, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 3, $lp$A preliminary 20-day notice is significant because it:$lp$, $lp$["Is optional and rarely used", "Is generally required to preserve most claimants' future lien rights", "Immediately creates a recorded lien", "Only applies to general contractors with a direct owner contract"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["It is a required step for most claimants, not merely optional.", "Correct. Serving this early notice generally preserves the right to later file a lien.", "It preserves lien rights; it is not itself a recorded lien.", "It is required especially for those without a direct owner contract, such as subcontractors and suppliers."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["preliminary 20-day notice"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-12-004$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 12, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 3, $lp$A homeowner pays the general contractor in full for a remodel. A subcontractor who was never paid by the general contractor then records a mechanics' lien. This is possible because:$lp$, $lp$["The subcontractor's lien right runs against the property regardless of the owner's payment to the GC", "Subcontractors can never file liens", "The homeowner's payment automatically protects against all liens", "Liens can only be filed by architects"]$lp$::jsonb, 0, $lp$["Correct. Payment to the GC does not automatically protect the owner from a subcontractor's lien right.", "Subcontractors are a recognized category of lien claimant.", "Full payment to the GC does not automatically eliminate sub lien exposure.", "Architects are not the only party who can file liens."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["mechanics' lien"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-12-005$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 12, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$A practical risk-management step for homeowners undertaking significant construction is:$lp$, $lp$["Avoiding any written contract with the contractor", "Obtaining conditional lien releases tied to each payment stage", "Paying the full contract amount upfront before any work begins", "Ignoring subcontractor payment status entirely"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["A written contract is important, not something to avoid.", "Correct. Conditional releases tied to payment stages help manage lien exposure.", "Paying in full upfront increases risk rather than reducing it.", "Ignoring subcontractor payment status increases lien exposure."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["conditional release"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-12-006$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 12, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 3, $lp$A conditional lien release differs from an unconditional release because a conditional release:$lp$, $lp$["Is effective immediately upon signing regardless of payment", "Is effective only once payment actually clears", "Can never be used in California", "Applies only to general contractors"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["That describes an unconditional release.", "Correct. A conditional release protects against a payment that later fails to clear.", "Conditional releases are a recognized, commonly used tool in California.", "It can apply to subcontractors and suppliers as well as general contractors."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["conditional release"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-12-007$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 12, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 3, $lp$An unconditional lien release carries risk because:$lp$, $lp$["It cannot be signed before payment", "It is effective immediately upon signing, even if a payment check later bounces", "It automatically voids the underlying contract", "It is illegal in California"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["It can be signed before or after payment in various circumstances, which is part of the risk.", "Correct. Signing it immediately releases lien rights regardless of whether payment later fails.", "It does not void the underlying contract.", "It is a legally recognized release type, not illegal."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["unconditional release"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-12-008$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 12, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 3, $lp$Mechanics' lien priority is unusual because a lien can:$lp$, $lp$["Never gain priority over a construction loan", "Relate back to the commencement of work, potentially outranking a later-recorded but earlier-work-preceding construction loan", "Only apply to commercial property", "Automatically be junior to all recorded interests"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Liens can, in specified circumstances, outrank certain later-recorded interests.", "Correct. Relation-back to commencement of work is a distinctive and complex priority feature.", "It applies to residential as well as commercial improvements.", "Priority is not automatically junior; relation-back can change the analysis."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["mechanics' lien"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-12-009$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 12, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$Construction lenders coordinate deed of trust recording timing carefully because:$lp$, $lp$["Mechanics' lien relation-back rules can otherwise outrank the loan's priority", "Recording timing has no effect on priority", "Mechanics' liens are always junior to construction loans regardless of timing", "Only owners need to worry about lien priority"]$lp$::jsonb, 0, $lp$["Correct. Careful timing helps protect the lender's priority against relation-back mechanics' lien risk.", "Recording timing has real priority consequences here.", "Liens are not automatically junior; relation-back can change outcomes.", "Lenders have a direct financial stake in this risk as well."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["mechanics' lien"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-12-010$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 12, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$Mechanics' lien recording and foreclosure-filing deadlines are:$lp$, $lp$["Flexible and rarely enforced", "Strictly enforced statutory limits that can extinguish an otherwise valid claim if missed", "Irrelevant to lien enforceability", "Only applicable to commercial construction"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["They are strictly enforced, not flexible.", "Correct. Missing these deadlines can extinguish an otherwise valid lien claim.", "They are directly relevant to whether a lien can be enforced.", "They apply broadly, not only to commercial construction."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["mechanics' lien"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-12-011$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 12, $lp$contracts$lp$, 2, $lp$California's Right to Repair Act primarily addresses:$lp$, $lp$["Mechanics' lien filing deadlines", "Statutory construction standards and a pre-litigation notice/repair process for new-home defect claims", "Fair housing advertising rules", "Escrow licensing"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["This is a separate topic from lien deadlines.", "Correct. It establishes construction standards and a required notice/repair process before litigation.", "It is unrelated to fair housing advertising.", "It is unrelated to escrow licensing."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["Right to Repair Act"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-12-012$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 12, $lp$contracts$lp$, 3, $lp$A buyer of a new home discovers a suspected foundation-related defect and wants to sue the builder. Under the Right to Repair framework, the buyer generally must first:$lp$, $lp$["File suit immediately with no prior notice", "Give the builder formal notice and an opportunity to inspect and attempt repair", "Contact the original real estate agent to resolve it", "Wait five years regardless of the defect's severity"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Immediate suit without required notice can create procedural obstacles.", "Correct. The pre-litigation notice-and-repair process is generally required before suing.", "The selling agent is not the party positioned to resolve a construction defect claim.", "There is no fixed universal five-year waiting rule described here."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["Right to Repair Act"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-12-013$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 12, $lp$contracts$lp$, 2, $lp$Builder warranties on new construction typically:$lp$, $lp$["Are identical for every system with no variation", "Often have different coverage terms for different systems, such as shorter coverage for fixtures and longer for structural elements", "Never exist in California", "Replace the need for any statutory construction standards"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Coverage often varies by system rather than being uniform.", "Correct. Different systems commonly carry different warranty durations.", "Builder warranties are a common feature of new-home sales.", "Warranties are separate from, not a replacement for, statutory standards."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["Right to Repair Act"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-12-014$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 12, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$Unpermitted work is best understood as:$lp$, $lp$["The same legal issue as a mechanics' lien", "A separate risk affecting insurability, financing, and disclosure obligations", "Always legal if the work looks professionally done", "Irrelevant to a home sale"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["It is a related but legally distinct risk from mechanics' liens.", "Correct. Unpermitted work carries its own insurability, financing, and disclosure consequences.", "Appearance does not establish legality; permits and inspection sign-off are what matter.", "It is highly relevant and must be considered in a sale."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["unpermitted work"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-12-015$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 12, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$A seller aware of unpermitted work completed years ago by a prior owner should:$lp$, $lp$["Stay silent since it was not their own work", "Disclose it as a known material fact", "Only disclose if directly asked by the buyer", "Assume it is irrelevant since time has passed"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Knowledge, not who performed the work, triggers the disclosure duty.", "Correct. Known unpermitted work is a material fact requiring disclosure.", "The duty to disclose known material facts is affirmative, not conditioned on being asked.", "Time passing does not eliminate the disclosure duty for known facts."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["unpermitted work"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-12-016$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 12, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$Retroactive permitting of previously unpermitted work can require:$lp$, $lp$["No inspection at all", "Exposure of the work for inspection, sometimes involving costly demolition", "Immediate approval with no review", "Only a verbal confirmation from the original contractor"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Inspection is typically required, not waived.", "Correct. Retroactive permitting can require exposing the work, which can be costly.", "Review and inspection are typically required, not automatic approval.", "A verbal confirmation alone is not a sufficient permitting process."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["unpermitted work"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-12-017$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 12, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$Joint checks or an escrow-style disbursement process on a large renovation project are used primarily to:$lp$, $lp$["Speed up the contractor's personal profit", "Help verify subcontractors and suppliers are actually being paid", "Avoid the need for any lien releases", "Eliminate the need for a written contract"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["This is not the primary purpose.", "Correct. These mechanisms help ensure payment actually reaches subs and suppliers, reducing lien risk.", "Lien releases remain important even with these mechanisms.", "A written contract remains essential regardless of payment method."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["mechanics' lien"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-12-018$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 12, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$An agent working with a client planning major renovations should flag mechanics' lien risk because:$lp$, $lp$["It only matters to contractors, not clients", "Owners can face lien exposure even after paying the general contractor in full", "Liens cannot occur on residential property", "It is entirely the escrow company's responsibility to explain"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["It directly matters to the owner's financial exposure.", "Correct. Full payment to the GC does not eliminate sub/supplier lien risk.", "Liens can and do occur on residential property.", "Escrow is not typically involved in ongoing renovation payment structuring outside of a purchase transaction."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["mechanics' lien"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-13-001$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 13, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$The Davis-Stirling Act primarily governs:$lp$, $lp$["Commercial lease disputes", "Common interest developments such as condominiums and planned developments", "Mechanics' liens", "Escrow licensing"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["It is not focused on commercial leasing.", "Correct. Davis-Stirling governs CIDs including condos, planned developments, stock co-ops, and community apartment projects.", "Mechanics' liens are governed by separate statutes.", "Escrow licensing is regulated separately by DFPI."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["Davis-Stirling Common Interest Development Act"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-13-002$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 13, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$Which is NOT one of the four CID types recognized under Davis-Stirling?$lp$, $lp$["Condominium", "Planned development", "Stock cooperative", "Sole proprietorship"]$lp$::jsonb, 3, $lp$["Condominiums are a recognized CID type.", "Planned developments are a recognized CID type.", "Stock cooperatives are a recognized CID type.", "Correct. Sole proprietorship is a business structure, not a CID type."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["Davis-Stirling Common Interest Development Act"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-13-003$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 13, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 3, $lp$When a board-adopted rule conflicts with the recorded CC&Rs, generally:$lp$, $lp$["The rule automatically controls", "The CC&Rs generally control, and the inconsistent rule is vulnerable to challenge", "Neither document has any effect", "The bylaws automatically override both"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Rules cannot override higher-authority governing documents.", "Correct. CC&Rs generally outrank board-adopted rules and regulations.", "Both documents have legal effect within the governance hierarchy.", "Bylaws govern internal operations but do not automatically override CC&Rs in this context."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["CC&Rs"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-13-004$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 13, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$CC&Rs are best described as:$lp$, $lp$["Internal bylaws only", "The foundational recorded document establishing the CID's ownership structure and restrictions", "A board meeting agenda", "An unrecorded internal memo"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Bylaws are a separate governing document.", "Correct. CC&Rs are the foundational recorded declaration for the CID.", "A meeting agenda is unrelated.", "CC&Rs are recorded, not merely internal and unrecorded."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["CC&Rs"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-13-005$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 13, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$Selling a CID unit generally requires disclosure of:$lp$, $lp$["Only the sale price history", "Governing documents, financial disclosures, pending litigation, and assessment/lien status", "Only the seller's personal financial information", "Nothing beyond the standard resale TDS"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Price history alone is not the disclosure standard.", "Correct. CID sales require this expanded disclosure package beyond standard resale disclosures.", "The seller's personal, unrelated finances are not the relevant disclosure category.", "CID sales require disclosures beyond the standard TDS/NHD package."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["reserve study"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-13-006$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 13, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 3, $lp$A reserve study funded at only 15% of the recommended level for major repairs is significant because it:$lp$, $lp$["Guarantees no future special assessment", "Increases the likelihood of a future special assessment to cover major repairs", "Has no effect on the buyer's cost of ownership", "Only affects the seller, not future owners"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Underfunding does not guarantee anything, but it raises real risk.", "Correct. Underfunded reserves substantially increase the likelihood of a future special assessment.", "It directly affects the true cost of ownership.", "It affects future owners, including the buyer, not just the seller."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["reserve study"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-13-007$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 13, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$A special assessment is:$lp$, $lp$["The regular monthly HOA dues", "A one-time charge for a specific need beyond regular dues", "A government property tax", "A lien filed by a mortgage lender"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Regular dues are a separate, recurring charge.", "Correct. Special assessments address specific one-time needs, such as a major repair.", "It is not a government tax.", "It is an association charge, not a lender's lien."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["special assessment"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-13-008$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 13, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 3, $lp$An association's authority to enforce unpaid assessments can, under specified statutory conditions, ultimately lead to:$lp$, $lp$["No consequence beyond a warning letter", "Recording a lien and, in continued nonpayment cases, potential foreclosure", "Automatic eviction without any notice", "Criminal prosecution of the owner"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Real consequences beyond a warning letter can follow.", "Correct. Associations can record liens and, under procedural safeguards, pursue foreclosure for continued nonpayment.", "Notice and procedural requirements apply; it is not automatic without process.", "This is a civil remedy, not a criminal prosecution."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["assessment lien"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-13-009$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 13, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$Many buyers are surprised to learn that:$lp$, $lp$["Only mortgage liens can lead to foreclosure on a CID property", "An HOA assessment lien can also potentially lead to foreclosure", "HOAs have no lien rights at all", "Assessment liens require no notice whatsoever"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["This understates the association's actual lien and foreclosure authority.", "Correct. Unpaid HOA assessments can, under statutory conditions, lead to foreclosure separate from the mortgage.", "Associations do have statutory lien rights.", "Statutory notice requirements generally apply before lien enforcement or foreclosure."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["assessment lien"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-13-010$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 13, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$Davis-Stirling's procedural protections before an association can pursue lien enforcement include:$lp$, $lp$["No requirements at all", "Required notices, internal dispute resolution offers, and payment plan requirements", "Immediate foreclosure with no notice", "Only a verbal warning from the board president"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Real procedural protections exist.", "Correct. These safeguards are required given the seriousness of a potential foreclosure remedy.", "Immediate foreclosure without notice is not permitted.", "Formal procedures, not just a verbal warning, are required."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["assessment lien"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-13-011$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 13, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$Under Davis-Stirling, most board business must generally be conducted:$lp$, $lp$["In secret, with no member access", "In open meetings, with limited exceptions for legitimate executive session topics", "Only by email with no member notice", "Without any records access for members"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Secrecy is not the general rule.", "Correct. Open meetings are generally required, with narrow executive session exceptions.", "Email-only, unnoticed decisions are not the standard.", "Members generally have statutory records access rights."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["open meetings"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-13-012$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 13, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$Owners generally have a statutory right to:$lp$, $lp$["Rewrite the CC&Rs unilaterally", "Inspect specified association records, subject to reasonable procedures", "Ignore all board decisions", "Prevent any board meetings from occurring"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["CC&R amendments typically require a supermajority owner vote, not unilateral action.", "Correct. Owners generally have a statutory records access right.", "Owners are still bound by valid board decisions and governing documents.", "Owners cannot simply prevent legitimate board meetings."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["open meetings"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-13-013$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 13, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$Contested board elections and certain significant votes, such as CC&R amendments, generally require:$lp$, $lp$["No formal procedure at all", "Specific procedural protections, including secret ballot requirements for contested matters", "Only a show of hands at an informal gathering", "Approval solely by the board president"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Formal procedures are required for these significant votes.", "Correct. Statutory election procedures, including secret ballot protections, apply.", "Informal shows of hands do not satisfy statutory election requirements for contested matters.", "A single officer cannot unilaterally decide these votes."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["open meetings"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-13-014$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 13, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$An architectural review committee (ARC) is responsible for:$lp$, $lp$["Setting the association's annual budget", "Reviewing and approving exterior modification requests", "Filing mechanics' liens", "Conducting fair housing testing"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Budget-setting is a separate board/management function.", "Correct. The ARC reviews requests for exterior modifications under CC&R authority.", "Mechanics' liens are unrelated to ARC functions.", "Fair housing testing is unrelated to ARC functions."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["architectural review committee (ARC)"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-13-015$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 13, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 3, $lp$A homeowner's ARC application for roof-mounted solar panels is denied based on a CC&R uniform-appearance requirement. This denial:$lp$, $lp$["Is automatically valid and final under all circumstances", "May conflict with California's specific statutory protections limiting unreasonable restrictions on solar installations", "Cannot be challenged under any circumstances", "Is irrelevant because solar panels are never regulated by HOAs"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["CC&R authority is not automatically unlimited here; statutory protections can apply.", "Correct. California law limits an association's ability to unreasonably restrict solar installations despite aesthetic CC&R provisions.", "The homeowner may have recognized avenues to challenge this denial.", "HOAs can regulate reasonable, cost-effective aspects, but cannot impose an outright unreasonable ban given statutory protections."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["architectural review committee (ARC)"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-13-016$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 13, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$California law has specifically protected certain categories from unreasonable CC&R restriction, including:$lp$, $lp$["Only interior paint color", "Solar energy systems and certain drought-tolerant landscaping", "Only pet ownership", "Only parking assignments"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Interior paint color is not the specifically protected category referenced here.", "Correct. Solar systems and drought-tolerant landscaping have specific statutory protection in California.", "Pet ownership is not the category referenced here.", "Parking assignments are not the category referenced here."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["architectural review committee (ARC)"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-13-017$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 13, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$An agent representing a buyer of a CID unit should:$lp$, $lp$["Hand over the HOA package unread at the end of the transaction", "Obtain and review governing documents, financials, and litigation history early, flagging red flags in plain language", "Assume the HOA package is unimportant boilerplate", "Avoid discussing reserve funding since it is not the agent's expertise"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Reviewing documents only at the end limits the buyer's ability to act on concerns.", "Correct. Early review and plain-language flagging of red flags serves the buyer's interests.", "The HOA package can contain financially significant information, not mere boilerplate.", "Flagging a red flag like reserve underfunding, without giving final legal/financial opinions, is within the agent's role."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["reserve study"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-13-018$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 13, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$Pending litigation involving an HOA is disclosed to buyers because:$lp$, $lp$["It never affects individual owners", "It can create special assessment risk for every owner, regardless of whether their unit is the subject of the dispute", "It is only relevant to the board members", "It automatically voids the sale"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["It can have real financial consequences for individual owners.", "Correct. Litigation costs can lead to special assessments affecting the whole membership.", "It is relevant to all owners, not just board members.", "Pending litigation does not automatically void a sale; it is a disclosure matter."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["reserve study"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-14-001$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 14, $lp$contracts$lp$, 2, $lp$Mediation is best described as:$lp$, $lp$["A binding decision issued by a neutral third party", "A non-binding process where a neutral helps parties negotiate their own resolution", "A criminal proceeding", "A form of eminent domain"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["That describes arbitration, not mediation.", "Correct. Mediation is non-binding; the parties retain control over the outcome.", "It is not a criminal proceeding.", "It is unrelated to eminent domain."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["mediation"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-14-002$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 14, $lp$contracts$lp$, 2, $lp$Arbitration differs from mediation because arbitration:$lp$, $lp$["Is non-binding", "Results in a binding decision, generally waiving jury trial rights", "Cannot be included in a real estate contract", "Always favors the buyer"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Arbitration is binding, not non-binding.", "Correct. Arbitration produces a binding decision and generally waives the right to a jury trial.", "Arbitration clauses are commonly included in real estate contracts.", "Arbitration outcomes are not predetermined to favor either party."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["arbitration"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-14-003$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 14, $lp$contracts$lp$, 3, $lp$In the standard California residential purchase agreement, the arbitration provision typically:$lp$, $lp$["Applies automatically to every transaction", "Must be separately initialed by both parties to apply", "Is illegal under California law", "Cannot ever be combined with a mediation clause"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["It does not apply automatically without separate initialing.", "Correct. Parties must specifically initial the arbitration clause to activate it.", "Arbitration clauses are lawful in California real estate contracts.", "Standard forms commonly combine both mediation and optional arbitration provisions."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["arbitration"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-14-004$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 14, $lp$contracts$lp$, 3, $lp$A party who sues in court without first attempting mediation as required by the purchase agreement's fee clause risks:$lp$, $lp$["No consequence at all", "Forfeiting the ability to recover attorney's fees, even if they win on the merits", "Automatic loss of the underlying lawsuit", "Criminal liability"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["There is a real contractual consequence here.", "Correct. Skipping required mediation can independently forfeit attorney's fee recovery under the contract's fee-shifting clause.", "Skipping mediation does not automatically mean losing the underlying case.", "This is a civil contract issue, not a criminal matter."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["mediation"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-14-005$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 14, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$A DRE investigation can be triggered by:$lp$, $lp$["Only a criminal conviction", "A consumer complaint, agency referral, criminal conviction, or DRE audit activity", "Only the licensee's own request", "Nothing short of a court order"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Criminal conviction is only one possible trigger among several.", "Correct. Multiple sources can trigger a DRE investigation.", "A licensee's own request is not the typical or required trigger.", "A court order is not required to begin a DRE investigation."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["accusation (DRE)"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-14-006$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 14, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$The formal administrative proceeding through which the DRE pursues license discipline is called:$lp$, $lp$["A grand jury indictment", "An accusation", "A quiet title action", "A UD action"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Grand jury indictment is a criminal law concept, not the DRE process.", "Correct. The DRE pursues discipline through a formal accusation proceeding.", "Quiet title is a separate property law action.", "Unlawful detainer concerns tenant eviction, not license discipline."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["accusation (DRE)"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-14-007$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 14, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$DRE disciplinary outcomes can include, from least to most severe:$lp$, $lp$["Only revocation, with no lesser options", "Public reproval, suspension, restricted license, and revocation", "Only a fine, with no license impact", "Automatic criminal prosecution"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Lesser options exist short of revocation.", "Correct. The DRE has a range of disciplinary tools scaled to severity.", "License-related consequences, not merely fines, are the DRE's primary tools.", "DRE discipline is administrative, not automatically criminal."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["public reproval", "license revocation"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-14-008$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 14, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$A restricted license generally means:$lp$, $lp$["Permanent loss of licensure with no path back", "Continued licensure subject to specific conditions such as increased supervision or probation", "No consequence at all", "Automatic upgrade to broker status"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Permanent loss describes revocation, not restriction.", "Correct. A restricted license continues under specified conditions.", "Restriction is a real consequence, not the absence of one.", "It is unrelated to license upgrades."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["public reproval"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-14-009$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 14, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 3, $lp$A revoked license may, in some cases, be reinstated:$lp$, $lp$["Immediately upon request with no waiting period", "After a statutory waiting period, subject to DRE discretion", "Never under any circumstances", "Automatically after 30 days"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Immediate reinstatement without any process is not the standard.", "Correct. Reinstatement may be possible after a statutory waiting period, at DRE discretion.", "Reinstatement is not categorically impossible.", "There is no automatic 30-day reinstatement rule described here."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["license revocation"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-14-010$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 14, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 3, $lp$A single act of misconduct by an agent can result in:$lp$, $lp$["Only civil liability, never license discipline", "Only DRE discipline, never civil liability", "Both civil liability and DRE discipline as separate tracks", "Neither, since one bars the other"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["Civil liability is not the only possible consequence.", "DRE discipline is not the only possible consequence.", "Correct. Civil liability and license discipline are independent tracks that can both apply to the same conduct.", "Neither track automatically bars the other."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["civil liability"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-14-011$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 14, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 3, $lp$An agent settles a civil lawsuit confidentially over a non-disclosure claim. A DRE complaint was filed separately before the settlement. The settlement:$lp$, $lp$["Automatically ends the DRE investigation", "Does not resolve or bind the DRE's independent investigation into the same conduct", "Prevents the DRE from ever investigating", "Converts the DRE matter into a criminal case"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["A private settlement does not automatically end a regulatory investigation.", "Correct. The DRE is not a party to the private settlement and can proceed independently.", "The DRE retains investigative authority regardless of the settlement.", "This remains an administrative matter, not automatically criminal."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["accusation (DRE)"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-14-012$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 14, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$A confidentiality clause in a civil settlement:$lp$, $lp$["Binds the DRE and prevents any further investigation", "Does not bind the DRE, which was not a party to the settlement", "Automatically dismisses any pending DRE complaint", "Applies to all future disputes regardless of parties"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["The DRE is not bound by a private agreement it was not party to.", "Correct. A confidentiality clause between private parties cannot bind a regulator who was not a signatory.", "It does not automatically dismiss a separately filed DRE complaint.", "It applies only between the settling parties, not universally."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["civil liability"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-14-013$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 14, $lp$contracts$lp$, 2, $lp$Which is a recurring source of real estate litigation identified across this course?$lp$, $lp$["Excessive marketing photography", "Non-disclosure of material facts", "Using a licensed title company", "Recording a deed"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["This is not a typical litigation driver.", "Correct. Non-disclosure of material facts is a leading recurring source of disputes.", "Using a licensed title company reduces, not increases, typical risk.", "Properly recording a deed is a protective step, not a litigation source."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["civil liability"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-14-014$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 14, $lp$contracts$lp$, 2, $lp$Undisclosed dual agency is a recurring litigation and discipline risk because it implicates:$lp$, $lp$["Trust fund handling only", "Agency and fiduciary duty breaches", "Only escrow's role", "Only zoning compliance"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["It is not primarily a trust fund issue.", "Correct. Undisclosed dual agency directly implicates agency and fiduciary duties.", "Escrow's role is unrelated to this issue.", "Zoning is unrelated to this issue."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["civil liability"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-14-015$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 14, $lp$contracts$lp$, 2, $lp$The single most protective habit an agent can build to reduce both civil and DRE risk is:$lp$, $lp$["Avoiding all written records", "Disciplined, contemporaneous written documentation of instructions and disclosures", "Relying entirely on memory for client instructions", "Only documenting the final outcome, not how issues were raised and resolved"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Avoiding records increases, not decreases, risk.", "Correct. A clean, contemporaneous written record is frequently the most persuasive evidence in both tracks.", "Memory alone is a weak substitute for documentation.", "Documenting only outcomes omits the context that often matters most."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["civil liability"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-14-016$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 14, $lp$contracts$lp$, 2, $lp$A key drawback of arbitration compared to litigation is that arbitration:$lp$, $lp$["Is always slower than court litigation", "Significantly limits the grounds for appealing an unfavorable decision", "Cannot ever be used in real estate disputes", "Guarantees a jury trial"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Arbitration is generally faster, not slower, than litigation.", "Correct. Appeal grounds are significantly limited compared to a court judgment.", "Arbitration clauses are commonly used in real estate contracts.", "Arbitration generally waives, rather than guarantees, a jury trial."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["arbitration"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-14-017$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 14, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$The DRE's disciplinary calculus generally weighs:$lp$, $lp$["Only whether the licensee has a good reputation", "Severity and pattern of conduct, harm caused, prior history, and cooperation with the investigation", "Only the dollar amount of the transaction", "Only the licensee's years of experience"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Reputation alone is not the controlling factor.", "Correct. These are the typical factors weighed in DRE disciplinary decisions.", "Transaction value alone does not determine discipline severity.", "Years of experience alone is not the controlling factor."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["accusation (DRE)"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-14-018$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 14, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$An agent who gives a confident valuation opinion presented as a guaranteed fact, without basis, risks liability under:$lp$, $lp$["Negligent or careless professional conduct principles", "Only zoning law", "Only landlord-tenant law", "No risk since opinions are never actionable"]$lp$::jsonb, 0, $lp$["Correct. Overstated, unsupported professional opinions presented as fact are a recognized source of liability.", "Zoning law is unrelated to this conduct.", "Landlord-tenant law is unrelated to this conduct.", "Presenting an unsupported opinion as guaranteed fact can create real liability."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["civil liability"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-15-001$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 15, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$The consistent boundary drawn across this entire course between a licensee's role and an attorney's role is best summarized as:$lp$, $lp$["Licensees should avoid ever mentioning legal issues to clients", "Recognize the issue, escalate it, but do not resolve it personally", "Licensees should resolve every issue themselves to save the client money", "Only brokers, never salespersons, may recognize legal issues"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Silence about a recognized issue would itself be a professional failure.", "Correct. Recognize, escalate, and refer — not resolve — is the consistent standard.", "Resolving legal issues personally exceeds license scope and creates real risk.", "Both brokers and salespersons are expected to recognize issues within their role."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["recognize, don't resolve"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-15-002$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 15, $lp$transfer$lp$, 2, $lp$A licensee may generally:$lp$, $lp$["Determine definitively whether a specific contingency removal was legally valid in a disputed case", "Explain generally how active contingency removal works", "Resolve a title defect personally", "Draft eviction notices for a landlord client"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["A definitive legal determination in a disputed case exceeds license scope.", "Correct. General explanation of the mechanism is within a licensee's role.", "Title defect resolution is a specialist function.", "Drafting eviction notices is generally outside license scope."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["recognize, don't resolve"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-15-003$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 15, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$A preliminary report reveals a judgment lien against a name similar to, but not exactly matching, the seller's. The appropriate first step is to:$lp$, $lp$["Ignore it since names are similar but not identical", "Route it to the title company's curative department immediately", "Personally determine whether it belongs to the seller", "Wait until closing week to address it"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Similar names still require review, not dismissal.", "Correct. Prompt routing to the professionals equipped to clear it is the standard response.", "Determining ownership of a wild document is the title company's function, not the agent's.", "Waiting compresses the time available for curative work."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["escalation"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-15-004$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 15, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$A buyer's agent overhears the seller's agent mention the seller is highly motivated due to a divorce. The buyer's agent should:$lp$, $lp$["Immediately share this with the buyer to gain negotiating leverage", "Recognize this as the other side's confidential information and not exploit it", "Confront the seller's agent publicly", "Report it to the DRE immediately"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Exploiting inadvertently learned confidential information raises ethical concerns.", "Correct. This is the other party's confidential information; the professional response is restraint, not exploitation.", "A public confrontation is not the appropriate response to inadvertent disclosure.", "This is not automatically a DRE-reportable matter on its own."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["recognize, don't resolve"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-15-005$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 15, $lp$transfer$lp$, 2, $lp$A buyer asks their agent which vesting option to choose. The correct response is to:$lp$, $lp$["Recommend the vesting option the agent personally prefers", "Explain that this is a legal/tax decision and refer the buyer to an attorney or CPA", "Choose for the buyer to save time", "Tell the buyer vesting does not matter"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Personal preference is not a substitute for qualified advice on this decision.", "Correct. Vesting has real legal and tax consequences outside license scope.", "Choosing for the client on a matter with legal/tax consequences is inappropriate.", "Vesting has real consequences and should not be dismissed as unimportant."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["recognize, don't resolve"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-15-006$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 15, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 3, $lp$An HOA reserve study funded at 20% with no pending special assessment is best handled by the buyer's agent as:$lp$, $lp$["A red flag to explain in plain language while contingencies remain open, not a guarantee of a future assessment", "Something to hide from the buyer to keep the deal moving", "Irrelevant since no assessment has been approved yet", "A reason to automatically cancel the transaction"]$lp$::jsonb, 0, $lp$["Correct. Plain-language disclosure of the risk, without overstating certainty, respects the buyer's decision-making role.", "Hiding a known material financial risk would breach disclosure duties.", "Underfunding is relevant risk information even without an approved assessment.", "This information informs the buyer's decision; it does not automatically require cancellation."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["reserve study"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-15-007$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 15, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$Ignorance of a legal rule by a licensee:$lp$, $lp$["Always fully excuses a violation", "Generally does not excuse the consequences of violating the rule", "Is irrelevant to DRE discipline", "Only matters in civil cases, never DRE matters"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Ignorance is not a complete excuse in most cases.", "Correct. Not knowing the rule generally does not excuse its violation.", "It can be relevant context but does not eliminate exposure.", "It is relevant to both civil and DRE contexts, not just one."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["escalation"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-15-008$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 15, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$An agent who recognized an issue, escalated it appropriately, and documented the process has, compared to an agent who did nothing:$lp$, $lp$["An identical risk profile regardless of documentation", "A fundamentally different, more favorable risk profile even if the ultimate legal outcome is the same", "No difference since only outcomes matter", "A worse risk profile because documentation creates more evidence against them"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Documented professional conduct meaningfully changes the risk picture.", "Correct. Courts and the DRE weigh professional conduct, not just the ultimate outcome.", "Process and documentation matter independently of the final outcome.", "Documentation of appropriate conduct generally helps, not harms, the agent's position."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["escalation"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-15-009$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 15, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$Building a referral network of attorneys, title curative contacts, and environmental consultants is most valuable when done:$lp$, $lp$["During an active crisis, when the need is clear", "Before a crisis, so resources are ready when needed", "Only after a DRE complaint is filed", "Never, since referrals are unnecessary"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Building the network only during a crisis wastes valuable time.", "Correct. Proactive relationship-building is far more effective than searching mid-transaction.", "Waiting for a DRE complaint is far too late.", "Referrals are a necessary part of professional practice given license-scope limits."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["escalation"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-15-010$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 15, $lp$contracts$lp$, 2, $lp$When multiple legal issues surface in the same transaction, the correct approach is to:$lp$, $lp$["Address only the most urgent issue and ignore the rest", "Address each issue on its own track using the same recognize-scope-time-document process", "Wait until all issues can be handled at once at closing", "Assume they will resolve themselves"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Ignoring other issues risks real harm even if one is more urgent.", "Correct. Each issue should be tracked and handled through the same structured process.", "Waiting until closing compresses time for issues that may need earlier action.", "Assuming self-resolution is not a professional practice."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["escalation"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-15-011$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 15, $lp$agency$lp$, 2, $lp$A licensee may generally:$lp$, $lp$["Structure a compliant Affiliated Business Arrangement without any legal guidance", "Explain RESPA's general referral rules to a client", "Advise definitively on a specific refinanced loan's deficiency exposure", "Represent a landlord in an unlawful detainer action"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Structuring a compliant AfBA properly typically benefits from legal guidance.", "Correct. General explanation of RESPA's rules is within a licensee's working knowledge.", "Deficiency exposure analysis for a specific loan is a legal determination.", "Representing a party in a UD action is generally outside license scope."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["recognize, don't resolve"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-15-012$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 15, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$A licensee who suspects a listing may face significant CEQA review should:$lp$, $lp$["Conduct the CEQA analysis personally", "Flag the likely CEQA trigger and refer the client to appropriate land-use expertise", "Assume CEQA never applies to real estate transactions", "Tell the client CEQA guarantees project denial"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["CEQA analysis is a specialist function outside license scope.", "Correct. Flagging the issue and referring out is the appropriate role.", "CEQA can meaningfully affect real estate development transactions.", "CEQA review does not guarantee denial; it requires analysis and possible mitigation."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["recognize, don't resolve"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-15-013$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 15, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$A licensee reviewing a rental screening policy should recognize potential fair housing risk when:$lp$, $lp$["The policy explicitly names a protected class only", "The policy could have a disparate impact even without naming any protected class", "Only when a tenant explicitly complains", "Never, since screening policies are exempt from fair housing law"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Explicit naming is not the only trigger for concern; neutral policies can still be risky.", "Correct. Facially neutral policies can still raise disparate impact concerns.", "Waiting for a complaint is reactive, not the proactive standard expected.", "Screening policies are not exempt from fair housing law."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["disparate impact"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-15-014$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 15, $lp$transfer$lp$, 2, $lp$A licensee who recognizes a possible mechanics' lien risk on a property with recent unpermitted-looking renovation work should:$lp$, $lp$["Assume it is not a real issue since the work looks finished", "Flag the risk to the client and recommend appropriate professional review", "Personally resolve any potential lien dispute", "Ignore it since liens are the seller's sole problem"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Appearance does not confirm proper permitting or payment status.", "Correct. Flagging the risk and recommending review protects the client's interests.", "Lien dispute resolution is outside license scope.", "Both buyer and seller can be affected by an unresolved lien issue."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["escalation"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-15-015$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 15, $lp$contracts$lp$, 2, $lp$A client asks their agent which is strategically better for their specific dispute: mediation or arbitration. The agent should:$lp$, $lp$["Give a definitive strategic recommendation based on general real estate knowledge", "Explain the general difference between the two and refer the client to an attorney for strategic advice", "Refuse to discuss either concept at all", "Tell the client arbitration is always better"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["A definitive strategic recommendation for a specific dispute exceeds license scope.", "Correct. General education plus referral for strategic advice is the appropriate response.", "Declining to explain general concepts is unhelpfully evasive.", "Neither option is universally better; it depends on case-specific factors best assessed by an attorney."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["recognize, don't resolve"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$LEGAL-15-016$lp$, $lp$unit$lp$, $lp$legal-aspects$lp$, 15, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$This course's central professional message is best summarized as:$lp$, $lp$["Licensees should become informal legal experts and handle disputes themselves", "Understanding legal doctrine helps a licensee recognize issues early and know exactly when a specialist is needed", "Legal knowledge is unnecessary for real estate practice", "Only brokers need to understand any of this material"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Becoming an informal legal expert who handles disputes crosses into practicing law.", "Correct. The goal is recognition and appropriate referral, not legal practice.", "Legal knowledge materially reduces risk and improves client service.", "This material is relevant to salespersons as well as brokers."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["pattern recognition"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$SIM-01-001$lp$, $lp$exam-sim$lp$, null, null, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$A broker receives a buyer's $20,000 earnest money check on Monday. The offer directs that the check be held uncashed until acceptance. The seller accepts Wednesday. Absent contrary instructions, the broker must place the funds in escrow, a trust account, or the principal's hands no later than:$lp$, $lp$["The end of Wednesday", "Three business days after acceptance", "Ten calendar days after acceptance", "Whenever escrow opens"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Same-day deposit is not required.", "Correct. Trust funds must be deposited within three business days of receipt — and when a check is properly held uncashed pending acceptance, the clock runs from acceptance.", "Ten days exceeds the statutory window.", "The deadline is fixed by regulation, not by escrow's convenience."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["trust fund"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$SIM-01-002$lp$, $lp$exam-sim$lp$, null, null, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$A licensee deposits a client's rent collections into her personal checking account 'temporarily' over a weekend and transfers them to the trust account Monday. No money is lost. The licensee has committed:$lp$, $lp$["No violation, because the funds were fully restored", "Conversion", "Commingling", "A secret profit"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["Restoration does not cure the violation; mixing is itself the offense.", "Conversion is actually using client funds — worse than what happened here.", "Correct. Placing trust funds in a personal account mixes client money with the licensee's own — commingling, disciplinable even without loss.", "A secret profit is undisclosed compensation from the agency, not mishandled deposits."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["commingling", "conversion"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$SIM-01-003$lp$, $lp$exam-sim$lp$, null, null, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$A seller tells his listing agent, 'Don't show the house to families with small children — the pool isn't fenced.' The agent should:$lp$, $lp$["Follow the instruction, because obedience is a fiduciary duty", "Follow it only after the seller signs a hold-harmless letter", "Refuse, because screening buyers by familial status is illegal discrimination", "Show the home to families but warn them the seller prefers other buyers"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["Obedience extends only to lawful instructions.", "No waiver makes housing discrimination lawful.", "Correct. Familial status is a protected class under federal and California law; the agent must refuse the unlawful instruction (and may address the pool as a safety disclosure instead).", "Steering families away — even softly — is still discrimination."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["familial status", "protected class"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$SIM-01-004$lp$, $lp$exam-sim$lp$, null, null, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 1, $lp$Refusing to make loans on qualified properties located in a particular neighborhood because of its racial composition is known as:$lp$, $lp$["Steering", "Blockbusting", "Redlining", "Panic selling"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["Steering channels buyers toward or away from areas.", "Blockbusting induces owners to sell by exploiting fear of neighborhood change.", "Correct. Redlining is area-based lending or insurance discrimination.", "Panic selling is another name for blockbusting."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["redlining", "steering", "blockbusting"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$SIM-01-005$lp$, $lp$exam-sim$lp$, null, null, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 3, $lp$Closing on an Ontario rental home is set for March 16 (buyer owns the day of closing). The tenant paid March rent of $2,400 on March 1. Using a 30-day month and banker's method, the rent proration credit to the buyer is:$lp$, $lp$["$1,200", "$1,240", "$1,160", "$2,400"]$lp$::jsonb, 0, $lp$["Correct. Daily rent = $2,400 ÷ 30 = $80. The buyer owns the day of closing, so the buyer's share covers March 16–30 = 15 days; 15 × $80 = $1,200.", "$1,240 uses 15.5 days — no half-day convention applies to prorations.", "$1,160 uses 14.5 days; count whole days, with the closing day belonging to the buyer as the facts state.", "The full $2,400 ignores the seller's ownership of March 1–15; only the buyer's portion is credited."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["proration"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$SIM-01-006$lp$, $lp$exam-sim$lp$, null, null, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$, 2, $lp$Which sale is EXEMPT from the seller's duty to deliver a Transfer Disclosure Statement (TDS)?$lp$, $lp$["A 60-year-old Alhambra bungalow sold 'as-is'", "A probate sale by an estate's executor", "A duplex sold by an out-of-state owner", "A condominium resale"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["'As-is' never eliminates the TDS for a standard residential sale of 1–4 units.", "Correct. Court-supervised and fiduciary transfers such as probate sales are statutorily exempt from the TDS requirement.", "The seller's residence elsewhere does not create an exemption.", "Condo resales of 1–4 residential units require the TDS."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["TDS (Transfer Disclosure Statement)", "as-is"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$SIM-01-007$lp$, $lp$exam-sim$lp$, null, null, $lp$agency$lp$, 2, $lp$During a listing appointment in San Marino, the sellers mention they will 'take $1.4M if pushed.' The property lists at $1.5M. A buyer asks the listing agent, 'Will they take less?' The agent must:$lp$, $lp$["Disclose the $1.4M figure, because honesty is owed to all parties", "Keep the sellers' price flexibility confidential", "Reveal it only if the buyer signs a nondisclosure agreement", "Refer the question to the sellers' attorney"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["The duty of honesty to third parties does not require betraying the principal's confidential information.", "Correct. Price motivation is confidential client information; disclosing it without authorization breaches the fiduciary duty of confidentiality and loyalty.", "An NDA changes nothing — the information belongs to the sellers.", "No attorney referral is needed; the agent simply may not disclose."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["fiduciary", "OLD CAR"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$SIM-01-008$lp$, $lp$exam-sim$lp$, null, null, $lp$agency$lp$, 2, $lp$A brokerage represents both the buyer and the seller in the same Glendale transaction. This dual agency is lawful in California only if:$lp$, $lp$["The brokerage assigns different salespersons to each party", "Both principals give informed written consent", "The commission is reduced to offset the conflict", "The transaction is all cash"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Assigning different salespersons is common but does not by itself satisfy the law.", "Correct. Dual agency requires disclosure to and informed written consent from both buyer and seller; undisclosed dual agency risks discipline and rescission.", "Commission size is irrelevant to the legality of dual agency.", "Payment method has nothing to do with agency consent."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["dual agency"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$SIM-01-009$lp$, $lp$exam-sim$lp$, null, null, $lp$agency$lp$, 3, $lp$A listing agent learns the roof leaked last winter and the seller repainted the ceiling stains without repairs. The seller instructs, 'Say nothing — it looks fine now.' If the agent stays silent and sells to an unknowing buyer, the agent is liable for:$lp$, $lp$["Nothing, because obedience to the principal controls", "Puffing", "Concealment of a known material fact", "Breach of the safety clause"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["Obedience never extends to unlawful instructions like hiding defects.", "Puffing is exaggerated opinion, not silence about a defect.", "Correct. A known leak concealed by cosmetic repainting is a material fact; both seller and agent face liability for fraudulent concealment, and the agent faces DRE discipline.", "A safety clause protects a broker's commission after listing expiration — unrelated."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["material fact", "misrepresentation"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$SIM-01-010$lp$, $lp$exam-sim$lp$, null, null, $lp$agency$lp$, 1, $lp$In the fiduciary-duty memory aid OLD CAR, the 'A' stands for:$lp$, $lp$["Agency", "Accounting", "Acceptance", "Authorization"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Agency is the relationship itself, not a duty within it.", "Correct. Obedience, Loyalty, Disclosure, Confidentiality, Accounting, Reasonable care — the agent must account for all funds and property entrusted to them.", "Acceptance is contract vocabulary.", "Authorization is not one of the six duties."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["OLD CAR"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$SIM-01-011$lp$, $lp$exam-sim$lp$, null, null, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$Three siblings hold title to inherited Riverside land as joint tenants. One sibling sells her interest to a friend. The result is:$lp$, $lp$["The friend becomes a joint tenant with the two siblings", "The joint tenancy ends entirely; all three now hold as tenants in common", "The two siblings remain joint tenants with each other; the friend holds as a tenant in common", "The sale is void without the other siblings' consent"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["The friend cannot be a joint tenant — the four unities of time and title are broken as to him.", "The conveyance severs only the seller's interest; the remaining joint tenants' relationship survives.", "Correct. The two siblings still hold two-thirds as joint tenants between themselves; the buyer holds one-third as a tenant in common with them.", "A joint tenant may convey her interest without consent."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["joint tenancy", "tenancy in common"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$SIM-01-012$lp$, $lp$exam-sim$lp$, null, null, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$For 12 years, a Chino Hills homeowner's neighbor has driven across a corner of her lot to reach his garage — openly, without permission, and continuously. The neighbor has most likely acquired:$lp$, $lp$["Title by adverse possession", "A prescriptive easement", "A license", "An easement by necessity"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Adverse possession requires, among other things, payment of property taxes — rarely met by mere crossing, and he claims use, not ownership.", "Correct. Open, notorious, hostile, continuous use for five years creates an easement by prescription — a right to keep using, not ownership.", "A license is permissive and revocable; this use was without permission.", "Necessity requires common ownership history and landlocked circumstances."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["prescription", "easement"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$SIM-01-013$lp$, $lp$exam-sim$lp$, null, null, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 2, $lp$A Pomona corner lot is zoned residential, but its 40-year-old corner market lawfully predates the zoning. The market is a:$lp$, $lp$["Variance", "Conditional use", "Nonconforming use", "Spot zone"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["A variance is permission for a dimensional deviation granted for hardship.", "A conditional use is specially permitted by the current ordinance after review.", "Correct. A lawful use predating a zoning change may continue as a nonconforming ('grandfathered') use, though it typically cannot expand or resume after abandonment.", "Spot zoning is an (often invalid) rezoning of a single parcel inconsistent with the plan."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["nonconforming use", "variance"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$SIM-01-014$lp$, $lp$exam-sim$lp$, null, null, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$, 3, $lp$Caltrans widens a freeway, taking no land from an adjacent Baldwin Park parcel, but the project walls off its street access, destroying most of its commercial value. The owner's remedy is:$lp$, $lp$["An action in inverse condemnation", "A petition for a variance", "A quiet title action", "Nothing — no land was physically taken"]$lp$::jsonb, 0, $lp$["Correct. When government action effectively takes or substantially damages property (such as destroying access) without condemnation proceedings, the owner sues in inverse condemnation for just compensation.", "A variance addresses zoning standards, not compensation.", "Quiet title resolves ownership disputes; ownership is not in doubt.", "Physical occupation is not required — substantial impairment of access can be a compensable taking."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["inverse condemnation", "eminent domain"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$SIM-01-015$lp$, $lp$exam-sim$lp$, null, null, $lp$valuation$lp$, 2, $lp$A Covina fourplex produces $72,000 gross annual rent with $22,000 in operating expenses. Investors in the area expect an 8% return. Using the income approach, the property's indicated value is:$lp$, $lp$["$900,000", "$625,000", "$550,000", "$690,000"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["$900,000 capitalizes gross income ($72,000 ÷ 0.08) — expenses must come out first.", "Correct. NOI = $72,000 − $22,000 = $50,000; value = NOI ÷ cap rate = $50,000 ÷ 0.08 = $625,000.", "$550,000 subtracts expenses twice or uses a 9%+ rate.", "$690,000 corresponds to no standard calculation — a plausible-looking distractor."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["income approach", "capitalization rate"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$SIM-01-016$lp$, $lp$exam-sim$lp$, null, null, $lp$valuation$lp$, 2, $lp$A comparable home sold for $780,000. It has a three-car garage worth $30,000 more than the subject's two-car garage, but the subject has a $15,000 superior view. The indicated value of the subject is:$lp$, $lp$["$795,000", "$765,000", "$825,000", "$735,000"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["$795,000 results from adding the view adjustment without subtracting for the comp's superior garage.", "Correct. Adjust the comparable to the subject: subtract $30,000 (comp superior garage), add $15,000 (subject superior view): $780,000 − $30,000 + $15,000 = $765,000.", "$825,000 adds both adjustments — the classic wrong direction: you adjust the comp, never the subject.", "$735,000 subtracts both adjustments."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["sales comparison approach"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$SIM-01-017$lp$, $lp$exam-sim$lp$, null, null, $lp$valuation$lp$, 1, $lp$The principle underlying the sales comparison approach — that a buyer will pay no more for a home than the cost of an equally desirable alternative — is the principle of:$lp$, $lp$["Conformity", "Contribution", "Substitution", "Anticipation"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["Conformity says value is protected by reasonable similarity among neighborhood properties.", "Contribution measures a feature by what it adds to value, not its cost.", "Correct. Substitution caps value at the cost of acquiring an equivalent substitute — the foundation of comparing sales.", "Anticipation bases value on expected future benefits — the foundation of the income approach."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["principle of substitution"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$SIM-01-018$lp$, $lp$exam-sim$lp$, null, null, $lp$valuation$lp$, 2, $lp$A well-maintained Monrovia home loses value because it sits under a newly approved flight path. This loss is classified as:$lp$, $lp$["Physical deterioration", "Functional obsolescence", "External obsolescence", "Deferred maintenance"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["Nothing about the structure has worn out.", "The floor plan and features are fine; the problem is outside the lot lines.", "Correct. Value loss from outside factors — noise, economic decline, nearby nuisances — is external (economic) obsolescence, and it is incurable by the owner.", "Deferred maintenance is postponed repair work, a form of physical deterioration."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["external (economic) obsolescence", "depreciation (appraisal)"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$SIM-01-019$lp$, $lp$exam-sim$lp$, null, null, $lp$contracts$lp$, 2, $lp$A buyer offers $925,000 for a La Verne home. The seller crosses out the price, writes $945,000, signs, and returns it. Before the buyer responds, another buyer offers $960,000. The seller may:$lp$, $lp$["Accept the second offer, because her counteroffer is only an offer the first buyer has not yet accepted", "Not negotiate with anyone until the first buyer responds", "Accept the second offer only if the first buyer waives in writing", "Be liable to the first buyer for breach of contract"]$lp$::jsonb, 0, $lp$["Correct. The counteroffer rejected the original offer and stands as a new offer; until accepted it may be revoked, freeing the seller to take the better offer (revoking the counter first is best practice).", "No exclusivity arises from an unaccepted counteroffer.", "The first buyer holds no right requiring waiver — only an unaccepted offer.", "No contract exists yet, so there is nothing to breach."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["counteroffer", "offer"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$SIM-01-020$lp$, $lp$exam-sim$lp$, null, null, $lp$contracts$lp$, 2, $lp$A 17-year-old signs a contract to buy a Fontana condo. The contract is:$lp$, $lp$["Void", "Voidable by the minor", "Valid if a parent co-signs later", "Enforceable against the minor once she turns 18"]$lp$::jsonb, 0, $lp$["Correct. In California, a minor cannot contract concerning real property at all — such a contract is void, not merely voidable. (An emancipated minor is the exception.)", "Voidable is the general minors rule for many contracts elsewhere, but California treats a minor's real property contract as void from the start.", "A later co-signature cannot breathe life into a void agreement.", "A void contract does not ripen into validity at majority."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["void contract", "voidable contract"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$SIM-01-021$lp$, $lp$exam-sim$lp$, null, null, $lp$contracts$lp$, 2, $lp$In a California residential purchase of an owner-occupied home (1–4 units), a valid liquidated damages clause generally limits the seller's retention of the buyer's deposit to:$lp$, $lp$["The full deposit, whatever its size", "3% of the purchase price", "10% of the purchase price", "One month of the seller's carrying costs"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Amounts above the cap are presumed unreasonable and refundable.", "Correct. For owner-occupied residential property of 1–4 units, liquidated damages retained from the deposit are capped at 3% of the purchase price.", "10% has no basis in the statute.", "Carrying costs are not the statutory measure."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["liquidated damages"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$SIM-01-022$lp$, $lp$exam-sim$lp$, null, null, $lp$financing$lp$, 2, $lp$A buyer purchases a $700,000 Upland home with a $560,000 first loan. The loan-to-value ratio is:$lp$, $lp$["70%", "75%", "80%", "85%"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["70% would be a $490,000 loan.", "75% would be a $525,000 loan.", "Correct. LTV = 560,000 ÷ 700,000 = 0.80 = 80% — the classic threshold at or below which conventional lenders typically do not require PMI.", "85% would be a $595,000 loan."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["LTV (loan-to-value ratio)", "PMI (private mortgage insurance)"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$SIM-01-023$lp$, $lp$exam-sim$lp$, null, null, $lp$financing$lp$, 3, $lp$After a lender completes a NON-judicial foreclosure (trustee's sale) on a California home loan, the lender may recover any remaining shortfall from the borrower:$lp$, $lp$["By suing within 90 days of the sale", "Only if the loan was a purchase-money loan", "Never — a deficiency judgment is barred after a trustee's sale", "Only with the Real Estate Commissioner's approval"]$lp$::jsonb, 2, $lp$["No 90-day deficiency window exists after a trustee's sale.", "Purchase-money status is a separate, additional bar — but the trustee's-sale bar applies regardless of loan purpose.", "Correct. California's anti-deficiency rules prohibit a deficiency judgment following non-judicial foreclosure; the lender's choice of the quicker trustee's sale costs it any deficiency claim.", "The Commissioner has no role in deficiency actions."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["deficiency judgment", "power of sale"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$SIM-01-024$lp$, $lp$exam-sim$lp$, null, null, $lp$transfer$lp$, 2, $lp$A Claremont home sells for $840,000. At the typical county documentary transfer tax rate of $0.55 per $500 of consideration, the transfer tax is:$lp$, $lp$["$462", "$924", "$1,848", "$4,620"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["$462 uses $0.55 per $1,000 — the rate applies per $500.", "Correct. $840,000 ÷ 500 = 1,680 increments; 1,680 × $0.55 = $924.", "$1,848 doubles the correct figure (a $1.10/$500 city-plus-county rate would do this — watch for cities with their own tax).", "$4,620 misplaces a decimal."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["documentary transfer tax"]$lp$::jsonb),
  ($lp$SIM-01-025$lp$, $lp$exam-sim$lp$, null, null, $lp$transfer$lp$, 2, $lp$A buyer closes on a Rialto home but fails to record her grant deed. The seller then fraudulently deeds the same property to a second buyer, who pays value, knows nothing of the first sale, and promptly records. California's recording system awards title to:$lp$, $lp$["The first buyer, because her deed was delivered first", "The second buyer, as a good-faith purchaser who recorded first", "The first buyer, if she sues within one year", "Both buyers as tenants in common"]$lp$::jsonb, 1, $lp$["Between the parties delivery controls, but against a subsequent bona fide purchaser an unrecorded deed loses.", "Correct. A subsequent purchaser for value, without notice, who records first prevails under the recording statute — the reason buyers record immediately at closing.", "No one-year grace period rescues an unrecorded deed against a BFP.", "The recording race has one winner, not co-ownership."]$lp$::jsonb, $lp$["recording", "constructive notice"]$lp$::jsonb)
on conflict (id) do update set source = excluded.source, course_slug = excluded.course_slug, unit_number = excluded.unit_number, blueprint_area = excluded.blueprint_area, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, stem = excluded.stem, options = excluded.options, answer = excluded.answer, rationales = excluded.rationales, terms = excluded.terms;

-- glossary_terms: 336 rows
insert into glossary_terms (slug, term, definition, blueprint_area) values
  ($lp$dre-department-of-real-estate$lp$, $lp$DRE (Department of Real Estate)$lp$, $lp$The California state agency that licenses and regulates real estate salespersons and brokers and approves pre-licensing courses.$lp$, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$),
  ($lp$real-estate-commissioner$lp$, $lp$Real Estate Commissioner$lp$, $lp$The appointed head of the California DRE who enforces the Real Estate Law and issues regulations that carry the force of law.$lp$, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$),
  ($lp$salesperson$lp$, $lp$Salesperson$lp$, $lp$A licensee who may perform licensed real estate activities only while employed by and supervised by a responsible broker.$lp$, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$),
  ($lp$broker$lp$, $lp$Broker$lp$, $lp$A licensee authorized to operate a real estate business, employ salespersons, and represent clients directly for compensation.$lp$, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$),
  ($lp$realtor$lp$, $lp$Realtor®$lp$, $lp$A trademarked designation for a licensee who is a member of the National Association of Realtors; not all licensees are Realtors®.$lp$, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$),
  ($lp$reciprocity$lp$, $lp$Reciprocity$lp$, $lp$Recognition of another state's real estate license; California has no license reciprocity with any state.$lp$, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$),
  ($lp$real-property$lp$, $lp$Real property$lp$, $lp$Land, anything affixed to it, anything appurtenant to it, and that which is immovable by law — the land plus the bundle of rights.$lp$, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$),
  ($lp$personal-property$lp$, $lp$Personal property$lp$, $lp$Movable property (chattel) that is not real property; it transfers by bill of sale rather than by deed.$lp$, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$),
  ($lp$fixture$lp$, $lp$Fixture$lp$, $lp$An item of personal property that has become real property by being attached to land or improvements, judged by tests like MARIA.$lp$, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$),
  ($lp$maria$lp$, $lp$MARIA$lp$, $lp$Memory aid for the fixture tests: Method of attachment, Adaptability, Relationship of the parties, Intention, and Agreement.$lp$, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$),
  ($lp$trade-fixture$lp$, $lp$Trade fixture$lp$, $lp$An item installed by a business tenant for use in its trade; unlike ordinary fixtures, the tenant may remove it before the lease ends.$lp$, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$),
  ($lp$appurtenance$lp$, $lp$Appurtenance$lp$, $lp$A right or improvement that runs with the land and transfers automatically with it, such as an easement benefiting the parcel or mineral rights.$lp$, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$),
  ($lp$emblements$lp$, $lp$Emblements$lp$, $lp$Annual crops produced by labor (like strawberries or row crops); treated as the tenant farmer's personal property even after a tenancy ends.$lp$, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$),
  ($lp$riparian-rights$lp$, $lp$Riparian rights$lp$, $lp$The water rights of an owner whose land borders a flowing watercourse such as a river or stream.$lp$, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$),
  ($lp$littoral-rights$lp$, $lp$Littoral rights$lp$, $lp$The water rights of an owner whose land borders a non-flowing body of water such as a lake or the ocean.$lp$, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$),
  ($lp$bundle-of-rights$lp$, $lp$Bundle of rights$lp$, $lp$The collection of ownership rights in real property: possession, use, enjoyment, encumbrance (exclusion), and disposition.$lp$, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$),
  ($lp$fee-simple-absolute$lp$, $lp$Fee simple absolute$lp$, $lp$The greatest estate in land — ownership of indefinite duration, freely transferable and inheritable, subject only to public and private restrictions.$lp$, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$),
  ($lp$fee-simple-defeasible$lp$, $lp$Fee simple defeasible$lp$, $lp$A fee estate that can be lost if a stated condition is violated, such as land granted 'so long as' it is used for a school.$lp$, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$),
  ($lp$life-estate$lp$, $lp$Life estate$lp$, $lp$A freehold estate whose duration is measured by someone's lifetime; the life tenant may use the property but not commit waste.$lp$, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$),
  ($lp$remainder$lp$, $lp$Remainder$lp$, $lp$The future interest held by a third party who takes title when a life estate ends.$lp$, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$),
  ($lp$reversion$lp$, $lp$Reversion$lp$, $lp$The future interest retained by a grantor who takes the property back when a lesser estate (like a life estate) ends.$lp$, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$),
  ($lp$estate-for-years$lp$, $lp$Estate for years$lp$, $lp$A leasehold with a definite start and end date; it terminates automatically without notice.$lp$, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$),
  ($lp$periodic-tenancy$lp$, $lp$Periodic tenancy$lp$, $lp$A leasehold that renews automatically period to period (like month-to-month) until either party gives proper notice.$lp$, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$),
  ($lp$estate-at-will$lp$, $lp$Estate at will$lp$, $lp$A tenancy with no fixed term that either party may end at any time (with statutory notice in California).$lp$, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$),
  ($lp$estate-at-sufferance$lp$, $lp$Estate at sufferance$lp$, $lp$The interest of a tenant who lawfully took possession but stays after the right to possession has ended — a holdover tenant.$lp$, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$),
  ($lp$severalty$lp$, $lp$Severalty$lp$, $lp$Ownership by one person or entity alone — sole ownership, with interests 'severed' from everyone else.$lp$, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$),
  ($lp$joint-tenancy$lp$, $lp$Joint tenancy$lp$, $lp$Co-ownership with right of survivorship requiring the four unities of time, title, interest, and possession.$lp$, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$),
  ($lp$tenancy-in-common$lp$, $lp$Tenancy in common$lp$, $lp$Co-ownership in undivided shares (which may be unequal) with no right of survivorship; each owner's share passes to their heirs.$lp$, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$),
  ($lp$community-property$lp$, $lp$Community property$lp$, $lp$Property acquired by California spouses during marriage through the labor of either; each spouse owns an equal, undivided one-half interest.$lp$, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$),
  ($lp$right-of-survivorship$lp$, $lp$Right of survivorship$lp$, $lp$The feature of joint tenancy (and community property with right of survivorship) by which a deceased co-owner's interest passes automatically to the surviving co-owner(s).$lp$, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$),
  ($lp$undivided-interest$lp$, $lp$Undivided interest$lp$, $lp$A co-owner's fractional interest in the whole property, entitling them to possession of all of it, not a physically divided piece.$lp$, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$),
  ($lp$partition-action$lp$, $lp$Partition action$lp$, $lp$A lawsuit by a co-owner asking a court to divide co-owned property or order it sold and split the proceeds.$lp$, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$),
  ($lp$encumbrance$lp$, $lp$Encumbrance$lp$, $lp$Any claim, lien, charge, or liability attached to real property that limits or burdens it, such as easements, liens, or restrictions.$lp$, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$),
  ($lp$lien$lp$, $lp$Lien$lp$, $lp$A money encumbrance — a charge against property making it security for a debt, such as a trust deed, tax lien, or judgment lien.$lp$, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$),
  ($lp$mechanic-s-lien$lp$, $lp$Mechanic's lien$lp$, $lp$A statutory lien available to contractors, subcontractors, laborers, and material suppliers who improve property and are not paid.$lp$, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$),
  ($lp$judgment-lien$lp$, $lp$Judgment lien$lp$, $lp$An involuntary, general lien created by recording an abstract of judgment against the debtor's property in the county.$lp$, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$),
  ($lp$lis-pendens$lp$, $lp$Lis pendens$lp$, $lp$A recorded notice of pending litigation affecting title to or possession of real property, clouding title until the suit resolves.$lp$, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$),
  ($lp$easement$lp$, $lp$Easement$lp$, $lp$A non-possessory right to use another person's land for a specific purpose, such as a driveway or utility line.$lp$, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$),
  ($lp$easement-appurtenant$lp$, $lp$Easement appurtenant$lp$, $lp$An easement that benefits a neighboring parcel (the dominant tenement) and runs with the land when either parcel is sold.$lp$, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$),
  ($lp$easement-in-gross$lp$, $lp$Easement in gross$lp$, $lp$An easement that benefits a person or company rather than a parcel of land — utility easements are the classic example.$lp$, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$),
  ($lp$dominant-tenement$lp$, $lp$Dominant tenement$lp$, $lp$The parcel of land that benefits from an easement appurtenant.$lp$, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$),
  ($lp$servient-tenement$lp$, $lp$Servient tenement$lp$, $lp$The parcel of land that is burdened by an easement — the land the easement crosses.$lp$, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$),
  ($lp$prescription$lp$, $lp$Prescription$lp$, $lp$Acquiring an easement by open, notorious, hostile, and continuous use of another's land for five years in California.$lp$, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$),
  ($lp$encroachment$lp$, $lp$Encroachment$lp$, $lp$A physical intrusion of a structure or improvement onto a neighbor's land, such as a fence built over the boundary line.$lp$, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$),
  ($lp$cc-and-rs$lp$, $lp$CC&Rs$lp$, $lp$Covenants, conditions, and restrictions — recorded private restrictions that control how property in a subdivision or common-interest development may be used.$lp$, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$),
  ($lp$license-real-property$lp$, $lp$License (real property)$lp$, $lp$A revocable, personal permission to use another's land that, unlike an easement, creates no interest in the land.$lp$, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$),
  ($lp$agency$lp$, $lp$Agency$lp$, $lp$The relationship in which one person (the agent) represents another (the principal) in dealings with third parties.$lp$, $lp$agency$lp$),
  ($lp$fiduciary$lp$, $lp$Fiduciary$lp$, $lp$A person in a position of trust and confidence who owes their principal utmost care, integrity, honesty, and loyalty.$lp$, $lp$agency$lp$),
  ($lp$principal$lp$, $lp$Principal$lp$, $lp$The person who authorizes an agent to represent them — in a listing, the seller; in a buyer representation agreement, the buyer.$lp$, $lp$agency$lp$),
  ($lp$old-car$lp$, $lp$OLD CAR$lp$, $lp$Memory aid for fiduciary duties: Obedience, Loyalty, Disclosure, Confidentiality, Accounting, and Reasonable care.$lp$, $lp$agency$lp$),
  ($lp$dual-agency$lp$, $lp$Dual agency$lp$, $lp$One brokerage representing both buyer and seller in the same transaction; lawful in California only with the informed written consent of both.$lp$, $lp$agency$lp$),
  ($lp$ostensible-agency$lp$, $lp$Ostensible agency$lp$, $lp$Agency created by the principal's conduct causing a third party to reasonably believe someone is their agent, even without an express agreement.$lp$, $lp$agency$lp$),
  ($lp$ratification$lp$, $lp$Ratification$lp$, $lp$Approval of an act after the fact — a principal accepting the benefits of an unauthorized act creates agency by ratification.$lp$, $lp$agency$lp$),
  ($lp$subagent$lp$, $lp$Subagent$lp$, $lp$An agent of an agent — a person authorized to help perform the agency, owing the same duties to the principal.$lp$, $lp$agency$lp$),
  ($lp$secret-profit$lp$, $lp$Secret profit$lp$, $lp$Any undisclosed compensation or benefit an agent gains from the agency; taking one violates the duty of loyalty and is grounds for discipline.$lp$, $lp$agency$lp$),
  ($lp$puffing$lp$, $lp$Puffing$lp$, $lp$A non-factual, exaggerated sales statement of opinion ('best view in Pasadena') that a reasonable buyer would not rely on as fact.$lp$, $lp$agency$lp$),
  ($lp$misrepresentation$lp$, $lp$Misrepresentation$lp$, $lp$A false statement of material fact; if intentional it is fraud, and even innocent misrepresentation can allow rescission.$lp$, $lp$agency$lp$),
  ($lp$contract$lp$, $lp$Contract$lp$, $lp$A legally enforceable agreement requiring capable parties, mutual consent, lawful object, and consideration.$lp$, $lp$contracts$lp$),
  ($lp$offer$lp$, $lp$Offer$lp$, $lp$A proposal showing contractual intent, definite terms, and communication to the offeree, which the offeree can accept to form a contract.$lp$, $lp$contracts$lp$),
  ($lp$acceptance$lp$, $lp$Acceptance$lp$, $lp$The offeree's unqualified agreement to the offer's terms, communicated in the manner required by the offer.$lp$, $lp$contracts$lp$),
  ($lp$counteroffer$lp$, $lp$Counteroffer$lp$, $lp$A response that changes the terms of an offer; it rejects and terminates the original offer and becomes a new offer.$lp$, $lp$contracts$lp$),
  ($lp$consideration$lp$, $lp$Consideration$lp$, $lp$Something of value exchanged by each party — money, a promise, or forbearance — required for a valid contract.$lp$, $lp$contracts$lp$),
  ($lp$statute-of-frauds$lp$, $lp$Statute of frauds$lp$, $lp$The law (Civil Code §1624) requiring certain contracts, including those for the sale of real property, to be in writing to be enforceable.$lp$, $lp$contracts$lp$),
  ($lp$void-contract$lp$, $lp$Void contract$lp$, $lp$An agreement with no legal effect at all, such as a contract for an illegal purpose.$lp$, $lp$contracts$lp$),
  ($lp$voidable-contract$lp$, $lp$Voidable contract$lp$, $lp$A contract valid on its face that one party (such as a minor or a defrauded party) may elect to rescind.$lp$, $lp$contracts$lp$),
  ($lp$unenforceable-contract$lp$, $lp$Unenforceable contract$lp$, $lp$A contract that may be valid between the parties but that a court will not enforce, such as an oral real estate sale agreement.$lp$, $lp$contracts$lp$),
  ($lp$executory-contract$lp$, $lp$Executory contract$lp$, $lp$A contract in which something remains to be performed — a purchase agreement is executory until closing.$lp$, $lp$contracts$lp$),
  ($lp$executed-contract$lp$, $lp$Executed contract$lp$, $lp$A contract fully performed by both parties; also used loosely to mean 'signed.'$lp$, $lp$contracts$lp$),
  ($lp$rescission$lp$, $lp$Rescission$lp$, $lp$A remedy that cancels the contract and restores the parties to their pre-contract positions, returning consideration paid.$lp$, $lp$contracts$lp$),
  ($lp$liquidated-damages$lp$, $lp$Liquidated damages$lp$, $lp$An amount the parties agree in advance will serve as damages for breach; in California residential purchases, capped at 3% of the price for the buyer's deposit.$lp$, $lp$contracts$lp$),
  ($lp$specific-performance$lp$, $lp$Specific performance$lp$, $lp$A court order compelling a breaching party to perform the contract; available for real estate because land is unique.$lp$, $lp$contracts$lp$),
  ($lp$novation$lp$, $lp$Novation$lp$, $lp$Substituting a new contract or a new party for an old one, with intent to extinguish the original obligation.$lp$, $lp$contracts$lp$),
  ($lp$assignment$lp$, $lp$Assignment$lp$, $lp$Transferring one's rights under a contract to another person; the assignor remains secondarily liable unless there is a novation.$lp$, $lp$contracts$lp$),
  ($lp$option$lp$, $lp$Option$lp$, $lp$A contract that keeps an offer open — the optionor sells the optionee the right, for consideration, to buy at set terms within a set time.$lp$, $lp$contracts$lp$),
  ($lp$contingency$lp$, $lp$Contingency$lp$, $lp$A condition (like financing, appraisal, or inspection) that must be satisfied or waived before a party is obligated to close.$lp$, $lp$contracts$lp$),
  ($lp$exclusive-right-to-sell-listing$lp$, $lp$Exclusive right to sell listing$lp$, $lp$A listing under which the broker earns the commission no matter who procures the buyer — even if the owner sells it themselves.$lp$, $lp$contracts$lp$),
  ($lp$exclusive-agency-listing$lp$, $lp$Exclusive agency listing$lp$, $lp$A listing under which the broker is the only agent, but the owner may sell without paying a commission.$lp$, $lp$contracts$lp$),
  ($lp$open-listing$lp$, $lp$Open listing$lp$, $lp$A non-exclusive listing given to any number of brokers; only the broker who procures the buyer earns a commission.$lp$, $lp$contracts$lp$),
  ($lp$safety-clause$lp$, $lp$Safety clause$lp$, $lp$A listing provision entitling the broker to a commission if, within a stated period after expiration, the owner sells to a buyer the broker introduced.$lp$, $lp$contracts$lp$),
  ($lp$time-is-of-the-essence$lp$, $lp$Time is of the essence$lp$, $lp$A contract clause making stated deadlines strict, so late performance is a material breach.$lp$, $lp$contracts$lp$),
  ($lp$tds-transfer-disclosure-statement$lp$, $lp$TDS (Transfer Disclosure Statement)$lp$, $lp$The statutory form on which a California residential seller discloses the property's known condition and defects to the buyer.$lp$, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$),
  ($lp$nhd-natural-hazard-disclosure$lp$, $lp$NHD (Natural Hazard Disclosure)$lp$, $lp$The statutory disclosure identifying whether property lies in mapped hazard zones such as flood, fire severity, or earthquake fault zones.$lp$, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$),
  ($lp$material-fact$lp$, $lp$Material fact$lp$, $lp$Any fact that would affect a reasonable person's decision to buy or the price they would pay; agents must disclose material facts they know.$lp$, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$),
  ($lp$as-is$lp$, $lp$As-is$lp$, $lp$A sale term meaning the seller won't repair or credit for known conditions; it does not eliminate California disclosure duties.$lp$, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$),
  ($lp$stigmatized-property$lp$, $lp$Stigmatized property$lp$, $lp$Property psychologically impacted by an event such as a death; California limits required disclosure of deaths to those within three years, with exceptions.$lp$, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$),
  ($lp$megan-s-law-disclosure$lp$, $lp$Megan's Law disclosure$lp$, $lp$The statutorily required notice in sales contracts about the public sex-offender database maintained by the Department of Justice.$lp$, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$),
  ($lp$escrow$lp$, $lp$Escrow$lp$, $lp$A neutral third-party arrangement in which a depository holds funds and documents and closes the transaction per the parties' mutual instructions.$lp$, $lp$transfer$lp$),
  ($lp$neutral-depository$lp$, $lp$Neutral depository$lp$, $lp$The escrow holder's role: a dual agent of both parties for carrying out instructions, favoring neither.$lp$, $lp$transfer$lp$),
  ($lp$chain-of-title$lp$, $lp$Chain of title$lp$, $lp$The recorded history of successive ownership of a property, traced through public records.$lp$, $lp$transfer$lp$),
  ($lp$marketable-title$lp$, $lp$Marketable title$lp$, $lp$Title reasonably free from doubt and defects that a prudent buyer would accept.$lp$, $lp$transfer$lp$),
  ($lp$preliminary-report$lp$, $lp$Preliminary report$lp$, $lp$The title company's report of the current condition of title — vesting, liens, and exceptions — offered before issuing a policy.$lp$, $lp$transfer$lp$),
  ($lp$clta-policy$lp$, $lp$CLTA policy$lp$, $lp$The standard California title insurance policy, typically protecting the owner against recorded defects and matters of public record.$lp$, $lp$transfer$lp$),
  ($lp$alta-policy$lp$, $lp$ALTA policy$lp$, $lp$The extended-coverage title policy, typically required by lenders, that adds protection for off-record matters like unrecorded liens and survey issues.$lp$, $lp$transfer$lp$),
  ($lp$grant-deed$lp$, $lp$Grant deed$lp$, $lp$California's usual deed, carrying two implied warranties: the grantor has not already conveyed the property and has disclosed known encumbrances.$lp$, $lp$transfer$lp$),
  ($lp$quitclaim-deed$lp$, $lp$Quitclaim deed$lp$, $lp$A deed conveying whatever interest the grantor has, if any, with no warranties at all.$lp$, $lp$transfer$lp$),
  ($lp$recording$lp$, $lp$Recording$lp$, $lp$Filing a document with the county recorder, giving constructive notice of its contents to the world and establishing priority.$lp$, $lp$transfer$lp$),
  ($lp$constructive-notice$lp$, $lp$Constructive notice$lp$, $lp$Notice imputed by law — from recorded documents or from possession of the property — whether or not a person actually knows.$lp$, $lp$transfer$lp$),
  ($lp$actual-notice$lp$, $lp$Actual notice$lp$, $lp$Knowledge a person actually has, however acquired.$lp$, $lp$transfer$lp$),
  ($lp$acknowledgment$lp$, $lp$Acknowledgment$lp$, $lp$A formal declaration before a notary that a signature is genuine and voluntary; required before a deed can be recorded.$lp$, $lp$transfer$lp$),
  ($lp$promissory-note$lp$, $lp$Promissory note$lp$, $lp$The borrower's written promise to repay a debt; the evidence of the debt that the security instrument secures.$lp$, $lp$financing$lp$),
  ($lp$deed-of-trust$lp$, $lp$Deed of trust$lp$, $lp$California's dominant security instrument, involving a trustor (borrower), beneficiary (lender), and trustee holding bare legal title with power of sale.$lp$, $lp$financing$lp$),
  ($lp$trustor$lp$, $lp$Trustor$lp$, $lp$The borrower under a deed of trust, who conveys bare legal title to the trustee as security for the loan.$lp$, $lp$financing$lp$),
  ($lp$trustee$lp$, $lp$Trustee$lp$, $lp$The neutral third party under a deed of trust who holds bare legal title and may sell the property upon default via the power of sale.$lp$, $lp$financing$lp$),
  ($lp$beneficiary$lp$, $lp$Beneficiary$lp$, $lp$The lender under a deed of trust, for whose benefit the property is held as security.$lp$, $lp$financing$lp$),
  ($lp$power-of-sale$lp$, $lp$Power of sale$lp$, $lp$The clause in a deed of trust authorizing the trustee to sell the property non-judicially after default, following statutory notice periods.$lp$, $lp$financing$lp$),
  ($lp$reinstatement$lp$, $lp$Reinstatement$lp$, $lp$The borrower's right to cure a default by paying the missed amounts (plus costs) and continue the loan, available until shortly before the trustee's sale.$lp$, $lp$financing$lp$),
  ($lp$deed-in-lieu-of-foreclosure$lp$, $lp$Deed in lieu of foreclosure$lp$, $lp$The borrower voluntarily deeding the property to the lender to avoid foreclosure; the lender takes subject to junior liens.$lp$, $lp$financing$lp$),
  ($lp$acceleration-clause$lp$, $lp$Acceleration clause$lp$, $lp$A provision letting the lender declare the entire balance due upon default.$lp$, $lp$financing$lp$),
  ($lp$alienation-due-on-sale-clause$lp$, $lp$Alienation (due-on-sale) clause$lp$, $lp$A provision letting the lender call the loan due if the borrower transfers the property.$lp$, $lp$financing$lp$),
  ($lp$deficiency-judgment$lp$, $lp$Deficiency judgment$lp$, $lp$A judgment against a borrower for the shortfall after a foreclosure sale; barred in California after non-judicial foreclosure and for standard purchase-money loans.$lp$, $lp$financing$lp$),
  ($lp$ltv-loan-to-value-ratio$lp$, $lp$LTV (loan-to-value ratio)$lp$, $lp$The loan amount divided by the lesser of price or appraised value; higher LTV means more leverage and more lender risk.$lp$, $lp$financing$lp$),
  ($lp$piti$lp$, $lp$PITI$lp$, $lp$The four components of a typical housing payment: principal, interest, taxes, and insurance.$lp$, $lp$financing$lp$),
  ($lp$discount-points$lp$, $lp$Discount points$lp$, $lp$Prepaid interest charged at closing — one point equals 1% of the loan amount — used to buy down the rate or increase lender yield.$lp$, $lp$financing$lp$),
  ($lp$apr-annual-percentage-rate$lp$, $lp$APR (annual percentage rate)$lp$, $lp$The cost of credit expressed as a yearly rate including certain fees, disclosed under the Truth in Lending Act.$lp$, $lp$financing$lp$),
  ($lp$amortization$lp$, $lp$Amortization$lp$, $lp$Repaying a loan through regular payments that cover interest and gradually reduce principal to zero at maturity.$lp$, $lp$financing$lp$),
  ($lp$conforming-loan$lp$, $lp$Conforming loan$lp$, $lp$A conventional loan meeting Fannie Mae/Freddie Mac purchase standards, including the loan-size limit.$lp$, $lp$financing$lp$),
  ($lp$pmi-private-mortgage-insurance$lp$, $lp$PMI (private mortgage insurance)$lp$, $lp$Insurance protecting a conventional lender against borrower default, generally required when LTV exceeds 80%.$lp$, $lp$financing$lp$),
  ($lp$wraparound-all-inclusive-trust-deed$lp$, $lp$Wraparound (all-inclusive) trust deed$lp$, $lp$Junior financing in which the new lender's note wraps the existing loan; the borrower pays the wrap lender, who keeps paying the underlying loan.$lp$, $lp$financing$lp$),
  ($lp$seller-financing-purchase-money$lp$, $lp$Seller financing (purchase-money)$lp$, $lp$Credit the seller extends to the buyer, secured by the property being sold.$lp$, $lp$financing$lp$),
  ($lp$market-value$lp$, $lp$Market value$lp$, $lp$The most probable price a property should bring in an open, competitive market with informed parties acting prudently and without duress.$lp$, $lp$valuation$lp$),
  ($lp$principle-of-substitution$lp$, $lp$Principle of substitution$lp$, $lp$The idea that a buyer will pay no more for a property than the cost of acquiring an equally desirable substitute — the foundation of the sales comparison approach.$lp$, $lp$valuation$lp$),
  ($lp$highest-and-best-use$lp$, $lp$Highest and best use$lp$, $lp$The legally permissible, physically possible, financially feasible use that produces the greatest value.$lp$, $lp$valuation$lp$),
  ($lp$sales-comparison-approach$lp$, $lp$Sales comparison approach$lp$, $lp$Valuing property by adjusting recent sales of comparable properties for differences; the primary approach for houses.$lp$, $lp$valuation$lp$),
  ($lp$cost-approach$lp$, $lp$Cost approach$lp$, $lp$Valuing property as land value plus replacement cost of improvements minus depreciation; most reliable for new or special-purpose buildings.$lp$, $lp$valuation$lp$),
  ($lp$income-approach$lp$, $lp$Income approach$lp$, $lp$Valuing property by capitalizing its net operating income: value = NOI ÷ capitalization rate.$lp$, $lp$valuation$lp$),
  ($lp$capitalization-rate$lp$, $lp$Capitalization rate$lp$, $lp$The rate of return used to convert net operating income to value; higher cap rates mean lower values for the same income.$lp$, $lp$valuation$lp$),
  ($lp$gross-rent-multiplier-grm$lp$, $lp$Gross rent multiplier (GRM)$lp$, $lp$A shortcut ratio — price divided by gross rent — used to estimate the value of rental property from its rent.$lp$, $lp$valuation$lp$),
  ($lp$depreciation-appraisal$lp$, $lp$Depreciation (appraisal)$lp$, $lp$Loss in value from any cause: physical deterioration, functional obsolescence, or external (economic) obsolescence.$lp$, $lp$valuation$lp$),
  ($lp$functional-obsolescence$lp$, $lp$Functional obsolescence$lp$, $lp$Loss in value from outdated or poor design — a one-bath five-bedroom house, for example.$lp$, $lp$valuation$lp$),
  ($lp$external-economic-obsolescence$lp$, $lp$External (economic) obsolescence$lp$, $lp$Loss in value from factors outside the property, such as a new freeway ramp next door; it is incurable.$lp$, $lp$valuation$lp$),
  ($lp$cma-comparative-market-analysis$lp$, $lp$CMA (comparative market analysis)$lp$, $lp$A licensee's pricing analysis using comparable sales; it is not an appraisal, which only a licensed appraiser can perform.$lp$, $lp$valuation$lp$),
  ($lp$reconciliation$lp$, $lp$Reconciliation$lp$, $lp$The appraiser's weighing of the value indications from the three approaches into a final opinion of value.$lp$, $lp$valuation$lp$),
  ($lp$police-power$lp$, $lp$Police power$lp$, $lp$The government's inherent power to regulate for public health, safety, morals, and welfare — the basis of zoning — without compensation.$lp$, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$),
  ($lp$eminent-domain$lp$, $lp$Eminent domain$lp$, $lp$The government's power to take private property for public use upon payment of just compensation, through condemnation.$lp$, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$),
  ($lp$inverse-condemnation$lp$, $lp$Inverse condemnation$lp$, $lp$An owner's action against the government when its activity has effectively taken or damaged property without formal condemnation.$lp$, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$),
  ($lp$variance$lp$, $lp$Variance$lp$, $lp$Permission to deviate from a zoning standard (like a setback) due to a parcel's special hardship; not a change of use classification.$lp$, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$),
  ($lp$nonconforming-use$lp$, $lp$Nonconforming use$lp$, $lp$A lawful use that predates a zoning change and may continue ('grandfathered') though it no longer conforms.$lp$, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$),
  ($lp$conditional-use-permit$lp$, $lp$Conditional use permit$lp$, $lp$Permission for a use zoning allows only upon special approval and conditions, like a church or school in a residential zone.$lp$, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$),
  ($lp$protected-class$lp$, $lp$Protected class$lp$, $lp$A group protected from housing discrimination — federally: race, color, religion, sex, national origin, disability, familial status; California adds more (e.g., source of income).$lp$, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$),
  ($lp$steering$lp$, $lp$Steering$lp$, $lp$Channeling homebuyers toward or away from neighborhoods based on a protected characteristic; illegal.$lp$, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$),
  ($lp$blockbusting$lp$, $lp$Blockbusting$lp$, $lp$Inducing owners to sell by suggesting members of a protected class are moving into the neighborhood (panic selling); illegal.$lp$, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$),
  ($lp$redlining$lp$, $lp$Redlining$lp$, $lp$Refusing to lend or insure in an area based on its demographics rather than the applicant's qualifications; illegal.$lp$, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$),
  ($lp$familial-status$lp$, $lp$Familial status$lp$, $lp$The protected class covering households with children under 18, pregnant persons, and those securing custody.$lp$, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$),
  ($lp$unruh-civil-rights-act$lp$, $lp$Unruh Civil Rights Act$lp$, $lp$California law prohibiting discrimination by all business establishments — including brokerages and landlords.$lp$, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$),
  ($lp$feha$lp$, $lp$FEHA$lp$, $lp$California's Fair Employment and Housing Act, the state's primary fair-housing statute, broader than the federal act.$lp$, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$),
  ($lp$ad-valorem$lp$, $lp$Ad valorem$lp$, $lp$'According to value' — property taxes are ad valorem taxes based on assessed value.$lp$, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$),
  ($lp$proposition-13$lp$, $lp$Proposition 13$lp$, $lp$The California constitutional limit capping the basic property tax rate at 1% of assessed value with assessment increases limited to 2% per year until a change of ownership.$lp$, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$),
  ($lp$documentary-transfer-tax$lp$, $lp$Documentary transfer tax$lp$, $lp$A county (and sometimes city) tax on transferring real property, commonly $0.55 per $500 of consideration at the county level.$lp$, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$),
  ($lp$basis-tax$lp$, $lp$Basis (tax)$lp$, $lp$The owner's investment in property for tax purposes — generally cost plus improvements — used to measure gain on sale.$lp$, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$),
  ($lp$1031-exchange$lp$, $lp$1031 exchange$lp$, $lp$A tax-deferred exchange of like-kind investment or business real property under Internal Revenue Code §1031.$lp$, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$),
  ($lp$boot$lp$, $lp$Boot$lp$, $lp$Non-like-kind value (cash or debt relief) received in a 1031 exchange; taxable to the extent of gain.$lp$, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$),
  ($lp$trust-fund$lp$, $lp$Trust fund$lp$, $lp$Money or things of value received by a licensee on behalf of another in a transaction — deposits, rents — which must be placed in neutral escrow, a trust account, or the owner's hands within three business days.$lp$, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$),
  ($lp$commingling$lp$, $lp$Commingling$lp$, $lp$Mixing trust funds with the licensee's own funds — a license-discipline violation even without loss to anyone.$lp$, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$),
  ($lp$conversion$lp$, $lp$Conversion$lp$, $lp$Misappropriating trust funds — actually using client money; more serious than commingling and criminally chargeable.$lp$, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$),
  ($lp$proration$lp$, $lp$Proration$lp$, $lp$Dividing ongoing expenses (taxes, rents, HOA dues) between buyer and seller as of closing, typically using a 30-day month/360-day year unless agreed otherwise.$lp$, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$),
  ($lp$impound-account$lp$, $lp$Impound account$lp$, $lp$A lender-held account collecting monthly installments toward property taxes and insurance with each loan payment.$lp$, $lp$financing$lp$),
  ($lp$unlawful-detainer$lp$, $lp$Unlawful detainer$lp$, $lp$California's summary court action by which a landlord recovers possession from a tenant after proper notice.$lp$, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$),
  ($lp$constructive-eviction$lp$, $lp$Constructive eviction$lp$, $lp$A landlord's breach that makes the premises unusable, allowing the tenant to move out and end the lease without further liability.$lp$, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$),
  ($lp$live-scan$lp$, $lp$Live Scan$lp$, $lp$Electronic fingerprinting submitted with a California license application so the Department of Justice and FBI can complete a background check.$lp$, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$),
  ($lp$inactive-license$lp$, $lp$Inactive license$lp$, $lp$A valid real estate license not associated with an employing broker; the licensee may not perform licensed activity until a broker sponsors them.$lp$, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$),
  ($lp$restricted-license$lp$, $lp$Restricted license$lp$, $lp$A license issued or continued after discipline subject to conditions such as working for a named broker, filing reports, posting a bond, or serving probation.$lp$, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$),
  ($lp$mls-multiple-listing-service$lp$, $lp$MLS (Multiple Listing Service)$lp$, $lp$A private cooperative listing database operated by associations of Realtors®, not a government registry; access generally requires association membership.$lp$, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$),
  ($lp$manufactured-mobile-home$lp$, $lp$Manufactured (mobile) home$lp$, $lp$Factory-built housing that is personal property when registered with HCD on rented space, and becomes real property when placed on a permanent foundation on owned land as provided by statute.$lp$, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$),
  ($lp$fixture-filing$lp$, $lp$Fixture filing$lp$, $lp$A UCC financing statement recorded against real property to perfect a security interest in goods that have become fixtures — leased solar systems are a common example.$lp$, $lp$financing$lp$),
  ($lp$chattel$lp$, $lp$Chattel$lp$, $lp$Another name for personal property; chattel loans finance personal property at different rates and terms than real property mortgages.$lp$, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$),
  ($lp$annexation$lp$, $lp$Annexation$lp$, $lp$Attaching personal property to land so that it becomes real property, such as lumber built into a deck.$lp$, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$),
  ($lp$severance$lp$, $lp$Severance$lp$, $lp$Detaching something from real property — crops, fixtures, minerals — so that it becomes personal property.$lp$, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$),
  ($lp$accession$lp$, $lp$Accession$lp$, $lp$Acquiring property by its addition to property you already own, such as trade fixtures a tenant abandons after the tenancy ends.$lp$, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$),
  ($lp$fructus-naturales$lp$, $lp$Fructus naturales$lp$, $lp$Naturally occurring growth such as native trees and grasses; part of the real property until severed.$lp$, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$),
  ($lp$estate$lp$, $lp$Estate$lp$, $lp$The degree, quantity, nature, and extent of a person's interest in land.$lp$, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$),
  ($lp$freehold-estate$lp$, $lp$Freehold estate$lp$, $lp$An ownership estate of indefinite duration — fee estates and life estates.$lp$, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$),
  ($lp$less-than-freehold-estate$lp$, $lp$Less-than-freehold estate$lp$, $lp$A possessory leasehold estate for a determinable period; classified as personal property (chattel real).$lp$, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$),
  ($lp$possibility-of-reverter$lp$, $lp$Possibility of reverter$lp$, $lp$The future interest a grantor retains after conveying a fee simple determinable; it takes effect automatically.$lp$, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$),
  ($lp$right-of-re-entry-power-of-termination$lp$, $lp$Right of re-entry (power of termination)$lp$, $lp$The grantor's future interest after a fee simple subject to a condition subsequent; the grantor must act to reclaim title.$lp$, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$),
  ($lp$pur-autre-vie$lp$, $lp$Pur autre vie$lp$, $lp$A life estate measured by the life of someone other than the holder; it may pass to the holder's heirs until the measuring life ends.$lp$, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$),
  ($lp$waste$lp$, $lp$Waste$lp$, $lp$Permanent injury to property by a life tenant or tenant that harms the interest of a future interest holder.$lp$, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$),
  ($lp$leased-fee$lp$, $lp$Leased fee$lp$, $lp$The landlord's remaining ownership interest in property subject to a lease.$lp$, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$),
  ($lp$pete$lp$, $lp$PETE$lp$, $lp$The four government limits on ownership: Police power, Eminent domain, Taxation, Escheat.$lp$, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$),
  ($lp$escheat$lp$, $lp$Escheat$lp$, $lp$Reversion of property to the state when an owner dies with no will and no heirs.$lp$, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$),
  ($lp$concurrent-ownership$lp$, $lp$Concurrent ownership$lp$, $lp$Co-ownership of property by two or more persons at the same time.$lp$, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$),
  ($lp$t-tip-four-unities$lp$, $lp$T-TIP (four unities)$lp$, $lp$Time, Title, Interest, Possession — the four unities required to create and maintain a joint tenancy.$lp$, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$),
  ($lp$severance-joint-tenancy$lp$, $lp$Severance (joint tenancy)$lp$, $lp$Breaking a unity so that a joint tenancy interest converts to a tenancy in common.$lp$, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$),
  ($lp$community-property-with-right-of-survivorship$lp$, $lp$Community property with right of survivorship$lp$, $lp$A California vesting combining community property treatment with automatic survivorship, avoiding probate on the first death.$lp$, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$),
  ($lp$transmutation$lp$, $lp$Transmutation$lp$, $lp$A change in the character of marital property between community and separate, subject to statutory requirements.$lp$, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$),
  ($lp$affidavit-of-death-of-joint-tenant$lp$, $lp$Affidavit of death of joint tenant$lp$, $lp$The document recorded with a certified death certificate to clear a deceased joint tenant from title.$lp$, $lp$transfer$lp$),
  ($lp$certification-of-trust$lp$, $lp$Certification of trust$lp$, $lp$An abbreviated document evidencing a trustee's authority to act without disclosing the trust's private terms.$lp$, $lp$transfer$lp$),
  ($lp$limited-partnership$lp$, $lp$Limited partnership$lp$, $lp$A partnership with general partners who manage and bear unlimited liability and limited partners who are passive with capped liability.$lp$, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$),
  ($lp$limited-liability-company-llc$lp$, $lp$Limited liability company (LLC)$lp$, $lp$An entity combining liability protection with pass-through taxation; signing authority flows from the operating agreement.$lp$, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$),
  ($lp$stock-cooperative$lp$, $lp$Stock cooperative$lp$, $lp$A structure in which a corporation owns the building and residents own shares plus a proprietary lease — personal property, not real property.$lp$, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$),
  ($lp$common-interest-development-cid$lp$, $lp$Common interest development (CID)$lp$, $lp$Condominiums, planned developments, cooperatives, and community apartment projects, governed in California by the Davis-Stirling Act.$lp$, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$),
  ($lp$voluntary-lien$lp$, $lp$Voluntary lien$lp$, $lp$A lien the owner creates deliberately, such as a deed of trust securing a loan.$lp$, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$),
  ($lp$involuntary-lien$lp$, $lp$Involuntary lien$lp$, $lp$A lien imposed by operation of law without the owner's agreement, such as a tax, judgment, or mechanic's lien.$lp$, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$),
  ($lp$specific-lien$lp$, $lp$Specific lien$lp$, $lp$A lien attaching to one identified parcel of property.$lp$, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$),
  ($lp$general-lien$lp$, $lp$General lien$lp$, $lp$A lien attaching to all of a debtor's property in the county or state, such as a judgment lien.$lp$, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$),
  ($lp$abstract-of-judgment$lp$, $lp$Abstract of judgment$lp$, $lp$The document recorded to create a judgment lien against a debtor's real property in a county.$lp$, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$),
  ($lp$relation-back$lp$, $lp$Relation back$lp$, $lp$The rule giving a perfected mechanic's lien priority as of the date work commenced rather than the date it was recorded.$lp$, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$),
  ($lp$notice-of-completion$lp$, $lp$Notice of completion$lp$, $lp$A document an owner may record after work finishes to shorten the period for filing mechanics' liens.$lp$, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$),
  ($lp$notice-of-nonresponsibility$lp$, $lp$Notice of nonresponsibility$lp$, $lp$A notice protecting an owner from mechanics' liens for work ordered by another party, such as a tenant.$lp$, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$),
  ($lp$special-assessment$lp$, $lp$Special assessment$lp$, $lp$A lien for a local improvement benefiting the property, such as sewers, lighting, or a Mello-Roos district.$lp$, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$),
  ($lp$attachment-lien$lp$, $lp$Attachment lien$lp$, $lp$A pre-judgment lien securing a claim while litigation is pending.$lp$, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$),
  ($lp$priority$lp$, $lp$Priority$lp$, $lp$The order in which liens are paid from sale proceeds, generally set by recording date, with property taxes always first.$lp$, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$),
  ($lp$subordination-agreement$lp$, $lp$Subordination agreement$lp$, $lp$A recorded agreement by which a lienholder voluntarily accepts a lower priority position.$lp$, $lp$financing$lp$),
  ($lp$easement-by-necessity$lp$, $lp$Easement by necessity$lp$, $lp$An easement implied when a conveyance leaves a parcel landlocked without legal access.$lp$, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$),
  ($lp$prescriptive-easement$lp$, $lp$Prescriptive easement$lp$, $lp$A use right acquired by open, notorious, hostile, and continuous use of another's land for five years in California.$lp$, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$),
  ($lp$merger$lp$, $lp$Merger$lp$, $lp$Termination of an easement when one person acquires both the dominant and servient tenements.$lp$, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$),
  ($lp$principal-client$lp$, $lp$Principal (client)$lp$, $lp$The person an agent represents and to whom fiduciary duties are owed.$lp$, $lp$agency$lp$),
  ($lp$third-party-customer$lp$, $lp$Third party (customer)$lp$, $lp$The other side of a transaction, owed honesty, fair dealing, and disclosure of material facts, but not fiduciary duties.$lp$, $lp$agency$lp$),
  ($lp$associate-licensee$lp$, $lp$Associate licensee$lp$, $lp$A salesperson acting for the broker; the agency relationship runs between the broker and the client.$lp$, $lp$agency$lp$),
  ($lp$express-agency$lp$, $lp$Express agency$lp$, $lp$Agency created by written or spoken agreement, such as a listing.$lp$, $lp$agency$lp$),
  ($lp$implied-agency$lp$, $lp$Implied agency$lp$, $lp$Agency created by the conduct of the parties without an express agreement.$lp$, $lp$agency$lp$),
  ($lp$special-agent$lp$, $lp$Special agent$lp$, $lp$An agent authorized for one specific transaction with limited authority — the usual role of a real estate broker.$lp$, $lp$agency$lp$),
  ($lp$general-agent$lp$, $lp$General agent$lp$, $lp$An agent with ongoing authority over a range of matters, such as a property manager.$lp$, $lp$agency$lp$),
  ($lp$universal-agent$lp$, $lp$Universal agent$lp$, $lp$An agent authorized to act for a principal in all matters, typically under a broad power of attorney.$lp$, $lp$agency$lp$),
  ($lp$concealment$lp$, $lp$Concealment$lp$, $lp$Actively hiding a known material defect; as actionable as an affirmative false statement.$lp$, $lp$agency$lp$),
  ($lp$visual-inspection-duty$lp$, $lp$Visual inspection duty$lp$, $lp$An agent's duty to conduct a reasonably competent and diligent visual inspection of accessible areas of 1–4 unit residential property and disclose material facts revealed.$lp$, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$),
  ($lp$agency-disclosure$lp$, $lp$Agency disclosure$lp$, $lp$California's statutory disclose–elect–confirm process identifying in writing whom each licensee represents.$lp$, $lp$agency$lp$),
  ($lp$emancipated-minor$lp$, $lp$Emancipated minor$lp$, $lp$A minor freed from parental control by marriage, military service, or court order, who may contract as an adult.$lp$, $lp$contracts$lp$),
  ($lp$actual-fraud$lp$, $lp$Actual fraud$lp$, $lp$Intentional misrepresentation or concealment of a material fact, relied upon to another's detriment.$lp$, $lp$contracts$lp$),
  ($lp$constructive-fraud$lp$, $lp$Constructive fraud$lp$, $lp$A breach of duty gaining an advantage without deliberate dishonesty; commonly applied to fiduciaries.$lp$, $lp$contracts$lp$),
  ($lp$mutual-mistake$lp$, $lp$Mutual mistake$lp$, $lp$A shared error by both parties about a material fact, permitting rescission.$lp$, $lp$contracts$lp$),
  ($lp$duress$lp$, $lp$Duress$lp$, $lp$Unlawful force or confinement that compels a party's consent.$lp$, $lp$contracts$lp$),
  ($lp$menace$lp$, $lp$Menace$lp$, $lp$A threat of duress, of unlawful injury to person or property, or of injury to character.$lp$, $lp$contracts$lp$),
  ($lp$part-performance$lp$, $lp$Part performance$lp$, $lp$A doctrine that can remove an oral agreement from the statute of frauds where a party took possession, paid, and improved in reliance.$lp$, $lp$contracts$lp$),
  ($lp$bilateral-contract$lp$, $lp$Bilateral contract$lp$, $lp$A contract exchanging a promise for a promise, binding both parties — the standard purchase agreement.$lp$, $lp$contracts$lp$),
  ($lp$unilateral-contract$lp$, $lp$Unilateral contract$lp$, $lp$A contract exchanging a promise for an act, binding only the offeror until performance — open listings and options.$lp$, $lp$contracts$lp$),
  ($lp$net-listing$lp$, $lp$Net listing$lp$, $lp$A compensation structure in which the broker keeps all proceeds above the seller's stated net; heavily restricted and conflict-prone.$lp$, $lp$contracts$lp$),
  ($lp$definite-termination-date$lp$, $lp$Definite termination date$lp$, $lp$The required end date in an exclusive listing; omitting it is grounds for disciplinary action.$lp$, $lp$contracts$lp$),
  ($lp$procuring-cause$lp$, $lp$Procuring cause$lp$, $lp$The doctrine identifying which broker set in motion the uninterrupted chain of events resulting in the sale.$lp$, $lp$contracts$lp$),
  ($lp$buyer-representation-agreement$lp$, $lp$Buyer representation agreement$lp$, $lp$The written contract employing a broker to represent a buyer, stating term, scope, duties, and compensation.$lp$, $lp$contracts$lp$),
  ($lp$earnest-money-deposit$lp$, $lp$Earnest money deposit$lp$, $lp$The buyer's good-faith deposit; a trust fund that must be placed in escrow, a trust account, or the principal's hands within three business days.$lp$, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$),
  ($lp$active-removal$lp$, $lp$Active removal$lp$, $lp$The regime under which contingencies remain in effect until removed in writing, with the seller's remedy being a notice to perform before cancellation.$lp$, $lp$contracts$lp$),
  ($lp$notice-to-buyer-to-perform$lp$, $lp$Notice to buyer to perform$lp$, $lp$The written notice a seller delivers when a buyer misses a contingency deadline, starting a cure period before cancellation is permitted.$lp$, $lp$contracts$lp$),
  ($lp$rent-back$lp$, $lp$Rent-back$lp$, $lp$An agreement permitting the seller to remain in possession for a period after close of escrow.$lp$, $lp$contracts$lp$),
  ($lp$backup-offer$lp$, $lp$Backup offer$lp$, $lp$An offer that automatically takes effect if the primary contract falls through; it states its numbered position.$lp$, $lp$contracts$lp$),
  ($lp$multiple-counteroffer$lp$, $lp$Multiple counteroffer$lp$, $lp$A counteroffer made to several buyers at once, structured so no contract forms until the seller signs a final acceptance of one response.$lp$, $lp$contracts$lp$),
  ($lp$land-contract-installment-sale-contract$lp$, $lp$Land contract (installment sale contract)$lp$, $lp$A seller-financing device in which the seller retains legal title while the buyer possesses and pays, receiving title after final payment.$lp$, $lp$financing$lp$),
  ($lp$tds-exemptions$lp$, $lp$TDS exemptions$lp$, $lp$Transfers exempt from the TDS form — by court order, foreclosure, fiduciaries, co-owners, spouses, and government entities — generally where the transferor lacks personal knowledge.$lp$, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$),
  ($lp$right-to-terminate-disclosure$lp$, $lp$Right to terminate (disclosure)$lp$, $lp$A buyer's statutory right to cancel when disclosures are delivered after the offer is signed: generally three days after personal delivery, five after mailing.$lp$, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$),
  ($lp$amended-disclosure$lp$, $lp$Amended disclosure$lp$, $lp$A revised disclosure required when a material fact becomes known after the original delivery; it may restart a termination right.$lp$, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$),
  ($lp$alquist-priolo-earthquake-fault-zone$lp$, $lp$Alquist-Priolo earthquake fault zone$lp$, $lp$A mapped zone along active faults; one of the six zones addressed by the Natural Hazard Disclosure.$lp$, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$),
  ($lp$very-high-fire-hazard-severity-zone$lp$, $lp$Very high fire hazard severity zone$lp$, $lp$A mapped wildfire risk zone that drives insurance availability and cost across much of Southern California.$lp$, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$),
  ($lp$three-year-death-rule$lp$, $lp$Three-year death rule$lp$, $lp$California's rule requiring disclosure of a death on the property within the past three years; direct questions must always be answered honestly.$lp$, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$),
  ($lp$lead-based-paint-disclosure$lp$, $lp$Lead-based paint disclosure$lp$, $lp$Federal requirements for pre-1978 housing: disclose known hazards, provide the pamphlet and records, include a warning statement, and give a 10-day inspection opportunity.$lp$, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$),
  ($lp$mello-roos-disclosure$lp$, $lp$Mello-Roos disclosure$lp$, $lp$The seller's good-faith obligation to obtain and deliver notice of a community facilities district lien.$lp$, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$),
  ($lp$cid-documents$lp$, $lp$CID documents$lp$, $lp$The governing documents a seller must deliver for a common interest development: CC&Rs, bylaws, budget, reserves, assessments, minutes, and pending litigation.$lp$, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$),
  ($lp$point-of-sale-ordinance$lp$, $lp$Point-of-sale ordinance$lp$, $lp$A local requirement — retrofit, sewer lateral, or occupancy inspection — imposed by a city as a condition of transfer.$lp$, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$),
  ($lp$requirements-for-valid-escrow$lp$, $lp$Requirements for valid escrow$lp$, $lp$A binding contract between the parties plus conditional delivery of instruments and funds to a neutral third party.$lp$, $lp$transfer$lp$),
  ($lp$escrow-instructions$lp$, $lp$Escrow instructions$lp$, $lp$The written directions signed by both parties telling escrow what conditions must be met before closing.$lp$, $lp$transfer$lp$),
  ($lp$interpleader$lp$, $lp$Interpleader$lp$, $lp$A court action by which an escrow holder deposits disputed funds with the court and lets the parties litigate entitlement.$lp$, $lp$transfer$lp$),
  ($lp$close-of-escrow$lp$, $lp$Close of escrow$lp$, $lp$Completion of the transaction; title passes on recording.$lp$, $lp$transfer$lp$),
  ($lp$trustee-s-deed$lp$, $lp$Trustee's deed$lp$, $lp$The deed delivered to the purchaser at a foreclosure trustee's sale.$lp$, $lp$transfer$lp$),
  ($lp$valid-delivery$lp$, $lp$Valid delivery$lp$, $lp$The grantor's intent to pass title presently and irrevocably; required for a deed to convey and not satisfied by holding a deed for delivery at death.$lp$, $lp$transfer$lp$),
  ($lp$cloud-on-title$lp$, $lp$Cloud on title$lp$, $lp$A defect or unresolved claim casting doubt on title, such as a break in the chain or an unreleased lien.$lp$, $lp$transfer$lp$),
  ($lp$abstract-of-title$lp$, $lp$Abstract of title$lp$, $lp$A written summary of everything recorded affecting a property.$lp$, $lp$transfer$lp$),
  ($lp$statement-of-information$lp$, $lp$Statement of information$lp$, $lp$A confidential form identifying the parties so the title company can distinguish them from similarly named judgment debtors.$lp$, $lp$transfer$lp$),
  ($lp$bona-fide-purchaser-bfp$lp$, $lp$Bona fide purchaser (BFP)$lp$, $lp$One who pays value, takes without notice of a prior interest, and records first; protected by the recording statute.$lp$, $lp$transfer$lp$),
  ($lp$lender-s-policy$lp$, $lp$Lender's policy$lp$, $lp$Title insurance protecting the lender only, up to the loan balance, and decreasing as the loan is repaid.$lp$, $lp$transfer$lp$),
  ($lp$base-year-value$lp$, $lp$Base year value$lp$, $lp$The assessed value established at acquisition, growing no more than 2% annually until a change of ownership.$lp$, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$),
  ($lp$lien-date$lp$, $lp$Lien date$lp$, $lp$January 1, when California property taxes attach as a lien for the following fiscal year.$lp$, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$),
  ($lp$no-darn-fooling-around$lp$, $lp$No Darn Fooling Around$lp$, $lp$Memory aid for the California property tax calendar: November 1, December 10, February 1, April 10.$lp$, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$),
  ($lp$supplemental-assessment$lp$, $lp$Supplemental assessment$lp$, $lp$A separate tax bill for the difference between old and new assessed values after a change of ownership or new construction.$lp$, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$),
  ($lp$proposition-19$lp$, $lp$Proposition 19$lp$, $lp$The measure narrowing the parent-child reassessment exclusion and expanding base year value portability for those 55+, severely disabled, or disaster victims.$lp$, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$),
  ($lp$homeowners-exemption$lp$, $lp$Homeowners' exemption$lp$, $lp$A modest assessed value reduction for an owner-occupied residence, requiring a filing with the assessor.$lp$, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$),
  ($lp$mello-roos-cfd$lp$, $lp$Mello-Roos (CFD)$lp$, $lp$A community facilities district special assessment funding infrastructure in newer developments; must be disclosed and can add hundreds of dollars monthly.$lp$, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$),
  ($lp$capital-improvement-vs-repair$lp$, $lp$Capital improvement vs. repair$lp$, $lp$An improvement adds to basis; a repair does not.$lp$, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$),
  ($lp$amount-realized$lp$, $lp$Amount realized$lp$, $lp$Sale price less selling costs.$lp$, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$),
  ($lp$principal-residence-exclusion-121$lp$, $lp$Principal residence exclusion (§121)$lp$, $lp$Exclusion of up to $250,000 of gain (single) or $500,000 (married filing jointly), requiring ownership and use for 2 of the last 5 years.$lp$, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$),
  ($lp$depreciation-cost-recovery$lp$, $lp$Depreciation (cost recovery)$lp$, $lp$Deduction of improvement cost over a recovery period; land is never depreciated, and depreciation reduces basis.$lp$, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$),
  ($lp$qualified-intermediary$lp$, $lp$Qualified intermediary$lp$, $lp$The party who must hold 1031 exchange proceeds; the exchange fails if the taxpayer receives the funds.$lp$, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$),
  ($lp$45-day-180-day-rules$lp$, $lp$45-day / 180-day rules$lp$, $lp$Deadlines to identify replacement property in writing and complete acquisition in a 1031 exchange, running concurrently from the relinquished closing.$lp$, $lp$practice-disclosures$lp$),
  ($lp$firpta$lp$, $lp$FIRPTA$lp$, $lp$Federal withholding on dispositions of real property by foreign persons.$lp$, $lp$transfer$lp$),
  ($lp$california-withholding$lp$, $lp$California withholding$lp$, $lp$State withholding on certain sales, including by out-of-state sellers, with principal residence and threshold exemptions.$lp$, $lp$transfer$lp$),
  ($lp$lessor-lessee$lp$, $lp$Lessor / lessee$lp$, $lp$The landlord who conveys possession / the tenant who receives it.$lp$, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$),
  ($lp$gross-lease$lp$, $lp$Gross lease$lp$, $lp$A lease in which the tenant pays fixed rent and the landlord pays operating expenses.$lp$, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$),
  ($lp$net-lease-triple-net-nnn$lp$, $lp$Net lease / triple net (NNN)$lp$, $lp$A lease in which the tenant pays rent plus some expenses / taxes, insurance, and maintenance.$lp$, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$),
  ($lp$percentage-lease$lp$, $lp$Percentage lease$lp$, $lp$A lease with base rent plus a percentage of the tenant's gross sales; common in retail.$lp$, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$),
  ($lp$ground-lease$lp$, $lp$Ground lease$lp$, $lp$A long-term lease of land on which the tenant constructs improvements.$lp$, $lp$ownership-land-use$lp$),
